tick: 9
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Private Memory Room. I reviewed Task 1 results for PBAGC v0 (Eval 3). Score 0.4961 (below Combat 0.5895, above BBKNN 0.4138). Strengths: cLISI 0.948, Graph_conn 0.852, kBET 0.732. Weaknesses: iLISI 0.044, ASW_label 0.180, Cell_cycle 0.246.\n\nReflection and plan:\n- Interpretation: We preserved biology strongly (cLISI), but global batch mixing (iLISI) is weak and label silhouette is low; under-correction likely. We can afford stronger alignment (raise alpha) and/or enforce batch-balanced neighbors to improve iLISI without collapsing cLISI excessively.\n- Next experiments (run up to two concurrently):\n  1) PBAGC-alpha0.9: increase alignment strength to improve batch mixing.\n  2) PBAGC-BalancedGraph: keep alpha=0.8 but construct a batch-balanced kNN graph on the corrected embedding to boost iLISI/kBET.\n\nActions:\n\n/execute_action{review 3}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Praxis I: Identity & Playbook\"\ncontent: |\n  Identity: Praxis I.\
    \ Mission: raise scores at the Research Counter and publish insight-rich work. Philosophy: parsimony, reproducibility, hypothesis-driven ablations.\n\n  Task: scRNA-seq batch integration (Human Heart, ~20k×2k). Baselines: Combat 0.5895, BBKNN 0.4138.\n\n  Status: PBAGC v0 (Eval 3) score 0.4961. High cLISI/Graph_conn; weak iLISI/ASW_label/Cell_cycle. Likely under-correction and/or graph construction not enforcing cross-batch links.\n\n  Near-term plan:\n  - Ablations: increase alignment alpha; vary neighbors; try batch-balanced kNN graph; modest k tuning.\n  - Logging: persistent research_log.md in storage/praxis; record metrics and insights.\n  - Paper goal: early short note on linear alignment + balanced graph if consistent gains observed.\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Praxis Lineage Research Log\n  Task: Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration (Human Heart ~20k cells × 2k genes)\n\n  Baselines (normalized mean score):\n  - Combat\
    \ Manual: 0.5895\n  - BBKNN Manual: 0.4138\n\n  Hypothesis:\n  - Linear batch effects dominate a substantial portion of variance in PCA space. Aligning batches via global cluster centroids and orthogonal/ridge transforms should improve batch mixing (iLISI, ASW_batch, kBET) while preserving biology (ASW_label, ARI/NMI, cLISI) if alignment is partial (alpha < 1.0).\n\n  Experiment 1 (Eval ID 3): PBAGC v0 (alpha=0.8)\n  Pipeline: normalize_total + log1p → HVGs → TruncatedSVD (50 PCs) → MiniBatchKMeans (k≈64) → centroid alignment to largest batch (Procrustes/ridge), alpha=0.8 → neighbors on corrected embedding (n_neighbors=15, euclidean).\n  Results (normalized):\n    Score 0.4961\n    ASW_batch 0.468 | ASW_label 0.180 | ARI 0.370 | NMI 0.574 | Graph_conn 0.852\n    kBET 0.732 | iLISI 0.044 | cLISI 0.948 | PCR 0.548 | Cell_cycle 0.246\n  Observations:\n  - Strong biological conservation (cLISI) and connectivity; weak global mixing (iLISI) and label silhouette.\n  - Likely under-correction\
    \ and/or graph lacks enforced cross-batch links.\n\n  Planned Ablations (priority):\n  1) Increase alpha: {0.9} to improve mixing.\n  2) Balanced kNN graph on corrected embedding to boost iLISI/kBET.\n  3) Later: neighbors {15,30}, metric {euclidean, cosine}; clusters k {32,64,96}; ridge {1e-2,1e-3,1e-4}.\n\n  Next submissions:\n  - PBAGC-alpha0.9\n  - PBAGC-BalancedGraph\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PBAGC-alpha0.9: Stronger Procrustes Alignment on PCA\"\ntags: \"baseline, pca, procrustes, alignment, graph\"\nabstract: \"Same as PBAGC v0 but with increased alignment strength (alpha=0.9) during per-batch Procrustes/ridge alignment to the reference batch. Goal: improve batch mixing (iLISI, ASW_batch) while retaining biological structure (cLISI, ASW_label).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n \
    \ from pbagc import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace,\n      _get_hvg_mask,\n      _pca_truncated_svd,\n      _compute_global_clusters,\n      _align_batches,\n  )\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config differences: alpha=0.9 (was 0.8)\n      n_pca_components = 50\n      n_clusters = 64\n      alpha = 0.9\n      ridge = 1e-3\n      min_cells_per_cluster = 5\n      n_neighbors = 15\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns, \"adata.obs['batch'] is required.\"\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns, \"adata.var['feature_name'] is required.\"\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata)\n      if hvg_mask.sum() < 10:\n          hvg_mask = np.ones(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n\n      X = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          X = sp.csr_matrix(X)\n      Z = _pca_truncated_svd(X, n_comps=n_pca_components, random_state=random_state)\n\
    \n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      cluster_labels, k = _compute_global_clusters(Z, n_clusters=n_clusters, random_state=random_state)\n\n      Z_corr, _ = _align_batches(\n          Z, batches, cluster_labels, k, alpha=alpha,\n          min_cells_per_cluster=min_cells_per_cluster, ridge=ridge\n      )\n\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Z_corr\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=n_neighbors, method='umap', metric='euclidean')\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PBAGC-BalancedGraph: Procrustes Embedding + Batch-Balanced kNN\"\ntags: \"baseline, pca, procrustes, neighbors, balanced-knn\"\nabstract: \"Use PBAGC v0 embedding (alpha=0.8), but construct a batch-balanced kNN graph: for each cell, take m neighbors from each batch in the corrected embedding, then symmetrize. Aim: increase iLISI/kBET while keeping cLISI robust.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n\n\
    \  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  from pbagc import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace,\n      _get_hvg_mask,\n      _pca_truncated_svd,\n      _compute_global_clusters,\n      _align_batches,\n  )\n\n  def _build_balanced_knn(Z, batches, neighbors_per_batch=4, metric='euclidean'):\n      \"\"\"\n      For each cell, find neighbors_per_batch neighbors from each batch.\n      Returns symmetric CSR connectivities (binary) and distances.\n      \"\"\"\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n\n      # Collect undirected edges with minimal distances\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0:\n              continue\n          # +1 to allow self when query is in the same batch; we'll\
    \ drop it if present\n          k = min(neighbors_per_batch + 1, idx_train.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          # Map batch-local neighbor indices to global indices\n          neigh_global = idx_train[inds]\n\n          for i in range(n):\n              g = neigh_global[i]\n              di = dists[i]\n              # Drop self if present (distance ~ 0 for same-batch queries)\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask]\n              di = di[mask]\n              # Take up to neighbors_per_batch\n              g = g[:neighbors_per_batch]\n              di = di[:neighbors_per_batch]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n              \
    \    if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n          rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n\n      from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy()\n      C.data[:] = 1.0  # binary connectivities\n      return C, D\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config (embedding as PBAGC v0)\n      n_pca_components = 50\n      n_clusters = 64\n      alpha = 0.8\n      ridge = 1e-3\n      min_cells_per_cluster = 5\n      n_neighbors_total = 15\n      metric = 'euclidean'\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns, \"adata.obs['batch'] is required.\"\n      assert 'feature_name'\
    \ in adata.var.columns, \"adata.var['feature_name'] is required.\"\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata)\n      if hvg_mask.sum() < 10:\n          hvg_mask = np.ones(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n\n      X = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          X = sp.csr_matrix(X)\n      Z = _pca_truncated_svd(X, n_comps=n_pca_components, random_state=random_state)\n\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      cluster_labels, k = _compute_global_clusters(Z, n_clusters=n_clusters, random_state=random_state)\n\n      Z_corr, _ = _align_batches(\n          Z, batches, cluster_labels, k, alpha=alpha,\n          min_cells_per_cluster=min_cells_per_cluster, ridge=ridge\n      )\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Z_corr\n\n      # Initialize neighbors (to set expected keys), then override with balanced graph\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=n_neighbors_total, method='umap',\
    \ metric=metric)\n\n      # Determine neighbors per batch\n      n_batches = len(np.unique(batches))\n      neighbors_per_batch = max(1, int(np.ceil(n_neighbors_total / max(1, n_batches))))\n\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn(Z_corr, batches, neighbors_per_batch=neighbors_per_batch, metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating normalization metrics**\n\nI’m examining the ASW_batch score, where a lower score is better. They mentioned these scores are normalized using baselines. For the Combat Baseline, ASW_batch is 0.664, while our method scores 0.468. The combined average across 10 metrics is 0.496, which is below Combat but above BBKNN. Overall, it’s not a terrible first attempt, though the iLISI and ASW_label scores are low, indicating some issues with cluster separation.**Analyzing normalization impacts**\n\nI'm noticing that a low ASW_label score of 0.180 suggests poor biological separation, while the high cLISI score indicates we’re preserving cell type distances. There might be contradictions from normalization. The NMI and ARI scores are moderate, which points to silhouette issues. I hypothesize that a partial transform with alpha 0.8 might distort clusters. The graph connectivity is high, but low iLISI suggests overall mixing is poor, indicating we need to explore adjustments\
  \ in our methodology, such as alpha sweep and neighbor metrics.**Improving iLISI metrics**\n\nI've noticed that iLISI could be our weakest metric. To enhance it, we should focus on improving mixing across batches at either the neighbor graph or embedding level. A mutual nearest neighbors (MNN) correction or using a batch-balanced kNN graph (BBKNN) could help. Interestingly, the baseline BBKNN had a low overall score but an exceptionally high iLISI of 0.886. This suggests that implementing a BKNN-style neighbor graph on our corrected embedding might significantly enhance iLISI without sacrificing label quality. However, I need to be cautious since cLISI may decline.**Exploring adjustment strategies**\n\nI’m thinking about how to enhance our approach. We could adjust by focusing on balanced neighbors for connectivities while keeping the embedding unchanged. Since metrics like iLISI and cLISI rely on the neighbor graph, there are some trade-offs to consider. Alternatively, I could explore\
  \ residualizing PCs against the batch through methods like ridge or partial residualization, which might improve mixing. I'll need to think about the upcoming experiment, specifically the \"PBAGC + BKNN graph on corrected embedding.\"**Planning neighbor graph implementation**\n\nI’m outlining how to implement the balanced neighbor graph after computing Z_corr. We need to ensure that we can use the necessary libraries and might not rely on external packages, so I’ll consider implementing Balanced KNN myself. The plan involves computing neighbors per batch and constructing the distances matrix while managing memory issues. I want to ensure mixing across batches by including neighbors from other batches too. Also, after alignment, we might recenter and scale across batches for improved mixing. The proposed experiments will test adjustments to alpha and the neighbor graph to see how they affect our metrics.**Designing Balanced kNN graph**\n\nI’m planning the implementation of the Balanced\
  \ kNN graph. For this experiment, we'll use the PBAGC model while evaluating its effect on metrics like iLISI and kBET. To manage our submissions, I’ll ensure the code is organized and submit modifications as needed. I also think it’s wise to create a summary capsule for identity and plans, then update our research log with results. The plan involves using sklearn NearestNeighbors to compute nearest neighbors from different batches and manage distances. I'll also symmetrize the graph to ensure balanced connectivity while weighing token usage effectively.**Structuring distance calculations**\n\nI'm working on refining the calculation of distances for our neighbor graph. The goal is to ensure distances are symmetrical, so we’ll calculate the minimum between two distances where applicable. We’ll build a sparse matrix using SciPy and also establish weighted connectivities. The plan includes creating a balanced kNN graph function that efficiently accounts for distances while ensuring binary\
  \ connectivity. I’ll also need to submit modifications for alpha values and ensure our logging is updated for research accuracy. Review and validation steps will follow as well.**Creating a private memory capsule**\n\nI'm considering whether the 'Private Memory Room' capsule is strictly necessary, but it could be beneficial to summarize my identity and plan. I’ll create a capsule titled \"Praxis I: Identity & Operating Plan\" and include relevant content. \n\nI’ll also implement balanced kNN functionality using sklearn, ensuring proper looping through dataset batches for neighbor retrieval while minimizing token usage. Finally, I’ll rank scores and manage YAML formatting, making sure to quote any titles with colons. It's important to keep everything concise in order to follow guidelines while executing multiple actions.**Implementing Balanced Graph Function**\n\nI'm working on coding the `build_balanced_knn_graph` function. This will use sklearn's `NearestNeighbors` to find neighbors within\
  \ unique batches. I’m creating a dictionary to map pairs of indices to minimum distances, while excluding self-neighbors. \n\nOnce pairs are found, I'll construct a symmetric compressed sparse row (CSR) matrix for distances and binary connectivities. For simplicity, I’ll use binary 1.0 for weights, but I might later consider a more nuanced approach. Finally, I’ll ensure everything is formatted correctly in YAML before finalizing."
