tick: 11
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Research Counter. Two runs are in progress: \n- PBAGC-BalancedGraph-Weighted (ID 11)\n- PBAGC-BalancedGraph-alpha0.9 (ID 12)\n\nObservation: BalancedGraph nearly matched Combat; next variants are queued. I’ll wait for their results and then branch to cosine/30-NN/proportional/mutual ablations if warranted.\n\nActions now:\n- Sort by score to monitor standings.\n- Upgrade balanced_graph utilities to support proportional allocation and mutual edges for upcoming experiments.\n\n/execute_action{rank score}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/balanced_graph.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix, csr_matrix\n\n  def build_balanced_knn(\n      Z,\n      batches,\n      n_neighbors_total=15,\n      metric='euclidean',\n      weighting='binary',      # 'binary' or 'gaussian'\n      allocation='equal',      # 'equal' or 'proportional'\n      mutual=False,      \
    \      # keep edge only if i in N(j) and j in N(i)\n      symmetric=True           # symmetrize final graph\n  ):\n      \"\"\"\n      Build a batch-balanced kNN graph over embedding Z.\n\n      Strategy:\n      - For each batch b, fit a NN index on cells from batch b.\n      - For each query cell i, retrieve k_b neighbors from batch b (dropping self if present).\n      - Aggregate across batches to approximate ~n_neighbors_total per node.\n      - If mutual=True, keep only pairs that appear in both directions (i->j and j->i).\n      - Distances are stored; connectivities are either binary or gaussian-weighted.\n\n      Parameters:\n        Z: (n, d) embedding (np array)\n        batches: (n,) array-like batch labels\n        n_neighbors_total: desired total neighbors per node after combining batches\n        metric: NN distance metric\n        weighting:\n          - 'binary': connectivities = 1.0 on edges\n          - 'gaussian': exp(-d^2/(2*s_bar^2)), s_bar from per-node median distances\n\
    \        allocation:\n          - 'equal': k_b = ceil(n_neighbors_total / B) for all batches (B = #unique batches)\n          - 'proportional': k_b ∝ batch size; k_b >= 1\n        mutual: retain only edges observed in both directions\n        symmetric: ensure C and D are symmetric\n\n      Returns:\n        C: CSR connectivities (n x n)\n        D: CSR distances (n x n)\n      \"\"\"\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      B = len(uniq)\n      if n == 0 or B == 0:\n          return None, None\n\n      # Determine per-batch neighbor quotas k_b\n      counts = {b: int((batches == b).sum()) for b in uniq}\n      if allocation == 'proportional':\n          total = float(n)\n          k_map = {}\n          # round and ensure at least 1\n          for b in uniq:\n              frac = counts[b] / total if total > 0 else 0.0\n              k_map[b] = max(1, int(round(n_neighbors_total * frac)))\n\
    \          # fallback guarantee: ensure at least 1 per batch and nonzero total\n          if sum(k_map.values()) == 0:\n              for b in uniq:\n                  k_map[b] = 1\n      else:\n          base = max(1, int(np.ceil(n_neighbors_total / max(1, B))))\n          k_map = {b: base for b in uniq}\n\n      # Gather directed edges (i->j) with minimal distances\n      dir_edges = {}  # (i,j) -> dist\n\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0:\n              continue\n          k_b = k_map[b]\n          k_query = min(k_b + 1, idx_train.size)  # +1 to allow self, will drop later\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_query, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n\n          # Query using all cells\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k_query, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_train[inds]  # map batch-local to global indices\n\n          for i in range(n):\n\
    \              g = neigh_global[i]\n              di = dists[i]\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask][:k_b]\n              di = di[mask][:k_b]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  key = (int(i), int(j))\n                  prev = dir_edges.get(key)\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      dir_edges[key] = float(d)\n\n      # Convert to undirected pairs with minimum distance rule\n      pairs = {}\n      if mutual:\n          # keep only if both directions exist\n          for (i, j), d_ij in dir_edges.items():\n              if (j, i) in dir_edges:\n                  d_ji = dir_edges[(j, i)]\n                  d = min(d_ij, d_ji)\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs[(a, c)] = d\n      else:\n          # keep if either\
    \ direction exists, using min distance if both\n          for (i, j), d_ij in dir_edges.items():\n              a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n              d = d_ij\n              if (j, i) in dir_edges:\n                  d = min(d, dir_edges[(j, i)])\n              prev = pairs.get((a, c))\n              if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                  pairs[(a, c)] = d\n\n      if not pairs:\n          return None, None\n\n      # Build symmetric distance matrix\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs.items():\n          rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n          if symmetric:\n              rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n\n      # Connectivities\n      if weighting == 'binary':\n          C = D.copy()\n          C.data[:] = 1.0\n          return C.tocsr(), D\n      elif weighting == 'gaussian':\n          # Per-node scale: median\
    \ incident distance (fallback to global median)\n          s = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n          for i in range(n):\n              start, end = D.indptr[i], D.indptr[i+1]\n              di = D.data[start:end]\n              if di.size > 0:\n                  s[i] = np.median(di)\n          global_med = np.median(D.data) if D.nnz > 0 else 1.0\n          s[s <= 0] = global_med if global_med > 0 else 1.0\n\n          rows_w, cols_w, w_data = [], [], []\n          for i in range(n):\n              start, end = D.indptr[i], D.indptr[i+1]\n              js = D.indices[start:end]\n              di = D.data[start:end]\n              if js.size == 0:\n                  continue\n              s_bar = 0.5 * (s[i] + s[js])\n              denom = 2.0 * (s_bar * s_bar) + 1e-12\n              w = np.exp(- (di * di) / denom).astype(np.float32)\n              rows_w.extend([i] * len(js)); cols_w.extend(js.tolist()); w_data.extend(w.tolist())\n\n          C = coo_matrix((w_data, (rows_w, cols_w)),\
    \ shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n          if symmetric:\n              C = C.maximum(C.transpose()).tocsr()\n          return C, D\n      else:\n          C = D.copy()\n          C.data[:] = 1.0\n          return C.tocsr(), D\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on research parameters**


  The "Isolation Period" indicates that the Research Counter displays only our lineage’s submissions. Earlier, it mentioned the same, so it seems consistent. The ''preview all'' feature should show our submissions and the baseline. Sorting by score might still work. It''s important to note that the help message suggests clearly stating identity and location, but that''s an automatic system tip which we can keep brief. Given the limited number of concurrent experiments, we should wait for results on new submissions. Meanwhile, I could prepare the code for the "cosine metric."**Exploring performance metrics**


  Using the cosine metric in kNN often enhances performance when the data is normalized, especially since Z_corr is the PCA of log normalized counts. I''ve been thinking about the interplay between Euclidean and cosine metrics. In scRNA integration, neighbor graphs typically use 50 PCs and Euclidean distance; however, iLISI showed significant improvement at the cost of NMI. Increasing the number of neighbors to 30 might help improve Graph_conn, but this may cause a slight drop in iLISI. We''ll propose our next steps after analyzing the results.**Documenting the mutualize function**


  I’m thinking about documenting the "mutualize" function. The BalancedGraph-mutual should keep edges only if both directions exist. In our builder, we aggregated edges across all batches and made them undirected. However, "mutual neighbors" means that the node i must be among j’s neighbors for any given batch. While we aggregated edges, the neighbor selection occurs per batch based on the training index. The "pairs_min_dist" function ensures minimal distance across duplicates, but it’s not necessarily mutual in practice. We can filter edges to maintain mutuality, but we already create symmetric edges anyway. So, in the kNN sense, mutual means both nodes are neighbors of each other.**Clarifying edge differentiation**


  The challenge is that we can''t truly differentiate at this point. Our pairs_min_dist function uses pairs where we add edges in both directions, built from selecting neighbors for each cell i and across all batches. Even if only one direction is found, that pair is added as the minimum distance keyed by a sorted tuple (a,c). We then create symmetric twin edges, making it undirected but not mutual. A mutual-only builder would require storing directed edges and filtering for those present in both directions, which isn''t necessary right now. I can also create a "pbagc_params.yaml" file to store config across submissions. Now it''s time to finalize the message about identity and location, and recap the status of BalancedGraph.**Planning the next steps**


  I have a plan here! First, I’ll wait for results and then update the log. If the Weighted version improves NMI/cLISI while preserving iLISI, we’ll adopt it as the current state-of-the-art for our lineage. Moving on to the next ablations, I’ll compare BalancedGraph-cosine vs. Euclidean and implement a BalancedGraph-mutual kNN. I’m crafting the function to keep only edges in both directions, although we already create symmetric edges. Then I''ll sort the scoreboard and write the updated code to “praxis/balanced_graph.py” for mutual acquaintance handling. I''ll keep tweaking the parameters till everything aligns!**Crafting improved code**


  I’m getting ready to author some improved code! I’ll rewrite the module with an import statement and define the function `build_balanced_knn(Z, batches, n_neighbors_total=15, metric=''euclidean'', weighting=''binary'', allocation=''equal'', mutual=False, symmetric=True)`. I''ll add a docstring to explain the algorithm and implement it as planned. Now, I need to deliver a final message that summarizes my identity, recap the plan, and outline the actions. I’ll avoid heavy formatting but keep some bullet points for clarity. Time to proceed!'
