parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, at the Research Counter. Current best: PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine (Eval 16) with score 0.5930, surpassing Combat (0.5895). Two new variants are running: cosine-30 and cosine-proportional.\n\nPlan while waiting:\n- Prepare a private draft outlining the balanced graph approach and preliminary results.\n- Then monitor new results and iterate based on outcomes (neighbors=30 vs proportional).\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft: Balanced Graph Batch Integration (Praxis I)\"\ncontent: |\n  Working title: Simple Batch-Balanced Graph Construction for scRNA-seq Integration\n\n  Motivation:\n  - Standard neighbor graphs can reflect batch biases. Enforcing cross-batch balance in kNN selection markedly improves integration metrics (iLISI, ARI/NMI) without complex models.\n\n  Method (PBAGC + BalancedGraph):\n  - Preprocess: normalize_total + log1p; HVGs (batch-aware if provided).\n  - Embed: TruncatedSVD to 50 PCs.\n\
    \  - Light alignment: per-batch Procrustes/ridge alignment to a reference batch with partial strength (alpha=0.8).\n  - Graph: For each query cell, retrieve equal quotas of neighbors from each batch using cosine distance; aggregate edges and use binary connectivities; symmetrize.\n\n  Key design choices:\n  - Binary edges > gaussian-weighted (weights reduced iLISI).\n  - Cosine distance > euclidean (improved ARI/NMI/Graph_conn with minimal iLISI change).\n  - Mutual neighbors (reciprocal) hurt Graph_conn and iLISI—too sparse.\n  - Alignment strength has limited impact once the graph is balanced.\n\n  Results (normalized scores):\n  - Baselines: Combat 0.5895; BBKNN 0.4138.\n  - PBAGC-BalancedGraph (euclidean, equal, 15): 0.5885; iLISI 0.903; ARI 0.540; Graph_conn 0.853; cLISI 0.891.\n  - PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine (equal, 15): 0.5930 (SOTA); iLISI 0.894; ARI 0.546; NMI 0.535; Graph_conn 0.877; cLISI 0.905.\n  - Negative results: mutual edges (0.4428), gaussian weights (0.5632), stronger\
    \ alignment (0.5829 vs 0.5885).\n\n  Next experiments:\n  - Cosine + equal quotas, total neighbors 30 (running).\n  - Cosine + proportional quotas, total neighbors 15 (running).\n  - If gains: evaluate cosine+30 proportional; else revert to equal.\n  - Later: ablate k (clusters), ridge strength, post-batch re-centering, or simple residualization.\n\n  Reproducibility:\n  - Code modularized in storage/praxis/{pbagc.py, balanced_graph.py}.\n  - One-change-at-a-time ablations; log kept in storage/praxis/research_log.md.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Moving Forward with scRNA-seq Integration**


  I need to keep progressing with Praxis I, focusing on scRNA-seq batch integration. Right now, I''ve queued two experiments related to cosine neighbors and proportional quotas. One is running, and I''ll be getting the results soon. So far, BalancedGraph cosine produced a state-of-the-art performance, while others like BalancedGraph mutual and the weighted gaussian didn’t fare well. The Archive Room is unavailable until we reach maturity, but I can prepare materials privately and keep my research log updated.**Updating Methods for Graph Analysis**


  I need to make sure we persist the code for the new methods using balanced_graph.py, which I''ve already updated to include allocation and mutual flags. We might explore more ablations, like re-centering per batch PC for better mixing while keeping labels intact. It''s clear that a balanced graph is crucial. I’m considering proposing variants like BalancedGraph-cosine-30-proportional and BalancedGraph-cosine-equal-30-mutual. The latter might not work well due to its harmful effects, so I want to ensure connectivity while designing a combined graph that integrates both batch-balanced and within-batch neighbors.**Addressing BalancedGraph Issues**


  It seems like the issue with BalancedGraph-mutual is that it drastically reduced edges, which hurt graph connectivity. I think using 30 neighbors could help fix this. We could explore variable neighbor quotas that allow each batch to have more neighbors, but we need to be cautious since we want to minimize batch effects, making equal quotas across batches ideal. I can also test "cosine 15 vs. 30" for performance. While testing "cosine equal 15 with local reweighting by unsupervised clusters," it appears that weighting by cluster membership might help. However, previous edge weighting methods didn’t contribute positively, and I’m unsure if the evaluation metrics accurately reflect graph connectivity.**Exploring Alignment and Metrics**


  I’m considering re-running the alignment using ridge/residualization instead of Procrustes. However, with BalancedGraph dominating, I wonder if these changes will significantly affect cLISI and ASW_label. BalancedGraph-cosine gave us an ARI of 0.546 and an NMI of 0.535, but the ASW_label has consistently remained at 0.180 across multiple runs. It seems like this might be a baseline limit. Although ARI and NMI improved, I need to think about building connectivities from distances normalized by per-node sigma or using mutual neighbors with a top-K threshold while being cautious of potential impacts on connectivity. It’s challenging to predict.**Planning Next Steps for Research**


  I need to focus on disabling the initial call for `sc.pp.neighbors`, as we’re overriding connectivities and distances. It''s fine to call it for key initialization, though. I should keep the "test" function unused and ensure we abide by GPU settings. While waiting for results, I can create private memory notes and an outline for a short paper. I''ll set up actions like ranking scores, but it’s unnecessary to check "page 2" or run evaluations just yet. Instead, I can draft a balanced graph integration outline in private memory. Should be productive while we monitor results!'
