tick: 15
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, at the Research Counter. Current best is PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-30 with score 0.5992 (SOTA). Two experiments are running (24 and 45 neighbors).\n\nReflection and plan:\n- Equal per-batch quotas with cosine distance and binary edges are consistently strong. Increasing neighbors from 15 to 30 improved overall score via Graph_conn and iLISI.\n- Proportional quotas harmed iLISI; mutual edges made the graph too sparse; gaussian weighting hurt iLISI.\n- Next improvement: cap the per-node union of cross-batch neighbors to exactly N total while guaranteeing at least one neighbor from each batch. This should reduce oversmoothing and may raise ARI/NMI/cLISI without losing iLISI.\n\nActions:\n- Implement a capped balanced graph builder in a new module (to avoid affecting running jobs).\n- Update the research log with the planned next experiment (cosine-30 capped).\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/balanced_graph_v2.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\
    \  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n\n  def build_balanced_knn_capped(\n      Z,\n      batches,\n      n_neighbors_total=30,\n      metric='cosine',\n      weighting='binary',      # 'binary' or 'gaussian'\n      allocation='equal',      # 'equal' or 'proportional'\n      ensure_min_per_batch=True,\n      symmetric=True\n  ):\n      \"\"\"\n      Build a batch-balanced kNN graph with an exact per-node cap on total neighbors.\n      Steps:\n        1) For each batch b, fit a NN index on cells from b.\n        2) For each query cell i, retrieve k_b neighbors from each batch (dropping self).\n        3) Union candidates across batches, then cap to n_neighbors_total by smallest distances.\n           If ensure_min_per_batch, preselect 1 neighbor per batch (if available) before capping.\n        4) Aggregate directed edges; return symmetric CSR connectivities and distances.\n\n      Returns:\n        C: CSR connectivities (binary or gaussian-weighted)\n\
    \        D: CSR distances\n      \"\"\"\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      B = len(uniq)\n      if n == 0 or B == 0 or n_neighbors_total <= 0:\n          return None, None\n\n      # Determine per-batch quotas\n      counts = {b: int((batches == b).sum()) for b in uniq}\n      if allocation == 'proportional':\n          total = float(n)\n          k_map = {}\n          for b in uniq:\n              frac = counts[b] / total if total > 0 else 0.0\n              k_map[b] = max(1, int(round(n_neighbors_total * frac)))\n          if sum(k_map.values()) == 0:\n              for b in uniq:\n                  k_map[b] = 1\n      else:\n          base = max(1, int(np.ceil(n_neighbors_total / max(1, B))))\n          k_map = {b: base for b in uniq}\n\n      # Fit per-batch NN indices and query all cells\n      dists_per_b = {}\n      neighs_per_b = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train\
    \ = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0:\n              continue\n          k_b = min(k_map[b] + 1, idx_train.size)  # +1 to allow self\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_b, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k_b, return_distance=True)\n          neighs_per_b[b] = idx_train[inds]  # global indices of neighbors\n          dists_per_b[b] = dists\n\n      # Collect capped edges\n      rows, cols, dist_vals = [], [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          cand = []\n          # Preselect one per batch if requested\n          preselected = []\n          if ensure_min_per_batch:\n              for b in uniq:\n                  if b not in neighs_per_b:\n                      continue\n                  g = neighs_per_b[b][i]\n                  di = dists_per_b[b][i]\n                  # drop self\n                  mask = g != i\n                  g = g[mask]\n         \
    \         di = di[mask]\n                  if g.size > 0:\n                      # take best 1 for preselection\n                      preselected.append((int(g[0]), float(di[0])))\n                      # also keep remaining as candidates\n                      for j, d in zip(g[1:], di[1:]):\n                          cand.append((int(j), float(d)))\n          # Add top (k_b) from each batch to candidates\n          for b in uniq:\n              if b not in neighs_per_b:\n                  continue\n              g = neighs_per_b[b][i]\n              di = dists_per_b[b][i]\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask]\n              di = di[mask]\n              k_b = k_map[b] if k_map[b] > 0 else 0\n              take = int(min(k_b, g.size))\n              if take > 0:\n                  for j, d in zip(g[:take], di[:take]):\n                      cand.append((int(j), float(d)))\n\n          # Merge duplicates keeping smallest distance\n          tmp = {}\n          for j,\
    \ d in preselected + cand:\n              prev = tmp.get(j)\n              if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                  tmp[j] = d\n\n          # Rank by distance and cap to n_neighbors_total\n          items = sorted(tmp.items(), key=lambda x: x[1])\n          selected = items[:n_neighbors_total]\n\n          for j, d in selected:\n              if i == j:\n                  continue\n              rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_vals.append(d)\n              if symmetric:\n                  rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_vals.append(d)\n\n      if not rows:\n          return None, None\n\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_vals, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n\n      # Build connectivities\n      if weighting == 'binary':\n          C = D.copy()\n          C.data[:] = 1.0\n      elif weighting == 'gaussian':\n          # Per-node scale: median incident distance (fallback to global median)\n          s = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n          for i in range(n):\n\
    \              start, end = D.indptr[i], D.indptr[i+1]\n              di = D.data[start:end]\n              if di.size > 0:\n                  s[i] = np.median(di)\n          global_med = np.median(D.data) if D.nnz > 0 else 1.0\n          s[s <= 0] = global_med if global_med > 0 else 1.0\n\n          rw, cw, wv = [], [], []\n          for i in range(n):\n              start, end = D.indptr[i], D.indptr[i+1]\n              js = D.indices[start:end]\n              di = D.data[start:end]\n              if js.size == 0:\n                  continue\n              s_bar = 0.5 * (s[i] + s[js])\n              denom = 2.0 * (s_bar * s_bar) + 1e-12\n              w = np.exp(- (di * di) / denom).astype(np.float32)\n              rw.extend([i]*len(js)); cw.extend(js.tolist()); wv.extend(w.tolist())\n          C = coo_matrix((wv, (rw, cw)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n          if symmetric:\n              C = C.maximum(C.transpose()).tocsr()\n      else:\n          C = D.copy()\n          C.data[:] =\
    \ 1.0\n\n      return C.tocsr(), D\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Praxis Lineage Research Log (updates)\n  SOTA: cosine, equal, 30 neighbors → 0.5992.\n  Hypothesis: Capping the union of per-batch neighbors to exactly N per node (while ensuring ≥1 per batch) will reduce oversmoothing and may improve ARI/NMI/cLISI without harming iLISI.\n\n  Implementation:\n  - Added storage/praxis/balanced_graph_v2.py with build_balanced_knn_capped (binary/gaussian, equal/proportional, exact cap, ensure_min_per_batch).\n  Next planned experiment (after slots free):\n  - PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-30-cap: use capped builder (binary, equal, cosine, N=30).\n  - If positive: sweep N in {24, 30, 36} with cap; compare to uncapped results.\n```\n\nI’ll wait for the two running evaluations (24 and 45 neighbors) to finish before submitting the capped-30 variant."
thinking_content: "**Evaluating experiment status**\n\nI need to respond to the latest status at the Research Counter. There are currently two experiments running with IDs 25 and 26. They were submitted and are still active in the Running Experiments table. Previously, in tick 14, we got results for experiments 20 and 21. Since we can only run a maximum of 2 evaluations per agent, we should update the research log and plan the next experiments for the queue once these finish. We might also start preparing for an Archive paper later!**Understanding archive room status**\n\nThe Archive Room is currently marked as unavailable because it requires maturity, which we don't have yet since we are at age 15 and need to be 50. I remember the earlier listing mentioning this after ascension. While we can't publish anything, we can prepare drafts in Private Memory. For our experiments, the balanced graph approach with the cosine neighbor metric is key—so far, 30 neighbors with equal quotas scored the\
  \ best at 0.5992. We're proposing to test 24 neighbors (running ID 25) and 45 neighbors (running ID 26).**Planning future experiments**\n\nI'm considering testing 36 neighbors and exploring not just equal quotas, but also minimum per batch and filling the remainder by distance. The earlier spec prohibits using cell types, so we're good. I can also experiment with the BalancedGraph without embedding alignment, setting alpha to 0.0, to see if alignment matters. Previous results hinted it doesn't, but I want to check. We might queue future experiments like PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine testing alpha versus 0.8. Additionally, I’m thinking about using Jaccard weighting for adjacency to enhance Graph_conn and labeling metrics!**Exploring weighting effects**\n\nI remember we've previously seen that certain weightings can harm iLISI, but maybe Jaccard weights derived from KNN won't be as detrimental. The implementation could be heavy but manageable, so testing this might be worthwhile. I also considered\
  \ using PAGA or connectivity weighting from the Scanpy method, which employs connectivities and distances. For our BalancedGraph, we have set connectivities as either binary or Gaussian, affecting our metrics. Jaccard might help maintain mixing while improving cluster separation, but I'm unsure. Plus, I want to measure neighbors per batch with equal quotas while accounting for small sample counts.**Refining neighbor selection process**\n\nI need to ensure we precisely control the total number of neighbors by globally selecting the best distances from combined sets while ensuring at least one from each batch. This could refine our edges. I'm considering implementing an improved builder that computes neighbors across batches, sorts them, and builds edges accordingly while reducing excess connections. The idea also includes introducing a \"balanced capped\" method. I'm leaning toward updating `balanced_graph.py` to support these changes but I might also create a new file like `balanced_graph_v2.py`\
  \ to avoid issues with ongoing evaluations. \n\nNow, I’ll outline the plan: update the research log, write the new module, and prepare for future experiments.**Implementing capped neighbor selection**\n\nTo avoid exceeding our total neighbor count, I’ll generate candidates for each batch, ensuring the same cell doesn’t duplicate across them. We can sort these by distance to select the top `n_neighbors_total`—which is good since duplicates aren't an issue.\n\nI'll implement the process using loops for symmetry, computing pairwise edges and distances. The complexity may be high, but with `sklearn`’s NearestNeighbors, it should be manageable. After writing the new module, I’ll prepare to submit but wait for results since we have running evaluations. \n\nFinally, I'll summarize the actions needed, including updating the research log and writing the new module.**Preparing neighbor selection and edge creation**\n\nI’m setting up the distance arrays for each batch and creating candidate lists\
  \ for neighbors. The goal is to cap the total number of neighbors by distance and ensure we select at least one from each batch if possible, while still adhering to the balanced equal quotas.\n\nI’ll implement a simple structure that defaults to ensuring at least one per batch. After selection, I'll add edges, using Gaussian weights. I'll handle memory efficiently and convert the results to the desired format. Once I’ve completed the coding and logging, I'll wrap it up and wait for results."
