parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, at the Research Counter. New result: cosine, equal, 60 neighbors achieved 0.6001 (new SOTA). One run is still pending (45 neighbors, no alignment).\n\nReflection:\n- Increasing total neighbors (equal quotas, cosine, binary) steadily raised Graph_conn and iLISI, nudging the overall score above 0.60 despite slight drops in ARI/NMI/cLISI.\n- Capped graphs modestly improved ARI/NMI but hurt iLISI; net effect worse than uncapped.\n- Next, test 75 neighbors to see if score climbs further. Then evaluate no-alignment result to decide on parsimony.\n\nActions:\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Praxis Lineage Research Log (updates)\n  SOTA progression (cosine, equal quotas, binary):\n  - 45 → 0.5999 | ARI 0.531, NMI 0.513, Graph_conn 0.954, iLISI 0.938, cLISI 0.889\n  - 60 → 0.6001 | ARI 0.529, NMI 0.512, Graph_conn 0.970, iLISI 0.935, cLISI 0.882\n  Capped variants (30/36) improved ARI/NMI slightly but reduced iLISI; overall\
    \ worse than uncapped.\n  Plan:\n  - Test 75 neighbors (uncapped) to see if Graph_conn/iLISI gains push score higher.\n  - Await 45-noalign result to decide if alignment can be dropped without hurting mixing.\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-75: Binary Balanced kNN with Cosine (75 neighbors)\"\ntags: \"pca, procrustes, neighbors, balanced-knn, cosine\"\nabstract: \"Extend the best-performing uncapped, binary, equal-quotas cosine graph to 75 total neighbors to test whether further gains in iLISI/Graph_conn increase the composite score beyond 0.600.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  from pbagc import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace,\n      _get_hvg_mask,\n      _pca_truncated_svd,\n      _compute_global_clusters,\n      _align_batches,\n  )\n  from balanced_graph import build_balanced_knn\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata:\
    \ ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config: cosine metric, equal allocation, total neighbors = 75 (uncapped)\n      n_pca_components = 50\n      n_clusters = 64\n      alpha = 0.8\n      ridge = 1e-3\n      min_cells_per_cluster = 5\n      n_neighbors_total = 75\n      metric = 'cosine'\n\n      print(f\"[Config] PCs={n_pca_components}, neighbors={n_neighbors_total}, metric={metric}, alpha={alpha}\")\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns, \"adata.obs['batch'] is required.\"\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns, \"adata.var['feature_name'] is required.\"\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata)\n      if hvg_mask.sum() < 10:\n          hvg_mask = np.ones(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n\n      X = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          X = sp.csr_matrix(X)\n      Z = _pca_truncated_svd(X, n_comps=n_pca_components, random_state=0)\n      print(f\"[Embed] Z shape: {Z.shape}\")\n\n      batches\
    \ = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      cluster_labels, k = _compute_global_clusters(Z, n_clusters=n_clusters, random_state=0)\n\n      Z_corr, _ = _align_batches(Z, batches, cluster_labels, k, alpha=alpha,\n                                 min_cells_per_cluster=min_cells_per_cluster, ridge=ridge)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Z_corr\n\n      # Initialize neighbor keys, then overwrite with balanced graph\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=n_neighbors_total, method='umap', metric=metric)\n\n      C, D = build_balanced_knn(\n          Z_corr, batches, n_neighbors_total=n_neighbors_total,\n          metric=metric, weighting='binary', allocation='equal', mutual=False, symmetric=True\n      )\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating scRNA-seq Integration**


  My main focus is on scRNA-seq integration, where I''ve achieved a score above 0.6. I might try using 75 neighbors, although that could lead to oversmoothing. I''ve noticed that increasing N affects iLISI and Graph_conn positively but seems to slightly reduce cLISI and ARI/NMI. The score improved from 0.5999 to 0.6001 when moving from 45 to 60 neighbors. I plan to test 75 and explore the option of using "cosine 60 no alignment" for possible improvements.**Exploring PCA and Cosine Variants**


  I''m considering proposing a "cosine 75" variant, along with "cosine 60" but with a low PCA dimension, perhaps 64 instead of the current 50 PCs. The spec mentions a default of 100, so testing 64 or 80 could be worthwhile. I''ll also contemplate switching from TruncatedSVD to sc.pp.pca, but sparse data makes SVD seem fine. Additionally, establishing a baseline for "residualization linear model PC correction" would be beneficial. Overall, I need to ensure we''re following our constraints while being open to distance weighting ideas.**Analyzing Batch Edges and Graph Construction**


  For four batches, with a base of around 15 for N=60, I noticed that resolving duplicate edges through minimal distances yields more than N edges. Increasing edges beyond N seems likely. Our balanced_graph.build_balanced_knn creates edges by merging across batches, resulting in a symmetric graph without capping neighbors per node. The number of neighbors is close to N, with duplicates being minor. We should consider quickly implementing "cosine 75" and "cosine 60 no alignment" if we have the slots available. But right now, we only have one running slot in use.**Scheduling New Evaluations**


  I see that there’s currently only one active evaluation (ID 35), so I can submit one additional experiment since the limit is two concurrent runs per agent. I’m thinking of choosing "75 neighbors" for the next run. Another option is "cosine 60" with improved HVG selection using ''batch_hvg''. I also want to test "n_pca_components=64" with 45 neighbors, but I’ll go ahead with "cosine-75" for now. I should update the research log with the new SOTA of 0.6001 and plan to implement some next steps too.**Organizing Evaluation Actions**


  I’m thinking about setting a "rank score" to keep the scoreboard sorted, but it might not be necessary. I also want to adopt "print logs" to assist with debugging, similar to what we did for the 60-noalign test. Reviewing evaluation IDs can help, but we have full logs available. I’ll reflect on our achievements, noting we’ve surpassed 0.6 SOTA and plan to push for 75 neighbors. Then, I’ll execute two actions: updating the research log and submitting the 75 neighbors variant.'
