tick: 44
role: model
parts:
- text: "Deeper diagnosis, bottlenecks, and robustness\n\n- Where the gains come from: Residualization reduces batch-predictable variance (PCR↓) and tightens batch silhouettes (ASW_batch↑). Balanced cross-batch edges ensure each local neighborhood spans batches, so iLISI/Graph_conn stay high even as we shrink batch axes. Because we only subtract BW (batch space), cell-type geometry largely remains (ARI/NMI/cLISI stable) until γ exceeds the point where batch and biology are confounded.\n\n- Why cosine helps: Residualization changes per-PC scales; cosine uses angles, not magnitudes, so neighbor selection is stable under variance redistribution. Euclidean would re-weight dimensions based on post-residualization variance, potentially overemphasizing residual high-variance axes.\n\n- The γ ridge and the 0.76 drop: Once γ passes the point where batch and biology share axes (e.g., when technical covariates correlate with cell state), subtracting BW removes legitimate biological variance aligned\
    \ with batch. This reduces label silhouette (ASW_label) and kBET, even if PCR improves. The knee at ~0.74–0.75 fits “stop when PCR improvements no longer buy you kBET/Graph_conn gains without hurting label structure.”\n\n- Why binary equal-quotas beat alternatives:\n  - Binary vs gaussian weights: With gaussian weights, very near, likely same-batch edges dominate, partially undoing quota effects and reducing mixing; binary keeps structural constraints primary.\n  - Equal vs proportional: Proportional quotas under-serve minority batches, depressing local mixing (kBET/iLISI).\n  - Mutual-only edges: Too sparse; degree↓ harms Graph_conn/iLISI; robustness suffers.\n\n- Remaining bottlenecks:\n  - Confounded axes: Some biology aligns with batch; aggressive γ removes it. This is intrinsic to linear residualization. A careful per-PC shrinkage (data-driven) could help without adding many parameters.\n  - Diminishing returns: Graph_conn saturates (~0.98), iLISI fixed by quotas; major levers are\
    \ near their plateau, so overshooting γ yields net losses.\n\nGeneralizable, minimal next steps (avoid overfitting)\n- Neighbor confirmatory check at γ≈0.75: 42/45/48 should be statistically tied; default 45 for robustness.\n- Keep l2=1e-3: 2e-3 neutral; 5e-4 harmed. The default is stable and simple.\n\nOptional, still-simple enhancements (one new scalar knob)\n- Per-PC shrinkage without labels: Use batch-only predictability per PC (R²_batch on each PC via ridge). Set per-PC γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j). That preserves PCs with low batch predictability while shrinking highly batch-predictable PCs more—one extra scalar λ, same pipeline shape, still parsimonious and label-free (uses batch labels only, which is allowed).\n\nHeuristics for automatic hyperparameters (to generalize)\n- γ selection (unsupervised wrt biology): Sweep γ and pick the smallest γ where PCR falls below a plateau while Graph_conn keeps rising and kBET (batch-only) stops improving materially. Avoid any use of cell-type\
    \ labels. This codifies “stop before confounded biology is shaved off.”\n- Neighbors selection: Choose the smallest k in {42,45,48} such that Graph_conn ≥ target (e.g., 0.98) and the change in mean neighbor distance stabilizes; default to 45 if tied.\n\nPublish plan refinements\n- Figures: (1) Metric trends vs γ (PCR↑ vs ASW_batch↑, kBET/iLISI/Graph_conn stable, ARI/NMI/cLISI flat then slight decline past 0.75); (2) neighbor sensitivity at SOTA γ; (3) negative results (gaussian weights, proportional quotas, mutual-only); (4) runtime/memory.\n- Pseudocode + config: 10–15 lines capturing normalize → PCA(50) → residualize (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3) → balanced cosine kNN (equal quotas, k=45, binary).\n- Reproducibility: fixed seeds, exact library versions, and result UUIDs; release storage modules; emphasize minimal knobs and dataset-agnostic defaults."
