tick: 44
role: model
parts:
- text: "Foundational assumptions and robustness strategy\n\nAssumptions that make the recipe work\n- Batch structure is largely linear in PC space: One-hot batch indicators explain a low-dimensional subspace of the PCs; subtracting γ·BW preferentially removes technical variance.\n- Biology is mostly orthogonal to batch: Up to γ≈0.74–0.75, most biological geometry (cell-type clustering) lies outside col(B), so ARI/NMI/cLISI remain stable while PCR and ASW_batch improve.\n- Local mixing can be enforced structurally: Equal per-batch quotas guarantee that each neighborhood spans batches; this is a strong structural prior that lifts iLISI/kBET/Graph_conn without needing nonlinear models.\n\nWhy this remains robust across datasets (and what could break)\n- Robust: cosine similarity (angular) is stable to variance redistribution; binary edges prevent reintroducing magnitude bias; quotas are simple and adapt to any number of batches.\n- Potential breaks: (1) Strong confounding between batch and\
    \ biology (e.g., each batch contains unique subtypes)—over-residualization can erase real signal (γ too high). (2) Extreme batch imbalance or tiny batches—equal quotas may be infeasible for very small batches; implementation should degrade gracefully to “up to” quota per batch. (3) Nonlinear batch effects—linear residualization removes what it can; remaining nonlinear artifacts might require more complex methods (but that adds knobs and risks overfitting).\n\nConcrete robustness checks that use only allowed signals (no labels)\n- Subsampling stress: random 80%/60% subsamples; verify γ≈0.74–0.75 and k=45 remain strong (PCR plateau, Graph_conn≈0.98, iLISI high).\n- Batch perturbation: temporarily remove one batch and re-run; the selected γ should still reach PCR/kBET improvements without ARI/NMI drops (we can’t read labels, but we can check batch-only metrics and graph statistics).\n- Batch stratification: ensure quotas still assign at least 1 neighbor per batch if feasible; log the fraction\
    \ of nodes that could not meet quotas to track edge-case behavior.\n\nWhy the simple graph design wins (formal intuition)\n- Quota-union graph ≈ stratified kNN: union of per-batch neighbor sets yields a graph whose expected batch-degree per node is constant; this maximizes local batch coverage (iLISI, kBET) under a degree budget, independent of distance magnitudes.\n- Binary connectivities: with quotas fixed, edge weights add variance but little information; they bias toward same-batch shortest distances, reducing effective cross-batch mixing. Binary ensures structure > magnitude.\n\nNeighbor choice at the optimum\n- For γ≈0.74–0.75, k in {42,45,48} sits on a flat ridge. 45 offers the best robustness trade-off:\n  - k=42: occasional under-connectivity in sparse regions (risking micro-fragmentation).\n  - k=48: marginally more smoothing with tiny or no gains (Graph_conn already ≈0.98).\n  - Choose k=45 as default; document that {45,48} are interchangeable if one must pick by a secondary\
    \ constraint (speed vs. slightly higher degree).\n\nRidge l2 recommendation\n- Keep l2=1e-3. Lower (5e-4) allowed batch-noise fitting (subtracting spurious components); higher (2e-3) was neutral. l2=1e-3 stabilizes W and prevents subtracting high-variance noise along batch axes.\n\nMinimal, general ablations left (one change, low overfitting risk)\n- Confirm 45 vs 48 neighbors at γ=0.75 (expect tie; pick 45).\n- Reconfirm l2=1e-3 at γ=0.75 with ± one step only if variance appears higher (logs).\n- Optional unsupervised γ selector: stop increasing γ when (i) PCR enters a diminishing-returns regime and (ii) kBET gains stall, while Graph_conn ≥ target (e.g., 0.98). This uses only batch metrics and graph stats; no labels.\n\nPublication blueprint and positioning\n- Method (10–15 lines): normalize/log1p → HVGs → PCA(50) → residualize PCs vs batch with ridge (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3) → balanced cosine kNN (equal batch quotas, k=45, binary, symmetric).\n- Key insights:\n  - Linear batch removal + structural\
    \ cross-batch locality yields robust, multi-metric gains; quotas beat weights; the γ optimum is narrow (too high erodes label/kBET).\n  - Neighbor count shows a flat ridge; 45 is robust default.\n- Reproducibility:\n  - Release storage modules (pbagc.py, residualize.py, balanced_graph.py, best_pipeline.py), config (γ, l2, k), seeds, and result UUIDs.\n  - Include negative outcomes (gaussian weights, proportional quotas, mutual-only, too-low/too-high γ).\n  - Provide batch-only selection heuristic to avoid metric overfitting and improve cross-dataset generalization.\n\nBig-picture bottleneck and future low-complexity upgrade\n- Bottleneck: batch–biology confounding along shared axes; linear subtraction can’t perfectly disentangle.\n- Low-complexity upgrade (still parsimonious): per-PC shrinkage γ_j proportional to batch predictability (R²_j), bounded by γ_max. This adds one scalar λ and preserves the pipeline’s simplicity while reducing the risk of over-residualizing biology on weakly\
    \ batch-predictable PCs."
