tick: 44
role: model
parts:
- text: "Closing synthesis and concrete defaults\n\nWhy the recipe works\n- Batch-linear cleanup: Ridge residualization subtracts the batch-predictable component in PC space (Z_resid = Z − γ·BW), lowering PCR and tightening batch silhouettes (ASW_batch↑) while preserving biology largely orthogonal to batch, so ARI/NMI/cLISI/iLISI remain stable up to a point.\n- Structural mixing: Balanced cosine kNN (equal per-batch quotas, binary edges) guarantees local cross-batch connectivity, boosting iLISI/Graph_conn/kBET without complex modeling. Cosine is robust to variance redistribution from residualization; binary edges avoid reintroducing magnitude bias.\n\nSweet spot for γ and why 0.76 dropped\n- The gain curve forms a ridge: γ increases steadily improve batch tests until biology that partially aligns with batch starts getting shaved away. Empirically, γ≈0.74–0.75 is optimal; 0.76 overshot—PCR kept improving, but kBET dipped and label geometry (ASW_label/ARI) nudged down, signaling over-residualization.\n\
    \nNeighbor and weight choices\n- Binary, equal-quotas beat Gaussian/proportional/mutual-only:\n  - Binary keeps the structural quota effect dominant; Gaussian weights re-amplify same-batch near edges and reduce mixing.\n  - Equal quotas avoid starving minority batches; proportional quotas bias to large batches and lower iLISI/kBET.\n  - Mutual-only collapses degree and harms Graph_conn/iLISI.\n- k in {42,45,48} sits on a flat ridge at SOTA γ; 45 is the robust default (no smoothing penalty, Graph_conn≈0.98, stable ARI/NMI/cLISI).\n\nRidge l2\n- l2=1e-3 is the right default: numerically stable and empirically best. 2e-3 was neutral; 5e-4 underperformed (likely subtracting spurious, noisy batch-predictable components). Keep 1e-3 unless strong evidence suggests otherwise.\n\nFinal default configuration (dataset-agnostic)\n- normalize_total + log1p\n- HVGs (batch-aware if provided)\n- PCA to 50 components\n- Ridge residualize PCs vs batch: γ = 0.75, l2 = 1e-3\n- Balanced kNN on Z_resid: cosine,\
    \ equal per-batch quotas, k = 45, binary, symmetric\n\nMinimal, general ablations to consolidate (low overfitting risk)\n- Confirm neighbors at γ=0.75: 42 vs 45 vs 48 (expect tie; fix 45 for default).\n- Sanity recheck l2 at γ=0.75 (5e-4 and 2e-3) only if variance looks unstable—current evidence already favors 1e-3.\n\nAutomatic, label-free selection heuristics (for future datasets)\n- γ: sweep and pick the smallest γ where PCR enters diminishing returns and kBET gains stall while Graph_conn≥target (e.g., 0.98). Uses only batch labels and graph stats; avoids biology labels.\n- k: choose the smallest k in {42,45,48} achieving Graph_conn≥target with stabilized mean neighbor distance variance.\n\nWhat to publish (simplicity, reproducibility, insight)\n- 10–15 lines of pseudocode capturing the entire pipeline and three knobs (γ, l2, k) with defaults (0.75, 1e-3, 45).\n- Metric trends vs γ showing the ridge: PCR/ASW_batch↑, kBET largely stable then up at 0.74–0.75, ARI/NMI/cLISI flat until\
    \ over-residualization.\n- Negative results: Gaussian weights, proportional quotas, mutual-only, too-low/too-high γ.\n- Reproducibility: release modules (pbagc.py, residualize.py, balanced_graph.py, best_pipeline.py), seeds, versions, and result UUIDs; report runtime and memory.\n- Positioning: a parsimonious baseline that cleanly separates linear batch removal from enforced local cross-batch mixing; robust across settings without dataset-specific tuning.\n\nBigger-picture constraint and optional upgrade\n- Constraint: batch–biology confounding along shared axes limits linear residualization. A minimal extension—per-PC shrinkage proportional to batch predictability (R² per PC) bounded by γ_max—adds a single scalar λ and may reduce over-residualization on weakly batch-predictable PCs while keeping the method simple.\n\nNext practical moves\n- Freeze the default config above; run the tiny neighbor/l2 confirmations at γ=0.75 only if needed.\n- Shift effort to writing the paper and packaging\
    \ code; include the batch-only γ/k selection heuristic for generalization and to avoid leaderboard overfitting."
