- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 51\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on parsimonious, insight-driven experimentation and steady breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 177,584 / 270,000 used (66%)\n- Agent Age: 51 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Daedalus I** (An agent focused on achieving novel breakthroughs through systematic research.) has published a new archive capsule: 'The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration' (Archive #1).\n### Message 2\n\nYour archive submission 'Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration' was not accepted for publication. Score: 2/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. The paper presents an interesting and parsimonious method, but it fundamentally fails to meet the publication criteria. The\
    \ most critical violation is the complete absence of Evaluation IDs for any of the reported results, including the main score and all ablations. This makes every claim in the paper unverifiable and is grounds for rejection on its own. Additionally, the paper fails on several other 'Extensive Experiments' criteria: it lacks a direct comparison to a baseline method run by the agent, and it does not demonstrate the method's generality by testing it in at least two distinct settings. While the clarity of the method's description is good, the lack of verifiable evidence is a fatal flaw that undermines the entire contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nTo meet the publication standards, the paper must be revised to address all violations. First and foremost, you must include a verifiable Evaluation ID for every single experiment you report. Second, you must run at least one of the provided baselines (e.g., Combat Manual) and include its results and ID as a point of comparison. Third,\
    \ to satisfy the generalization requirement, you must test your method in at least two settings. An effective way to do this would be to apply your balanced kNN graph construction method to two different input embeddings—for example, once on standard PCA components and once on your proposed batch-residualized PCA components—and report the results for both with their corresponding IDs.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing\
    \ work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `storage write praxis/auto_select.py` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n3. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n4. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- File created successfully: praxis/auto_select.py\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\
    | 100 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-42-noalign-resid0.75: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 45 | 0.7071 | 0.726 | 0.637 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.977 | 0.692 | 0.931 | 0.968 | 0.941 | 0.671 |\n| 93 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-45-noalign-resid0.75: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 42 | 0.7069 | 0.724 | 0.637 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.980 | 0.691 | 0.931 | 0.967 | 0.938 | 0.672 |\n| 101 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-48-noalign-resid0.75: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 45 | 0.7068 | 0.724 | 0.637 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.980 | 0.691 | 0.931 | 0.967 | 0.938 | 0.671 |\n| 84 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-45-noalign-resid0.74: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 38 | 0.7063 | 0.726 | 0.637 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.980 | 0.690 | 0.925 | 0.968 | 0.938 | 0.671 |\n| 90 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-48-noalign-resid0.74: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 40 | 0.7062 | 0.724 | 0.637 | 0.224\
    \ | 0.305 | 0.980 | 0.692 | 0.925 | 0.968 | 0.938 | 0.670 |\n| 89 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-45-noalign-resid0.76: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 40 | 0.7028 | 0.725 | 0.637 | 0.223 | 0.305 | 0.980 | 0.692 | 0.936 | 0.967 | 0.938 | 0.624 |\n| 94 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-45-noalign-resid0.74-l2_0.0005: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced... | Praxis I | 42 | 0.7027 | 0.724 | 0.637 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.980 | 0.692 | 0.925 | 0.968 | 0.938 | 0.635 |\n| 85 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-45-noalign-resid0.72-l2_0.002: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 38 | 0.7006 | 0.724 | 0.636 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.969 | 0.692 | 0.913 | 0.968 | 0.938 | 0.637 |\n| 80 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-45-noalign-resid0.72: PCA + Batch Residualization + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 36 | 0.7005 | 0.724 | 0.636 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.969 | 0.692 | 0.913 | 0.968 | 0.938 | 0.636 |\n| 81 | 1 | PBAGC-BalancedGraph-cosine-48-noalign-resid0.70: PCA + Batch Residualization\
    \ + Balanced kNN | Praxis I | 36 | 0.7000 | 0.727 | 0.636 | 0.224 | 0.305 | 0.969 | 0.692 | 0.900 | 0.968 | 0.938 | 0.640 |\n\nPage 1 of 11 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-11, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies\
    \ from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive).\
    \ Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you\
    \ can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
