- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Token Management Room. I will prune large consecutive blocks with concise, information-complete summaries to reduce context size while preserving essential details (methods, EIDs, results, conclusions).\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"10-23\"\n    summary: |\n      Early BalancedGraph development and baselines:\n      - Introduced batch-balanced kNN (equal per-batch quotas, binary) on PCA; explored graph variants.\n      - Key evaluations:\n        • EID 16 (cosine, equal, k=15) → 0.5930; EID 17 (mutual edges) → 0.4428 (sparse graph harms Graph_conn/iLISI).\n        • Neighbor/cosine gains: EID 20 (k=30) → 0.5992; EID 21 (proportional quotas) → 0.5714 (mixing worse).\n        • Finer k: EID 25 (k=24) → 0.5972; EID 26 (k=45) → 0.5999 (peak among early runs).\n      - Insight: Balanced equal-quotas + cosine + binary edges dominate; mutual/weighted/proportional underperform.\n      - Next step set: introduce embedding-side\
    \ improvements (residualization) to move beyond ~0.60.\n\n  - ticks: \"24-43\"\n    summary: |\n      Residualization (ridge) + BalancedGraph era; steady SOTA climb:\n      - Added linear residualization in PC space: Z_resid = Z − γ·BW (l2=1e-3), kept BalancedGraph.\n      - γ sweep:\n        • EID 55 (γ=0.4) → 0.6697; EID 59 (0.5) → 0.6826; EID 63 (0.6) → 0.6927;\n        • EID 66 (0.62) → 0.6945; EID 67 (0.65) → 0.6962; EID 70 (0.68) → 0.6984; EID 76 (0.70) → 0.7000;\n        • EID 80 (0.72) → 0.7005; EID 84 (0.74) → 0.7063.\n      - k at strong γ:\n        • EID 71/90 (γ≈0.70–0.74, k=48) ≈ k=45 (flat ridge).\n      - l2 ablation:\n        • EID 85 (γ=0.72, l2=2e-3) ≈ baseline; EID 94 (γ=0.74, l2=5e-4) worse. l2=1e-3 is robust default.\n      - Best by end: γ≈0.74–0.75 expected; neighbor effects mild; publication drafting began (first draft lacked EIDs and was later revised outside this block).\n      - Conclusion: γ has a narrow sweet spot; BalancedGraph structure + cosine metric\
    \ stable; SOTA ~0.706 taking shape.\n\n  - ticks: \"45-50\"\n    summary: |\n      Final defaults consolidation and readiness for publication:\n      - Neighbor sweep at γ=0.75:\n        • EID 93 (k=45) → 0.7069; EID 100 (k=42) → 0.7071 (best); EID 101 (k=48) → 0.7068.\n      - Wrote README/defaults; finalized frozen config: γ=0.75, l2=1e-3, k=42, cosine, equal quotas, binary, PCA=50.\n      - Prepared Archive materials; maturity reached at tick 50.\n\n  - ticks: \"52-60\"\n    summary: |\n      Verification, baseline, publication (accepted), and negative results:\n      - Agent-run baseline:\n        • EID 105 (ComBat + PCA + kNN) → 0.6178.\n      - Archive resubmission (with EIDs, baseline, two settings) accepted (9/10):\n        • Two settings: (A) raw PCA + BalancedGraph EID 35 → 0.6034; (B) residualized PCA + BalancedGraph EID 100 → 0.7071.\n      - Adaptive per-PC residualization (negative results at k=42):\n        • EID 108 (lam=1.0) → 0.6206 (PCR 0.121)\n        • EID 109 (lam=1.5)\
    \ → 0.6281 (PCR 0.193)\n        • EID 113 (lam=0.5) → 0.6123 (PCR 0.044)\n        Conclusion: adaptive shrinkage collapsed PCR and harmed composite score; global γ=0.75 remains best.\n      - Synergy test:\n        • EID 115 (ComBat-PCA + BalancedGraph k=42) → 0.7029 (above ComBat baseline; below best residualized PCA).\n      - Token housekeeping begun; defaults reaffirmed; next direction noted: optional label-free heuristics and per-PC R²-based shrinkage as future research (not superior yet).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reviewing timeline data**\n\nI need to summarize the timeline effectively. It seems we’re working with ticks from 0 to 60, and I need to capture key milestones like the development of the BalancedGraph method, along with EIDs and results.\n\nIt's important to prune large blocks to reduce token usage below 60%. I see that tick 24 had 3044 words and tick 12 is also large at 2998, but I can't prune tick 12. I’ll need to focus on pruned blocks carefully.**Pruning timeline blocks**\n\nI’m pleased to see that we can prune blocks 10-13, including tick 12. We just need to make sure the blocks we prune are consecutive and don’t include any protected ticks. \n\nTick 9 is protected, but ticks 10-13 are fine. There are also other large blocks to consider, like tick 24 with 3044 words. I see I can prune earlier ticks but not the current tick, which is 61. That gives me a clear path to reduce word counts efficiently!**Targeting token reduction**\n\nI want to significantly reduce tokens,\
  \ so I’m considering targeting large ranges for pruning while ensuring we capture comprehensive summaries of EIDs and results. We can prune ticks only if they’re consecutive, which means I can include large block 24-43, but tick 44 is protected. \n\nSummarizing these ticks may create a long summary, but it’s still better than keeping all content. \n\nIt's important to highlight key experiments and results, especially the development timeline and improvements by tick 20.**Summarizing key results**\n\nFor ticks 24-26, I noticed gamma residualization sweeps improved to 0.6697 and 0.6826, finally reaching 0.7071 at tick 45. Details like the final defaults freeze at tick 47 and README updates at tick 49 are also crucial. \n\nWe should create multiple prune blocks. For Block 1, covering ticks 10-14 seems practical since it includes initial BalancedGraph experiments and key EIDs. I’ll ensure the blocks are consecutive and summarize the important findings and improvements effectively. Block 2\
  \ looks like it’ll start with tick 15.**Pruning plan and summary**\n\nI see tick 15 has 1722 words and could include ticks 15-23. I need to summarize neighbor count sweeps and BalancedGraph improvements for ticks earlier than 24.\n\nBlock 3 will cover ticks 24-43, focusing on gamma sweeps and publication attempts, though we can’t include protected ticks like 51. \n\nFor Block 4, ticks 45-50 will summarize details about defaults and resubmission acceptance at tick 54. Finally, Block 5 will encompass ticks 52-60, where we need to be mindful of token usage while still providing essential EIDs and conclusions! \n\nI'll craft the YAML for the pruning blocks and ensure conciseness in the summary."
