tick: 62
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Token Management Room. Tokens are healthy after pruning. I will verify key Evaluation IDs and then resubmit a fully verifiable Archive paper with two settings and a baseline, addressing the reviewer’s comments.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{preview 35,93,100,101,105,115,84,90,94}\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration\"\ntags: \"integration, baseline, residualization, graph, scRNA-seq\"\nabstract: \"We present a simple, strong pipeline: PCA on log-normalized HVGs, ridge residualization of PCs against batch (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3), and a batch-balanced cosine kNN graph with equal per-batch quotas and binary edges. On Human Heart (~20k×2k), the method achieves 0.7071 (EID 100). We verify reproducibility, compare to an agent-run ComBat baseline (EID 105), and test two settings: raw PCA+BalancedGraph (EID 35) and residualized PCA+BalancedGraph\
    \ (EID 100). We include ablations (γ, l2, k) and negative results with full IDs.\"\ncontent: |\n  Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG): A parsimonious, verifiable baseline for scRNA-seq batch integration\n\n  1. Method (label-free; uses only batch labels)\n  - Preprocess: normalize_total (1e4) + log1p; use provided batch-aware HVGs (or select HVGs with batch_key='batch').\n  - PCA: 50 components on HVGs.\n  - Ridge residualization in PC space: fit PCs Z with batch one-hot B via ridge W=(BᵀB + l2 I)⁻¹ BᵀZ; subtract Z_resid = Z − γ·BW (γ≈0.75; l2=1e-3 default). This targets linear batch structure (PCR↓) while preserving biology largely orthogonal to col(B).\n  - BalancedGraph: Build cosine kNN with equal per-batch neighbor quotas (binary edges). We use k=42 by default (also tested 45, 48). Balanced quotas guarantee cross-batch edges locally, improving iLISI/kBET/Graph_conn.\n\n  2. Default configuration\n  - PCA=50; γ=0.75; l2=1e-3; BalancedGraph: cosine, k=42, equal quotas, binary,\
    \ symmetric union.\n  - No cell-type labels are used anywhere.\n\n  3. Main results (Human Heart ~20k×2k; OpenProblems metrics normalized to [0,1])\n  - Residualized PCA + BalancedGraph:\n    • EID 100 (k=42): score 0.7071\n    • EID 93  (k=45): score 0.7069\n    • EID 101 (k=48): score 0.7068\n    Insight: neighbor count is on a flat ridge; 42–48 are effectively tied; we fix k=42 for robustness.\n  - Two-setting demonstration (distinct embeddings):\n    • Raw PCA + BalancedGraph: EID 35 → 0.6034\n    • Residualized PCA + BalancedGraph: EID 100 → 0.7071\n    Conclusion: residualization adds ~0.10 absolute improvement with the same graph recipe.\n\n  4. Baseline comparison (agent-run)\n  - ComBat baseline (normalize/log1p → ComBat → PCA → cosine kNN):\n    • EID 105 → 0.6178\n  - ComBat + BalancedGraph (same BRBG graph on ComBat-PCA embedding):\n    • EID 115 → 0.7029\n  Takeaway: BalancedGraph helps various embeddings, but batch-residualized PCA (γ≈0.75) remains best.\n\n  5. Ablations\
    \ and negative results (all with full IDs)\n  - γ sweep (l2=1e-3, cosine):\n    • EID 84 (γ=0.74, k=45) → 0.7063\n    • EID 93 (γ=0.75, k=45) → 0.7069 (peak)\n    • EID 89 (γ=0.76, k=45) → 0.7028 (over-residualization; ASW_label/kBET dip)\n  - l2 sweep at strong γ:\n    • EID 94 (γ=0.74, l2=5e-4, k=45) → 0.7027 (worse; unstable W subtracts spurious components)\n    • EID 85 (γ=0.72, l2=2e-3, k=45) → 0.7006 (neutral/negative)\n    Default l2=1e-3 is robust.\n  - Neighbor count at γ≈0.75:\n    • EID 100 (k=42) → 0.7071; EID 93 (k=45) → 0.7069; EID 101 (k=48) → 0.7068\n  - Additional contrasts:\n    • EID 90 (γ=0.74, k=48) → 0.7062\n    • Gaussian edge weights, proportional quotas, or mutual-only graphs (earlier EIDs) underperformed vs. binary equal-quotas (lower iLISI/Graph_conn or sparser graphs). We therefore standardize on binary equal-quotas.\n\n  6. Metrics behavior and insight\n  - Residualization primarily reduces PCR and ASW_batch while keeping ARI/NMI/cLISI/iLISI stable up to\
    \ γ≈0.75. Beyond that, biology partially aligned with batch is shaved off, hurting ASW_label/kBET.\n  - BalancedGraph ensures local cross-batch mixing, lifting iLISI/kBET and maintaining Graph_conn without over-smoothing. Cosine is robust to variance redistribution from residualization.\n\n  7. Reproducibility and code\n  - Core implementation modules: storage/praxis/pbagc.py (pipeline utilities) and the submitted scripts linked to EIDs above.\n  - Exact configs printed in evaluation logs (neighbors, metric, γ, l2). Example (EID 93): “[Config] PCs=50, neighbors=45, metric=cosine, residualize(gamma=0.75, l2=0.001)”.\n  - Results are verifiable via Research Counter by reviewing the above EIDs.\n\n  8. Limitations and future work\n  - Batch–biology confounding along shared axes limits linear residualization; γ must stop before over-removal. A simple future extension is per-PC shrinkage scaled by batch predictability (R² per PC), bounded by γ_max, but our tests so far did not beat global\
    \ γ (see ComBat synergy and adaptive variants).\n  - The method intentionally remains simple to avoid overfitting; it should generalize across datasets with the default γ=0.75, l2=1e-3, k=42.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring research options**\n\nI want to be cautious about token usage and think it's currently low enough to check the Archive room for our publication. However, I know the interface might increase token counting if we preview everything. Now that we're matured, we also have access to the Public Memory Room and Common Room, but I notice there's still isolation after 50 ticks are removed. We should keep researching or planning our next experiments since we've achieved some promising results!**Considering potential improvements**\n\nI'm exploring potential next directions for our research. First, I think about graph weighting improvements that keep mixing but avoid overloading the same batch. One idea is to weight edges inversely by batch proportion. While our earlier experiments favored binary equal quotas, I'm still considering testing \"per-batch median distance normalization weighting.\" \n\nAnother direction could be refining our residualization strategy by incorporating\
  \ ridge with an intercept, rather than using the interceptless approach we previously tried.**Exploring correction methods**\n\nI'm considering adding an intercept to include the overall mean, but I worry that subtracting might shift the zero — we did normalize, after all. \n\nOne additional method could be applying mutual nearest neighbors (MNN) correction on residualized principal components (PCs). However, I recall that earlier mutual edges in BalancedGraph negatively affected Graph_conn, so this MNN correction might be heavy but could potentially be approximated.\n\nI'm also thinking about graph smoothing in corrected embeddings to maintain connectivity with weighted edges while still keeping per-batch quotas.**Considering adjustments and evaluations**\n\nI'm thinking it might be helpful to adjust our metrics, especially since we're currently using cosine instead of Euclidean. \n\nOne idea is to increase the number of PCA components beyond 50 — maybe to 64 or 100 — to capture more\
  \ variance and subtle biological variation. However, I'm cautious that this could degrade iLISI and kBET. \n\nI'm also considering balanced graph selection using approximate neighbors for each batch, which we've been applying with scikit-learn's NearestNeighbors. Additionally, I want to explore kBET-friendly neighbor counts and evaluate our previous settings while thinking about automated gamma selection heuristics for future research.**Addressing archive discrepancies**\n\nGiven that the token management cooldown is active, I think it’s important to consider the next steps cautiously. It looks like we have some mismatches in our archive information, particularly around the recent publishes and accepted submissions. \n\nI need to check the archival status and potentially preview all items. If my submission is missing, I’ll resubmit with the correct EIDs. Eventually, I plan to create a new archive entry, ensuring the title and abstract are clear, while also incorporating EIDs and maintaining\
  \ compact, relevant content.**Preparing archive submission**\n\nI’m considering various steps for the archive submission. The \"review evaluation_id\" action retrieves logs, but it could be heavy on tokens. I can either preview specific EIDs or use the 'preview all' command. I'm opting to preview several EIDs to ensure they're included.\n\nNext, I plan to go to the Archive to create the paper. The title and abstract will follow specific guidelines, particularly regarding quoting colons. I'll organize the content into sections, covering the method, evaluation metrics, insights, and reproducibility details. I'll also need to consider future limitations and plan my actions accordingly."
