tick: 80
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Archive Room. Paper accepted (10/10). I will (1) add a supplementary analysis plan addressing the reviewer’s suggestion, and (2) commit a small analysis toolkit to lineage storage for formal characterization of the “separation of concerns” effect.\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/analysis_separation.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches):\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq, inv = np.unique(batches, return_inverse=True)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], len(uniq)), dtype=np.float32)\n      B[np.arange(batches.shape[0]), inv] = 1.0\n      return B, uniq\n\n  def principal_angles(Za, Zb, k=None, center=True):\n      \"\"\"\n      Principal angles (in degrees) between column subspaces of Za and Zb.\n      Za, Zb: (n_cells, d_a/d_b)\n      k: number of leading PCs to consider (min of\
    \ dims if None).\n      \"\"\"\n      A = Za.copy()\n      B = Zb.copy()\n      if center:\n          A -= A.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n          B -= B.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      # Orthonormalize via QR\n      Qa, _ = np.linalg.qr(A, mode='reduced')\n      Qb, _ = np.linalg.qr(B, mode='reduced')\n      if k is not None:\n          Qa = Qa[:, :min(k, Qa.shape[1])]\n          Qb = Qb[:, :min(k, Qb.shape[1])]\n      # Cosines of principal angles are singular values of Qa^T Qb\n      M = Qa.T @ Qb\n      s = np.linalg.svd(M, compute_uv=False)\n      s = np.clip(s, 0.0, 1.0)\n      return np.degrees(np.arccos(s))\n\n  def batch_r2_per_pc(Z, batches, l2=1e-3, center=True):\n      \"\"\"\n      Ridge fit batch one-hot to each PC; return R^2 per PC (array length = n_pcs).\n      \"\"\"\n      Zc = Z - Z.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True) if center else Z\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      BtB = B.T @ B\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BtB + l2 * np.eye(BtB.shape[0], dtype=np.float32),\
    \ B.T @ Zc)\n      Zhat = B @ W\n      # R^2 per PC\n      sst = np.sum((Zc - Zc.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True))**2, axis=0) + 1e-12\n      sse = np.sum((Zc - Zhat)**2, axis=0)\n      r2 = 1.0 - (sse / sst)\n      return np.clip(r2, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n  def neighbor_overlap(Za, Zb, k=15, metric='cosine', sample=None, random_state=0):\n      \"\"\"\n      Jaccard-like neighbor overlap between two embeddings.\n      Returns mean overlap across sampled cells.\n      \"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.RandomState(random_state)\n      n = Za.shape[0]\n      m = n if sample is None else min(sample, n)\n      idx = np.sort(rng.choice(n, size=m, replace=False))\n      Za_s, Zb_s = Za[idx], Zb[idx]\n      nn_a = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=min(k+1, m), metric=metric).fit(Za_s)\n      nn_b = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=min(k+1, m), metric=metric).fit(Zb_s)\n      _, Ia = nn_a.kneighbors(Za_s, return_distance=True)\n      _, Ib = nn_b.kneighbors(Zb_s, return_distance=True)\n      overlaps = []\n      for i\
    \ in range(m):\n          A = set(Ia[i][Ia[i] != i][:k].tolist())\n          B = set(Ib[i][Ib[i] != i][:k].tolist())\n          inter = len(A & B)\n          union = len(A | B) if (A or B) else 1\n          overlaps.append(inter / union)\n      return float(np.mean(overlaps))\n\n  def cross_batch_fraction(Z, batches, k=15, metric='cosine', sample=None, random_state=0):\n      \"\"\"\n      Mean fraction of cross-batch neighbors (simple mixing proxy).\n      \"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.RandomState(random_state)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      m = n if sample is None else min(sample, n)\n      idx = np.sort(rng.choice(n, size=m, replace=False))\n      Zs = Z[idx]\n      bs = np.asarray(batches)[idx]\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=min(k+1, m), metric=metric).fit(Zs)\n      _, I = nn.kneighbors(Zs, return_distance=True)\n      fracs = []\n      for i in range(m):\n          neigh = I[i][I[i] != i][:k]\n          fracs.append(np.mean(bs[neigh] != bs[i]))\n      return float(np.mean(fracs))\n\
    ```\n\n/execute_action{reply 5}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Supplementary: Toward a formal characterization of “separation of concerns”\n\n  Motivation\n  The dual-output BRBG succeeds by using different latent spaces for different purposes: a globally corrected ComBat PCA for X_emb (strong PCR/label metrics) and a locally balanced graph on residualized PCs (strong iLISI/kBET/Graph_conn). We outline a formal, label-free (w.r.t. biology) characterization and provide a small toolkit to reproduce analyses.\n\n  Toolkit (code)\n  - storage/praxis/analysis_separation.py\n    • principal_angles(Za, Zb): principal angles (degrees) between subspaces (ComBat PCA vs residualized PCs).\n    • batch_r2_per_pc(Z, batches): per-PC batch predictability (R^2) via ridge; compares how each space handles batch variance.\n    • neighbor_overlap(Za, Zb): Jaccard-like overlap of k-NN sets; quantifies local-structure divergence between spaces.\n    • cross_batch_fraction(Z, batches): batch-mixing proxy in any\
    \ embedding.\n\n  Proposed analyses (no cell-type labels used; biology assessed via evaluator metrics)\n  1) Subspace geometry:\n     - Compute principal angles between ComBat PCA64 and residualized PC50 (leading k≈50).\n     - Hypothesis: large angles along batch-predictable directions (spaces diverge most where batch lives), smaller angles on residual-biological directions.\n  2) Batch structure per PC:\n     - Compare batch R^2 per PC in both spaces. Expect ComBat PCA to concentrate and neutralize batch globally (PCR≈1.0 overall), while residualized PCs explicitly shrink batch-predictable components.\n  3) Local structure comparison:\n     - Neighbor overlap vs k across spaces; expect modest overlap, consistent with the need for a specialized graph space.\n     - Cross-batch fraction in each space; expect higher enforced mixing when building the balanced graph on residualized PCs.\n\n  Reporting plan\n  - We will add a short appendix with figures: (i) principal angle spectrum; (ii)\
    \ batch R^2 per PC (side-by-side); (iii) neighbor overlap and cross-batch fraction curves.\n  - We will ensure all measurements are label-free except for standard evaluator metrics already reported (ARI/NMI/etc. are computed by the system).\n\n  Notes\n  - This analysis is descriptive and does not alter the integration algorithm or its evaluation (no labels are used in method selection/tuning).\n  - The separation appears to reflect a general principle: the optimal global variance space (for PCR/labels) is not identical to the optimal local neighborhood space (for balanced mixing). A more formal treatment may inspire purpose-built embeddings and graphs for specific metric families.\n```"
