parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Archive Room. I will explore a simple, label-permitted improvement to the graph: calibrated balanced kNN (CBKNN). Idea: equal per-batch quotas remain, but distances from each batch are rescaled by a batch-specific factor estimated from within-batch distance medians, equalizing heterogeneous batch dispersions before quota selection. This preserves parsimony and uses only batch labels.\n\nActions:\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/cbknn.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n\n  def _batch_distance_scales(Z: np.ndarray, batches, metric='cosine', k_calib=16, clamp=(0.5, 2.0)):\n      \"\"\"\n      Estimate per-batch distance scales using within-batch neighbor distances.\n      Returns dict: batch -> scale_factor, where distances from that batch will be multiplied by scale_factor.\n      scale_factor is normalized so that\
    \ the median over batches is ~1.0 and clamped to avoid extremes.\n      \"\"\"\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      per_batch_meds = {}\n      all_meds = []\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx.size < 3:\n              per_batch_meds[b] = 1.0\n              all_meds.append(1.0)\n              continue\n          k = min(k_calib + 1, idx.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z[idx], n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          # drop self (distance ~0 at position 0)\n          d_no_self = []\n          for i in range(idx.size):\n              di = dists[i]\n              gi = inds[i]\n              mask = gi != i  # drop self\n              di = di[mask]\n              if di.size == 0:\n                  continue\n              d_no_self.append(np.median(di))\n\
    \          if len(d_no_self) == 0:\n              med = 1.0\n          else:\n              med = float(np.median(np.asarray(d_no_self)))\n          per_batch_meds[b] = med\n          all_meds.append(med if med > 0 else 1.0)\n      ref = float(np.median(np.asarray(all_meds))) if len(all_meds) else 1.0\n      scales = {}\n      lo, hi = clamp\n      for b, med in per_batch_meds.items():\n          if med <= 0:\n              s = 1.0\n          else:\n              s = ref / med\n          s = float(np.clip(s, lo, hi))\n          scales[b] = s\n      return scales\n\n  def build_balanced_knn_calibrated(Z: np.ndarray, batches, k_total=48, metric='cosine', k_calib=16, clamp=(0.5, 2.0)):\n      \"\"\"\n      Balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas and per-batch distance calibration.\n      - Equal quotas across batches (remainder spread by batch order)\n      - Distances from batch b are multiplied by scale[b] (estimated from within-batch medians)\n      - Binary connectivities (1.0); distances\
    \ are calibrated distances; symmetric union\n      Returns (C, D) CSR matrices.\n      \"\"\"\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq_batches = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      nb = len(uniq_batches)\n      if nb == 0 or n == 0:\n          return None, None\n\n      # Quotas\n      base = k_total // nb\n      rem = k_total - base * nb\n      quotas = {b: base + (1 if i < rem else 0) for i, b in enumerate(uniq_batches)}\n\n      # Per-batch calibration scales\n      scales = _batch_distance_scales(Z, batches, metric=metric, k_calib=k_calib, clamp=clamp)\n\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq_batches:\n          idx_b = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          q = quotas[b]\n          if idx_b.size == 0 or q == 0:\n              continue\n          k = min(q + 1, idx_b.size)  # +1 for self; will drop it\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_b])\n\
    \          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_b[inds]\n          scale_b = float(scales.get(b, 1.0))\n          for i in range(n):\n              g = neigh_global[i]\n              di = dists[i] * scale_b  # calibrate distances from batch b\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask][:q]\n              di = di[mask][:q]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n          rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n\n \
    \     D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy()\n      C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C, D\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BRBG-Dual-CBKNN: ComBat PCA64 Embedding + Calibrated Balanced kNN on Residualized PCs (γ=0.74, k=48)\"\ntags: \"dual, combat, residualization, balanced-knn, calibration, cosine, pca\"\nabstract: \"We keep the dual-output design (ComBat PCA64 embedding; residualized-PC graph with γ=0.74, l2=1e-3) but replace the graph with calibrated balanced kNN (CBKNN): equal per-batch quotas with per-batch distance rescaling based on within-batch median distances. This equalizes batch dispersions before quota selection, aiming to improve iLISI/kBET without harming cLISI/label metrics.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  from praxis.auto_gamma_brbg import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace,\n\
    \      _get_hvg_mask,\n      _pca_truncated_svd,\n      _one_hot_batches,\n      _ridge_batch_fit_predict,\n  )\n  from praxis.cbknn import build_balanced_knn_calibrated\n\n  def _ensure_dense_X(adata):\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          adata.X = adata.X.A\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config (same core as best dual), plus CBKNN params\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      gamma = 0.74\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      k_calib = 16\n      clamp = (0.5, 2.0)\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize/log1p and HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata)\n      if hvg_mask.sum() < 10:\n          hvg_mask = np.ones(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n\n      # Graph source: residualized PCs\n      X = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          X\
    \ = sp.csr_matrix(X)\n      Z = _pca_truncated_svd(X, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Z_hat, _ = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=l2)\n      Z_corr = Z - gamma * Z_hat\n\n      # CBKNN graph on Z_corr\n      C, D = build_balanced_knn_calibrated(Z_corr, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, k_calib=k_calib, clamp=clamp)\n\n      # Embedding: ComBat PCA(HVGs)\n      _ensure_dense_X(adata)\n      sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n      adata.var['highly_variable'] = hvg_mask\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_pca']\n\n      # Initialize and override neighbors\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances']\
    \ = D.tocsr()\n\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating cooldown and research options**\n\nI'm estimating that the cooldown should be four ticks, but I see it's only three ticks since the last update. I might want to check this further, especially since we pruned certain ticks. Right now, I think we may have one tick left to go, and the token percentage is comfortable at 20%.  \n\nI'm considering proposing further research beyond dual BRBG, like exploring \"Batch adjacency propagation\" while optimizing without labels. However, evaluation limitations might prevent me from using cell type labels effectively. I'll need to consider proxies like cross-batch fractions for computation.**Exploring optimization methods**\n\nI'm thinking about proposing a method that learns a small invertible linear map on principal components (PCs) to enhance cross-batch fractions while preserving local structure. This would be an unsupervised optimization that uses batch labels. Alternatively, I could suggest \"spherical normalization\"\
  \ to equalize scale differences within each batch, similar to whitening.  \n\nAnother promising direction could involve a multi-resolution balanced kNN approach, using different k values to improve both connectivity and local mixing. We also considered eliminating the ComBat approach in favor of using residualized PCs to see how it affects embedding and balanced graphs.**Considering collaboration and code sharing**\n\nI’m exploring the idea of replacing ComBat with a method for global confounder removal using ridge residualization in gene space followed by PCA instead of full ComBat. We can certainly test this as prior attempts utilized unsupervised residualization. \n\nI also want to prepare to share code modules with other agents. It seems shared storage is available for all to access, but I'm confused about the low-level collaboration rules. The Codex forbids importing code, but if shared storage is allowed, we might use it for general utilities. To be cautious, I’ll keep things in\
  \ lineage storage.**Proposing edge scoring and research updates**\n\nI’m considering proposing a \"Confidence scoring for neighbor edges\" that uses cross-batch distances normalized by per-batch scales to slightly weight edges. However, previous attempts at incorporating weights weren’t successful. Alternatives like \"throttled weights\" or \"ordinal weights\" might work. \n\nGiven our evidence that binary edges perform best, I’m thinking weights should minimize scores. We can explore a new paper on \"Separation of concerns\" with a formal analysis, since they previously asked for it. I'll also update the published capsule to reflect these plans, specifically focusing on multidataset generalization across different internal pipelines.**Exploring adaptive techniques for graph algorithms**\n\nI’m considering implementing a \"Batch-stratified angular transformation (BSAT)\" to compute per-batch mean directions in principal component space. This would involve an orthonormal rotation to maintain\
  \ lengths and angles, but it feels similar to earlier approaches. Another direction I'm contemplating is the \"Graph-of-graphs\" method, which looks complex yet promising.  \n\nI could also refine \"Label-free gamma selection,\" noting previous underperformance. Proposing \"Edge smoothing with cross-batch densification\" could also help minority representation. I might combine adaptive quotas per cell with different distance metrics to ensure better mixing. It feels a bit complex with many parameters, but I'm eager to explore these ideas and submit for testing!**Implementing calibrated BalancedGraph**\n\nI'm proposing a new approach for BalancedGraph that incorporates an \"intra-batch cap\" on distances. This would help maintain context by restricting same-batch edges, while still utilizing equal quotas. I also want to explore \"Graph reweighting\" using batch-inverse-degree weights for debiasing. However, I think I’ll focus on \"Rescaled distances per batch,\" which could help equalize\
  \ geometry across batches.  \n\nTo do this, I’ll compute a scale for each batch based on median distances within that batch. This way, I can influence which neighbors are chosen during the cross-batch searches. I can implement the function to adjust distances accordingly, ensuring it adds minimal complexity to the overall process. I feel confident about moving forward with this plan!"
