parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Archive Room. Adaptive residualization (EID 185) improved our lineage best to 0.7209. I will (1) announce the update in Public Memory with code path, (2) add an auto-parameterization utility (label-free) to storage for future work, and (3) reflect in the Reflection Chamber on next fundamental pivots.\n\n/execute_action{goto public_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update: Per-PC adaptive residualization improves Dual BRBG to 0.7209\n  - Best config (EID 185): gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9 (per-PC γ_j = min(gamma_max, λ·R^2_j) on graph PCs), k=48 balanced cosine kNN; embedding = ComBat+PCA64 on HVGs.\n  - Score: 0.7209 (Graph_conn 0.972, cLISI 0.970, ARI 0.732, NMI 0.694, PCR 1.000).\n  - Code paths:\n    • Frozen adaptive pipeline: storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_adapt.py\n    • Frozen non-adaptive baseline (~0.719): storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg.py\n  - Notes:\n    • Conservative adaptive (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9) outperforms more aggressive\
    \ (0.75, 1.0).\n    • Negative results to avoid: robust ComBat variants (PCR collapse), graph PCs>50 (ARI/NMI drop), PC post-weighting (no gain).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/per_pc_resid_auto.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches):\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq, inv = np.unique(batches, return_inverse=True)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], len(uniq)), dtype=np.float32)\n      B[np.arange(batches.shape[0]), inv] = 1.0\n      return B, uniq\n\n  def _ridge_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BtB = B.T @ B\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BtB + l2 * np.eye(BtB.shape[0], dtype=np.float32), B.T @ Z)\n      Zhat = B @ W\n      return Zhat, W\n\n  def _columnwise_r2(y: np.ndarray, yhat: np.ndarray):\n      y = np.asarray(y, dtype=np.float32)\n      yhat = np.asarray(yhat, dtype=np.float32)\n      sse = np.sum((y - yhat) ** 2, axis=0)\n      y_center\
    \ = y - np.mean(y, axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      sst = np.sum(y_center ** 2, axis=0) + 1e-8\n      r2 = 1.0 - (sse / sst)\n      return np.clip(r2, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n  def adaptive_residualize_auto(Z: np.ndarray,\n                                batches,\n                                l2: float = 1e-3,\n                                gamma_max_list=(0.72, 0.74, 0.75),\n                                lam_list=(0.8, 0.9, 1.0),\n                                eps_plateau=1e-4):\n      \"\"\"\n      Label-free selector for (gamma_max, lambda) using only batch predictability proxies.\n      Procedure:\n        - Fit ridge B->Z to get Zhat and pre-correction r2.\n        - For each (gamma_max, lam): gamma_j = min(gamma_max, lam * r2); Zcorr = Z - Zhat * gamma_j.\n        - Refit ridge on Zcorr to obtain Zcorr_hat and r2_resid; summarize mean_r2_resid.\n        - Choose the smallest (gamma_max, lam) whose mean_r2_resid is within eps of the minimum (plateau rule).\n      Returns: (Zcorr_best,\
    \ r2_pre, r2_resid_best, gamma_j_best, chosen_params)\n      \"\"\"\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat_pre, _ = _ridge_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=l2)\n      r2_pre = _columnwise_r2(Z, Zhat_pre)\n\n      best = None\n      results = []\n      for gmax in gamma_max_list:\n          for lam in lam_list:\n              gamma_j = np.minimum(gmax, lam * r2_pre).astype(np.float32)\n              Zcorr = Z - (Zhat_pre * gamma_j[None, :])\n              Zhat_corr, _ = _ridge_fit_predict(B, Zcorr, l2=l2)\n              r2_resid = _columnwise_r2(Zcorr, Zhat_corr)\n              mean_r2 = float(np.mean(r2_resid))\n              results.append((mean_r2, gmax, lam, gamma_j, Zcorr, r2_resid))\n\n      results.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))  # prefer lower mean_r2, then smaller params\n      min_mean = results[0][0]\n      # Select smallest params within plateau\n      chosen = next((r for r in results if r[0] <= min_mean + eps_plateau), results[0])\n      mean_r2, gmax, lam, gamma_j_best,\
    \ Zcorr_best, r2_resid_best = chosen\n      return Zcorr_best, r2_pre, r2_resid_best, gamma_j_best, {'gamma_max': gmax, 'lam': lam, 'mean_r2_resid': mean_r2}\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto reflect}\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Next fundamental pivots to explore beyond 0.72:\n  - Embedding path: Is ComBat+PCA64 the simplest global optimum? Could whitened PCA (sphering) after ComBat stabilize neighbor selection while preserving ARI/NMI, with no extra knobs? Or per-gene robust z-scores before PCA?\n  - Graph path: Quotas are strong. Try a single-parameter soft quota via stochastic mixing with temperature τ, then project to a fixed-degree graph; ensure τ=0 recovers equal quotas.\n  - End-to-end heuristic: Implement label-free auto-selection for (gamma_max, λ) using our per_pc_resid_auto proxies (PCR plateau + minimal params), avoiding multi-dataset overfitting.\n  - Alternative feature sets: BIVG underperformed; consider variance-of-residuals HVGs—compute HVGs on residualized\
    \ counts (post batch regression) using only batch labels, then run Dual BRBG.\n  - Generalization plan: package two frozen baselines (adaptive and non-adaptive), plus auto-selector; document negative results to prevent rabbit holes.\ntick: 3\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating research strategies**\n\nI think we could create a polished second paper on adaptive residualization improvement, but maybe a simpler addendum would suffice. The test encourages deep reflection, although the last attempt at 'reflect' wasn't recognized in Research Counter. I'll head to the Reflection Chamber and use a prompt instead. My token usage is at 41%, which isn't urgent yet, but I might want to prune tokens again soon. I could propose building a heuristic for auto-tuning gamma_max and lam for a better dynamic selection module. But I need to be cautious since Auto-γ v2 didn't perform well before.**Simplifying the approach**\n\nI’m considering a more straightforward method: maximizing lam by focusing on Graph_conn with a constant PCR plateau and ensuring no drop in neighbor distance variance. However, during evaluation, I don't have metrics available in real-time, so I'll need to compute them offline using the dataset. Printing is allowed, but I can't\
  \ read labels during evaluation. I can calculate PCR and Graph_conn only after creating the integrated graph, and I can also compute proxies in the code by myself.**Choosing parameters efficiently**\n\nTo choose gamma_max and lam, I’m planning to conduct a sweep with a time limit of 30 minutes. I could set up a small grid of {0.72, 0.74} for gamma_max and {0.8, 0.9, 1.0} for lam, incorporating an early stopping mechanism using proxies. The evaluation environment expects just one result, but I can adaptively select parameters per run by analyzing the dataset. For evaluating Graph_conn as a normalized metric, I'll compute raw graph connectivity as a proxy and proceed to implement per_pc_resid_auto.py for calculating R^2 and other metrics.**Simplifying proxy metrics**\n\nI'm looking at using simpler proxies for my calculations. First, I'll consider PCR_proxy, which is the mean R^2 after correction computed from Zcorr and predicted values. Since Zcorr isn't directly predicted by B, I’ll recompute\
  \ Zcorr_hat and derive R^2_resid, aiming for a lower value. Next, Graph_conn_proxy will involve calculating the fraction of nodes in the main connected component after balancing the graph. I'll also preserve the embedding and measure variance retention. My goal is to choose parameters with minimal R^2_resid while maintaining LCC above 0.99, and then implement plateau detection to refine my selections!**Planning next steps**\n\nI'm realizing that computing BalancedGraph for each parameter combo might take a lot of time, especially with 20,000 cells and 9 combinations. Since the edges depend on Zcorr distances, I think it might be better to skip building BalancedGraph and just focus on R^2_resid instead. I'll compute proxies based on Zcorr and maybe look at the mean pairwise distance between batches to keep things simple. \n\nGiven my earlier findings, reverting to EID 185 values as defaults makes sense. I’m considering moving to the Reflection Chamber for deeper reflection on next actions\
