parts:
- text: "Embedding path: ComBat+PCA64 vs whitening/robust scaling\n- Why baseline works: ComBat (location/scale) across full genes removes most batch-predictable mean/variance shifts; PCA64 on HVGs preserves dominant biological axes. Cosine in the graph path makes scale changes in embedding less critical, but ARI/NMI/ASW_label are sensitive to how top PCs are weighted.\n- Whitening (sphering) after ComBat:\n  - Effect: forces unit variance per PC; equalizes contribution across components.\n  - Risk: top PCs often carry meaningful biology; flattening can blur inter-type margins, hurting ASW_label/ARI/NMI. Cosine already reduces sensitivity to scale, so whitening likely offers little benefit while risking label degradation.\n  - If tried: implement PCA then divide by singular values; no new knobs. Watch ASW_label/ARI/NMI and cell_cycle. Expect neutral-to-slight negative vs PCA64.\n- Robust per-gene z-scores (median/MAD) before PCA:\n  - Effect: gene-wise robust centering/scaling can stabilize\
    \ PCA when rare outliers/batch tails remain after ComBat.\n  - Risk: reweights low-variance genes upward, possibly inflating noise and shrinking signal PCs, again risking label separation.\n  - If tried: a single toggle (on/off). Prefer running on embedding path only (keep graph path unchanged). Expect mixed outcomes; likely neutral or slightly negative given already strong baseline.\n\nGraph path: soft quotas with a single temperature τ\n- Goal: retain quota structure but allow flexibility near batch boundaries to avoid “forced” cross-batch links that cut through genuine biological gaps.\n- Design (one knob τ):\n  - For each batch b and cell i, compute per-batch candidate distances d_{i,j}, convert to scores s_{i,j} = exp(−d_{i,j}/τ). Allocate an expected per-batch quota q_b = floor(k/nb) plus remainder, then choose edges with highest s_{i,j} subject to expected counts (water-filling across batches). Symmetrize union; binarize.\n  - Limits: τ → 0 recovers equal quotas (deterministic\
    \ per batch); τ → ∞ approaches global nearest neighbors (risk of same-batch dominance).\n- Expected behavior:\n  - Small τ (>0) could slightly improve ARI/NMI by avoiding pathological cross-batch edges while preserving iLISI/kBET and Graph_conn; too large τ risks mixing collapse. Start with τ in {0.05, 0.1, 0.2}. Keep k fixed (48) and graph PCs=50.\n\nEnd-to-end, label-free auto-selection of (gamma_max, λ)\n- Use batch-only proxies; never biology labels:\n  - Pre-residualization: fit B→Z (ridge) to get r2_pre per PC.\n  - For each (gamma_max, λ): apply γ_j = min(gamma_max, λ·r2_pre_j), obtain Z_corr; refit B→Z_corr to get r2_resid; compute mean_r2_resid (lower is better).\n  - Graph proxies on Z_corr: cross-batch fraction in naive kNN (cosine, k=15), and a cheap connectivity proxy (size of the largest connected component on a small subsample).\n- Selection rule (parsimonious):\n  - Choose the smallest (gamma_max, λ) that (i) attains mean_r2_resid within ε of the minimum (plateau), and\
    \ (ii) keeps cross-batch fraction above a fixed threshold (e.g., ≥ baseline quantile), with no drop in connectivity proxy.\n  - Tie-break in favor of smaller parameters. This mirrors our “conservative > aggressive” result (0.74, 0.9 > 0.75, 1.0), and avoids overfitting.\n\nAlternative feature sets beyond BIVG\n- Why BIVG underperformed: penalizing between-batch mean shifts (SSB) at the gene level likely discarded some genuinely informative genes that also varied across batches (confounded axes), reducing ARI/NMI.\n- Variance-of-residuals HVGs (VoR-HVG):\n  - Procedure: regress out batch dummies per gene (ridge) on normalized/log1p counts to obtain residuals R; compute per-gene residual variance Var(R_g); pick top 2000. This retains genes with high within-biology variance after removing batch-predictable mean effects, using batch labels only.\n  - Pros vs BIVG: targets the “what remains after batch” signal rather than penalizing SSB directly; aligns with our successful PC-level residualization.\n\
    \  - Risks: per-gene regressions are heavier but tractable on 2k HVGs; ensure no leakage of cell-type info (we use batch labels only). Expect neutral-to-slight positive on ARI/NMI with minimal impact on mixing.\n\nGeneralization and packaging\n- Baselines to ship:\n  - Frozen non-adaptive Dual BRBG (~0.719): best_dual_brbg.py\n  - Frozen adaptive Dual BRBG (0.7209): best_dual_brbg_adapt.py\n  - Auto-selector utility: per_pc_resid_auto.py with plateau rule and tie-break toward smaller params.\n- Documentation:\n  - Include negative results (robust ComBat variants, graph PCs>50, PC post-weighting, BIVG) and why they fail (PCR collapse; ARI/NMI loss; no mixing gains).\n  - Provide a minimal playbook: run frozen adaptive; if unstable, fallback to non-adaptive; if tuning needed, use auto-selector; only then consider VoR-HVG and soft quota τ as single-knob experiments."
thinking_content: "**Considering batch effects**\n\nI'm thinking about how ComBat removes batch effects while keeping structure intact. Whiten could potentially reduce anisotropy and the dominance of top principal components (PCs). For cosine distances, that doesn't matter. The embedding path uses PCA64 for X_emb, and neighbors are computed with n_neighbors, around 15. I'm curious about how the neighborhood assignments influence the graph evaluation, especially since the embedding path affects ARI/NMI. I need to be cautious because whitening may flatten biological discrepancies.**Testing whiten strategy**\n\nI might want to test the whiten technique without any adjustments. My plan involves measuring ARI/NMI/ASW_label against a baseline and keeping the code minimal. \n\nI’m considering robust per-gene z-scores before PCA, which means I would subtract the median and divide by the MAD for each gene. It might prevent distortion of distributions. \n\nFor the algorithm, I’ll compute candidate\
  \ neighbors and their distances for each source cell—assigning probabilities with softmax. The goal is to build a weighted adjacency based on this approach.**Selecting top edges**\n\nI'm planning to select the top edges across all batches while ensuring that the expected per-batch counts match the quotas. I can use a water-filling approach to allocate edges based on normalized scores. As τ approaches 0, I would choose the nearest edges while ensuring equal quotas; as τ increases, the method focuses on global nearest neighbors, neglecting batch quotas. But keeping one parameter, τ, is crucial. \n\nAdditionally, I'll precompute some metrics like PCR_pre and Graph_conn_pre during the initial residualization, but without labels, I can’t use ARI/NMI.**Utilizing metrics for selection**\n\nI'm considering how to use PCR_resid dropping and increasing Graph_conn considerations. Metrics like iLISI and kBET require labels, and while they can use batch labels, our selection process can’t call those\
  \ metrics directly. Instead, I can use simpler proxies like the mean neighbor cross-batch fraction, graph connectivity, and silhouette scores based on batches. \n\nI may propose minimizing the mean batch R² after residualization while ensuring thresholds for cross-batch fraction and Graph_conn are met. I’ll also keep slope tests for flattening in mind and have a preference for smaller parameters. \n\nAs for alternative feature sets, I might compute the residualization of gene expression at the gene level using a linear model.**Exploring alternative approaches in scRNA counts**\n\nI’m considering an alternative approach for analyzing scRNA counts by running linear regression of gene expression against batch dummies for each gene, then computing residuals. From these, I plan to derive gene variance and select the top 2,000 genes. There’s a risk of increased run time, but using sparse computation could help manage that.\n\nAnother option would be to calculate within-batch variance and average\
  \ it across batches, subtracting between-batch variance from the total. This could provide better residualization at the gene level, potentially replicating ComBat mean correction. I need to make sure to choose highly variable genes (HVGs) based on residualized counts, avoiding those dominated by batch effects.\n\nFor the generalization plan, I’ll focus on packaging and clearly documenting any negative results. I want my reflection to be clear and structured, covering the analysis on embeddings, graph soft quotas, and heuristics. I’ll also outline plans for future experiments while keeping the reflection content straightforward."
