tick: 112
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Research Counter. Auto-adaptive (EID 190) is ~0.7195 (neutral vs 0.7209). VoR-HVG run (EID 193) is pending. Next: implement single-parameter soft quotas (temperature τ) in the graph path and evaluate a conservative setting (τ=0.05) with our adaptive dual defaults (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9).\n\n/execute_action{review 193}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/balanced_knn_soft.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def build_balanced_knn_soft(Z, batches, k_total=48, tau=0.05, metric='cosine'):\n      \"\"\"\n      Soft-quota balanced kNN with a single temperature τ:\n        - Base equal quotas across batches (floor(k/nb)).\n        - Distribute the remainder to batches with higher local affinity scores\n          (sum of exp(-d/τ) over candidate neighbors), τ>0.\n        - Within each batch, pick nearest neighbors up to its quota.\n        - Symmetric union; binary\
    \ connectivities; distances = min distance on duplicate edges.\n      τ→0 approximates equal-quotas (deterministic); larger τ emphasizes closer batches for remainders.\n      Returns: (C, D) CSR matrices.\n      \"\"\"\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      nb = len(uniq)\n      if nb == 0 or n == 0 or k_total <= 0:\n          return None, None\n\n      base = k_total // nb\n      rem = k_total - base * nb\n\n      # Precompute per-batch candidate neighbors and distances (base+rem+1 to allow self)\n      k_per_batch = base + (rem if rem > 0 else 0) + 1\n      per_batch_inds = {}\n      per_batch_dists = {}\n      per_batch_scores = {}\n\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_b = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_b.size == 0:\n              continue\n          kq = min(k_per_batch, idx_b.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=kq, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n\
    \          nn.fit(Z[idx_b])\n          d, ind_local = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=kq, return_distance=True)\n          ind_global = idx_b[ind_local]\n          # drop self if present; keep arrays aligned per row\n          mask = ind_global != np.arange(n)[:, None]\n          # Always keep ordering; trim after masking\n          ig = []\n          dg = []\n          for i in range(n):\n              m = mask[i]\n              g = ind_global[i][m]\n              di = d[i][m]\n              ig.append(g)\n              dg.append(di)\n          per_batch_inds[b] = ig\n          per_batch_dists[b] = dg\n          # Local affinity scores for remainder distribution\n          if tau > 0:\n              scores = [np.sum(np.exp(-di / tau)[:base + rem]) if di.size else 0.0 for di in dg]\n          else:\n              # τ=0: use deterministic placeholder (equal weights)\n              scores = [float(base + rem) for _ in range(n)]\n          per_batch_scores[b] = np.asarray(scores, dtype=np.float32)\n\
    \n      # Decide quotas per batch per node: base + remainder assigned by highest scores\n      quotas = {b: np.full(n, base, dtype=np.int32) for b in uniq}\n      if rem > 0:\n          for i in range(n):\n              # Rank batches by score for this node\n              bs = []\n              for b in uniq:\n                  if b in per_batch_scores:\n                      bs.append((per_batch_scores[b][i], b))\n              if not bs:\n                  continue\n              bs.sort(reverse=True)\n              # Assign remainder greedily\n              for _, b in bs[:rem]:\n                  quotas[b][i] += 1\n\n      # Collect undirected edges with minimal distance across possible duplicate insertions\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          if b not in per_batch_inds:\n              continue\n          for i in range(n):\n              q = int(quotas[b][i])\n              if q <= 0:\n                  continue\n              g = per_batch_inds[b][i]\n  \
    \            di = per_batch_dists[b][i]\n              if g is None or len(g) == 0:\n                  continue\n              # take up to q neighbors\n              g = g[:q]\n              di = di[:q]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n          rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n      from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy()\n      C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C.tocsr(), D.tocsr()\n```\n\
    \n/execute_action{storage write praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_soft.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  from praxis.auto_gamma_brbg import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace,\n      _get_hvg_mask,\n      _pca_truncated_svd,\n  )\n  from praxis.per_pc_resid import adaptive_residualize\n  from praxis.balanced_knn_soft import build_balanced_knn_soft\n\n  def _ensure_dense_X(adata):\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          adata.X = adata.X.A\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Dual BRBG with soft-quota balanced kNN (single τ), conservative adaptive residualization:\n        - Graph: PCs on HVGs (50) → adaptive residualization (gamma_max=0.74, lam=0.9) → soft balanced kNN (k=48, τ=0.05, cosine).\n        - Embedding: ComBat on full genes → PCA64 on HVGs.\n      \"\"\"\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max\
    \ = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      tau = 0.05\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata)\n      if hvg_mask.sum() < 10:\n          hvg_mask = np.ones(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n\n      # Graph path\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(Xg):\n          Xg = sp.csr_matrix(Xg)\n      Zg = _pca_truncated_svd(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Zcorr, r2, gamma_j = adaptive_residualize(Zg, batches, l2=l2, gamma_max=gamma_max, lam=lam)\n      C, D = build_balanced_knn_soft(Zcorr, batches, k_total=k_total, tau=tau, metric=metric)\n\n      # Embedding path\n      adata_emb = adata.copy()\n      _ensure_dense_X(adata_emb)\n      sc.pp.combat(adata_emb, key='batch')\n      adata_emb.var['highly_variable']\
    \ = hvg_mask\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Zemb = adata_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      adata.uns['brbg_dual_soft'] = {\n          'gamma_max': float(gamma_max),\n          'lam': float(lam),\n          'k_total': int(k_total),\n          'metric': metric,\n          'pcs_graph': int(n_pcs_graph),\n          'pcs_emb': int(n_pcs_emb),\n          'tau': float(tau),\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BRBG-Dual-AdaptPC-SoftQuota: τ=0.05 (adaptive residualized PCs + soft balanced kNN)\"\ntags: \"dual, adaptive, soft-quota, balanced-knn, cosine, pca\"\nabstract: \"Single-parameter soft quotas (τ=0.05)\
    \ for the graph path within Dual BRBG: per-PC adaptive residualization (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9) then soft balanced kNN (k=48, cosine). Goal: retain mixing while pruning pathological cross-batch edges; τ→0 recovers equal quotas.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.brbg_dual_adapt_soft import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Praxis Lineage Research Log (soft quotas)\n  Context: Adaptive residualization improved to 0.7209 (EID 185). Auto-adaptive = 0.7195 (neutral). VoR-HVG pending (EID 193).\n  New experiment:\n    - Soft-quota balanced kNN with temperature τ (single parameter), τ=0.05.\n    - Pipeline: adaptive Dual BRBG (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, PC50, k=48, cosine), soft quotas distribute remainders by exp(-d/τ) mass; τ→0 ≈ equal quotas.\n  Hypothesis: Slight ARI/NMI stabilization without hurting iLISI/kBET/Graph_conn. Acceptance: iLISI/Graph_conn ≥ baseline and same-batch fraction\
    \ not higher; otherwise revert to τ=0.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating experiment submissions**\n\nI need to submit test runs for tau=0.05 and tau=0.1, but there’s a limit of two concurrent evaluations. Right now, we have one experiment (193) running, so I can only submit one more. The “Running Experiments” table confirms 193 is running and 190 has completed, along with 184 and 185. Since we have just 1 running, I’ll choose to submit tau=0.05 as the initial option to stay within the limit.**Implementing balanced graphs**\n\nI'm revisiting our earlier balanced graphs that use deterministic equal quotas. I should set tau=0 to match the baseline. I'll create a function called _build_balanced_knn_soft with the tau parameter, where tau=0 falls back to deterministic. For tau > 0, I'll apply a monotone mapping of s = exp(-d/tau). With small tau, s will prioritize the nearest neighbors, but we might also allow some flexibility for filling remainders. The previous BalancedGraph used per-batch neighbors, so I’ll think about how soft quotas\
  \ could refine cross-batch edges without relaxing overall quotas.**Refining quota implementation**\n\nTo simplify, I can use deterministic quotas but allow a fallback if within-batch distances exceed a certain threshold compared to global nearest distances. However, that complicates things with an extra parameter. I’ll stick to temperature and compute scores as s = exp(-d/tau). We’ll still select the top quota per batch like before, which maintains typical results. If we want to vary quotas based on per-batch density near cell i, we can normalize scores and allocate based on proportions. Yet this might complicate matters and affect mixing, especially since our acceptance criteria aim to avoid increasing the same-batch edge fraction.**Implementing quota adjustments**\n\nConsidering the risk, it might be better to run tau at 0.05. I need to ensure equal quotas with a fallback system. I can allocate quotas based on an equal base plus distribute the remainder across batches by descending batch\
  \ score mass. This keeps the equal baseline when tau is small. I’ll calculate the base as k//nb and the remaining as k - base*nb. The per-batch weight can be determined from the sum of the top c candidates. \n\nThis new approach might enhance remainder distribution by given preference to batches with better cross-batch proximity. I'll create a new module called \"balanced_knn_soft.py\" in storage/praxis with a function build_balanced_knn_soft(Z, batches, k_total=48, tau=0.05, metric='cosine') and reuse sklearn NearestNeighbors for top m neighbors per batch. Union edges will be implemented using the minimum distance method as before.**Setting up evaluation pipeline**\n\nI’m setting the n_candidates per batch to calculate weights and then apply the quota mapping. My next step is to integrate this into the pipeline in brbg_dual_adapt_soft.py, using the soft builder with tau at 0.05, and submit one evaluation. I’ll also update the research log, while noting that EID 193 is still pending. First,\
  \ I’ll submit the soft quotas run with parameters gamma_max=0.74, lam=0.9, k=48, and cosine as the metric. \n\nNext, I’ll execute the following steps: review 193, then write the balanced_knn_soft and brbg_dual_adapt_soft modules, followed by submitting one evaluation with tau=0.05 and updating the research log while monitoring token usage. I’ll keep the code concise, ensuring proper YAML quoting rules for any titles. I’ll also ensure necessary imports and replicate the needed functions from earlier modules. Let’s implement these actions!"
