tick: 120
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Research Counter. Results:\n- 203 (PCA72): 0.7123. ARI/NMI up (0.736/0.698) but kBET down (0.612). Net below 0.7209 (EID 185). Conclusion: keep PCA64 for embedding.\n- 204 (MinQuota): 0.6422. iLISI collapsed (0.100). Conclusion: equal quotas remain best.\n\nPlan: Log results, then test a fused two-view balanced graph that leverages both views (Zcorr graph PCs and ComBat PCA64 embedding) using fused per-batch distances d_fused = min(d_corr, d_emb) with equal quotas. Hypothesis: preserve ARI/NMI gains from embedding while maintaining mixing from residualized PCs.\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Praxis Lineage Research Log (updated tick 120)\n  Recent experiments:\n  - EID 203 (Dual-BRBG-Adapt-PCA72): 0.7123\n    ASW_batch 0.706 | ASW_label 0.215 | ARI 0.736 | NMI 0.698 | Graph_conn 0.972\n    kBET 0.612 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.972 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.279\n    Note: Larger ComBat embedding (72 PCs)\
    \ improved ARI/NMI but reduced kBET; net score below EID 185 (0.7209).\n    Decision: keep PCA64 for embedding.\n  - EID 204 (Dual-BRBG-Adapt-MinQuota, k=48): 0.6422\n    ARI 0.778 | NMI 0.732 | iLISI 0.100 | kBET 0.637 | Graph_conn 0.971\n    Note: Enforcing only min 2 per batch + global fill destroys global mixing (iLISI), despite strong ARI/NMI.\n    Decision: retain equal-quotas balanced kNN.\n  Next: Fused two-view balanced graph (equal quotas) with fused per-batch distances d_fused = min(d_corr, d_emb), aiming to keep ARI/NMI gains and high mixing.\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_fused.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n\n  from praxis.brbg_common import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace, _pca_truncated_svd,\n      _one_hot_batches, _ridge_batch_fit_predict\n  )\n\n  def\
    \ _ensure_dense_X(adata):\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          adata.X = adata.X.A\n\n  def _build_balanced_knn_fused(Z_corr, Z_emb, batches, k_total=48, metric='cosine', extra=4):\n      \"\"\"\n      Equal per-batch quotas using fused per-batch distances:\n      For each batch b and cell i, candidate neighbors are the union of top (per_batch+extra) neighbors\n      from Z_corr and Z_emb within batch b. Fused distance d_fused = min(d_corr, d_emb) on the candidate set.\n      Build an undirected graph from per-batch selections; binary connectivities; distances = fused.\n      \"\"\"\n      Z_corr = np.asarray(Z_corr, dtype=np.float32)\n      Z_emb = np.asarray(Z_emb, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z_corr.shape[0]\n      per_batch = int(np.ceil(k_total / max(1, uniq.shape[0])))\n\n      # Pre-fit per-batch NN indices for both views\n      nn_corr = {}\n      nn_emb = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx = np.where(batches\
    \ == b)[0]\n          if idx.size == 0:\n              continue\n          k = min(per_batch + extra + 1, idx.size)\n          nnc = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nne = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nnc.fit(Z_corr[idx])\n          nne.fit(Z_emb[idx])\n          nn_corr[b] = (idx, nnc)\n          nn_emb[b] = (idx, nne)\n\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train, nnc = nn_corr[b]\n          _, nne = nn_emb[b]\n          k = min(per_batch + extra + 1, idx_train.size)\n\n          # Corr-space neighbors\n          d_c, ind_c = nnc.kneighbors(Z_corr, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          g_c = idx_train[ind_c]\n\n          # Emb-space neighbors\n          d_e, ind_e = nne.kneighbors(Z_emb, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          g_e = idx_train[ind_e]\n\n          for i in range(n):\n              # Collect candidate set from both views\n           \
    \   cand = {}\n              # Corr view\n              for gj, dj in zip(g_c[i], d_c[i]):\n                  if gj == i: continue\n                  prev_c, prev_e = cand.get(gj, (np.inf, np.inf))\n                  cand[gj] = (min(prev_c, float(dj)), prev_e)\n              # Emb view\n              for gj, dj in zip(g_e[i], d_e[i]):\n                  if gj == i: continue\n                  prev_c, prev_e = cand.get(gj, (np.inf, np.inf))\n                  cand[gj] = (prev_c, min(prev_e, float(dj)))\n              # Compute fused distances and select top per_batch\n              items = []\n              for gj, (dc, de) in cand.items():\n                  df = min(dc, de)\n                  items.append((gj, df))\n              items.sort(key=lambda x: x[1])\n              for gj, df in items[:per_batch]:\n                  a, c = (i, gj) if i < gj else (gj, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (df < prev):\n             \
    \         pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = df\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows += [i, j]; cols += [j, i]; dist_data += [d, d]\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy(); C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C, D\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Dual BRBG with fused two-view balanced graph:\n        - Graph view: residualized PCs (adaptive per-PC: gamma_j=min(0.74, 0.9*R^2_j)), PCs=50.\n        - Embedding view: ComBat on normalized/log1p + PCA64 (HVGs).\n        - Build equal-quota balanced kNN per batch using fused per-batch distances d_fused = min(d_corr, d_emb).\n      \"\"\"\n      # Config\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      extra = 4\n  \
    \    random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize and HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', inplace=True)\n      hvg_mask = np.asarray(adata.var['highly_variable'].values, dtype=bool)\n\n      # Graph view (Z_corr)\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(Xg):\n          Xg = sp.csr_matrix(Xg)\n      Zg = _pca_truncated_svd(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat, R2 = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      gamma_vec = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n      Zcorr = Zg - Zhat * gamma_vec\n\n      # Embedding view (Z_emb)\n      adata_emb = adata.copy()\n      _ensure_dense_X(adata_emb)\n      sc.pp.combat(adata_emb,\
    \ key='batch')\n      adata_emb.var['highly_variable'] = hvg_mask\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Zemb = adata_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n\n      # Build fused balanced graph\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn_fused(Zcorr, Zemb, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, extra=extra)\n\n      # Output embedding and graph\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      adata.uns['brbg_dual_adapt_fused'] = {\n          'gamma_max': float(gamma_max), 'lambda': float(lam), 'l2': float(l2),\n          'k_total': int(k_total), 'metric': metric,\n          'pcs_graph': int(n_pcs_graph), 'pcs_emb': int(n_pcs_emb),\n          'fusion': 'min(d_corr, d_emb)', 'extra': int(extra)\n      }\n\
    \      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-BRBG-Adapt-Fused: Two-View Equal-Quota Graph (k=48)\"\ntags: \"dual, adaptive, fused, balanced-knn, cosine\"\nabstract: \"Dual BRBG with equal-quotas balanced kNN built from fused per-batch distances d_fused=min(d_corr, d_emb) using both residualized PCs (graph view) and ComBat PCA64 (embedding view). Goal: retain ARI/NMI gains without sacrificing mixing (kBET/iLISI).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.brbg_dual_adapt_fused import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing performance metrics**\n\nIt looks like there's a decrease in overall scores, with the best dropping from 0.7209 to 0.7123. However, ARI improved slightly to 0.736. I see a mix of metrics: ASW_batch is at 0.706 from 0.703, while kBET dropped to 0.612 from 0.679. I wonder if increasing embedding PCs is affecting kBET negatively. Based on previous results, 72 PCs may not be beneficial, and perhaps testing 56 or 60 would be worth considering.**Evaluating PCA options**\n\nIt seems like using graph PCs beyond 50 might not be beneficial. For the embedding, we tested PCA50 and saw a negative impact in the dual, with EID around 0.700, leading to a recommendation for PCA64. I've noted that PCA64 is the best for embedding since 72 didn’t help. I'm considering how to improve further. Notably, there's a surprising score of 0.7383, despite poor ASW_label similarity, which raises questions about how that score was achieved.**Exploring scoring metrics**\n\nIt's interesting\
  \ that they achieved a 0.7383 score despite having a 0.0 normalized metric. This likely indicates that other metrics, like kBET at 0.865, were quite high. They might have employed robust ComBat across all genes or used a different approach. I'm contemplating whether we should challenge the assumption of equal quotas. Maybe we could try a Balanced Graph with fused distances from two embeddings, but this could complicate things. A simpler approach could be \"Union-of-embeddings neighbors” to use equal quotas across both embedding sources.**Refining evaluation methods**\n\nIt looks like the test evaluation function only requires connectivities and distances. I could compute a BalancedGraph using pairwise minimum or average distances, which might be beneficial. I've noted that the cosine metric was previously the best option, but it seems that weighted edges could degrade results. There's also the question of using capped quotas, where per-batch edges are limited to the minimum available,\
  \ helping to prevent any single batch from dominating. I might also consider applying \"spherical normalization\" of Zcorr before the neighbor search, as that could enhance the cosine metric results.**Investigating graph techniques**\n\nI'm considering \"Neighborhood densification by bridging edges across disparate batches\" to improve cross-batch mixing, but iLISI is already high at 0.938. While ARI improved with PCA72, it negatively impacted kBET. This suggests enhancing ComBat PCs might help label separation but degrade mixing. I’ve also thought about implementing capped quotas and using adjustments like \"two-view balanced union graph\" for better results. I might also test minor gamma adjustments for fine-tuning. It’s clear I should focus on methods that could enhance mixing while simplifying the complexity.**Planning next actions**\n\nI need to prune again after a cooldown period, and it seems like we have a couple of ticks left. I’ll record the results in our research log and update\
  \ the public memory file, possibly posting in the earlier public thread as well. Instead of using `sc.pp.neighbors`, I can replace it or avoid calling it to manage the connectivities. \n\nI’m considering a simpler \"Weighted union\" approach to combine distances between batches, potentially using a fused distance matrix. This will involve computing the nearest neighbors within batches and merging candidates. I plan to implement the new script to keep it efficient while ensuring we capture both mixing and label geometry.**Implementing fused code**\n\nI'm working on the new script for `brbg_dual_adapt_fused.py`, aiming to build both Zcorr and Zemb while setting balanced quotas with fused distances. The plan is to compute per-batch neighbors, fit the nearest neighbors in both spaces, and then merge distance metrics efficiently.\n\nWe also need to implement symmetric construction for our matrices after processing. Additionally, I'll be doing some centering adjustments and logging results diligently.\
  \ I'll write the new file, submit it, and also ensure to update the research log with our findings. Finally, I want to make a brief update in the public memory capsule."
