role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Research Counter. 203 (PCA72) = 0.7123; 204 (MinQuota) = 0.6422. Equal quotas remain best; keep PCA64 embedding. I’ll review 207 (fused graph) and launch one cautious variant using a geometric-mean fusion to require support from both views (may improve label structure without losing mixing).\n\n/execute_action{review 207}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_fused_geom.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n\n  from praxis.brbg_common import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace, _pca_truncated_svd,\n      _one_hot_batches, _ridge_batch_fit_predict\n  )\n\n  def _ensure_dense_X(adata):\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          adata.X = adata.X.A\n\n  def _build_balanced_knn_fused_geom(Z_corr, Z_emb, batches, k_total=48, metric='cosine', extra=4):\n      \"\"\"\
    \n      Equal per-batch quotas using fused per-batch distances:\n      Candidate set = union of top (per_batch+extra) neighbors from Z_corr and Z_emb within each batch.\n      Fused distance df = geometric_mean(dc, de) when both present; otherwise min(dc, de).\n      \"\"\"\n      Z_corr = np.asarray(Z_corr, dtype=np.float32)\n      Z_emb  = np.asarray(Z_emb,  dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z_corr.shape[0]\n      per_batch = int(np.ceil(k_total / max(1, uniq.shape[0])))\n\n      # Pre-fit per-batch NN for both views\n      nn_corr, nn_emb = {}, {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx.size == 0:\n              continue\n          k = min(per_batch + extra + 1, idx.size)\n          nnc = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nne = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nnc.fit(Z_corr[idx]); nne.fit(Z_emb[idx])\n\
    \          nn_corr[b] = (idx, nnc)\n          nn_emb[b]  = (idx, nne)\n\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train, nnc = nn_corr[b]\n          _,         nne = nn_emb[b]\n          k = min(per_batch + extra + 1, idx_train.size)\n\n          d_c, ind_c = nnc.kneighbors(Z_corr, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          g_c = idx_train[ind_c]\n          d_e, ind_e = nne.kneighbors(Z_emb, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          g_e = idx_train[ind_e]\n\n          for i in range(n):\n              cand = {}\n              # Collect corr-view\n              for gj, dj in zip(g_c[i], d_c[i]):\n                  if gj == i: continue\n                  prev_c, prev_e = cand.get(gj, (np.inf, np.inf))\n                  cand[gj] = (min(prev_c, float(dj)), prev_e)\n              # Collect emb-view\n              for gj, dj in zip(g_e[i], d_e[i]):\n                  if gj == i: continue\n                  prev_c, prev_e = cand.get(gj, (np.inf, np.inf))\n\
    \                  cand[gj] = (prev_c, min(prev_e, float(dj)))\n\n              # Fuse distances and select top per_batch\n              items = []\n              for gj, (dc, de) in cand.items():\n                  if np.isfinite(dc) and np.isfinite(de):\n                      df = float(np.sqrt(max(dc * de, 0.0)))\n                  else:\n                      df = min(dc, de)\n                  items.append((gj, df))\n              items.sort(key=lambda x: x[1])\n              for gj, df in items[:per_batch]:\n                  a, c = (i, gj) if i < gj else (gj, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (df < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = df\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows += [i, j]; cols += [j, i]; dist_data += [d, d]\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)),\
    \ shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy(); C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C, D\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Dual BRBG with fused two-view balanced graph (geometric-mean fusion):\n        - Graph view: residualized PCs (adaptive per-PC: gamma_j=min(0.74, 0.9*R^2_j)), PCs=50.\n        - Embedding view: ComBat on normalized/log1p + PCA64 (HVGs).\n        - Build equal-quota balanced kNN per batch using dfused=geomean(dc, de) (fallback to min if one missing).\n      \"\"\"\n      # Config\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      extra = 4\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3',\
    \ batch_key='batch', inplace=True)\n      hvg_mask = np.asarray(adata.var['highly_variable'].values, dtype=bool)\n\n      # Graph view\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(Xg): Xg = sp.csr_matrix(Xg)\n      Zg = _pca_truncated_svd(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat, R2 = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      gamma_vec = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n      Zcorr = Zg - Zhat * gamma_vec\n\n      # Embedding view\n      adata_emb = adata.copy()\n      _ensure_dense_X(adata_emb)\n      sc.pp.combat(adata_emb, key='batch')\n      adata_emb.var['highly_variable'] = hvg_mask\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Zemb = adata_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n\n      # Fused equal-quota balanced graph\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn_fused_geom(Zcorr,\
    \ Zemb, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, extra=extra)\n\n      # Output\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      adata.uns['brbg_dual_adapt_fused_geom'] = {\n          'gamma_max': float(gamma_max), 'lambda': float(lam), 'l2': float(l2),\n          'k_total': int(k_total), 'metric': metric, 'pcs_graph': int(n_pcs_graph), 'pcs_emb': int(n_pcs_emb),\n          'fusion': 'geometric-mean(dc, de) with min fallback', 'extra': int(extra)\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-BRBG-Adapt-FusedGeom: Two-View Equal-Quota Graph (k=48, geomean fusion)\"\ntags: \"dual, adaptive, fused, geometric-mean, balanced-knn, cosine\"\nabstract: \"Dual BRBG with equal-quotas balanced kNN built from fused per-batch distances\
    \ using geometric mean of residualized-PC and ComBat-PCA64 per-batch distances (fallback to min if one is missing). Tests whether requiring agreement from both views boosts ARI/NMI without losing mixing.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.brbg_dual_adapt_fused_geom import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```"
thinking_content: "**Assessing evaluations and experiments**\n\nI need to respond as Praxis I at Research Counter. I've just submitted fused method evaluation 207, and while waiting for results, I've seen new results for evaluations 203 and 204. Interestingly, PCA72 improved ARI/NMI but decreased kBET, and MinQuota dramatically reduced iLISI, suggesting equal quotas are better. I'm considering submitting reviewed 207 while thinking about future experiments and the possibility of publishing if gains are realized. Since I can still run one more evaluation, I should decide quickly!**Evaluating PCA configurations**\n\nI'm considering testing the PCA64 embedding with a fused graph, comparing k=51 to k=48, and using different aggregators like \"min(distance)\" versus \"harmonic mean\". However, I want to stick to minimal changes based on principle. The fused method applies equal per-batch quotas using fused distances. While I plan to test the min aggregator first, I might explore others like geometric\
  \ mean later. Historically, k=48 performed best for dual fixed situations. To avoid overspending tokens, I’ll focus on the fused-geomean aggregator or k=51. I need to choose wisely!**Evaluating PCA64 with adjustments**\n\nI'm considering evaluating the PCA64 embedding with a gamma_max of 0.75 and lam=0.9, although we've previously found that 0.74 was the best gamma_max. It's important to log these results. The \"Automatic System Tips\" mention that implementation details count, so I need to fine-tune instead of giving up. I'll attempt to review evaluation 207 while submitting the \"Dual-BRBG-Adapt-Fused-Geom\" using fused distance. I realize the cosine distances may not be directly comparable, but it should be alright since their ranges are similar.**Planning fused aggregation strategies**\n\nI'm focusing on using equal quotas computed per batch. For the fused method, I'll create a candidate union from the top options of both views. The min() function for fused distance will help ensure\
  \ we include edges that are strongly supported by either view, potentially mixing while preserving ARI. I could suggest using a geomean aggregator to penalize weak edges in either view; this may enhance label preservation, albeit with some cost. I plan to stick to minimal modifications, testing with extra parameters and implementing the geometric mean aggregator over time.**Implementing the fused graph**\n\nI’m working on modifying the _build_balanced_knn_fused function to use the fused distance formula dfused = sqrt(dc * de) along with a candidate union. I’ll add a small epsilon and log the changes in the research_log. I'll execute review 207, write the storage changes, and submit a new evaluation, 208. \n\nMaintaining proper YAML quoting is important, so I’ll ensure everything is set correctly and keep the responses brief, as the desired verbosity is 3. I also need to confirm that we use the 'praxis' path in the code. \n\nLet’s craft the necessary commands!"
