    \ PCA64 embedding.\n  - EID 204 (Min-Quota graph: min 2 per batch + global fill) = 0.6422 | UUID 3ef3d219-66d7-476d-bf2b-b2513a9d4d92\n    iLISI collapsed (0.100) despite strong ARI/NMI; rejected. Equal quotas remain best.\n  - Fused two-view graphs (graph view = residualized PCs; emb view = ComBat PCA64):\n    • EID 207 (min fusion: d_fused=min(d_corr, d_emb)) = 0.7177 | UUID 07abbb07-0a52-4687-80b4-d061df1c7f9f\n    • EID 208 (geometric-mean fusion) = 0.7133 | UUID 74506e25-5b9a-442d-95a5-a2a1737db993\n    • EID 209 (harmonic-mean fusion) = 0.7137 | UUID 23a60044-5b1d-4144-a32a-462ae2ba0907\n    Observation: modest ARI/NMI changes but mixing (kBET/iLISI) or net score worsens; fusion variants underperform equal-quotas baseline.\n  - Degree sweep (adaptive baseline):\n    • EID 210 (k=45) = 0.7178 | UUID 800c8743-86c3-4dbe-aa34-49c813b3891f\n    • EID 212 (k=42) = pending\n    Observation: k=48 remains preferred; 45 underperforms; 42 pending.\n\n  Consolidated recommendation:\n  - Freeze\
    \ EID 185 configuration as primary: γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3; balanced kNN (equal per-batch quotas), k=48; cosine; binary; embedding = ComBat PCA64; HVGs = Seurat v3 (batch-aware).\n  - Negative results (documented above): PCA72 embedding, min-quota graph, fused graphs (min/geomean/harmonic), and k=45 all underperform the primary.\n\n  Next steps (planned):\n  - Complete k=42 evaluation (EID 212). If tied, keep k=48 for robustness.\n  - Analytical note (label-free): characterize per-PC batch R² spectrum under ComBat vs residualized PCs and its link to kBET/iLISI, to explain the narrow optimum around γ≈0.74–0.75.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning research updates**


  I need to craft my next response, including identity and location, which is in Research Counter. They''ve reviewed 210 and got a score of 0.7178, which is lower than the target of 0.7209. Meanwhile, 212 is currently running with k=42. My plan is to freeze EID 185 as the current best result and consider publishing a follow-up, or adding to the archive. With 78,006 tokens used of a 270,000 budget, we should proceed carefully and consider updating the research log.**Summarizing research decisions**


  I’m thinking we might want to consolidate our findings and publish a short archive addendum. This would summarize the best configuration we''ve identified (EID 185) and highlight the negative fusion results. The station has an archive room, and we’ve previously published an accepted work on "Dual BRBG.” I believe it’s reasonable to publish "Adaptive Residualization improves to 0.7209," assertive of EID 185''s improvement. Plus, it’ll be good to update the public memory and reflect on the underperformance of other models, considering new directions for future work.**Planning storage actions**


  Since the user hasn’t requested additional actions, I should focus on creating an archive addendum and updating the research log. I’m also considering token management, especially to prune older ticks, but it looks like we just pruned a cooldown, so I can do it again now. However, with 78,006 tokens used, it may not be necessary to prune right away. I want to make sure to write the best code into storage at the path we established before, ensuring everything is correctly saved.**Creating an archive update**


  I’m preparing to write an Archive reply or possibly a new capsule. The Archive Room shows our main capsules; I can either add an addendum to the existing #5 or create a new one. Since the earlier work— "Dual BRBG"— is capsule ID #5, I can reply with results for EID 185 (0.7209) and others like 207, 208, and so on, including their RESULT_UUIDs. However, I need to find EID 185''s UUID, as it wasn''t included in the previous logs.**Planning evaluation review and archive update**


  I''m noticing there''s a lack of UUID information for EID 185 in the logs, which isn’t ideal. I could just list it as "UUID unknown," but instead, I’ll add an action to review EID 185 to fetch those logs. Once I do that, I can plan to go to the Archive Room and reply to capsule #5 with an addendum that includes our findings, plus a summary of all IDs and their scores. Let''s also think about proposing an idea for spectral preconditioning or per-cell shrinkage as a future direction. Time to implement these actions!'
