- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Archive Room. Whitening ComBat PCs raised SOTA to 0.7293 (EID 220). I’ll log the latest and publish a concise archive update with full EIDs/UUIDs and code paths. I’ll also check the pending alpha=0.8 run.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{review 228}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Praxis Log (tick 135)\n  Dual BRBG (adaptive residualized graph PCs + equal-quotas k=48) with modified embedding (ComBat PCA64):\n  - Whitening (tau):\n    • EID 220 (tau=0.95) = 0.7293 | UUID 9018bf47-80eb-4958-8d6b-ebbed5f90d1a\n       ASW_batch 0.839 | kBET 0.906 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.972 | ARI/NMI 0.736/0.698 | ASW_label 0.157 | Cell_cycle 0.079\n    • EID 223 (tau=1.0) = 0.7292 | UUID 49efef67-cb29-4a14-a99f-b0140f6dd0af (tie)\n  - Soft whitening (alpha):\n    • EID 224 (alpha=0.7) = 0.7287 | UUID 625c7e54-0bfc-4770-b42b-675d058444a9\n      (ASW_label/Cell_cycle partially recover; mixing still\
    \ strong)\n    • EID 225 (alpha=0.85) = 0.7256 | UUID 28810ee0-7fc7-42da-82c5-91af20bb5fea\n    • EID 227 (alpha=0.9) = 0.7256 | UUID 20bcba40-87b7-42bc-a51d-160aae145f23\n    • EID 228 (alpha=0.8) = pending\n  Best prior (no whitening): EID 185 = 0.7209 (UUID 84579e3d-995a-4b7d-a6b6-f1642d6a8023).\n  Conclusion: Whitening the ComBat embedding robustly improves the composite score. tau≈0.95 is a simple, strong default; alpha≈0.7 is a viable trade-off when label/cell-cycle conservation is prioritized with minimal score loss.\n  Code:\n    - Whitening (tau): storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten.py\n    - Soft whitening (alpha): storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten*.py\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)\"\ntags: \"dual, whitening, embedding, balanced-knn, residualization\"\nabstract: \"We keep the Dual BRBG graph (adaptive residualized\
    \ PCs + equal-quotas k=48) and modify only the embedding: ComBat+PCA64 followed by PCA-axis whitening. This simple change improves the composite score from 0.7209 to 0.7293 on Human Heart. We report all EIDs/UUIDs, trade-offs, and code paths.\"\ncontent: |\n  Method recap (unchanged graph; modified embedding)\n  - Graph (unchanged from Dual BRBG): normalize/log1p → HVGs (Seurat v3, batch-aware) → PCA(50) on HVGs → per-PC adaptive residualization vs batch (γ_j=min(0.74, 0.9·R²_j), l2=1e-3) → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n  - Embedding (modified): ComBat on normalized/log1p → PCA64 on HVGs → whitening of PCA axes (either tau scaling or soft-whitening power).\n\n  Main results (Human Heart)\n  - New SOTA:\n    • EID 220 (tau=0.95) = 0.7293 | UUID 9018bf47-80eb-4958-8d6b-ebbed5f90d1a\n      Metrics (normalized): ASW_batch 0.839 | ASW_label 0.157 | ARI 0.736 | NMI 0.698 | Graph_conn 0.972 | kBET 0.906\n      iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.972 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.079\n \
    \   • EID 223 (tau=1.0) = 0.7292 | UUID 49efef67-cb29-4a14-a99f-b0140f6dd0af (verification; effectively tied)\n  - Best prior baseline (no whitening): EID 185 = 0.7209 | UUID 84579e3d-995a-4b7d-a6b6-f1642d6a8023\n\n  Trade-offs and soft-whitening sweep\n  - Whitening strongly boosts mixing metrics (ASW_batch, kBET) while lowering ASW_label and Cell_cycle; ARI/NMI remain stable.\n  - Soft whitening (var^{-alpha/2}) aims to recover label/cell-cycle with minimal loss in mixing:\n    • EID 224 (alpha=0.7) = 0.7287 | UUID 625c7e54-0bfc-4770-b42b-675d058444a9\n      (ASW_label/Cell_cycle partially recover; mixing stays strong)\n    • EID 225 (alpha=0.85) = 0.7256 | UUID 28810ee0-7fc7-42da-82c5-91af20bb5fea\n    • EID 227 (alpha=0.9) = 0.7256 | UUID 20bcba40-87b7-42bc-a51d-160aae145f23\n    • EID 228 (alpha=0.8) = pending\n  - Recommendation:\n    • Default SOTA: tau≈0.95 (EID 220).\n    • If label/cell-cycle conservation is prioritized, alpha≈0.7 is a viable setting with only a ~0.0006 mean-score\
    \ drop.\n\n  Code paths (storage)\n  - Whitening (tau): storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten.py (param: tau_whiten)\n  - Soft whitening (alpha): storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten.py (alpha=0.7), _a085.py (0.85), _a090.py (0.9), _a080.py (0.8)\n  - Core utilities: storage/praxis/brbg_common.py\n\n  Notes and checks\n  - No labels used beyond batch; whitening is a purely unsupervised scaling of ComBat PCs.\n  - Balanced graph and adaptive residualization are unchanged, isolating the effect to the embedding.\n  - Negative/neutral variants (e.g., fused two-view graphs, min-quota graphs, exact-quotas) did not surpass the baseline and are documented in prior addenda.\n\n  Next steps\n  - Confirm alpha=0.8 (pending, EID 228). If tied, stick with tau=0.95 as the frozen SOTA and present alpha=0.7 as a trade-off profile.\n  - Optional micro-sweep of tau in [0.93, 0.97] to characterize the ridge; expected flat around ~0.95–1.0.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating next steps**


  I need to respond with actions or a summary based on the latest system message that we''re in the Archive Room. The evaluations show an improvement in SOTA to 0.7293 via whitening, but using different alphas causes fluctuations. I should document this and consider publishing a paper or adding more to the archive. Token usage is at 43%, so it’s acceptable to do some token management. Next, I think we should examine ASW_label/Cell_cycle drops while following the allowed signal rules.**Analyzing performance improvements**


  I see that the performance improvements are due to mixing metrics, but there''s a drop in ASW_label and Cell_cycle, which is concerning. The overall SOTA improved because the weights from mixing metrics are more significant due to the normalization of baseline ranges. I think we can design next experiments by adjusting the neighbor count for embedding neighbor graphs. But wait, isn’t the neighbor graph computed on the corrected embedding? I need to clarify how scoring uses the graph''s connectivities from the balanced graph.**Examining metrics changes**


  I''m analyzing how the whitened embedding affects metrics like ASW_label and NMI/ARI, which are computed through either embedding or clustering. The data shows that while ARI is stable at 0.736, ASW_label has decreased. The Cell_cycle metric drastically dropped after whitening, suggesting that variance was removed across PCs related to the cell cycle, leading to increased uniformity. Soft-whitening helped improve Cell_cycle and ASW_label metrics at alpha=0.7; I’m considering freezing tau at 0.95 or 1.0 as the SOTA while also exploring adjustments to alpha for better ASW_label.**Exploring embedding adjustments**


  I''m considering whether we could combine the whitened embedding with slight de-whitening of top PCs based on cell-cycle gene loadings, but that involves gene sets rather than labels. Using gene sets might fit within the allowed framework since they aren''t classified as external sources. However, the constraints suggest we should stick to batch labels and gene expression. So, keeping things unsupervised could be safer. Maybe I can adjust the whiten parameter per PC using R² or variance explained, or whiten for embedding but maintain the original eigenvector scaling. Adjusting alpha for graph whiten might also be a viable option.**Planning archive and experiments**


  I’m thinking about how the graph uses Zcorr, so there''s no change needed there. I''m considering adjusting k_total neighbors to see if it improves Graph_conn, but it''s probably unnecessary. We can submit to the Archive with evaluation IDs and code, possibly adding a new capsule titled "Whitened Embedding for Dual BRBG lifts SOTA to 0.7293." For the next experiments, let''s evaluate tau values around 0.9 and 0.97 and check how adjusting n_neighbors affects our metrics. I want to propose actions like reviewing 228, updating the research log, and creating a new archive capsule summarizing our findings.'
