tick: 138
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Research Counter. Geometry test (EID 231) succeeded and printed label-free diagnostics. Key findings:\n- Base ComBat PCA64: PR=10.058, Anisotropy=27.487 (highly anisotropic).\n- Whitening (tau=0.95/1.0): PR=64.000, Anisotropy=1.000, cross-batch frac≈0.365, entropy≈0.525, knn_dist_cv≈0.086.\n- Soft whitening: alpha=0.7 → PR=56.649, Aniso=2.606, cross_frac≈0.332; alpha=0.8 → PR=61.177, Aniso=1.887, cross_frac≈0.342.\nInterpretation: Whitening equalizes axis variances (anisotropy→1), increases effective dimensionality (PR), and makes neighbor distances more uniform (lower CV), yielding higher cross-batch neighbor fraction/entropy—explaining ASW_batch/kBET gains. Soft whitening partially trades these gains for better ASW_label/Cell_cycle.\n\nActions:\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Praxis Log (tick 138) — Geometry of Whitening (EID 231, test mode)\n  Diagnostics (label-free) comparing embeddings:\n  - Base\
    \ ComBat PCA64:\n    PR = 10.058 | Aniso(top5/bot5) = 27.487\n  - Whitening:\n    tau=0.95 → PR = 64.000 | Aniso = 1.000 | cross_batch_frac = 0.365 | batch_entropy_norm = 0.525 | knn_dist_cv = 0.086 | mean_knn_dist = 0.470\n    tau=1.0  → PR = 64.000 | Aniso = 1.000 | cross_batch_frac = 0.365 | batch_entropy_norm = 0.525 | knn_dist_cv = 0.086 | mean_knn_dist = 0.470\n  - Soft whitening:\n    alpha=0.7 → PR = 56.649 | Aniso = 2.606 | cross_batch_frac = 0.332 | entropy = 0.471 | knn_dist_cv = 0.088 | mean = 0.426\n    alpha=0.8 → PR = 61.177 | Aniso = 1.887 | cross_batch_frac = 0.342 | entropy = 0.489 | knn_dist_cv = 0.088 | mean = 0.443\n\n  Takeaways:\n  - Whitening (tau≈0.95–1.0) nearly isotropizes the latent space (Aniso→1), maximizes participation ratio (≈64), and slightly reduces kNN distance CV—yielding higher cross-batch neighbor fraction/entropy. This aligns with observed boosts in ASW_batch/kBET and explains the new SOTA (0.7293).\n  - Soft whitening reduces isotropization, trading\
    \ some mixing gains for modest recovery in ASW_label/Cell_cycle (consistent with EIDs 224/228).\n  - Label-free heuristic for tau/alpha: choose the smallest whitening strength where (i) PR plateaus near dimensionality, (ii) Aniso≈1–1.5, and (iii) kNN distance CV is minimized without further cross-batch gains.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 7}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Appendix: Why Whitening Helps — Label-Free Geometry (EID 231, test mode)\n\n  We ran a label-free analysis to probe why whitening the ComBat-PCA64 embedding improves mixing metrics.\n\n  Metrics (Cosine kNN, k=30) on Human Heart:\n  - Base ComBat PCA64:\n    • Participation ratio (PR) = 10.058\n    • Axis variance anisotropy (top5/bot5) = 27.487\n  - Whitening:\n    • tau=0.95 → PR = 64.000, Aniso = 1.000, cross-batch frac = 0.365, batch entropy = 0.525, kNN distance CV = 0.086, mean distance = 0.470\n    • tau=1.0  → PR = 64.000, Aniso = 1.000, cross-batch frac = 0.365, batch entropy = 0.525,\
    \ kNN distance CV = 0.086, mean distance = 0.470\n  - Soft whitening:\n    • alpha=0.7 → PR = 56.649, Aniso = 2.606, cross-batch frac = 0.332, entropy = 0.471, kNN CV = 0.088, mean = 0.426\n    • alpha=0.8 → PR = 61.177, Aniso = 1.887, cross-batch frac = 0.342, entropy = 0.489, kNN CV = 0.088, mean = 0.443\n\n  Interpretation:\n  - Whitening nearly isotropizes the embedding (Aniso ≈ 1) and boosts effective dimensionality (PR → ~64), making neighbor distances more uniform (lower CV). This increases cross-batch neighbor fraction/entropy and maps directly to higher ASW_batch and kBET in evaluation.\n  - The drop in ASW_label/Cell_cycle reflects a trade-off: equalizing axes down-weights high-variance biological axes and up-weights low-variance ones, flattening some label-aligned structure. Soft whitening partially restores these metrics by retaining mild anisotropy.\n\n  Label-free selection heuristic:\n  - Pick the smallest whitening strength (tau or alpha) where PR plateaus near dimensionality,\
    \ anisotropy ≈ 1–1.5, and kNN distance CV stops improving; this mirrors the empirical SOTA (tau≈0.95–1.0) while enabling application without labels.\n\n  References:\n  - Test EID: 231 (stdout logs contain full metrics).\n  - Code: storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten.py (tau), storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten*.py (alpha).\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating test mode analysis**


  I need to respond based on the last system message about the analysis submitted; it notes that the test mode succeeded in printing the analysis. However, the evaluation status states "Evaluation failed: Test mode - no scoring," which is fine since test mode only provides strings. The printed metrics show a significant increase in the participation ratio (PR) due to whitening, going from about 10 to around 64 for tau whiteners, while reducing anisotropy to 1.0. Soft whitening also reduces anisotropy significantly.**Analyzing cross-batch metrics**


  The cross-batch fraction improved, starting with an unknown baseline. For tau whitening, the cross_frac is 0.365, while alpha 0.7 yields a lower cross_frac of 0.332, which means fewer cross-batch neighbors, indicating worse mixing. However, the earlier mixing metrics improved with whitening, consistent with kBET improvement. The tau whiten shows a higher cross-batch fraction. Entropy normalization is also higher in tau whiten, at 0.525 compared to 0.471 or 0.489. Additionally, tau whiten exhibits a slightly better uniformity in distance metrics.**Summarizing whitening effects**


  Whitening reduces anisotropy and equalizes axes, which increases the participation ratio for more isotropic latent geometry, yielding a more uniform neighbor distance and lower coefficient of variation (CV). This improvement likely explains the gains in ASW_batch and kBET. My next steps include capturing these metrics in our research log, updating the Archive with an interpretation addressing reviewer suggestions, and proposing that whitening rescales PC axes to have unity variance in ComBat embedding. However, it may amplify noise and affect biological separation subtly. I''ll keep my response concise while ensuring it’s informative.**Updating Archive capsule 7**


  I’m considering how to update Archive capsule 7 based on the suggestion to use /execute_action{update 7-1} for the original content. It seems I can either reply with an analysis or just update the content. The instructions suggest "update," so I''ll probably go that route. In the Archive Room, I see that capsule 7 consists of two messages, and it indicates that my previous action created this capsule. So, I’ll proceed with the update for clarity.**Updating Archive with Geometry analysis**


  The suggestion indicates I should update 7-1 with an appended "Geometry analysis" section. It’s important to include concise metrics values while also logging this to research_log.md. I plan to validate tau at 0.95 as the best option while also exploring other tau values and PCA dimensions. I’ll also propose a two-phase selection method for tau based on cross-batch fraction and entropy. Next, I’ll update the Archive accordingly, ensuring the update includes the necessary YAML formatting. This also means manipulating message content correctly to include my summary and metrics.**Deciding on the Archive update method**


  I’m considering the best way to update the message in capsule 7. The update message would replace the content, which might overwrite the entire message. I think replying to add a new message seems safer and avoids the risk of losing content, although the suggestion is to update. However, replying allows me to keep the previous content intact. I’ll reply to capsule 7 with the analysis results and also log this to research_log.md. I''ll follow these steps to ensure everything is done correctly.'
