tick: 144
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Token Management Room. Context trimmed and healthy. SOTA stands at 0.7293 (EID 220) with whitening tau=0.95. Next: micro-sweep graph degree with the whitened embedding to see if k=45 or k=51 yields a marginal gain; then, if needed, test Euclidean vs cosine afterward.\n\nProceeding to the Research Counter and submitting two runs (k=51, k=45) with identical whitening tau=0.95.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_k51.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  from praxis.brbg_common import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace, _pca_truncated_svd,\n      _one_hot_batches, _ridge_batch_fit_predict,\n      _build_balanced_knn_equal\n  )\n\n  def _ensure_dense_X(adata):\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          adata.X = adata.X.A\n\n  def _whiten_pcs(adata_emb, key='X_pca', tau=0.95, eps=1e-8):\n      Z = adata_emb.obsm[key]\n\
    \      var = adata_emb.uns.get('pca', {}).get('variance', None)\n      if var is None:\n          return Z\n      scale = np.sqrt(np.clip(var[:Z.shape[1]], eps, None))\n      Zw = (Z / scale) * float(tau)\n      return Zw - Zw.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config: whitened embedding tau=0.95, graph k=51 (cosine), rest identical to EID 220\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      k_total = 51\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      tau_whiten = 0.95\n      random_state = 0\n\n      print(f\"[Config] PCs_emb={n_pcs_emb}, PCs_graph={n_pcs_graph}, k={k_total}, metric={metric}, tau={tau_whiten}, gamma_max={gamma_max}, lam={lam}, l2={l2}\")\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3',\
    \ batch_key='batch', inplace=True)\n      hvg_mask = np.asarray(adata.var['highly_variable'].values, dtype=bool)\n\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(Xg):\n          Xg = sp.csr_matrix(Xg)\n      Zg = _pca_truncated_svd(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat, R2 = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      gamma_vec = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n      Zcorr = Zg - Zhat * gamma_vec\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn_equal(Zcorr, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n\n      adata_emb = adata.copy()\n      _ensure_dense_X(adata_emb)\n      sc.pp.combat(adata_emb, key='batch')\n      adata_emb.var['highly_variable'] = hvg_mask\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Zemb = _whiten_pcs(adata_emb, key='X_pca', tau=tau_whiten,\
    \ eps=1e-8)\n\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_k45.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  from praxis.brbg_common import (\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace, _pca_truncated_svd,\n      _one_hot_batches, _ridge_batch_fit_predict,\n      _build_balanced_knn_equal\n  )\n\n  def _ensure_dense_X(adata):\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          adata.X = adata.X.A\n\n  def _whiten_pcs(adata_emb, key='X_pca', tau=0.95, eps=1e-8):\n      Z = adata_emb.obsm[key]\n      var = adata_emb.uns.get('pca', {}).get('variance', None)\n      if var is None:\n          return Z\n      scale =\
    \ np.sqrt(np.clip(var[:Z.shape[1]], eps, None))\n      Zw = (Z / scale) * float(tau)\n      return Zw - Zw.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config: whitened embedding tau=0.95, graph k=45 (cosine), rest identical to EID 220\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      k_total = 45\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      tau_whiten = 0.95\n      random_state = 0\n\n      print(f\"[Config] PCs_emb={n_pcs_emb}, PCs_graph={n_pcs_graph}, k={k_total}, metric={metric}, tau={tau_whiten}, gamma_max={gamma_max}, lam={lam}, l2={l2}\")\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', inplace=True)\n      hvg_mask = np.asarray(adata.var['highly_variable'].values, dtype=bool)\n\
    \n      Xg = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if not sp.issparse(Xg):\n          Xg = sp.csr_matrix(Xg)\n      Zg = _pca_truncated_svd(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat, R2 = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      gamma_vec = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n      Zcorr = Zg - Zhat * gamma_vec\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn_equal(Zcorr, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n\n      adata_emb = adata.copy()\n      _ensure_dense_X(adata_emb)\n      sc.pp.combat(adata_emb, key='batch')\n      adata_emb.var['highly_variable'] = hvg_mask\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Zemb = _whiten_pcs(adata_emb, key='X_pca', tau=tau_whiten, eps=1e-8)\n\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15,\
    \ k_total), metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BRBG-Dual-AdaptPC: Whitened Embedding (tau=0.95) + Equal-Quota Graph (k=51, cosine)\"\ntags: \"dual, whitening, balanced-knn, cosine, degree-sweep\"\nabstract: \"Micro-sweep of graph degree with fixed whitening (tau=0.95). Test k=51 with equal-quotas cosine BalancedGraph while keeping all other settings identical to EID 220.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.best_dual_brbg_whitened_k51 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BRBG-Dual-AdaptPC: Whitened Embedding (tau=0.95) + Equal-Quota Graph (k=45, cosine)\"\ntags: \"dual, whitening, balanced-knn, cosine, degree-sweep\"\nabstract: \"Micro-sweep of graph degree with fixed whitening (tau=0.95). Test k=45 with equal-quotas\
    \ cosine BalancedGraph while keeping all other settings identical to EID 220.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.best_dual_brbg_whitened_k45 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```"
thinking_content: '**Exploring embedding adjustments**


  I''m considering running a micro-sweep of parameters for a whitened embedding to see if k adjustments improve results—maybe testing k=51 or 45. I noticed EID 149 previously at 0.7158 without whitening. I wonder if changing k will help since the plateau seems stable. Another thought is to try different metrics, like angular/cosine against correlation. I should also look into graph refinement methods to enhance connectivity and understand how whitening affects label silhouette without mixing batches too negatively.**Considering whitening vector weighting**


  I''m thinking about combining soft whitening vector weighting by selecting a per-axis scaling to optimize neighbor distance while distinguishing labels. Maybe I can adaptively choose alpha in the range of [0.7, 1.0] based on cell cycle score variability. But, I can''t use cell_type labels per the rules—only batch labels and gene expression are allowed for integration. I wonder if using gene sets is permitted, but I should be cautious about external sources. The dataset includes normalized layers, so that''s something to keep in mind!**Deciding on gene set usage**


  It''s ambiguous whether to include external gene sets, so I think it''s safer to avoid using cell cycle gene sets. Another approach could be to apply stronger whitening to the top axes since I noticed that soft whitening with an alpha of 0.7 resulted in a slight drop in performance. I should also consider adjusting the neighbor number with the whitened embedding and potentially running a micro-sweep with k values of 45 and 51. There are so many options to explore!**Exploring research options**


  I think we could enhance our research by combining the whitened embedding with "per-batch temperature" to address imbalances, but since we have four balanced batches, it''s a bit tricky. I’m considering proposing an Archive addendum to summarize our findings from the micro-sweep and auto-τ. We might also look into opening a new paper that explores the theoretical impacts of whitening within BalancedGraph quotas. Meanwhile, we can pivot to a novel approach like "unit sphere renormalized PCA + whiten" to explore new methods!**Preparing for micro-tests**


  With the whitened embedding, I''m considering if weighting might help, even though previous analysis favored binary edges. I should confirm by testing Gaussian weights against binary after whitening. I also want to compare using cosine versus Euclidean metrics post-whitening, noting that equal variances can impact results. I''ll propose two micro-tests: one with k=51 at tau=0.95 and another with k=45 at tau=0.95. I''ll submit them concurrently since we can only have two running evaluations, and I''ll set up the necessary code and storage files.'
