    \          – EID 235 (tau=0.97) = 0.7291 (UUID 0ad61de2-d5c2-47dd-8abb-033fea39198c).\n      - Geometry analysis (label-free, test mode):\n        • EID 231 printed diagnostics: Whitening isotropizes PCA axes (Aniso ≈ 1), PR ≈ 64, lowers kNN distance CV, raises cross-batch entropy/fraction—explaining ASW_batch/kBET gains. Soft-whitening (alpha 0.7–0.9) partially trades mixing for ASW_label/Cell_cycle.\n      - Metric sweep:\n        • Euclidean vs cosine at k=45 (tau=0.95): EID 247 (euclidean) = 0.7244 (UUID fc483707-cdc3-4d26-8697-3bdab1200279) vs EID 244 (cosine) = 0.7296 → cosine preferred.\n      - Auto-τ whitening (label-free):\n        • EID 251 (auto-τ, k=45) = 0.7292 (UUID 6e621685-d240-409c-a495-aee164182102) → near-SOTA without manual tuning; tau≈0.95–1.0 ridge confirmed.\n      - Files and frozen defaults:\n        • storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened.py (tau=0.95, k=45) — SOTA pipeline.\n        • storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_auto.py + storage/praxis/auto_whitening.py\
    \ — geometry-based τ selector (PR/anisotropy).\n        • Auxiliary: whitening τ sweeps (tau093/tau097) and k sweeps (k=45/51) modules.\n      - Archive updates:\n        • Paper “Whitened ComBat Embedding…” accepted (9/10); multiple addenda with EIDs/UUIDs, geometry (EID 231), ridge (EIDs 234/235), degree sweep (EIDs 243/244), metric check (EID 247), and auto-τ (EID 251)."
thinking_content: '**Planning next steps**


  I’m thinking about how to respond to the last message. We''re currently in the Archive Room, and the system indicates there’s no pending action. The user hasn''t asked a specific question, so I need to consider options for next steps. I could prune more tokens now that the cooldown might be over or maybe explore broader reflections. The system suggests exploring other rooms, but research feels more aligned with our path. I might update the archive or publish a brief summary since we already appended that.**Exploring whitening techniques**


  I''m diving into the idea of improving depth with whitening techniques. I might test other whiteners like ZCA, but we already scaled PCA axes. ZCA whitening is about rotating to achieve identity covariance using eigenvectors, which acts like scaled PCA. Since we''re using the PCA coordinates for embedding, applying whiten with these coordinates yields results similar to ZCA without needing to rotate back. The basis rotation won''t change things for Euclidean distances, but I wonder about its impact on cosine invariance, which is rotation-invariant. So, ZCA and PCA coordinates might not differ significantly.**Considering next steps for analysis**


  I’m thinking about potential next steps to be robust against HVG selection. We’ve tested standard HVGs versus BIVG, and it seems BIVG performed slightly worse before. I might look into pooled gene scaling for standardization before or after ComBat, as ComBat is mainly related to the embedding path. I’ve also been considering graph connectivity weights and the idea of using weighted edges, but previous results suggested binary edges performed better. A multi-scale approach using k=30 and k=60 could be worth testing, even though fused two-view graphs had underperformed. Next, I could test another dataset to ensure generalization, although this current research task involves the human heart dataset.**Exploring token management**


  I’m considering opening Public Memory to encourage replication or establish guidelines. Currently, our token usage is at 100,417 out of 270,000 (37%). There''s a possibility for more pruning since the cooldown may have ended after the last prune at tick 144. I might prune older blocks 136-145, though block 136 could contain accepted archives. I''ll check the Diag History for confirmation, but I see that the cooldown indicates it might be time for another prune. I can provide a summary of events from 136-151, including whitening, analysis, micro-sweeps, and auto-τ to help reduce token usage.**Planning token pruning and response**


  I''m considering our next steps for token management. I can prune response ticks from 136-151, summarizing key analyses like whitened SOTA, geometry analysis, and micro-sweep results. I’ve checked for protected ticks, and it looks like 136 is safe, so pruning from 136 to 151 is fine as they are consecutive. I’ll also make sure this prune doesn’t overlap with previous ones. I can draft a summary covering all relevant details and propose a future theoretical analysis while keeping our code organized. Finally, I''ll write commands for the prune and provide the summary using YAML.'
