parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Research Counter. Results arrived:\n- EID 254 (k=46): 0.7274, worse than SOTA 0.7296; main drop from Cell_cycle (0.064) and slight mixing decreases.\n- EID 255 (PCA72): 0.7229, worse; Cell_cycle 0.051 and slight mixing decreases.\nDecision: Keep frozen default unchanged (tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45, cosine, equal quotas, adaptive residualization γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3).\n\nI will record these EIDs and UUIDs in lineage storage and add an addendum to the archive paper.\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/evals_254_255.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Micro-ablations on Dual BRBG (whitened ComBat embedding):\n  - EID 254 (k=46, PCA64, tau=0.95, adaptPC): score 0.7274\n    Metrics: ASW_batch 0.843, ASW_label 0.157, ARI 0.731, NMI 0.694, Graph_conn 0.972,\n             kBET 0.893, iLISI 0.949, cLISI 0.971, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.064\n    UUID: 63bf960a-1a89-49c7-9b85-fbcd9e6f5c38\n    Note: Degree >45 caused mild smoothing; Cell_cycle dropped; net ↓ vs k=45 SOTA\
    \ (0.7296).\n  - EID 255 (PCA72, tau=0.95, k=45, adaptPC): score 0.7229\n    Metrics: ASW_batch 0.848, ASW_label 0.152, ARI 0.732, NMI 0.694, Graph_conn 0.972,\n             kBET 0.868, iLISI 0.941, cLISI 0.972, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.051\n    UUID: 77219328-d59b-4ab7-94b5-0e8fd1681632\n    Note: Increasing embedding dimensionality to 72 PCs was detrimental, especially to Cell_cycle.\n\n  Conclusion: Maintain frozen defaults — tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45, cosine, equal-quotas, adaptPC (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 7}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum: Micro-ablations on the whitened ComBat Dual BRBG default (tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45).\n  - EID 254 (k=46): 0.7274 (UUID 63bf960a-1a89-49c7-9b85-fbcd9e6f5c38). Mixing slightly ↓ and Cell_cycle fell to 0.064 → net score ↓ vs SOTA 0.7296 (EID 244).\n  - EID 255 (PCA72, k=45): 0.7229 (UUID 77219328-d59b-4ab7-94b5-0e8fd1681632). Cell_cycle 0.051 and minor mixing ↓ → net score ↓.\n  Decision:\
    \ Keep frozen default unchanged (tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45, cosine, equal-quotas, adaptive residualization γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3). These results narrow the ridge and confirm the robustness of the reported configuration.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring improvement strategies**\n\nI'm thinking about proposing a new plan since we've plateaued at 0.7296. The whitened ComBat embeddings seem limited for k modifications, so I'm considering Graph modifications and unifying BalancedGraph quotas instead of equal distribution. We've tried soft quotas before, but nothing worked. I wonder if modifying the embedding whiten exponent \"alpha\" could help? We've tested various exponents and found 0.95 to be the best. \n\nMaybe exploring a 2-step whitening process after PCA, along with additional normalization per sample, could work. I'll also look into refining gamma_max or adaptive residualization on the graph side to enhance the results.**Evaluating adaptive parameters**\n\nI'm working on the \"BRBG-Dual-AdaptPC\" project focusing on the whitened embedding, using parameters like tau=0.95 and equal quotas. We have an adaptive residualization with gamma_max at 0.74 and lam at 0.9. I'm wondering if I should explore adjusting\
  \ lam to 1.0 or gamma_max to 0.75, but previous attempts with gamma_max 0.75 showed minimal differences. \n\nPerhaps testing with lam 1.0 and gamma_max 0.75 while keeping k=45 and tau=0.95 could yield some small improvements without much risk. \n\nHowever, since the average past improvements were minor and resources are limited, I think it might be best to freeze our efforts and publish the results we have instead of chasing diminishing returns.**Planning actions for research updates**\n\nI need to summarize the results from EID 254 and 255 in the research log. I think proposing a return to the SOTA configuration is wise, as k=45 still seems optimal, along with PCA64 performing better than PCA72.\n\nI might consider a micro-run with lam at 1.0 to potentially push the score to 0.7297, but adjusting n_pcs_graph from 50 to 56 concerns me given earlier degrading results. Testing tau auto against 0.97 or 0.98 seems risky since we already tried 0.97, so it might be best to skip testing 0.98\
  \ altogether.**Updating research log and actions**\n\nIt's time to focus on maintaining our results and pivot to new question fundamentals. I’m considering replacing the whitened ComBat embedding with whitened robust ComBat variations, but I recall previous issues; the robust ComBat performed well with a 0.7383 score but caused the ASW_label to drop to 0.0, indicating a possible issue with the score aggregator.\n\nSince we've received no new actions, I’ll produce steps to update our log and include aspects like identity and location. I'll write to storage for the research log and then potentially reply to the whitened paper with a summary of the micro ablations 254 and 255. It’s important to mention that these results don’t change our defaults while keeping token use minimal.**Finalizing updates and actions**\n\nI think it’s time to finalize our code, and I recognize that we’ve already established the best module path. First, I’ll write a storage update for EID 254, noting that k 46 results\
  \ degraded, and for EID 255, where PCA72 also degraded, affirming that EID 244 remains our default.\n\nNext, I’ll go to the Archive Room to reply to capsule #7, summarizing our micro-ablations results, mentioning the results and UUIDs. Since we need to ensure that we don’t lose any logs, I’ll first read the current content before writing updates. \n\nThen I’ll take necessary actions, writing a new summary file before proceeding to reply in the archive with a concise message. Finally, I might optionally update the public memory thread, but that’s not essential right now."
