tick: 156
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Archive Room. Results for micro-ablations confirm the frozen default: tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45, cosine, equal quotas, adaptive residualization (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3). Next, I’ll probe a principled, low-complexity change to adaptive residualization: sublinear per-PC shrinkage γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j^p). Hypothesis: p<1 preserves moderately batch-predictable PCs (potentially improving Cell_cycle/ASW_label) while keeping strong mixing.\n\nActions:\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/dual_whitened_adaptpc_p.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  EPS = 1e-8\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n    \
    \  sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _get_hvg_mask(adata: ad.AnnData, n_top=2000):\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          m = np.asarray(adata.var['batch_hvg'].values, dtype=bool)\n          if m.sum() > 0:\n              return m\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=n_top, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', inplace=True)\n      return np.asarray(adata.var['highly_variable'].values, dtype=bool)\n\n  def _ensure_dense_X(adata):\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          adata.X = adata.X.A\n\n  def _pca_truncated_svd(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      n_comps = max(2, min(n_comps, X.shape[1]-1 if X.shape[1] > 1 else 2))\n      svd = TruncatedSVD(n_components=n_comps, n_iter=7, random_state=random_state)\n      return svd.fit_transform(X)\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches):\n      cats, inv = np.unique(batches, return_inverse=True)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      m = cats.shape[0]\n\
    \      B = np.zeros((n, m), dtype=float)\n      B[np.arange(n), inv] = 1.0\n      return B, cats\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B\n      M = BTB + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1])\n      W = np.linalg.solve(M, B.T @ Z)\n      Zhat = B @ W\n      return Zhat, W\n\n  def _r2_columns(Z, Zhat):\n      Zc = Z - Z.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      Zhatc = Zhat - Zhat.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      varZ = np.var(Zc, axis=0) + EPS\n      varRes = np.var(Zc - Zhatc, axis=0)\n      R2 = np.clip(1.0 - varRes / varZ, 0.0, 1.0)\n      return R2\n\n  def _balanced_knn(Z, batches, k_total=45, metric='cosine'):\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      nb = len(uniq)\n      base = k_total // nb\n      rem = k_total % nb\n      quota = {b: base + (i < rem) for i, b in enumerate(uniq)}\n\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train = np.where(batches\
    \ == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0 or quota[b] == 0:\n              continue\n          k = min(quota[b] + 1, idx_train.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_train[inds]\n          for i in range(n):\n              g = neigh_global[i]\n              di = dists[i]\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask][:quota[b]]\n              di = di[mask][:quota[b]]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n   \
    \   for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.extend([i, j]); cols.extend([j, i]); dist_data.extend([d, d])\n\n      from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy()\n      C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C, D\n\n  def run_pipeline_p(adata: ad.AnnData,\n                     n_pcs_emb=64, alpha=0.95,\n                     n_pcs_graph=50, gamma_max=0.74, lam=0.9, p=0.75, l2=1e-3,\n                     k_total=45, metric='cosine', random_state=0) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Dual BRBG with whitened ComBat embedding and sublinear per-PC residualization:\n        gamma_j = min(gamma_max, lam * (R2_j ** p)), with 0 < p <= 1.\n      \"\"\"\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      hvg = _get_hvg_mask(adata)\n      if hvg.sum() < 10:\n          hvg = np.ones(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n\
    \n      # Graph path\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hvg]\n      if not sp.issparse(Xg):\n          Xg = sp.csr_matrix(Xg)\n      Zg = _pca_truncated_svd(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat, _ = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      R2 = _r2_columns(Zg, Zhat)\n      gamma_j = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * np.power(R2, p))\n      Zcorr = Zg - Zhat * gamma_j.reshape(1, -1)\n      C, D = _balanced_knn(Zcorr, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n\n      # Embedding path\n      adata_emb = adata.copy()\n      _ensure_dense_X(adata_emb)\n      sc.pp.combat(adata_emb, key='batch')\n      adata_emb.var['highly_variable'] = hvg\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Zemb = adata_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      std = np.std(Zemb - Zemb.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True), axis=0)\
    \ + EPS\n      Zemb = Zemb / (std ** alpha)\n\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      adata.uns['dual_brbg_whitened_sublinear'] = {\n          'n_pcs_emb': int(n_pcs_emb), 'alpha': float(alpha),\n          'n_pcs_graph': int(n_pcs_graph), 'gamma_max': float(gamma_max),\n          'lam': float(lam), 'p': float(p), 'l2': float(l2),\n          'k_total': int(k_total), 'metric': metric\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-BRBG-Whitened: Sublinear Residualization p=0.75 (k=45)\"\ntags: \"dual, whitening, adaptive-residualization, sublinear, p=0.75, balanced-knn\"\nabstract: \"Test sublinear per-PC shrinkage γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j^p) with p=0.75 to preserve moderately batch-predictable PCs and potentially improve\
    \ Cell_cycle/ASW_label while maintaining mixing. Embedding: ComBat-PCA64 soft-whitened (alpha=0.95), graph k=45 cosine.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.dual_whitened_adaptpc_p import run_pipeline_p\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      return run_pipeline_p(adata,\n                            n_pcs_emb=64, alpha=0.95,\n                            n_pcs_graph=50, gamma_max=0.74, lam=0.9, p=0.75, l2=1e-3,\n                            k_total=45, metric='cosine')\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual-BRBG-Whitened: Sublinear Residualization p=0.50 (k=45)\"\ntags: \"dual, whitening, adaptive-residualization, sublinear, p=0.50, balanced-knn\"\nabstract: \"Stronger sublinearity (p=0.50) to further preserve moderate R² PCs. Expectation: possible Cell_cycle/ASW_label gains; risk: slight iLISI/kBET decrease. Embedding: ComBat-PCA64 soft-whitened (alpha=0.95), graph k=45 cosine.\"\
    \ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.dual_whitened_adaptpc_p import run_pipeline_p\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      return run_pipeline_p(adata,\n                            n_pcs_emb=64, alpha=0.95,\n                            n_pcs_graph=50, gamma_max=0.74, lam=0.9, p=0.50, l2=1e-3,\n                            k_total=45, metric='cosine')\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing metrics score**\n\nI noticed that a score of 0.7383 is above our threshold of 0.7296, but something feels off. Some metrics are very high while the ASW_label is at 0.0, which affects interpretation. The arithmetic mean might give a positive average despite one zero metric because the others are near 1.0. This suggests uniform weighting among metrics, with others averaging around 0.82. But the ASW_label at 0.0 indicates an issue with label separation, which isn't beneficial for our scientific goals.**Exploring new methods**\n\nI'm considering our method, which balances mixing and preservation. I wonder if we could introduce a more principled approach such as whitened embedding or adaptive residualization. Potential next steps include global graph diffusion or spectral alignment. For better generality, I’d like to test across a second dataset, though right now, we're focused on the human heart dataset. We could also explore steps like \"Hessian alignment\" or\
  \ \"harmonic mean\" across metrics. A conceptual next step is covariance factorization using cross-batch canonical correlation to adjust PCs, utilizing \"Batch-contrastive PCA\" while noting we’ve already done BIVG and whitened ComBat.**Questioning fundamental metrics**\n\nI’m trying to pinpoint the fundamental question here. The neighbor graph quotas ensure mixing, but I'm concerned they might negatively impact the cell-cycle metric. Our earlier ablation work showed that the cell cycle metric decreased at k=46 and PCA72, landing at 0.083 in our state-of-the-art (SOTA). This is significantly lower than the previous 0.305. It seems our improved whitened embedding did reduce cell-cycle variation, but the current low value indicates poor cell-cycle conservation. While my SOTA is at 0.7296, the cell_cycle is troublingly low at 0.083. Yet, I noticed the robust ComBat method achieved 0.710, which is high, even with an ASW_label of 0.000. That’s an interesting find!**Improving cell cycle retention**\n\
  \nI’m thinking about how our whitened method significantly traded off the Cell_cycle metric. I wonder if we can enhance cell cycle retention while maintaining mixing. One approach could be to introduce gene-level weighting to help preserve the cell-cycle signal, but we must avoid using cell-type labels. While we lack labels for the cell cycle genes, we could leverage known gene sets from external libraries. It might be permissible to scan libraries as long as we don’t use cell-type info directly. However, I'm cautious that relying on external resources could impact overall fairness in our analysis.**Evaluating metrics and proposing steps**\n\nI’m reflecting on our ability to compute the \"cell cycle variance ratio pre vs post\" without labels. The evaluation metric for 'Cell_cycle' measures correlation from a set of genes, but I can’t access it directly. I wonder if we can preserve specific latent variance while blending whitened embedding with original components. Maybe we could use gene-level\
  \ weighting, while being cautious about using external resources. I think it might be helpful to consider different methods like \"nonlinear embedding\" or \"Mahalanobis scaled kNN\" while ensuring we don’t compromise mixing or labels. \n\nSo, I need to propose a plan: I'll summarize earlier results and suggest a geometry-preserving approach, perhaps adjusting 'alpha' for whitened embedding to see if that influences the Cell_cycle metric. However, there’s a complexity involved in measuring how neighbors affect it. Overall, I need to focus on clear next steps without initiating new experiments for now.**Proposing next research steps**\n\nI’m considering our approach to soft-whitening with an exponent of alpha=0.9, which didn’t yield good results at a drop of 0.7256. Maybe whitening only the top 32 PCs could help preserve the cell cycle signal, but that feels unnatural. I plan to publish an addendum to verify our negative results while maintaining a stable SOTA of 0.7296. I propose reflecting\
  \ on our next research pivot, considering a second dataset and implementing dynamic quotas.\n\nWe can explore experiments using per-PC shrink methods and generate Local Balanced Quotas for better sample representation while improving on earlier soft quotas. However, new implementations could be heavy, so I’ll propose to maintain a focus on reflection and plan further experiments without running any major code right now. I’ll structure actions accordingly to keep the workflow organized."
