thinking_content: "**Evaluating graph and metrics**\n\nThe best pipeline right now uses a graph based on residualized PCA with embeddings from ComBat whitened. I'm wondering if switching to a graph from whitened embeddings might affect results negatively, but testing could clarify. Whitened embedding seems to improve mixing somewhat, yet could disrupt label structure.  \n\nRegarding metrics, ASW_label is quite low at 0.157 normalized. I’m considering adjusting the shrink parameter gamma_max to potentially boost ASW_label and ARI/NMI, but findings suggest subtle changes might not be effective.**Testing parameters and metrics**\n\nIncreasing the degree previously didn’t yield positive results; in fact, the cell cycle worsened. So, I’m considering reducing gamma_max to 0.72 or 0.70. Though this could affect the interplay with iLISI and kBET, the whitened step should help retain mixing, which may improve ASW_label and ARI, even if there's a minor drop in mixing overall.  \n\nI'm also looking\
  \ at the embedding path, proposing adjusting the whiten parameter alpha from 1.0 to 0.95, considering that we achieved SOTA with tau 0.95. And I won’t change from using cosine for the graph metric type since Euclidean performed worse before.**Exploring embedding adjustments**\n\nI’m considering different approaches, like potentially weighting by global edges instead of distances or exploring per-batch barycentric edges, though that might be overly complex.  \n\nAnother idea is to implement “PC smoothing” on a minimal spanning subgraph from the whitened embedding for denoising, but that could be tricky. I’m also thinking about using kernel alignment for residualization to eliminate correlations with batch data.  \n\nAdditionally, I wonder if adding cell cycle correction before embedding could help improve the Cell_cycle metric, but we need to confirm if computing cell cycle scores is allowed based on gene sets.**Considering cell cycle metrics**\n\nThe evaluation focuses on the \"Cell_cycle\"\
  \ metric, which measures the conservation of known cell cycles. I’m realizing that any pre-processing aimed at regressing out the cell cycle might actually harm the metric. It’s crucial because a higher score indicates better preservation, so I definitely shouldn't regress it out.  \n\nThe whitened method has resulted in a very low normalized \"Cell_cycle\" score of only 0.083, which is concerning since it suggests that conservation is reduced. Given previous negative results from sublinear residualization impacting Graph_conn a bit, I think it’s worth noting that no improvements were made, perhaps even as an addendum to the paper.**Planning next steps and reflections**\n\nI'm thinking about pruning tokens again, but we currently have 66,539 used, which is only 25% of the budget, so it’s not urgent. Our next steps include logging metrics for EID 258 and 259, adding an archive reply addendum, and possibly heading to the Reflection Chamber for brainstorming new ideas.  \n\nOne consideration\
  \ is exploring a \"Graph-of-graphs\" approach, integrating batch-controlled graphs, while also thinking about rank-weighted connectivities to improve Graph_conn metrics. However, we should focus on simpler ideas and avoid adding heavy complexity right now. I’ll proceed with the storage write and archive tasks before entering the reflection phase.**Executing planned actions**\n\nI’m planning to ensure our quoting process includes the necessary details, and I have a series of actions to execute. First, I’ll write to storage with the designated file, then I'll head to the archive, followed by summarizing a response for previous content. Finally, I’ll go to the reflection phase.  \n\nFor the reflection prompt, I want to center on optimizing metrics, maybe exploring a \"Weighted mean\" and proposing an \"Entropy-constrained neighborhood selection\" to enhance cross-batch mixing while preserving mutual adjacency. I’ll dig into manifold rescaling using whitened ComBat as well. Let’s get started!"
