role: model
parts:
- text: "Deepening the three ideas with concrete specs, safeguards, and expected metric trade-offs\n\n1) Rank-weighted balanced kNN (mutual-rank on quota-union graph)\n- Exact weighting:\n  - Build equal-per-batch quota neighbor sets as usual; take their union U(i) for node i (size ≈ k_total).\n  - For each undirected edge (i, j) in the union, compute ranks:\n    r_i(j): rank of j in i’s U(i) by cosine distance (1 = closest).\n    r_j(i): analogously.\n  - Symmetric weight:\n    w_ij = 1 / (1 + r_i(j) + r_j(i))  in (0, 0.5], or a softer variant\n    w_ij = exp(-(r_i(j) + r_j(i)) / t) with t ≈ 10  (bounded dynamic range).\n  - Keep distances as the symmetric min distance (as now). Set connectivities = w_ij (symmetric).\n- Safeguards:\n  - Floor: w_min = 1/(1+2k_total) or a small epsilon so no edge is truly weightless.\n  - Degree stability: normalize per-node weights to sum to k_total (optional), preventing hubness shifts.\n  - Preserve quotas: only weights change; the presence of cross-batch\
    \ edges is guaranteed by quotas.\n- Complexity: O(n k_total log k_total) for rank sorting; same asymptotics as current graph build.\n- Expected outcomes:\n  - ARI/NMI/ASW_label: slight ↑ (manifold fidelity from ranking without distance-scale bias).\n  - Graph_conn: stable or ↑ (weights help spectral connectivity).\n  - iLISI/kBET: stable (edges unchanged), slight ↓ possible if high-rank (farther) cross-batch edges get very small weights; mitigate with t or w_min.\n- Acceptance: ≥ +0.0005 mean, or neutral mean with clear ARI/NMI/ASW_label ↑ and no mixing loss.\n\n2) Cross-batch axis reweighting (label-free, batch-only)\n- Goal: Within ComBat-PCA64 (whitened), reweight axes to favor PCs that best respect cross-batch neighborhood geometry (proxy for biology) without using labels.\n- Score per PC j (bio-likelihood proxy):\n  - For each cell i, let N×(i) be its cross-batch neighbors from the pooled, whitened embedding (e.g., all neighbors not in i’s batch within U(i)).\n  - Compute vectors\
    \ over N×(i):\n    d_i: pooled cosine distances to neighbors.\n    δ_i^j: absolute difference along PC j, |Z_emb[i, j] − Z_emb[n, j]|.\n  - s_i^j = SpearmanCorr(δ_i^j, d_i) ∈ [−1, 1]; high means PC j’s local differences align with pooled geometry.\n  - Aggregate s_j = mean_i max(0, s_i^j) to avoid penalizing noisy negatives; rescale to [0, 1].\n- Axis weights:\n  - a_j = max(a_min, (s_j^β) / mean(s^β)), with a_min ≈ 0.7 and β ∈ {0.5, 0.75, 1.0}.\n  - Apply post-whitening: Z_emb[:, j] ← a_j · Z_emb[:, j], then optionally renormalize overall scale to keep RMS per-axis ≈ 1.\n- Safeguards:\n  - Avoid over-downweighting: enforce a_min; optionally cap a_max ≤ 1.3 to prevent axis domination.\n  - Robustness: compute s_j on a 10–20% subsample for speed; use the same random seed for reproducibility.\n- Expected outcomes:\n  - ARI/NMI/ASW_label: modest ↑ if bio-aligned axes are emphasized.\n  - iLISI/kBET: stable if reweighting is mild; whitening already isotropized magnitudes.\n  - Cell_cycle:\
    \ could ↑ if cycle PCs show strong cross-batch concordance.\n- Acceptance: same as above; also confirm no degradation in Graph_conn.\n\n3) γ_max micro-reduction under whitening (0.74 → 0.72 → 0.70)\n- Rationale: Whitening already pushes mixing near a ceiling; reducing γ_max can recover subtle label geometry.\n- Design:\n  - Keep everything fixed (tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45, cosine, quotas, adaptive residualization form with lam=0.9).\n  - Evaluate γ_max ∈ {0.72, 0.70}.\n- Expected outcomes:\n  - ASW_label/ARI/NMI/Cell_cycle: slight ↑.\n  - iLISI/kBET/ASW_batch: should remain very high; small ↓ acceptable only if mean ↑ overall.\n- Acceptance: ≥ +0.0005 mean or neutral mean with label metrics ↑ and mixing unchanged.\n\nExecution order and rollback\n- Start with 1) rank-weighted quotas (lowest conceptual risk to mixing; magnitude-free).\n- If neutral, try 3) γ_max micro-reduction (tiny knob change).\n- If still neutral, trial 2) axis reweighting with β ∈ {0.75, 0.5} and a_min=0.7 (one scalar\
    \ knob, bounded effect).\n- Any negative or inconclusive result → revert to frozen default (EID 244). All runs documented with EIDs/UUIDs."
thinking_content: "**Analyzing batch variance**\n\nI think we should focus on using batch labels to compute the ratio of between-batch versus within-batch variance after applying ComBat. We could also consider calculating contributions from kBET or iLISI at the PC level. Another idea is to assess local cross-batch correlations to understand how PCs maintain neighbor identity, as this indicates underlying biology. For unsupervised alignment, I’m thinking about PCA whitening to adjust axis weights. It's essential to keep the complexity manageable while evaluating these factors and past suggestions like gamma_max around 0.72 or 0.70.**Augmenting neighbor weighting**\n\nI’m considering ways to improve neighbor weighting, like using degree normalization to prevent hubness. Mutual rank weighting could align with UMAP's fuzzy simplicial set, but we still want to maintain equal quotas. We’ll compute ranks per node by uniting edges across batches, calculating ranks for the top neighbors. \n\nFor\
  \ each pair, I plan to determine rankings using cosine distance and define weights based on these ranks. I'll keep the weights harmonic and propose connectivities as the rank-based weights, while also introducing stress tests and acceptance metrics.**Exploring potential synergies**\n\nI’m thinking about how adding rank weights to a whitened embedding can work well without degrading performance since it’s already uniform. It’s essential to keep reflection mode and provide cohesive content. \n\nI want to draft a plan that includes formal functions for the weights, ensuring they are symmetric and bounded. I'll also outline pseudo-code with an O(nk log k) complexity, emphasizing safeguards like a minimum weight to maintain cross-batch connections. Lastly, I’ll explore alternative \"bio-likelihood\" signals involving neighbor stability and batch predictability.**Investigating normalization and correlation**\n\nSo, I'm noting that the PCR normalized results show a 1.0 scale, but batch predictability\
  \ is low. I think we can compute R²_j in a graph representation rather than relying solely on the embedding.\n\nFor local batch entropy improvements, I'm considering setting PC j to zero, which means reembedding, but I want to keep complexity low and focus on correlation methods. I’ll use a formula to calculate distances from neighbors, compute absolute differences, and then assess Spearman correlation for cross-batch neighbors to ensure clarity without within-batch interference.**Normalizing and estimating effects**\n\nI'm thinking about normalization to maintain isotropy without inflating the scale. I’ll renormalize so that the RMS of axis weights equals 1, using equations that adjust for minimum and maximum values. Setting a_min to 0.7 would work, and I could also focus on weighing the top PCs for simplicity.\n\nI want to provide expected metric effects, noting that adjusting gamma could improve label metrics while minimizing mixing effects. If lowering gamma to around 0.72 raises ASW_label\
  \ by 0.002-0.004, I plan to set acceptance thresholds and prepare a fallback to the default approach as needed."
