role: model
parts:
- text: "Closing synthesis and next steps (no actions in this turn)\n\n- Prioritized path\n  1) Rank-weighted balanced kNN (mutual-rank on quota-union) is the cleanest one-change lever likely to lift ARI/NMI/ASW_label without reintroducing distance-scale bias. Implement first.\n  2) If neutral/negative, try γ_max micro-reduction (0.72, 0.70) under whitening to recover subtle biology with minimal mixing cost.\n  3) If still neutral, try cross-batch axis reweighting in the whitened ComBat embedding with one knob β and a bounded floor a_min.\n\n- Rank-weighted quotas: concrete spec\n  - Build equal per-batch quota neighbor sets exactly as now; take their union per node (edges unchanged).\n  - For edge (i, j) in the union:\n    r_i(j) = rank of j in i’s union by cosine distance (1 = closest); similarly r_j(i).\n    Weight w_ij = 1 / (1 + r_i(j) + r_j(i)); symmetric, magnitude-free, bounded in (0, 0.5].\n  - Safeguards:\n    - Floor w_min = 1/(1 + 2k_total) to prevent vanishing cross-batch edges.\n\
    \    - Optional per-node normalization so sum_j w_ij ≈ k_total to keep degree-like mass and avoid hubness shifts.\n    - Keep distances D as the current symmetric min distances; only connectivities become rank-weighted.\n  - Complexity: O(n k log k) atop existing build; same memory footprint.\n\n- Expected metric shifts and guardrails\n  - ARI/NMI/ASW_label: slight increase from better manifold fidelity (ranks encode relative geometry).\n  - Graph_conn: stable or up (weighted connectivity can strengthen spectral connectivity).\n  - Mixing (ASW_batch, iLISI, kBET): quotas unchanged; should stay high. If weights overly penalize longer cross-batch edges, floor/normalization should prevent mixing loss.\n  - Cell_cycle: small uptick possible if improved local geometry reduces over-smoothing.\n\n- Acceptance criteria (pre-registered)\n  - Adopt if mean score improves by ≥ 0.0005 over EID 244 (0.7296), or is within ±0.0002 with a clear gain in ARI/NMI/Cell_cycle and no drop (>0.002) in iLISI/kBET/ASW_batch.\n\
    \  - Otherwise revert to binary connectivities.\n\n- γ_max micro-reduction under whitening\n  - Keep tau=0.95, PCA64, k=45, quotas, cosine, adaptive residualization form with λ=0.9.\n  - Test γ_max ∈ {0.72, 0.70}; accept per the criteria above, focusing on label metrics gains with mixing flat.\n\n- Cross-batch axis reweighting (label-free, batch-only)\n  - Compute a bio-likelihood per PC j: over cross-batch neighbors, Spearman correlation between |Δ on PC j| and pooled cosine distances; aggregate to s_j ∈ [0,1].\n  - Axis weight a_j = max(a_min, (s_j^β) / mean(s^β)), with a_min ≈ 0.7 and β ∈ {0.75, 0.5}.\n  - Apply post-whitening to the ComBat-PC64 embedding. Keep changes mild; recheck Graph_conn and mixing.\n  - Acceptance per the same criteria.\n\n- Attribution and documentation discipline\n  - One change per run; no stacking until an effect is established.\n  - Record all EIDs/UUIDs, configs, and metric deltas; append negatives and positives to the archive capsule.\n  - If rank-weighted\
    \ quotas succeed, consider a tiny follow-up (e.g., temperature t in an exp-rank variant) only after establishing the baseline benefit.\n\n- Risk and rollback\n  - If any change decreases mixing (iLISI/kBET/ASW_batch) beyond threshold, immediately revert to the frozen default (EID 244).\n  - Keep token usage in check; prune after batches of experiments with comprehensive summaries.\n\nThis plan keeps parsimony, uses only batch information, and targets fundamentals (edge representation and axis contribution) rather than hyperparameter grinding."
thinking_content: '**Planning Research Reflection**


  I need to focus on providing reflection content without any action parsing since it''s the third reflection tick. The plan involves selecting the first experiment with rank-weighted quotas and detailing the rationale, implementation steps, risk mitigations, expected metric shifts, and acceptance criteria. I should also mention the complexity, ablation matrix, fallback options, and timeline, ensuring everything is concise. Though I need to avoid heavy technical details, I can use pseudo-code or bullet points to clarify things.**Evaluating Weighting Methods**


  It seems there''s a conclusion that Gaussian weights negatively impact mixing, while rank-based approaches could preserve it. I''m considering both distances and connectivities for evaluation. Previously, they switched to binary connectivities, which might help Graph_conn. However, some metrics may rely heavily on distances. There''s also a proposal for cross-batch axis reweighting using Spearman correlation to assess alignment with geometry. Another idea is measuring stability among cross-batch neighbors through mini subsamples, though that might add some complexity. Lastly, I''m looking into a gamma micro reduction approach.**Finalizing Research Reflection**


  I need to conclude with a prioritized plan that focuses on one change at a time and includes acceptance criteria, pre-registration, and tokens. First, I’ll succinctly summarize the three proposals and select the top one for implementation. Then, I''ll outline the weight formula and its constraints: \( w_{ij} = \frac{1}{1 + r_i(j) + r_j(i)} \) with a minimum floor of \( w_{min} = \frac{1}{1 + 2 \cdot k_{total}} \). There’s also an option to renormalize the weights per node so their sum equals the degree.**Detailing Graph Building and Metrics**


  I want to maintain minimal symmetric distances to uphold metrics, while using connectivities as weights. For graph building, I’ll keep quotas stable, compute a union of neighbors, sort by node, and limit ranks to that union, ensuring symmetry. The acceptance criteria will require at least a delta of +0.0005 or unchanged metrics, with no mixing drop greater than 0.002. If there''s a negative result, I''ll have a fallback plan to revert to binary. We''ll also run a small guard experiment on weighting toggles, starting simply. For subsequent phases, I’ll explore gamma_max micro reduction and axis reweighting, keeping in mind potential negative impacts from weighted edges.**Planning Novel Implementation**


  I''m focusing on creating a synergy between structural mixing and geometry-coded edge weights. The timeline will have me implement the first approach immediately, with a plan to revert to the third approach if the outcome is negative. Otherwise, I''ll move on to the second approach. I won''t run combinations simultaneously to ensure I can attribute effects accurately. I also need to include disclaimers about adhering to rules and outline the plan for token management, keeping everything clear and concise without heavy formatting.'
