tick: 171
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Token Management Room. Cooldown is active; no further pruning now. I’ll pursue a fundamental variation: per-batch variance equalization in the embedding to align second-order structure across batches (using only batch labels), layered on top of our ComBat-PCA64 embedding with global soft-whitening. Graph remains the proven residualized balanced kNN. I will implement and evaluate two mild settings (alpha_pb=0.3, 0.5).\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/perbatch_var_equalization.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from numpy.linalg import eigh\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  def _center(Z):\n      mu = Z.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      return Z - mu, mu\n\n  def global_soft_whiten(Z: np.ndarray, tau: float = 0.95, eps: float = 1e-8):\n      \"\"\"\n      Soft-whitening of columns by exponent tau in [0,1]:\n        Z_wh = (Z - mean) @ U @ diag((S + eps)^(-tau/2)) @ U.T, where S are eigenvalues of Cov(Z).\n\
    \      tau=1.0 gives full whitening; tau=0 reduces to identity.\n      \"\"\"\n      Zc, mu = _center(Z)\n      # Covariance in feature space (d x d), d is small (~64)\n      d = Zc.shape[1]\n      C = (Zc.T @ Zc) / max(1, Zc.shape[0] - 1)\n      # Symmetric EVD\n      S, U = eigh(C)\n      S = np.maximum(S, 0.0)\n      S_pow = np.power(S + eps, -0.5 * tau)\n      T = (U * S_pow) @ U.T  # U @ diag(S_pow) @ U.T\n      Zw = Zc @ T\n      return Zw, mu\n\n  def perbatch_diag_var_equalize(Z: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, alpha: float = 0.3, eps: float = 1e-8):\n      \"\"\"\n      Per-batch diagonal variance equalization in a fixed feature basis:\n        For each PC j, and batch b:\n          scale s_bj = (var_all_j / (var_bj + eps))^(alpha/2)\n        Apply: Z_bj := (Z_bj - mu_all_j) * s_bj + mu_all_j\n      alpha in [0,1]; alpha=0 no change; alpha=1 matches per-batch variances to global per PC.\n      \"\"\"\n      Zc, mu = _center(Z)\n      n, d = Z.shape\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n\
    \      var_all = Zc.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + eps\n\n      Z_eq = Zc.copy()\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx = (batches == b)\n          if not np.any(idx):\n              continue\n          Zb = Zc[idx, :]\n          var_b = Zb.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + eps\n          s = np.power(var_all / var_b, 0.5 * alpha)  # shape (d,)\n          Z_eq[idx, :] = Zb * s\n\n      return Z_eq + mu\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/dual_brbg_whiten_pbve.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  from praxis.perbatch_var_equalization import global_soft_whiten, perbatch_diag_var_equalize\n\n  # -------------------------\n  # Utilities (self-contained)\n  # -------------------------\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata,\
    \ target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _get_hvg_mask(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          m = np.asarray(adata.var['batch_hvg'].values, dtype=bool)\n          if m.sum() > 0:\n              return m\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', inplace=True)\n      return np.asarray(adata.var['highly_variable'].values, dtype=bool)\n\n  def _pca_truncated_svd(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      # Use scanpy PCA if dense, otherwise TruncatedSVD via sc.pp.pca is more involved; keep dense for 2k HVGs\n      import warnings\n      Xd = X\n      if sp.issparse(X):\n          Xd = X.A  # densify (2k genes × 20k cells manageable for PCA(50/64))\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n\
    \      m = len(cats)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, m), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B, cats\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      # Solve W = (B^T B + l2 I)^-1 B^T Z; predict Zhat = B W\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      Zhat = B @ W\n      return Zhat, W\n\n  def _build_balanced_knn(Z: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, k_total=45, metric='cosine'):\n      \"\"\"\n      Equal per-batch quota k = ceil(k_total / n_batches). Binary, symmetric graph.\n      \"\"\"\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      k_pb = max(1, int(np.ceil(k_total / max(1, uniq.size))))\n\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0:\n          \
    \    continue\n          k = min(k_pb + 1, idx_train.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_train[inds]\n          for i in range(n):\n              g = neigh_global[i]\n              di = dists[i]\n              # drop self if appears\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask][:k_pb]\n              di = di[mask][:k_pb]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n\n      from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for\
    \ (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n          rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy()\n      C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C.tocsr(), D.tocsr()\n\n  # -------------------------\n  # Main method\n  # -------------------------\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Dual BRBG with per-batch variance equalization in the embedding:\n        - Embedding path: Normalize/log1p → HVGs → ComBat → PCA(64) →\n          global soft-whitening (tau≈0.95) → per-batch diagonal variance equalization (alpha_pb) → X_emb.\n        - Graph path: Normalize/log1p (same) → HVGs → PCA(50) →\n          ridge residualize vs one-hot batch with adaptive per-PC shrinkage gamma_j = clip(λ R²_j, 0, γ_max) →\n          Balanced equal-quota kNN (k=45, cosine, binary) → connectivities/distances.\n      \"\
    \"\"\n      # Configs\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      tau_whiten = 0.95\n      alpha_pb = 0.3  # per-batch equalization strength (variant 1: 0.3; we will also run 0.5)\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      k_total = 45\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      # HVGs\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata)\n      if hvg_mask.sum() < 10:\n          hvg_mask = np.ones(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n\n      # ---------------- Embedding path ----------------\n      ad_emb = adata.copy()\n      # Keep only HVGs for PCA\n      Xh = ad_emb.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if sp.issparse(Xh):\n          Xh = Xh.A\n      # ComBat on expression (dense)\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xh, obs=adata.obs.copy(), var=adata.var.loc[hvg_mask].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_tmp, key='batch')\n\
    \      # PCA(64)\n      Zemb = _pca_truncated_svd(ad_tmp.X, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, random_state=random_state)\n      # Global soft-whiten\n      Zw, mu = global_soft_whiten(Zemb, tau=tau_whiten)\n      # Per-batch diagonal variance equalization (fixed basis)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Zemb_eq = perbatch_diag_var_equalize(Zw, batches=batches, alpha=alpha_pb)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb_eq\n\n      # Initialize neighbors for embedding bookkeeping\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n\n      # ---------------- Graph path ----------------\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if sp.issparse(Xg):\n          Xg = Xg.A\n      Zg = _pca_truncated_svd(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      # Ridge residualization with adaptive per-PC gamma\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat, _ = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      # Per-PC R^2\n     \
    \ var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = (Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1)).clip(min=0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)  # shape (d,)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)  # broadcast over PCs\n\n      # Balanced equal-quota kNN graph\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D\n\n      # Record config\n      adata.uns['dual_brbg_pbve'] = {\n          'tau_whiten': float(tau_whiten),\n          'alpha_pb': float(alpha_pb),\n          'k_total': int(k_total),\n          'metric': metric,\n          'pcs_emb': int(n_pcs_emb),\n          'pcs_graph': int(n_pcs_graph),\n          'gamma_max': float(gamma_max),\n          'lambda': float(lam),\n          'l2': float(l2),\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit\
    \ 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual BRBG: Global Soft-Whiten + Per-Batch Variance Equalization (alpha_pb=0.3)\"\ntags: \"dual, whitening, per-batch, variance-equalization, residualization, balanced-knn\"\nabstract: \"Building on the whitened ComBat PCA64 embedding (tau=0.95), we equalize per-batch variances per PC in a fixed basis with alpha_pb=0.3 to reduce second-order batch discrepancies. Graph remains residualized PC50 with adaptive per-PC shrinkage and equal-quota balanced cosine kNN (k=45).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.dual_brbg_whiten_pbve import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from praxis.dual_brbg_whiten_pbve import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as _base_fn\n  # Re-export with alpha_pb=0.5 by monkey-patching inside the imported module\n  # Reloading the module with new alpha requires\
    \ redefining the function.\n  # Here we copy the function and adjust alpha at runtime by wrapping.\n\n  # Since we cannot easily mutate constants inside the compiled function,\n  # we reimplement a thin wrapper that adjusts adata.uns after calling _base_fn,\n  # and then rescales embedding per-batch with alpha_pb=0.5 using the same operators.\n\n  # Simpler: re-define the function here importing the tools.\n  from praxis.perbatch_var_equalization import global_soft_whiten, perbatch_diag_var_equalize\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _get_hvg_mask(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          m = np.asarray(adata.var['batch_hvg'].values,\
    \ dtype=bool)\n          if m.sum() > 0:\n              return m\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', inplace=True)\n      return np.asarray(adata.var['highly_variable'].values, dtype=bool)\n\n  def _pca_truncated_svd(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      if sp.issparse(X):\n          Xd = X.A\n      else:\n          Xd = X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B, cats\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n \
    \     Zhat = B @ W\n      return Zhat, W\n\n  def _build_balanced_knn(Z: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, k_total=45, metric='cosine'):\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      k_pb = max(1, int(np.ceil(k_total / max(1, uniq.size))))\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0:\n              continue\n          k = min(k_pb + 1, idx_train.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_train[inds]\n          for i in range(n):\n              g = neigh_global[i]\n              di = dists[i]\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask][:k_pb]\n              di = di[mask][:k_pb]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n      \
    \                continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n      from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n          rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy()\n      C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C.tocsr(), D.tocsr()\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Configs (alpha_pb=0.5 variant)\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      tau_whiten = 0.95\n      alpha_pb = 0.5\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      k_total = 45\n      metric\
    \ = 'cosine'\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns\n      assert 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      hvg_mask = _get_hvg_mask(adata)\n      if hvg_mask.sum() < 10:\n          hvg_mask = np.ones(adata.n_vars, dtype=bool)\n\n      # Embedding: ComBat PCA64 → global whiten → per-batch var equalization\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if sp.issparse(Xh):\n          Xh = Xh.A\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xh, obs=adata.obs.copy(), var=adata.var.loc[hvg_mask].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_tmp, key='batch')\n      Zemb = _pca_truncated_svd(ad_tmp.X, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, random_state=random_state)\n      Zw, mu = global_soft_whiten(Zemb, tau=tau_whiten)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Zemb_eq = perbatch_diag_var_equalize(Zw, batches=batches, alpha=alpha_pb)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zemb_eq\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15,\
    \ k_total), metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph: residualized PCs + balanced kNN\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hvg_mask]\n      if sp.issparse(Xg):\n          Xg = Xg.A\n      Zg = _pca_truncated_svd(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      B, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat, _ = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = (Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1)).clip(min=0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      C, D = _build_balanced_knn(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n      if C is not None and D is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = D\n\n      adata.uns['dual_brbg_pbve'] = {\n          'tau_whiten': float(tau_whiten), 'alpha_pb': float(alpha_pb),\n          'k_total': int(k_total), 'metric': metric,\n          'pcs_emb':\
    \ int(n_pcs_emb), 'pcs_graph': int(n_pcs_graph),\n          'gamma_max': float(gamma_max), 'lambda': float(lam), 'l2': float(l2),\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dual BRBG: Global Soft-Whiten + Per-Batch Variance Equalization (alpha_pb=0.5)\"\ntags: \"dual, whitening, per-batch, variance-equalization, residualization, balanced-knn\"\nabstract: \"Same as alpha_pb=0.3 but with stronger per-batch variance equalization (alpha_pb=0.5). Tests whether further aligning batch-specific second-order structure in the embedding improves ASW_batch/kBET without harming ARI/NMI/cLISI.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from praxis.dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating metrics in analysis**\n\nI'm considering the difference between angular (cosine) and correlation metrics. Essentially, cosine measures angles, while correlation adjusts for sample centering. With log1p normalized counts, centering could lose cell-specific scale. Since we're dealing with PC embedding, correlation in this space might mimic cosine, but I want to test this out. \n\nThe evaluation scoreboard shows a low ASW_label of 0.157, yet our SOTA performs well overall. I wonder if we can enhance label silhouette while maintaining mixing to boost the overall mean.**Increasing ASW_label considerations**\n\nTo boost ASW_label, I’m thinking we need to avoid oversmoothing across cell types. One approach could be using a \"graph barrier\" with highly variable genes (HVGs) to make sure neighbor picks don’t cross label boundaries, but we lack labels. \n\nWithout them, we might estimate structural boundaries through ensembles. Using \"local PCA anisotropy\" could\
  \ help assess heterogeneity, and looking at \"neighborhood density differences across batches\" might be useful too, although it could risk degrading ARI if we oversmooth. A general approach is what I need!**Exploring neighbor matching techniques**\n\nI'm evaluating the idea of \"Cross-batch nearest neighbor matching with monotone shrink,\" similar to Mutual Nearest Neighbors (MNN) alignment but focused solely on computing edges. We've ensured quotas from each batch, and the MNN variant I’m considering requires reciprocal relationships across at least one batch pair. This could help foster alignment while keeping label boundaries intact. \n\nPreviously, \"mutual edges\" in BalancedGraph performed poorly due to degree collapse when seeking full mutuality. I think I can try \"mutual across cross-batch edges only\" while preserving the union of all edges to maintain degree!**Analyzing early tick summary**\n\nIn my early tick summary, EID 17 shows a score of 0.4428 with mutual edges, but that\
  \ might stem from using mutual connections across the entire neighbor set, leading to sparsity. I’m considering implementing \"mutual boosting,\" giving extra weight to edges if mutual across batches, but we purposefully used binary edges. \n\nWeighted edges can degrade mixing, so what if we keep the binary edges and add mutual edges as extras if they aren't counted yet? That could create a superset. \n\nI’m also thinking about a \"Graph-of-graphs\" approach using whitened embedding for neighbor selection while applying balanced quotas with whitened ComBat. We might need to whiten the residualized PC before neighbor selection since the adjacency metric is \"cosine,\" which may not change with whitening since it scales features to unit variance.**Exploring batch handling and whitening**\n\nI'm diving deep into how we handle cross-batch issues and the implications of using cosine for metrics. Whiten can lessen anisotropy, but I’m not sure if we’re using different PCA dimensions for graphs\
  \ versus embeddings. \n\nI’m thinking of computing \"harmonized whitened alignment\" across batches with shared correlation subspace and domain adversarial direction removal, possibly using regression methods. But implementing batch-specific whitening, or calibrating variances, could unify distributions while maintaining biological relevance. \n\nI really need to ensure that the embedding remains consistent across batches, as transforming them individually might misalign their coordinates. This balancing act seems crucial for effective mixing!**Refining variance equalization**\n\nI'm considering how to ensure that our overall effect remains mild while maximizing performance. The earlier global whiten with tau=0.95 provided state-of-the-art results, but introducing per-batch whiten might conflict with that. Instead of a double whiten process, I’m proposing to replace global whiten with per-batch whiten at tau=0.5. \n\nCalculating per-batch eigenvalue distributions could help equalize the\
  \ variance and make the process more effective without being overly complicated. Using a simpler method like “Variance match per PC” might be the way to go. \n\nI’ll compute the ComBat PCA embedding, calculate per-batch variances, and adjust accordingly. I think I’ll implement these calculations in a new script and integrate with existing functions. Keeping variations milder, like alpha at 0.7 or 0.9, could allow us to test different formulations effectively!**Implementing improved embeddings**\n\nI see our final state-of-the-art (SOTA) EID 244 uses \"BRBG-Dual-AdaptPC\" with whitened embedding (tau=0.95) and Equal-Quota Graph with k=45. We need to implement the graph, likely importing the \"auto_gamma_brbg\" if available. We might stick with a fixed gamma at 0.75 for adaptation, though it’s best if we adapt. \n\nSince our aim is to test a new idea, I’m considering two variants: one with whitened embedding and the other with per-batch equalization. Implementing the functions for each will\
  \ help us achieve our goals and ensure everything aligns with existing structures. The calculations of variances and ensuring proper covariance decomposition will be key in crafting effective embeddings!**Coding dual functions for graph embeddings**\n\nI’m working on the second file, 'dual_brbg_whiten_pbve.py.' I’m importing necessary functions and setting up the BalancedGraph structure, using the ones defined previously. First, I’ll normalize log1p and choose HVGs based on provided criteria, then proceed to set up the graph with one-hot encoding and ridge regression predictions.\n\nI want to keep each function independent to avoid duplication, so I’ll reimplement certain functionalities directly in the file. Ultimately, I plan to store the results for two evaluations: one with alpha_pb set at 0.3 and another at 0.5."
