    \  # Praxis Lineage Research Log (snapshot at tick 172)\n  Task: scRNA-seq batch integration (Human Heart ~20k×2k)\n\n  Current SOTA (Praxis):\n  - EID 244 = 0.7296 (UUID 3fc52313-...eb0c): Dual BRBG with ComBat-PCA64 whitening (tau=0.95), cosine, k=45; graph: residualized PC50 (adaptive, γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3) + equal-quotas balanced kNN.\n  - Surrounding ridge: EIDs 220/223/235/234/251 ≈ 0.7291–0.7293 confirm tau≈0.95–1.0 and k=45 as robust.\n\n  New Direction: Per-Batch Variance Equalization (PBVE) in the embedding\n  - Idea: After global soft-whitening, remaining per-batch diagonal variance mismatch per PC can be reduced using only batch labels: for batch b and PC j, scale by (var_all_j/var_bj)^(alpha_pb/2). Alpha_pb in [0,1].\n  - Goal: Improve ASW_batch/kBET while retaining ARI/NMI/cLISI by keeping alpha_pb small (0.3–0.5). Graph unchanged to isolate embedding effects.\n\n  Experiments (running):\n  - EID 286: Dual BRBG + global soft-whiten (tau=0.95) + PBVE alpha_pb=0.3;\
    \ graph fixed (adaptive residualized PCs, k=45 cosine).\n  - EID 287: Same but alpha_pb=0.5 (stronger equalization).\n\n  Expected outcomes:\n  - Small gains in ASW_batch/kBET; Graph_conn stable (~0.97–0.98); ARI/NMI/cLISI should not drop if alpha_pb is mild.\n  - Risks: Oversmoothing reduces cLISI/ASW_label; if so, dial down alpha_pb or abandon PBVE.\n\n  Decision tree:\n  - If max(EID 286/287) ≥ 0.7305 (+0.001 over SOTA) with ARI/NMI/cLISI stable: confirm with alpha_pb ∈ {0.2, 0.4}.\n  - If neutral (±0.0005) or negative: revert to whitening-only embedding; document PBVE as negative/neutral.\n\n  Label-free diagnostics to run (test mode, future):\n  - Per-batch PC variance ratios (var_bj/var_all_j) distribution before/after PBVE.\n  - Anisotropy and participation ratio changes; cross-batch neighbor fraction/entropy under naive kNN on X_emb.\n\n  Files:\n  - storage/praxis/perbatch_var_equalization.py (global soft-whitening + PB diag equalization)\n  - storage/praxis/dual_brbg_whiten_pbve.py\
    \ (alpha_pb=0.3 variant)\n  - storage/praxis/dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05.py (alpha_pb=0.5 variant)\n\n  Notes:\n  - All methods use only batch labels and expression; no cell-type usage.\n  - Graph path remains unchanged to attribute effects to embedding only.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning evaluation updates**


  I''m thinking it''s best to wait for the results before submitting any additional evaluations. We could implement a test mode to compute from the dataset, but running code outside the evaluation environment isn''t an option. I want to keep track of log updates and plan for version control on the new modules I''ve written. It seems we should also prepare to update the Archive later but not now. There might be a need to write a summary capsule in Private Memory for clarity. My token budget looks good at 21%.**Exploring metric learning techniques**


  I’m considering exploring "fundamentals" as auto tips suggested. I’ve already worked on whitened embedding and am now looking into per-batch variance equalization. Other alternatives could include reversed graph building or across-batch distance calibration. I’m also thinking about approaches like "global rotation alignment to whitened ComBat" or "harmonic mean of per-batch covariance with shrinkage." However, I’ll wait for results before diving into designing anything. It might be wise to update the research log and plan for possible ablations depending on the outcomes!**Planning metric updates**


  The station is using 20k x 64 dimensions, and full covariance per batch is 64 x 64, which feels feasible. While implementing the SPD matrix transform might be heavy, I think we can stick to mild diagonal equalization for now. I want to propose computing a "per-batch anisotropy metric" and automatically choosing alpha_pb later. For now, I’ll update the research log and plan to run alpha_pb at 0.0 as a control. I''ll also record the new experiments and hypotheses in the log.**Reviewing results and planning adjustments**


  Private Memory certainly aids in reading inheritance later. I''m thinking we should wait for the results of reviews 286 and 287 before jumping in; we can''t review while they''re still running. I might call to execute the review, but if it''s still in progress, it''ll just ask us to wait. I want to maintain an identity statement at the top, reflecting that we improved SOTA with whitening. Now, I’m addressing second-order per-batch variance mismatch. I have expectations that ASW_batch and kBET might modestly improve, while being mindful that cLISI and ASW_label could degrade due to oversmoothing. I’m considering a mild alpha of 0.3 and 0.5, with a fallback plan if performance declines.**Preparing to update research log**


  Since there are two evaluations running, I can’t submit any more for now, but I can prepare code for alpha values of 0.2 and 0.0 for later. I need to ensure the directory uses relative imports earlier and that we’ve added the pbve modules. The densification of a 20k x 2k matrix is considerable, but I think it''s manageable. I’m going to rewrite the research log to include key baselines and new pbve experiments (EIDs 286 and 287), summarizing expectations. It’s okay to write a new snapshot that captures the entire history. I’ll also consider future directions if diagonal equalization doesn''t work. Let''s craft the YAML for storage write.**Crafting a concise log summary**


  I’m putting together a comprehensive log that recaps SOTA EID 244, introducing the new pbve concept for per-batch variance equalization at the embedding stage. I’ll include details about the implementation, like global soft-whitening with tau=0.95 and diagnostics per PC. Experiments 286 and 287 will have alpha values of 0.3 and 0.5, respectively, with hypotheses aiming for improvements in ASW_batch and kBET while managing risks of over-smoothing. I’ll also outline future plans to compute label-free metrics and consider a fallback for the best dual BRBG whitened step. It’s essential to organize the actions clearly too!'
