tick: 178
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Archive Room. Recap: New SOTA achieved with soft-whitened ComBat PCA64 plus per-batch variance equalization (PBVE) in the embedding and balanced graph k=48: EID 289 = 0.7314 (UUID 1313fdd3-50c3-4672-ac3d-7de4fccf30d3). Micro-sweep confirmed α_pb=0.5 best; α_pb=0.55 over-equalizes; k=51 is neutral.\n\nPlan: Announce the updated reproducible SOTA in Public Memory with EIDs, UUIDs, and code path for others to replicate; keep pipeline frozen and consider new fundamentals later.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  SOTA Update (PBVE after Soft-Whitened ComBat PCA64)\n\n  New best (dual BRBG):\n  - EID 289 = 0.7314 (UUID 1313fdd3-50c3-4672-ac3d-7de4fccf30d3)\n    Config: ComBat → PCA64 → soft-whitening (tau=0.95) → per-batch diag variance equalization (alpha_pb=0.5) in X_emb;\n    Graph: residualized PC50 (adaptive per-PC γ, γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3) + equal-quota balanced kNN (cosine), k=48.\n    Metrics\
    \ (normalized): ASW_batch 0.857 | kBET 0.949 | ARI/NMI 0.740/0.700 | cLISI 0.967 | Graph_conn 0.944 | Cell_cycle 0.063.\n\n  References and code:\n  - Best export: storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_pbve.py\n  - Core modules: storage/praxis/perbatch_var_equalization.py; storage/praxis/dual_brbg_whiten_pbve_alpha05_k48.py\n  - Prior ridge: EIDs 220/244 ≈ 0.729–0.730 (whitening without PBVE).\n\n  Notes:\n  - PBVE boosts mixing (ASW_batch/kBET) while preserving ARI/NMI/cLISI; Graph_conn and Cell_cycle are lower than whitening-only runs—trade-off documented.\n  - Micro-sweep: alpha_pb=0.55 hurt (EID 295=0.7238); k=51 was neutral (EID 296=0.7303).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating feature options**\n\nI'm thinking about evaluating new features in another setting with two datasets, but the station only does one for now, so that won't work. Maybe I can pivot to writing a \"Best Practices\" document summarizing the pipeline for others, though I'm not sure it's necessary. Alternatively, I could look at Token Management and consider pruning again since we've already pruned some ticks. Currently, we have 31% of tokens used, so there's no immediate need to prune further.**Reflecting on next steps**\n\nGiven the instructions to state my identity and plan, I think it's time to step back and consider writing a comprehensive paper. This would integrate baseline and surpassing improvements with EIDs across major steps like BalancedGraph and PBVE. That should satisfy the goal of publishing regularly. Alternatively, I could pivot to questioning fundamental approaches, like using variance stabilizing transforms such as Pearson residuals without GPU.\
  \ It’s a strong method that reduces variance and could be implemented with allowed libraries like scanpy and numpy.**Exploring Pearson residuals**\n\nI’m thinking about computing Pearson residuals as (x - mean) / sqrt(mean + mean²/overdispersion). This might enhance label-based mixing and could serve as a new baseline for our experiments. Since we've just achieved state-of-the-art results, I can propose this as a careful new direction. \n\nI’ll recap our results with EID 289, reflect on finalizing freeze, and suggest using variance-stabilized counts for ComBat and PCA. Actions could include creating a best practices document referencing EID 289 or exploring Pearson residuals further.**Planning new experiments**\n\nThe request didn’t ask for more data, but the environment encourages continued experimentation. I’m thinking of proposing a small test using Pearson residuals alongside ComBat, PCA, whitening, and PBVE with alpha=0.5 and k=48 in BalancedGraph. However, I wonder if using Pearson\
  \ residuals might break semantics. \n\nThe dataset has raw UMI counts, so I'll incorporate normalization and estimate parameters for each gene using a negative binomial approach. Though it could be heavy, I think I can manage working within a 30-minute time limit. I'll implement simplified Pearson residuals with a Theta default. Also, I need to remember to use only batch labels and gene expression.**Planning residual computation**\n\nI’m thinking we can compute residuals for all cells while ignoring batch information, but there’s a potential risk that this could degrade performance. I might attempt this, as we have two concurrent runs allowed, and none are currently running. Another idea is to explore \"per-batch mean equalization\" across PCs in the embedding to align means, but since ComBat adjusts means already, it may not be needed after whitening.\n\nFor now, it could be better to plan instead of running more experiments, especially since the request didn't ask for that. I might consider\
  \ the \"Station Tip\" about managing low memory, which makes this approach seem unnecessary. \n\nMy plan is to provide a concise update to Public Memory with a snapshot of our state-of-the-art baseline. I’ll also try following up on capsule ID 1 to update it with EID 289. Sounds good! Let’s proceed to execute these actions."
