tick: 180
role: model
parts:
- text: "Clarifications, pitfalls, and concrete designs with adoption checks\n\n1) GCCA/MCCA-style shared subspace\n- Caveat: Classical (multi-set) CCA assumes matched samples across views, which we don’t have (cells differ across batches). Naively forming cross-covariances Zp^T Zq between unmatched cells is not meaningful (expectation ~0 under independence).\n- Viable linear alternative (distribution-level alignment):\n  - Covariance barycenter subspace: Treat each batch b as Gaussian in PC space with covariance Σb. Compute a weighted barycenter Σ̄ (e.g., arithmetic mean or Bures–Wasserstein barycenter) and use its top eigenvectors as the shared subspace. This emphasizes directions carrying consistent variance across batches without needing sample correspondences. We already approximate this via pooled ComBat+PCA; the Bures barycenter could be a principled refinement (simultaneous “averaged whitening”).\n  - Unsupervised centroid alignment: Cluster each batch in X_emb (e.g., k≈64), match\
    \ cross-batch centroids by Hungarian assignment on cosine distances, and learn a linear orthogonal alignment per batch (as in our early Procrustes approach), but in the whitened ComBat PC space. Project all batches to a common frame by composing their alignments. This is CCA-like without labels or correspondences and can better preserve shared biology if centroid matching is reliable.\n- Expected effect: modest ARI/NMI/ASW_label/Cell_cycle ↑; mixing metrics should remain high since quotas govern the graph. Adoption only if mean ≥ +0.001 with ARI/NMI/cLISI non-decreasing.\n\n2) Restoring connectivity with minimal change (and minimal mixing impact)\n- Why Graph_conn dropped after PBVE: per-batch equalization reduces redundant local links; the quota graph is structurally sparse within putative same-type neighborhoods. A tiny, structure-aware augmentation can help.\n- Two safe augmentations:\n  - Component-bridge augmentation (label-free, minimal): Find connected components in the current\
    \ quota graph. If >1, connect components by adding a single shortest edge (in X_emb) between each adjacent pair in the component tree (O(C−1) edges total). Binary, symmetric. Expected: Graph_conn ↑ toward previous ~0.97 with negligible change to mixing (edges are minimal and geometry-consistent).\n  - Cluster-bridge augmentation: KMeans on X_emb (k≈64). For each cluster, add a 1-NN intra-cluster edge per node if absent (binary, symmetric). This preferentially densifies geometrically coherent neighborhoods (often same-type) without batch preference. Expected: Graph_conn ↑ with small/no change in ASW_batch/kBET; cLISI unchanged.\n- Avoid: global diffusion/weights unless binarized post-hoc; we’ve seen weights reintroduce magnitude bias.\n\n3) PBVE selectivity (auto-gated per PC)\n- Rationale: Uniform α_pb=0.5 helps mixing but slightly hurts Cell_cycle and Graph_conn. Most variance mismatches live in a few PCs; target only those.\n- Mechanism:\n  - Compute per-PC deviation dj = max_b |log(var_bj/var_all_j)|.\n\
    \  - Auto-threshold τ = median(d) + 1.5·MAD(d) (robust) or 80th percentile. Set αj = 0.5 for dj > τ else 0.\n  - Apply per-batch scaling only where αj > 0.\n- Expected: keep ASW_batch/kBET gains while recovering some Cell_cycle/Graph_conn; aim for +0.0003–0.0007 mean. No manual knob (τ is auto), preserves parsimony.\n\n4) HVG refinements that are minimally invasive\n- Mean-shift pruning (batch-only ANOVA signal): On normalized/log1p, compute SSB and SSW per gene; drop top q% by SSB/(SSB+SSW) with small q (2.5–5%), then refill to 2000 by next-best HVGs. This removes the most batch-shifted genes while preserving dispersion logic. Expected: mild ASW_label/Cell_cycle ↑, mixing neutral.\n- IHVG (frequency across batches): Select genes flagged HVG in ≥ f batches (e.g., ≥2/4), then choose top 2000 by pooled dispersion within that set. Risk: over-strict intersections can reduce nuanced state markers and hurt cLISI; prefer mean-shift pruning first.\n\n5) Shared subspace paired with milder residualization\n\
    - If a better shared basis (via barycenter or centroid alignment) drops per-PC batch predictability R², our adaptive γj already shrinks subtraction. Optionally, lower γ_max to 0.70 only if a broad R² decrease is observed (to avoid over-residualizing subtle biology). Acceptance gate: ARI/NMI/ASW_label/Cell_cycle non-decreasing; mixing unchanged.\n\nUnsupervised diagnostics to guide adoption\n- For connectivity augmentations:\n  - Number of connected components; size of second-largest component; algebraic connectivity (λ2) of graph Laplacian on quota vs augmented graph (label-free proxy for Graph_conn).\n- For PBVE gating:\n  - Proportion of PCs gated; variance of neighbor distances; change in cross-batch neighbor fraction under naive kNN in X_emb (should remain high).\n- For HVG pruning:\n  - Distribution shift in SSB/(SSB+SSW); ensure retained genes cover broad functional diversity (e.g., do not collapse to housekeeping-like sets).\n- For shared subspace:\n  - Agreement (principal angles)\
    \ between batch-subspaces before/after; R²_batch per PC; effective dimension (participation ratio). Aim: lower R², higher PR, stable anisotropy.\n\nPrioritized execution (single-change steps, low risk)\n- S1: Component-bridge augmentation on current best (EID 289 config). Success = Graph_conn ≥ 0.96 with no drop in ARI/NMI/cLISI; mean ≥ 0.732.\n- S2: PBVE auto-gating (robust τ). Success = mean ≥ 0.7319; no ARI/NMI/cLISI regressions; Cell_cycle/Graph_conn non-decreasing.\n- S3: Mean-shift HVG pruning (q≈3%). Success = ASW_label/Cell_cycle ↑ with mean non-decreasing.\n- S4: Distribution-level shared subspace (barycenter or centroid alignment) with unchanged graph; adopt only if ≥ +0.001 mean and biology metrics non-decreasing.\n\nGeneral guardrails\n- Keep edges binary and symmetric after any augmentation.\n- Keep new knobs automatic (percentiles, MAD-based thresholds) rather than hand-tuned.\n- Log full configs, EIDs/UUIDs, and negative outcomes; adopt only with clear, reproducible benefits."
thinking_content: '**Exploring backbone augmentation**


  I''m considering the adoption of "backbone augmentation" with minimal edges for better connectivity. The algorithm would compute connected components in the current symmetric graph, then connect these components using nearest-pair edges; I’d continue adding edges until there’s a single component. This might improve the graph connectivity metric significantly. The Graph_conn metric appears to measure connectivity across cell types, but it seems label-free approaches could be utilized without impacting quotas. The baseline connectivity is higher than before, and I think it could work well!**Proposing robust connectivity**


  I’m thinking about the "robustization of connectivities scaling," especially since the metric relies on connectivity values. However, I''ve noticed that previous weights didn''t really help, so a minimal backbone addition with binary edges might be useful. I could also propose a parameterless gating threshold using the median absolute deviation to select outlier PCs without heavy reliance on percentiles. For the HVG intersection, I should unify batches by a frequency threshold, but I’m cautious since the dataset already uses batch-aware HVGs, which may affect results.**Considering cell cycle penalties**


  I’m looking into improving the "cell cycle penalty." A low Cell_cycle metric suggests decreased retention, so I’m thinking about applying a mild ridge penalty with specific gene sets. The rules don’t allow for cell_type information but do permit gene sets. Using external cell cycle gene sets seems like standard knowledge, but I’m unsure if it counts as external data use. I need to ensure that I’m adhering to the specification, which allows only batch labels and gene expression, while keeping any changes minimal.**Proposing gating threshold methods**


  I’m considering computing the gating threshold solely based on the per-batch variance, without the need for hyperparameters. I could unify the Graph_conn and gating processes by first applying gating and then using a minimal backbone to connect components. Additionally, I might suggest a "consistency across data splits" test, although it may not be essential. It’s important to stay within reflection tick and present a clear, structured analysis with defined success criteria, debug logs, and a 30-minute HPC/time constraint. The complexity appears manageable!**Analyzing metrics correlations**


  I’m proposing an offline analysis to see how various metrics correlate. The drop in Graph_conn may relate to BalancedGraph reducing local redundancy. I’m interested in how weighted connectivities interact and whether binary weights will be okay. For PPR diffusion, I wonder if these weights affect connectivity. It seems that the Graph_conn metric focuses on component connectivity rather than just edge weights. I’m also considering PC gating to reduce variance and address cluster fragmentation while exploring alignment techniques across batches, specifically avoiding cell-type labels. I need to prioritize caution in this complex analysis!**Refining analysis proposals**


  I’m considering a new approach called "embedding-of-embedding" alignment, which might bring small improvements, though we already have strong mixing. Upon reflection, I realize that our previous GCCA suggestion isn’t suitable due to the lack of sample correspondences. Instead, I''m proposing a "distribution-level shared subspace" using barycenters to align clusters. Additionally, we could enhance connectivity through MST bridging across components while ensuring we maintain label-level connectivity. I''ll outline algorithmic details for minimal backbone bridging and gating thresholds. Caution will be needed about using weighted versus binary edges, as the implications could vary.'
