- text: "Identity: Praxis I, in the Archive Room. Recap: Best remains EID 289 = 0.7314 (soft-whitened ComBat PCA64 + PBVE α=0.5, balanced k=48). Cluster-bridge augmentation was neutral (EID 307 = 0.7309). PBVE auto-gating was negative (EID 308 = 0.7255). HVG mean-shift pruning hurt (EID 311 = 0.7111; EID 314 = 0.7224). I patched the HVG helper to use variances_norm for seurat_v3, but the approach is still detrimental. I’ll freeze EID 289 as default and prune recent verbose ticks to reduce context.\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"171-176\"\n    summary: |\n      PBVE and micro-sweeps; new SOTA:\n      - EID 287 (PBVE α=0.5, k=45) = 0.7303; EID 289 (α=0.5, k=48) = 0.7314 (SOTA; UUID 1313fdd3-50c3-4672-ac3d-7de4fccf30d3).\n      - α=0.4 (EID 288) = 0.7266; α=0.55 (EID 295) = 0.7238 (over-equalization hurts).\n      - Degree k=51 (EID 296) = 0.7303 (neutral vs k=48).\n      - Archived addendum with EIDs/UUIDs\
    \ and code paths; frozen export at storage/praxis/best_dual_brbg_whitened_pbve.py.\n\n  - ticks: \"177-184\"\n    summary: |\n      Minimal trials beyond PBVE and HVG pruning outcomes:\n      - Cluster-bridge graph augmentation (EID 307) = 0.7309 (neutral; Graph_conn stayed 0.944).\n      - PBVE auto-gating by per-PC variance deviation (EID 308) = 0.7255 (negative).\n      - HVG mean-shift pruning (drop top SSB/(SSB+SSW) genes, refill):\n        • q=3% (EID 311) = 0.7111; q=2% (EID 314) = 0.7224 (both negative).\n        • Debugger identified seurat_v3 column mismatch; lineage patch applied to use variances_norm, but pruning still harms biology (ARI/NMI ↓).\n      - Decision: keep EID 289 as frozen default; close HVG-pruning line; document negatives in Archive addenda.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Deciding on next steps**


  I see that the user has provided a wealth of context. We''re currently reviewing results from some actions we performed, like writing code fixes and sending a reply. EID 311 and 314 both came back negative, which suggests our mean-shift HVG prunings need attention. I think we ought to consider our next steps carefully—perhaps summarizing findings or pruning tokens again. Looks like connectivity options are on the table, so exploring a simpler global 1-NN augmentation could help.**Reviewing Graph_conn metrics**


  I noticed that the cluster-bridge approach didn’t improve our Graph_conn, which stayed at 0.944. This raises questions about whether the metric might be saturated, especially since baseline 244 was better at 0.972. I’m considering alternatives like "balanced-graph plus minimal all-batch 1-NN augmentation,” but I''m skeptical about significant improvement. Exploring "seeded diffusion weights" is another option, despite historical degradation of mixing. Given that we''ve plateaued, perhaps it’s time to pause and consolidate findings instead of complicating matters further.**Finalizing next steps**


  I’m considering a few complex options like "distribution-level shared subspace" or "simultaneous diagonalization" for unifying covariances. However, it seems like the best plan is to declare EID 289 as our frozen default, update the archive, and revert the HVG code fix while documenting everything. Since we''ve already added an addendum, I think it’s best not to run any more code right now and instead plan to prune tokens later. I''ll summarize findings and outline our actions clearly.**Considering token management**


  I’m exploring several potential actions. I could update our reference in storage, or perhaps share the negative results publicly. When it comes to pruning tokens, we have previously pruned and might be due for another round, especially since we can remove older, heavy content from ticks 171 to 185. Since there’s been a cooldown since our last prune, I think we could move forward with it now, focusing on the larger, less critical code logs while keeping important information intact.**Planning next steps and pruning tokens**


  I need to create a concise summary that’s still information-complete. Instead of pruning, I could shift focus back to research and finalize content. I''m proposing we pivot to "centroid-aligned Procrustes in whitened ComBat embeddings" but test it later to avoid complexity and token bloat. I''ll report recent results showing that EIDs 311 and 314 were negative. I’ll freeze EID 289 as default and proceed with token pruning, targeting blocks 171-176 and 177-184 while summarizing findings neatly. Let''s move to token management for this.'
