- text: "Local SBT yields modest ARI/NMI gains but remains below PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). I’ll log these results and append an archive addendum, then wait for BCE results.\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log_addendum_sbt.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Addendum: Sliced Batch Transport (SBT) trials\n  Tick: 190\n\n  Motivation:\n  - Address non-linear batch effects not removed by ComBat/residualization via sliced optimal transport in 1D projections (quantile maps), with small steps to avoid over-correction.\n\n  Pipelines:\n  - SBT-Emb (EID 328): ComBat-PCA64 → whitening (tau=0.95) → SBT (n_dirs=32, eta=0.2) → PBVE (α=0.5) for embedding; graph PCs=50 with adaptive residualization (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3) → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n  - SBT-Graph (EID 329): Embedding as baseline (whitened + PBVE). Apply SBT (n_dirs=32, eta=0.15) on residualized graph PCs, then build equal-quotas graph (k=48).\n\n  Results:\n  - EID 328 (SBT-Emb): 0.7219\
    \ | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | ASW_batch 0.852 | kBET 0.813 | iLISI 0.935 | Graph_conn 0.946 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.099. UUID 75383761-....\n  - EID 329 (SBT-Graph): 0.7208 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.704 | ASW_batch 0.852 | kBET 0.818 | iLISI 0.935 | Graph_conn 0.946 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.081. UUID 4653bd81-....\n\n  Comparison:\n  - PBVE baseline (EID 289): 0.7314.\n  - SBT increased ARI/NMI slightly but did not improve the mean score; mixing metrics stayed high but not superior to PBVE baseline.\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - SBT (both embedding and graph) is neutral-to-negative overall on this dataset with our current knobs. Line closed; documented for completeness.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 7}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum: Sliced Batch Transport (SBT) — non-linear alignment trials\n\n  We tested SBT (quantile-based sliced OT) as a lightweight non-linear correction:\n\n  • EID 328 (SBT on whitened embedding, 32 dirs, η=0.2):\
    \ 0.7219 (UUID 75383761-...).\n  • EID 329 (SBT on residualized graph PCs, 32 dirs, η=0.15): 0.7208 (UUID 4653bd81-...).\n\n  Both increased ARI/NMI slightly but remained below the PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). Mixing and connectivity stayed strong but not improved enough to lift the mean. We keep the PBVE baseline and close the SBT line. BCE (CORAL-like covariance equalization) runs are pending (EIDs 332, 333).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Assessing next steps**\n\nI need to figure out our next action steps based on the current station. The user posted the state at tick 191, and we have two BCE evaluations queued. In the context of Agent Praxis I's improvements, the previous attempts weren't as high as we hoped. \n\nWe could either wait for the results or focus on some additional research tasks. However, I should be mindful of the concurrency limit since we already have two evaluations running.**Evaluating next actions**\n\nI can reflect on our current situation or log it for later. Checking token management cooldown seems wise, especially since the previous prune's cooldown has likely expired. Token usage is at 28%, so pruning might not be necessary right now. \n\nFor the next steps, I’m considering testing BCE at lower rho values of 0.2 and 0.1, which carry less risk. I’m also pondering if using BCE after whitening might be more effective, even though it could be redundant. Another option is to explore\
  \ \"Sliced barycentric mapping along principal axes,\" though that could add complexity.**Exploring new strategies**\n\nI'm considering the potential of using \"mutual nearest neighbors across batches\" to create cross-batch anchors for adjusting embedding, similar to what Haghverdi et al. proposed. This approach is more complex, but it could be beneficial, even though it goes against our earlier pivot from incremental improvements.\n\nEID 289 is quite high at 0.7314, so I wonder if using MNN could push us past that. Another possibility is \"Power-balanced quotas,\" but we had negative results with local scaling before. We might also explore \"Graph densification using Steiner nodes\" or \"Feature-level ComBat\" versus gene-level, as we are already using ComBat.**Assessing the pipeline**\n\nThe current pipeline uses embeddings for 'X_emb' and a separate graph 'Zcorr' derived from original lognormalized high-variance genes (HVGs). I wonder if we could unify these by computing the neighbor\
  \ graph with whitened embeddings and quotas. However, the past pipeline \"BRBG Dual\" utilized equal quotas from Zcorr, and I worry that building neighbors on whitened embedding might affect labels adversely.\n\nWhile we wait for BCE results, it's a good moment to update the research log and plan our next steps. I could also check other agents' archives for new ideas, even if it uses some tokens.**Planning next steps**\n\nI can preview the abstract for 6 and should focus on the request to produce next actions, primarily limited to logging and waiting. The instructions say only lines with /execute_action are parsed, so I should log a BCE addendum describing the plan and expected outcomes, then wait for results.\n\nOnce both BCEs finish, I might suggest a low-rank Procrustes across ComBat whitened embeddings, anchored on per-batch centroids, but I must remember that additional Procrustes could lead to over-correction. So I'll log the SBT results for now, then proceed with logging actions\
  \ and await the BCE outcomes before proposing further modifications."
