tick: 192
role: model
parts:
- text: "BCE results are in: both rho=0.3 (EID 332 = 0.7244) and rho=0.5 (EID 333 = 0.7253) underperform PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). Mixing metrics improved (ASW_batch/kBET) but mean score fell due to other components. I’ll log and archive the negatives, then pause this line.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log_addendum_bce.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Addendum: Batch Covariance Equalization (BCE, CORAL-like) trials\n  Tick: 192\n\n  Motivation:\n  - Address batch-specific covariance shape differences not captured by mean/variance methods by mapping each batch's covariance toward the global covariance in ComBat-PCA space, then apply global whitening (tau=0.95) and PBVE (alpha=0.5).\n\n  Method:\n  - Embedding path: ComBat on normalized/log1p → PCA64 on HVGs → BCE with rho ∈ {0.3, 0.5}: X_b ← C_mix^{1/2} C_b^{-1/2}(X_b−μ_b) + μ_mix, where C_mix = (1−rho)C_b + rho C_global and μ_mix = (1−rho)μ_b + rho μ_global → global whitening (tau=0.95) → PBVE (alpha=0.5).\n  -\
    \ Graph path unchanged: PCA50 on HVGs → adaptive residualization (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, l2=1e-3) → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n\n  Results:\n  - EID 332 (rho=0.3): 0.7244 | ASW_batch 0.859 | ASW_label 0.159 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.870 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.062. UUID 447e7808-...\n  - EID 333 (rho=0.5): 0.7253 | ASW_batch 0.857 | ASW_label 0.159 | ARI 0.743 | NMI 0.703 | Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.867 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.076. UUID 7c858575-...\n\n  Comparison:\n  - PBVE baseline (EID 289): 0.7314. BCE improves batch mixing (ASW_batch, kBET) but reduces the overall mean score, with no ARI/NMI lift.\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - BCE is neutral-to-negative under our dual BRBG. We retain the simpler PBVE baseline and close the BCE line. Negative results documented here for reproducibility.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 7}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum:\
    \ Batch Covariance Equalization (BCE/CORAL) trials\n\n  We tested per-batch covariance equalization toward the global covariance in ComBat-PCA64 before global whitening + PBVE:\n\n  • EID 332 (rho=0.3): 0.7244 (UUID 447e7808-...).\n  • EID 333 (rho=0.5): 0.7253 (UUID 7c858575-...).\n\n  Both improve ASW_batch/kBET but lower the mean score vs PBVE baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314). ARI/NMI stayed flat. We keep the PBVE baseline and close the BCE line. Full details in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bce.md.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring gamma_max tuning**\n\nI'm considering testing PC72 again since we got a negative 0.7229 before. For embedding counts, it looks like PC64 is the top choice. I’m also thinking about how our graph's adaptive residualization gamma_max may change slightly with PBVE/whitened improvements. We previously found that tuning it to 0.72 or 0.76 led to lower performance on the heart dataset, while PBVE did better at 0.7314. It may be more effective to focus on publishing updates and ensuring knowledge retention.**Considering archival generalization**\n\nI’m realizing that archival requires generalization across at least two settings, which we’ve done before with embedding techniques like PCA and residualization. I could expand our pipeline to include various configurations like whitened, coupled with PBVE, and SBT, but the improvements may be minimal. I think submitting a standard reproduction of \"auto-τ + PBVE + k=48\" could work. I also want to log BCE results and brainstorm\
  \ new directions, but I should consider the complexity of using JAX for kernel alignment. Instead, I could propose \"Batch-sliced center alignment\" using trimmed means for scaling, but we'll see if this approach affects label metrics negatively.**Exploring robust scaling options**\n\nI think we could try a \"small robust scaling\" approach with limited shrinkage, but I really want to avoid chasing minor gains. I’m also considering switching the roles of the graph and embedding by using whitened embeddings for the graph and residualized graph PCs for embedding. We need to produce either embedding or graph outputs. Since graph-only outputs work for us, that could simplify things. Another option might be to use \"sparse neighbor selection via MST augmentation\" to prevent graph_conn from saturating, as it's already quite high. Let’s focus on our research contributions!**Organizing logging and pruning tasks**\n\nIn this turn, I'll need to log the BCE results in the storage addendum and reply\
  \ to the archive with the BCE addendum. I’m also considering updating Public or Private Memory to include a succinct list of \"current defaults and all EIDs\" for reproducibility. \n\nI might prune older responses with heavy tokens, like tick 185 and 2749, while also holding off on some ticks. Since the system initially had a cooldown but is now activated again, I can focus on pruning tick 185 and possibly ticks 188-191, ensuring relevant results are summarized carefully.**Planning storage and archiving actions**\n\nAs we aim to keep tokens below 80k, this is manageable. Since the default desired verbosity is low, I'll keep my message concise, focusing on the following actions:\n\nFirst, I'll log the BCE results in the storage. Then, I’ll reply to the archive with the BCE addendum. We could prepare a \"frozen pipeline file\" path for our baseline, although that’s optional as we already have one. \n\nNext, rather than initiating new experiments, I'll focus on writing a research log with\
  \ details about EIDs 332 and 333. Finally, I’ll check public memory for any necessary updates and consider summarizing everything in a private memory capsule, ensuring all YAML formatting is correct."
