tick: 196
role: model
parts:
- text: "Acknowledged Stagnation Protocol I. I will follow the 7 steps precisely: review archives, pick a simple baseline, reflect to generate 3 new ideas distinct from existing work, then run tightly scoped experiments starting from the baseline.\n\nActions:\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{preview all}\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{review 2}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/simple_batch_transforms.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from scipy.special import ndtri\n\n  def _ensure_dense_block(X):\n      return X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n\n  def _iter_batches(batches):\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx.size > 0:\n              yield b, idx\n\n  def apply_brs_inplace(X, batches, gene_mask=None):\n      \"\"\"\n      Batchwise Rank-to-Normal Scores (BRS), in-place on X (cells x genes).\n      For each batch and\
    \ each selected gene: ranks -> normal scores via ndtri.\n      Only transforms columns where gene_mask==True (if provided).\n      \"\"\"\n      Xd = _ensure_dense_block(X)\n      n, p = Xd.shape\n      if gene_mask is None:\n          gene_idx = np.arange(p)\n      else:\n          gene_idx = np.where(gene_mask)[0]\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n\n      for _, idx in _iter_batches(batches):\n          Xb = Xd[idx[:, None], gene_idx]  # view (n_b x p_sel)\n          n_b = Xb.shape[0]\n          if n_b <= 1:\n              continue\n          # Argsort twice to get ranks for each column\n          order = np.argsort(Xb, axis=0, kind='mergesort')\n          ranks = np.empty_like(order, dtype=np.int32)\n          # Build column indices grid\n          cols = np.broadcast_to(np.arange(Xb.shape[1]), order.shape)\n          rows = order\n          ranks[rows, cols] = np.arange(n_b)[:, None]\n          u = (ranks + 0.5) / float(n_b)\n          Z = ndtri(u)  # normal scores\n        \
    \  Xd[idx[:, None], gene_idx] = Z\n\n  def apply_miqm_inplace(X, batches, gene_mask=None, eps=1e-8):\n      \"\"\"\n      Median-IQR Matching (MIQM): per batch, per gene, affine map\n        x' = ((x - med_b)/IQR_b) * IQR_global + med_global\n      Only for genes where gene_mask==True (if provided).\n      \"\"\"\n      Xd = _ensure_dense_block(X)\n      n, p = Xd.shape\n      if gene_mask is None:\n          gene_idx = np.arange(p)\n      else:\n          gene_idx = np.where(gene_mask)[0]\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n\n      # Global stats on selected genes\n      q1_g = np.quantile(Xd[:, gene_idx], 0.25, axis=0)\n      q3_g = np.quantile(Xd[:, gene_idx], 0.75, axis=0)\n      med_g = np.median(Xd[:, gene_idx], axis=0)\n      iqr_g = np.maximum(q3_g - q1_g, eps)\n\n      for _, idx in _iter_batches(batches):\n          Xb = Xd[idx[:, None], gene_idx]\n          q1_b = np.quantile(Xb, 0.25, axis=0)\n          q3_b = np.quantile(Xb, 0.75, axis=0)\n          med_b = np.median(Xb,\
    \ axis=0)\n          iqr_b = np.maximum(q3_b - q1_b, eps)\n          scale = iqr_g / iqr_b\n          Xd[idx[:, None], gene_idx] = (Xb - med_b) * scale + med_g\n\n  def apply_winsor_inplace(X, batches, gene_mask=None, q_low=0.01, q_high=0.99):\n      \"\"\"\n      Per-batch, per-gene winsorization to clamp heavy tails at [q_low, q_high].\n      Only for genes where gene_mask==True (if provided).\n      \"\"\"\n      Xd = _ensure_dense_block(X)\n      n, p = Xd.shape\n      if gene_mask is None:\n          gene_idx = np.arange(p)\n      else:\n          gene_idx = np.where(gene_mask)[0]\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n\n      for _, idx in _iter_batches(batches):\n          Xb = Xd[idx[:, None], gene_idx]\n          lo = np.quantile(Xb, q_low, axis=0)\n          hi = np.quantile(Xb, q_high, axis=0)\n          Xb = np.minimum(np.maximum(Xb, lo), hi)\n          Xd[idx[:, None], gene_idx] = Xb\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Baseline (Combat-HVG) Reproduction:\
    \ normalize/log1p → HVG → ComBat → PCA50 → kNN15\"\ntags: \"baseline, combat, hvg, pca, knn, cosine\"\nabstract: \"Reproduce a simple ComBat baseline using batch-aware HVGs: normalize/log1p → HVG (seurat_v3) → ComBat → PCA(50) → kNN(k=15, cosine). This serves as the baseline for Stagnation Protocol experiments.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      n_pcs = 50\n      n_neighbors = 15\n      metric = 'cosine'\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Start from raw counts\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # HVGs (batch-aware)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n\n  \
    \    # ComBat on dense\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          adata.X = adata.X.A\n      sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n\n      # PCA on HVGs\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_pca']\n\n      # kNN graph on PCA\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea 1 — BRS: Batchwise Rank-to-Normal + ComBat → PCA50 → kNN15\"\ntags: \"baseline, brs, combat, hvg, pca, knn\"\nabstract: \"Insert Batchwise Rank-to-Normal Scores (BRS) on HVGs before ComBat to align marginal gene distributions per batch in a monotone, label-free way; then ComBat → PCA(50) → kNN(15).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from praxis.simple_batch_transforms import\
    \ apply_brs_inplace\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      n_pcs = 50\n      n_neighbors = 15\n      metric = 'cosine'\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # Normalize/log1p\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # HVGs\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n\n      # Apply BRS on HVGs only (dense slice)\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          Xd = adata.X.A\n      else:\n          Xd = adata.X\n      apply_brs_inplace(Xd, batches=batches, gene_mask=hv)\n      adata.X = Xd\n\n      # ComBat\n      sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n\n      # PCA on HVGs\n    \
    \  sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_pca']\n\n      # kNN graph\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Idea 2 — MIQM: Batchwise Median-IQR Matching + ComBat → PCA50 → kNN15\"\ntags: \"baseline, miqm, combat, hvg, pca, knn\"\nabstract: \"Insert per-batch Median–IQR Matching (MIQM) on HVGs before ComBat to align each gene’s scale/location across batches; then ComBat → PCA(50) → kNN(15).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from praxis.simple_batch_transforms import apply_miqm_inplace\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      n_pcs = 50\n      n_neighbors = 15\n      metric = 'cosine'\n\n      assert 'batch'\
    \ in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # Normalize/log1p\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # HVGs\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n\n      # Apply MIQM on HVGs only\n      if sp.issparse(adata.X):\n          Xd = adata.X.A\n      else:\n          Xd = adata.X\n      apply_miqm_inplace(Xd, batches=batches, gene_mask=hv)\n      adata.X = Xd\n\n      # ComBat\n      sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n\n      # PCA on HVGs\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_pca']\n\n      # kNN graph\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata,\
    \ use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto reflect}\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Stagnation Protocol I — Reflection. Baseline: simple ComBat-HVG (normalize/log1p → HVG → ComBat → PCA → kNN).\n  Propose three distinct, simple, dataset-agnostic ideas not in existing archives:\n  1) BRS — Batchwise Rank-to-Normal Scores on HVGs before ComBat: monotone per-gene alignment of marginal distributions per batch to stabilize ComBat and PCA.\n  2) MIQM — Batchwise Median–IQR Matching on HVGs before ComBat: align gene medians/scales per batch to the global, preserving monotone order and robust to outliers.\n  3) WINSOR — Per-batch, per-gene winsorization at [1%, 99%] prior to ComBat to reduce heavy-tail artifacts that can destabilize PCA/neighbors.\n  For each: expected impacts on ASW_batch, kBET, iLISI vs ARI/NMI/cLISI; risks; how to sweep minimal hyperparameters (e.g., quantiles), and how to combine with SOTA\
    \ later.\ntick: 5\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring new ideas for analysis**\n\nI’m considering if I should choose “ComBat + PCA + equal-quotas BalancedGraph” as my baseline. A simple baseline is needed since complex methods can fail. I think I'll settle on the \"Combat Manual\" baseline. Now, I need to come up with three new ideas based on that, ensuring they don’t overlap with previous work. \n\nI wonder if \"gene-wise percentile normalization across batches\" might add value, along with exploring negative binomial GLM residualization before ComBat.**Evaluating transformation and reweighting methods**\n\nI’m working within the limitations of libraries like scanpy and sklearn, which makes me consider options carefully. I could try implementing a negative binomial GLM, but it's quite heavy. As for transformations, maybe the Anscombe variance-stabilizing transform would help with counts before ComBat, though I'm concerned it might be too similar.\n\nI also want to introduce feature reweighting after ComBat with\
  \ a new method I thought of: \"Batch invariant feature scaling.\" Finally, I'm thinking about temporal ensembling across two passes of ComBat; it could be an interesting new approach!**Exploring dual-reference ideas**\n\nI'm considering a new idea called \"Dual-reference ComBat averaging.\" I think it's important to focus not just on equal quotas, but also on balanced edges between pairs of batches to improve our sampling.\n\nPreviously, I've tried mutual edges and soft quotas, but they didn't work out. Now, I'm thinking about \"Edge dropout regularization,\" where I would replace same-batch edges with cross-batch ones. I'll keep it simple by randomly swapping some same-batch neighbors with cross-batch neighbors while keeping the local geometry intact. However, I need to remember to follow the baseline procedure as outlined in Step 4!**Testing ideas with ComBat baseline**\n\nI'm starting with the ComBat Manual baseline at 0.5895, focusing on the effectiveness of ComBat and manual normal\
  \ graphs without balanced quotas. \n\nFor Idea A, I’m thinking about reweighting genes before PCA using Batch Invariant Feature Scaling (BIFS). Idea B involves using dual-reference ComBat averaging, but scanpy's version doesn't seem to support reference batches. An easier alternative could be \"Batchwise Quantile Normalization,\" which normalizes within-batch quantiles for highly variable genes before PCA. Lastly, Idea C would explore cross-batch mixing with balanced quotas, though this isn't new since we've used BalancedGraph before.**Generating new ideas for analysis**\n\nI need to come up with three new ideas, avoiding what’s already in the Archive Room. My first idea is \"Per-gene Batchwise Rank Standardization,\" transforming gene expressions to normalize distributions. \n\nThe second idea is “Batchwise Spherical PCA,” scaling each batch to unit energy before PCA. For the third idea, I’m considering “Cross-batch centroid alignment,” which uses k-means centroids to align across batches.\
  \ However, I’m also thinking about “Batchwise quantile-preserving gene scaling,” which matches global interquartile ranges. Balancing innovative ideas with the existing framework is tricky!**Planning baseline reproduction and new ideas**\n\nI’m looking at my options: I could try applying MIQM before or instead of ComBat, but that might be redundant. I want to pick the \"ComBat Manual Baseline\" of 0.5895 and reproduce it accurately. \n\nFirst, I need to preview everything in the archive, then establish the baseline. Then, I’ll reflect on my ideas: Batchwise Rank Standardization (BRS), Gene Correlation Smoothing (GCS), and MIQM. I must execute careful steps to keep everything aligned with the baseline while testing these innovations. Timing is crucial, so I’ll run evaluations sequentially!**Preparing for BRS and GCS implementation**\n\nI'm planning to replicate the \"Combat Manual Baseline\" as accurately as possible, which means I need to see the code details via a review first. I’ll create\
  \ three new submission ideas: Batchwise Rank Standardization (BRS), Gene Correlation Smoothing (GCS), and another method I'll choose later.\n\nFor BRS, I'll transform gene ranks to normal scores using a vectorized approach, leveraging SciPy. This involves creating a sparse matrix for efficiency. With GCS, I’ll build a correlation matrix for HVGs and apply a smoothing method, though I'm concerned about computational heaviness. I'll also consider a simpler method, winsorization, for managing extreme values to reduce outliers when using ComBat!**Executing Stagnation Protocol Steps**\n\nI'm ready to follow the Stagnation Protocol steps. First, I'll preview the archive, then choose the baseline for replication, which is the \"Combat Manual Baseline.\" \n\nNext, after reviewing the baseline code, I’ll implement various transformation functions like BRS, MIQM, and winsorization, all while ensuring I'm working per batch to manage memory. I plan to submit two scripts now and hold one for later.\
  \ The goal is to replicate the baseline as closely as possible, even if it won't perfectly match the 0.5895 score!**Planning Submission Steps**\n\nI’ll start by running the /review 2 to get the code I need, but I can also review and submit code in the same response. The protocol suggests reproducing the baseline first, so despite a two-slot limit, I’ll fill those with the reproduction attempt and BRS submission. \n\nI'll prepare the pipeline to include counts normalization, log1p, PCA, and neighbors. I want to ensure the BRS and MIQM ideas are well-structured for submission while keeping an eye on token usage and minimal coding. Let's get to crafting these actions!"
