- text: "High-level expectations and risks\n\nIdea 1 — BRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal on HVGs before ComBat)\n- Mechanism: Per-batch, per-gene monotone map to N(0,1). Equalizes marginal shapes across batches, stabilizing ComBat’s parametric fit and PCA scales.\n- Expected metric shifts:\n  - ASW_batch↑, kBET↑, iLISI↑ (batch-predictable marginal skew/scale removed).\n  - ARI/NMI/cLISI ~ flat or slight ↑ (monotone per gene preserves intra-batch rank structure; cross-batch alignment should not collapse multi-gene patterns).\n  - PCR↓ (good).\n  - Cell_cycle: neutral to mild ↑ from reduced noise in PCs.\n- Risks:\n  - Ties/zero-inflation can produce many equal ranks; mapping may over-compress rare high-expression markers and slightly reduce ASW_label.\n  - Extremes after ndtri can inflate variance unless implicitly standardized downstream (ComBat+PCA helps).\n- Minimal sweeps:\n  - HVG-only vs all genes: prefer HVG-only.\n  - Blend factor λ in [0.7, 1.0]: X’ = λ·BRS(X) + (1−λ)·X to avoid over-normalization.\n\
    - SOTA combo:\n  - Apply BRS before ComBat in the dual BRBG pipeline; re-test whitening ridge (τ≈0.95–1.0) and PBVE 0.5. Expect batch isotropy gains with minimal hit to label geometry.\n\nIdea 2 — MIQM (Batchwise Median–IQR Matching on HVGs before ComBat)\n- Mechanism: Robust affine match per gene and batch to global median/scale; removes location/scale batch drift while preserving order and outlier robustness.\n- Expected metrics:\n  - ASW_batch↑, kBET↑; iLISI↑ (less than BRS if heavy-tail shape differences persist).\n  - ARI/NMI/cLISI ~ flat (monotone affine preserves within-batch ranks; cross-batch alignment is gentle).\n  - PCR↓.\n- Risks:\n  - If true biology shifts gene medians across batches (e.g., different subtype proportions), MIQM may partially blur label contrasts (ASW_label slight ↓).\n- Minimal sweeps:\n  - Shrinkage ρ ∈ {0.5, 0.75, 1.0} toward global stats: x’ = med_g + (x−med_b)·(IQR_g/IQR_b)^ρ.\n- SOTA combo:\n  - Pre-ComBat MIQM before whitened ComBat embedding; it\
    \ should reduce variance heterogeneity and make whitening less aggressive.\n\nIdea 3 — WINSOR (Per-batch winsorization on HVGs at [q, 1−q] before ComBat)\n- Mechanism: Clamp heavy tails per gene/batch to stabilize ComBat and PCA; reduces hubness in neighbor graphs downstream.\n- Expected metrics:\n  - ASW_batch↑, kBET↑ (distance stability); iLISI↑ or ≈ (depends on q).\n  - ARI/NMI/cLISI slight ↓ if rare marker extremes carry label separation.\n  - PCR↓ (less tail-driven batch variance).\n- Risks:\n  - Over-clamping (q too high) erodes rare but biologically meaningful extremes; ASW_label/ARI/NMI can drop.\n- Minimal sweeps:\n  - q ∈ {0.5%, 1%, 2%}; HVG-only; optionally combine with mild MIQM (ρ=0.5) if tails remain batch-specific.\n- SOTA combo:\n  - Use light winsor before ComBat in dual BRBG; expect more stable embedding whitening and similar/better kBET without harming cLISI if q is small.\n\nDesign choices to keep experiments clean\n- Keep baseline scaffold fixed: normalize/log1p\
    \ → HVG(seurat_v3) → [transform] → ComBat → PCA50 → kNN15 (cosine).\n- Change one knob per run; record EIDs and result UUIDs.\n- Batch-only selection of hyperparameters (no labels): prefer configs that drop PCR and increase ASW_batch/kBET while keeping Graph_conn ≥ 0.94.\n\nSuccess criteria and next synthesis path\n- Any idea that beats the reproduced baseline by ≥+0.005 mean score with ARI/NMI not worse than −0.005 qualifies as “promising.”\n- Synthesis: port the best pre-ComBat transform (BRS or MIQM or light WINSOR) into the dual BRBG SOTA pipeline (whitened ComBat + PBVE + adaptive residualized graph with equal-quotas). Recheck τ ridge and k=45–48 plateaus."
