    \ + (1−λ)·X on HVGs.\n- Minimal sweep:\n  - λ ∈ {0.8, 0.9, 1.0}; HVG-only (2k genes).\n  - Optional: cap transformed z-scores at |z|≤3.5 to bound extremes.\n- Label-free diagnostics to log (test mode or stdout):\n  - Per-batch per-PC variance CV before/after ComBat; global PR (participation ratio) in PCA; anisotropy (mean/std of per-PC variances).\n  - Batch predictability of PCs (R²_batch via ridge on one-hot) should drop.\n- SOTA integration:\n  - Insert BRS before ComBat in the dual BRBG embedding path, keep whitening (τ≈0.95–1.0) and PBVE 0.5; expect slight τ-insensitivity and equal-quotas graph unchanged.\n\nIdea 2 — MIQM (Batchwise Median–IQR Matching on HVGs)\n- Mechanics with ComBat: MIQM aligns robust location/scale per gene and batch to the global. ComBat then handles residual batch-specific mean/variance and empirical Bayes shrinkage across genes; PCA gets cleaner, comparable axes.\n- Expected metric profile:\n  - ASW_batch: up; kBET/iLISI: up (less per-gene scale drift).\n\
    \  - ARI/NMI/cLISI: ≈flat (affine monotone per-gene).\n- Risks and mitigations:\n  - True biology changing medians across batches (composition shift) could be partly compressed. Use fractional alignment exponent ρ ∈ {0.5, 0.75, 1.0} so X’ = med_g + (X−med_b)·(IQR_g/IQR_b)^ρ; pick ρ<1 for gentler mapping.\n  - IQR≈0 for a gene in a tiny batch: add floor iqr_b=max(iqr_b, eps).\n- Minimal sweep:\n  - ρ ∈ {0.5, 0.75, 1.0}; HVG-only.\n- Diagnostics:\n  - Per-gene across-batch IQR ratio dispersion should tighten; per-PC batch R² drop; whitening τ ridge should remain near 0.95–1.0 in SOTA combo.\n- SOTA integration:\n  - Replace BRS with MIQM in the dual BRBG embedding path; everything else constant.\n\nIdea 3 — WINSOR (Per-batch winsorization on HVGs)\n- Mechanics: Clamp tails per gene/batch at [q, 1−q], reducing heavy-tail artifacts that amplify unfavorable distances and hubness in kNN. Stabilizes ComBat’s batch/variance estimates and PCA.\n- Expected metric profile:\n  - ASW_batch/kBET:\
    \ up; iLISI: up or ≈ (more stable neighbors); Graph_conn stable.\n  - ARI/NMI/cLISI: slight risk of ↓ if biologically meaningful markers live in tails.\n- Risks and mitigations:\n  - Over-clamp: keep q small and HVG-only; optionally pair with gentler MIQM (ρ=0.5) if per-batch scale still differs.\n- Minimal sweep:\n  - q ∈ {0.005, 0.01, 0.02}; HVG-only.\n  - Optionally symmetric vs asymmetric clamping (asymmetric if strong right-skew).\n- Diagnostics:\n  - Kurtosis of HVG distributions per batch should drop; neighbor distance CV decreases; kNN hubness score reduces (max in-degree/mean in-degree).\n\nExperiment plan specifics (baseline scaffold fixed)\n- Scaffold: normalize/log1p → HVG(seurat_v3) → [transform] → ComBat(dense) → PCA50(use HVGs) → neighbors(k=15, cosine).\n- Reproduce baseline first; record EID and metrics. Then run:\n  - BRS: λ ∈ {0.8, 0.9, 1.0}.\n  - MIQM: ρ ∈ {0.5, 0.75, 1.0}.\n  - WINSOR: q ∈ {0.005, 0.01}.\n- Acceptance:\n  - ≥+0.005 over reproduced baseline mean score\
    \ AND ARI/NMI not worse than −0.005; Graph_conn ≥0.94; log UUIDs and config.\n- Follow-on to SOTA:\n  - Take best single setting and insert into dual BRBG embedding; recheck τ ridge and k=45–48; keep equal-quotas graph and adaptive residualization unchanged.\n- Compute/runtime:\n  - BRS O(n log n) per gene (argsort); feasible at 20k×2k with dense block ops.\n  - MIQM/winsor quantiles: vectorized np.quantile over batches/genes; ensure dense arrays."
