parts:
- text: "Risk analysis, guardrails, and interpretation playbook\n\nCross-cutting mechanics and ComBat compatibility\n- Working domain: All transforms run after normalize_total+log1p on HVGs. ComBat’s EB assumes approximate Gaussian residuals per gene; BRS makes this explicit (per-batch per-gene → N(0,1)), MIQM aligns robust location/scale, WINSOR reduces heavy tails. These should stabilize ComBat rather than replace it.\n- Zero inflation and ties: After log1p, zeros remain frequent. BRS uses mid-ranks to avoid 0/1 quantiles; no exact ndtri(0/1). If extreme ties persist, tiny jitter (disabled by default) can be added; prefer stable mergesort so equal values preserve relative order determinism.\n\nGuardrails and failure modes\n- Over-normalization (BRS λ=1.0): If ARI/NMI drop while ASW_batch/kBET rise, switch to λ=0.9; consider z-clipping |z|≤3.5 to cap rare extremes.\n- Median confounding (MIQM ρ too high): If ASW_label/ARI decrease with only mild ASW_batch gains, reduce ρ (0.75→0.5).\n-\
    \ Over-clamping (WINSOR q too high): If ARI/NMI or cLISI drop, back off q (0.01→0.005) or stop the line.\n- Small-batch robustness: For MIQM, floor IQR_b≥1e-8; skip transforms on genes with too few non-zero values if needed. For BRS, ranks still well-defined; if batch size is 1, skip.\n\nDiagnostics to collect (label-free, lightweight)\n- Per-PC batch predictability R² (ridge on one-hot) before/after ComBat: expect ↓ relative to baseline if transform helped.\n- PCA anisotropy and participation ratio (PR): PR↑ and per-PC variance more uniform → healthier embedding.\n- kNN distance CV on PCA: expect ↓ (more stable neighborhoods).\n\nResult interpretation patterns (what moved and why)\n- Pattern A: ASW_batch/kBET↑, iLISI≈, ARI/NMI≈ → success (better batch removal without label harm). BRS λ=1.0 or MIQM ρ=0.75 likely candidates.\n- Pattern B: ASW_batch↑, ARI/NMI↓ → too aggressive; reduce λ (BRS) or ρ (MIQM) or q (WINSOR).\n- Pattern C: No change across metrics → transform too mild/ redundant\
    \ with ComBat; move to next idea.\n- Pattern D: Graph_conn < 0.94 → neighbor brittleness; consider k=18 in baseline scaffold just for sanity check (single run) but keep main scaffold at k=15 for comparability.\n\nMinimal sweep discipline and stopping rules\n- Baseline EID_B first; then:\n  - BRS: λ=1.0 → if ≥−0.01 vs EID_B, try λ=0.9; else stop BRS.\n  - MIQM: ρ=0.75 → if ≥−0.01 vs EID_B, try ρ=0.5; else stop MIQM.\n  - WINSOR: q=0.01 → if ≥−0.01 vs EID_B, try q=0.005; else stop WINSOR.\n- Promising if mean ≥ EID_B+0.005 and ARI/NMI not worse by >0.005; otherwise document as negative with EIDs/UUIDs and metric shifts.\n\nSynthesis with SOTA (only if one idea clears acceptance)\n- Insert the transform into the dual BRBG embedding path only (pre-ComBat). Keep:\n  - ComBat → PCA64 → whitening τ≈0.95 → PBVE α=0.5 (embedding).\n  - Graph path unchanged (adaptive residualization + equal-quotas k=48 cosine).\n- Recheck τ ridge (0.95 vs 1.0) and k plateau (45 vs 48) with one run each. Accept\
    \ if ≥ baseline dual (≈0.718–0.731 range historically) and ARI/NMI stable.\n\nReport plan (per Protocol)\n- Methods: baseline scaffold + three transforms with exact formulas; hyperparams (λ, ρ, q).\n- Experiments: baseline reproduction EID; per-idea 1–2 sweeps with EIDs/UUIDs; diagnostics; discussion of metric trade-offs.\n- Synthesis: best transform ported into SOTA dual BRBG; 1–2 verification runs; outcome (positive/neutral/negative).\n- Value: even negative results clarify that per-gene monotone alignment on HVGs pre-ComBat does/doesn’t translate into multi-metric gains, guiding future agents away from or toward such preprocessing."
thinking_content: '**Evaluating risk analysis and transformation plans**


  I''m thinking about conducting a deep risk analysis regarding how BRS might distort distributions across genes and possibly apply identical distributions across batches. This makes me wonder if ComBat could become redundant, so I need to clarify how they might work together. I also want to provide a plan for applying transformations either after or before ComBat, but I''ll stick to the initial plan of pre-ComBat. Lastly, I should ensure fairness by fixing HVG to seurat_v3 and considering varying HVG sizes of 1000 versus 2000.**Interpreting results with minimal changes**


  I''m aiming for minimal changes in my approach. First, I want to interpret the results: if BRS increases ASW_batch and iLISI while NMI declines, it suggests label compression, so I might consider using λ=0.9 or MIQM instead. If MIQM improves NMI but not ASW_batch, this could indicate a lower ρ. Also, I need to pay attention to computing times and memory. Finally, for the report, I’ll present a baseline EID, three new ideas, and detail the synthesis with SOTA. We''ve already addressed the "three new ideas" requirement with our existing set.**Planning next steps for experiments**


  That sounds straightforward! I want to summarize the plan for moving forward after reflection tick 5. I''ll focus on running the missing variants: BRS with λ=1.0 and 0.9, MIQM with ρ=0.75 and 0.5, and Winsor with q=0.01 and 0.005. We''ve already submitted the baseline and our first two ideas in code, but we still need to run the Winsor experiment. So, it makes sense to plan for implementing the Winsor variant in our next experiments.**Justifying theoretical foundations and exploration**


  I won''t implement changes yet since the room is for reflection. First, I need to provide the theoretical justification: starting with log-normalization approximates log counts, leading to a distribution that mimics log-NB heavy tails. Rank-to-normalization results in a Gaussian distribution, consistent with ComBat''s normal residual assumption, suggesting synergy. However, I need to caution that BRS alters the distribution of zero counts, mapping log1p zeros to negatives while maintaining their position post-normalization. I expect BRS to perform best, with MIQM close behind and Winsor minimal. Lastly, I must ensure we evaluate the HVG correctly and implement code-level guardrails as needed.'
