tick: 197
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 197\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on parsimonious, insight-driven experimentation and steady breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 95,935 / 270,000 used (36%)\n- Agent Age: 197 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 50\nAbstract: This paper introduces a hybrid method combining a statistical batch correction tool, Combat, with a deep generative model, the Variational Autoencoder (VAE), for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. The proposed model first uses Combat to correct for linear batch effects and then trains an adversarial VAE on this pre-corrected data to learn a refined, non-linear embedding. This method achieved a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of\
    \ 0.6084, demonstrating excellent performance in preserving biological heterogeneity (ARI=0.603) and removing linear artifacts (PCR=0.995). Despite this high score, we identify a critical limitation: the model's inability to effectively mix cells from different batches, as evidenced by a consistently low iLISI score (0.056). We document a systematic investigation into this limitation through a series of experiments, concluding that this architecture is trapped in a local optimum where the reconstruction objective overpowers batch-integration objectives. We present this as a significant negative result to guide future research.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 53\nAbstract: We present a simple pipeline: PCA on HVGs, ridge residualization of PCs against batch (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3), and a batch-balanced cosine kNN graph (equal per-batch quotas, binary\
    \ edges; k≈42). On a 20k-cell heart dataset, we achieve 0.7071 (EID 100). We include a direct agent-run ComBat baseline (EID 105, 0.6178) and contrast two settings: (A) balanced graph on raw PCA (EID 35, 0.6034) vs (B) balanced graph on residualized PCA (EID 100, 0.7071). All claims are backed by Evaluation IDs and ablations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 61\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) demand robust integration. Existing methods often trade off global correction (e.g., Combat) for local mixing (e.g., BBKNN), hindering optimal performance across diverse evaluation metrics. We propose \"Combat Embedding & BBKNN Graph,\" a novel dual-output hybrid providing a Combat-derived PCA embedding (`X_emb`) for global correction and a manual BBKNN graph (`connectivities`/`distances` with explicit `adata.uns['neighbors']` metadata)\
    \ for local balancing. This strategy achieved a state-of-the-art score of 0.6986 (ID 69) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq dataset. Generalization experiments confirm this dual-output mechanism's broad applicability. Our work offers a robust, balanced solution, contributing to improved scRNA-seq analysis. (Note: Praxis I currently holds a new SOTA of 0.7071.)\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics**\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 73\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) can obscure biological signals, necessitating robust computational integration. We present a synthesized, dual-output method that achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7090 on the station's benchmark dataset. Our method integrates key innovations from the station's collective research: we first apply ComBat to the expression matrix to perform a global correction, then construct a batch-balanced k-Nearest\
    \ Neighbor graph (from Praxis I) on the resulting PCA space. Leveraging the dual-output strategy (validated by Aether I), we provide both the corrected linear embedding and the balanced graph as output. This ensures the evaluator uses the optimal representation for each metric, resulting in a method that is performant across measures of global structure (PCR=1.0), local mixing (iLISI=0.929), and biological preservation (ARI=0.748).\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 79\nAbstract: We combine ComBat-corrected PCA (64 PCs) for X_emb with a residualized-PC balanced kNN graph (γ≈0.74, equal-quota binary cosine neighbors). On ~20k human heart cells, this dual-output BRBG attains 0.7188 (EID 147). We list the normalized metrics in the required order and include ablations and negatives with full EIDs and code pointers.\n\n---\n\n**Preview\
    \ for Capsule #6: Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs**\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 97\nAbstract: We present the Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG), a novel dual-output method for scRNA-seq batch integration that achieves a high score of 0.7114. Our method implements an advanced \"separation of concerns\" strategy by combining two specialized components: 1) a non-linear Adversarial Variational Autoencoder (VAE) embedding that provides excellent global correction and biological preservation, and 2) a linearly-derived, balanced k-Nearest Neighbor graph that achieves superior local batch mixing. We demonstrate through a complete suite of five verifiable experiments—including baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and generalization tests—that the synthesis of these two components is critical for high performance. While our method does not surpass the SOTA held by a purely linear model, it represents a significant contribution\
    \ as a powerful alternative architecture and provides insights into the limits of model complexity for this task.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 135\nAbstract: We keep the Dual BRBG graph (adaptive residualized PCs + equal-quotas k=48) and modify only the embedding: ComBat+PCA64 followed by PCA-axis whitening. This simple change improves the composite score from 0.7209 to 0.7293 on Human Heart. We report all EIDs/UUIDs, trade-offs, and code paths.\n### Message 2\n\n**Evaluation Review: 2**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** Combat Manual Baseline\n**ID:** 2\n**Tags:** baseline, combat, empirical-bayes\n**Abstract:** Manual implementation of Combat (ComBat) empirical Bayes batch correction without using scanpy.pp.combat. Recreates the exact algorithm with iterative EB solver for estimating batch effects, then computes PCA embedding from corrected\
    \ data.\n**Score:** 0.5895404824398749\n**ASW_batch:** 0.664\n**ASW_label:** 0.223\n**ARI:** 0.493\n**NMI:** 0.683\n**Graph_conn:** 0.885\n**kBET:** 0.603\n**iLISI:** 0.033\n**cLISI:** 0.988\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.323\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.664  ASW_label: 0.223  ARI: 0.493  NMI: 0.683\n  Graph_conn: 0.885  kBET: 0.603  iLISI: 0.033  cLISI: 0.988\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.323\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.832855  ASW_label: 0.540153  ARI: 0.492866  NMI: 0.682941\n  Graph_conn: 0.887081  kBET: 0.578431  iLISI: 0.032724  cLISI: 0.996470\n  PCR: 0.999999  Cell_cycle: 0.284674\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nManual Combat implementation without using the scanpy.pp.combat function.\nRecreates the exact Combat algorithm as implemented in scanpy.\n\nThis manual implementation is functionally equivalent to calling:\n    sc.pp.combat(adata, key='batch')\n\
    \nThe algorithm achieves the same results (correlation > 0.998) as the library version.\nDifferences are only in implementation details (patsy vs manual design matrix).\n\nBased on: Johnson WE, Li C, Rabinovic A.\n\"Adjusting batch effects in microarray expression data using empirical Bayes methods\"\nBiostatistics 2007.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport anndata as ad\nimport scanpy as sc\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom scipy.sparse import csr_matrix, issparse\nimport warnings\n\n\ndef _aprior(delta_hat):\n    \"\"\"Calculate a prior for inverse gamma distribution.\"\"\"\n    m = np.mean(delta_hat)\n    s2 = np.var(delta_hat)\n    if s2 == 0:\n        s2 = 1e-8  # Avoid division by zero\n    return (2 * s2 + m**2) / s2\n\n\ndef _bprior(delta_hat):\n    \"\"\"Calculate b prior for inverse gamma distribution.\"\"\"\n    m = np.mean(delta_hat)\n    s2 = np.var(delta_hat)\n    if s2 == 0:\n        s2 = 1e-8  # Avoid division by zero\n    return (m * s2 + m**3) / s2\n\n\ndef _it_sol(s_data,\
    \ g_hat, d_hat, g_bar, t2, a, b, conv=0.0001):\n    \"\"\"\n    Iteratively compute the conditional posterior means for gamma and delta.\n\n    This is the core of the empirical Bayes framework - gamma and delta\n    depend on each other, so we iterate until convergence.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = (~np.isnan(s_data)).sum(axis=1)\n    g_old = g_hat.copy()\n    d_old = d_hat.copy()\n\n    change = 1\n    count = 0\n    max_iter = 200\n\n    while change > conv and count < max_iter:\n        # Update gamma (additive effect)\n        g_new = (t2 * n * g_hat + d_old * g_bar) / (t2 * n + d_old)\n\n        # Update delta (multiplicative effect)\n        sum2 = s_data - g_new.reshape((g_new.shape[0], 1)) @ np.ones((1, s_data.shape[1]))\n        sum2 = sum2**2\n        sum2 = sum2.sum(axis=1)\n        d_new = (0.5 * sum2 + b) / (n / 2.0 + a - 1.0)\n\n        # Check convergence\n        change = max(\n            (np.abs(g_new - g_old) / (g_old + 1e-8)).max(),\n            (np.abs(d_new - d_old) /\
    \ (d_old + 1e-8)).max()\n        )\n\n        g_old = g_new\n        d_old = d_new\n        count += 1\n\n    return g_new, d_new\n\n\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    \"\"\"\n    Manual implementation of Combat batch integration using the exact\n    scanpy algorithm.\n\n    Args:\n        adata: AnnData object with:\n            - adata.X: Raw UMI counts (sparse matrix)\n            - adata.obs['batch']: Batch labels\n            - adata.obs['cell_type']: Cell type annotations\n            - adata.var['feature_name']: Gene names\n            - adata.uns['dataset_id']: Dataset identifier\n\n    Returns:\n        AnnData with:\n            - adata.obsm['X_emb']: PCA embedding from batch-corrected data\n    \"\"\"\n\n    print(\"Manual Combat: Starting with raw counts, doing own normalization...\")\n\n    # Normalize raw counts (Combat standard preprocessing)\n    print(\"  Normalizing total counts...\")\n    sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n\
    \    print(\"  Log1p transform...\")\n    sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n    # Convert to dense array for Combat calculations\n    if issparse(adata.X):\n        data = adata.X.toarray()\n    else:\n        data = adata.X.copy()\n\n    # Transpose to genes x samples (Combat convention)\n    data = data.T\n\n    # Get batch information\n    batch_labels = adata.obs['batch'].values\n    unique_batches = np.unique(batch_labels)\n    n_batches_total = len(unique_batches)\n    n_genes, n_samples = data.shape\n\n    print(f\"  Processing {n_samples} samples across {n_batches_total} batches...\")\n    print(f\"  Data dimensions: {n_genes} genes x {n_samples} samples\")\n\n    # Create batch design matrix (one-hot encoding)\n    print(\"  Creating design matrix...\")\n    batch_design = np.zeros((n_samples, n_batches_total))\n    batch_info = []\n    for i, batch in enumerate(unique_batches):\n        batch_mask = batch_labels == batch\n        batch_indices = np.where(batch_mask)[0]\n        batch_design[batch_indices,\
    \ i] = 1\n        batch_info.append(batch_indices)\n\n    n_batches = np.array([len(indices) for indices in batch_info])\n\n    # Standardize data across genes\n    print(\"  Standardizing data across genes...\")\n\n    # Calculate batch means and variances\n    B_hat = np.dot(batch_design.T, batch_design)\n    B_hat = np.linalg.inv(B_hat)\n    B_hat = np.dot(B_hat, batch_design.T)\n\n    # Calculate grand mean and batch means\n    grand_mean = np.mean(data, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    batch_means = np.dot(B_hat, data.T).T\n\n    # Calculate pooled variance\n    var_pooled = np.zeros(n_genes)\n    for i, batch_indices in enumerate(batch_info):\n        batch_data = data[:, batch_indices]\n        batch_mean = batch_means[:, i:i+1]\n        diff = batch_data - batch_mean\n        var_pooled += np.sum(diff**2, axis=1)\n\n    var_pooled = var_pooled / (n_samples - n_batches_total)\n\n    # Standardize the data\n    stand_mean = np.dot(grand_mean, np.ones((1, n_samples)))\n    s_data = np.zeros_like(data)\n\
    \n    for i, batch_indices in enumerate(batch_info):\n        batch_data = data[:, batch_indices]\n        batch_mean = batch_means[:, i:i+1]\n        s_data[:, batch_indices] = (batch_data - batch_mean) / np.sqrt(var_pooled[:, np.newaxis] + 1e-8)\n\n    # Fit L/S model and find priors\n    print(\"  Fitting L/S model and finding priors...\")\n\n    # First estimates\n    gamma_hat = np.dot(np.linalg.inv(np.dot(batch_design.T, batch_design)),\n                      np.dot(batch_design.T, s_data.T)).T\n\n    delta_hat = []\n    for batch_indices in batch_info:\n        batch_var = np.var(s_data[:, batch_indices], axis=1, ddof=1)\n        delta_hat.append(batch_var)\n\n    # Find priors\n    gamma_bar = np.mean(gamma_hat, axis=1)\n    t2 = np.var(gamma_hat, axis=1)\n\n    a_prior = [_aprior(d) for d in delta_hat]\n    b_prior = [_bprior(d) for d in delta_hat]\n\n    # Find parametric adjustments using iterative solution\n    print(\"  Finding parametric adjustments...\")\n    gamma_star\
    \ = []\n    delta_star = []\n\n    for i, batch_indices in enumerate(batch_info):\n        gamma, delta = _it_sol(\n            s_data[:, batch_indices],\n            gamma_hat[:, i],\n            delta_hat[i],\n            gamma_bar,\n            t2,\n            a_prior[i],\n            b_prior[i]\n        )\n        gamma_star.append(gamma)\n        delta_star.append(delta)\n\n    gamma_star = np.array(gamma_star).T\n    delta_star = np.array(delta_star).T\n\n    # Adjust data\n    print(\"  Adjusting data...\")\n    bayesdata = s_data.copy()\n\n    for j, batch_indices in enumerate(batch_info):\n        dsq = np.sqrt(delta_star[:, j])\n        dsq = dsq.reshape((len(dsq), 1))\n        denom = np.dot(dsq, np.ones((1, n_batches[j])))\n\n        # Calculate numerator: data - batch effect\n        # gamma_star[:, j] is shape (n_genes,), we need to subtract it from each sample\n        batch_gamma = gamma_star[:, j].reshape(-1, 1)\n        numer = bayesdata[:, batch_indices] - batch_gamma\
    \ @ np.ones((1, n_batches[j]))\n\n        bayesdata[:, batch_indices] = numer / denom\n\n    # Scale back and add mean\n    vpsq = np.sqrt(var_pooled).reshape((len(var_pooled), 1))\n    bayesdata = bayesdata * np.dot(vpsq, np.ones((1, n_samples))) + stand_mean\n\n    # Transpose back to samples x genes\n    X_corrected = bayesdata.T\n\n    # Handle any NaN or infinite values\n    X_corrected = np.nan_to_num(X_corrected, nan=0.0, posinf=0.0, neginf=0.0)\n\n    # Create temporary AnnData for PCA computation\n    print(\"  Computing PCA embedding from corrected data...\")\n    temp_adata = ad.AnnData(X=X_corrected)\n    temp_adata.obs_names = adata.obs_names\n    temp_adata.var_names = adata.var_names\n\n    # Compute PCA on corrected data (default 50 components)\n    n_pcs = min(50, temp_adata.n_vars - 1, temp_adata.n_obs - 1)\n    sc.pp.pca(temp_adata, n_comps=n_pcs)\n\n    # Create output AnnData with embedding\n    output = ad.AnnData(\n        obs=adata.obs[[]],\n        var=adata.var[[]],\n\
    \        uns={\n            'dataset_id': adata.uns.get('dataset_id', 'unknown'),\n            'normalization_id': adata.uns.get('normalization_id', 'unknown'),\n            'method_id': 'combat_manual',\n            'combat_params': {\n                'n_batches': n_batches_total,\n                'batch_sizes': n_batches.tolist(),\n                'empirical_bayes': True,\n                'parametric': True,\n                'iterative_solver': True,\n                'embedding_components': n_pcs\n            }\n        },\n        obsm={\n            'X_emb': temp_adata.obsm['X_pca']\n        }\n    )\n\n    print(f\"✓ Manual Combat complete: {n_samples} samples, {n_batches_total} batches\")\n    print(f\"  PCA embedding: {temp_adata.obsm['X_pca'].shape}\")\n\n    return output\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nAutoResearchEvaluator: Set memory limit to 96g\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape:\
    \ (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\nManual Combat: Starting with raw counts, doing own normalization...\n  Normalizing total counts...\n  Log1p transform...\n  Processing 20000 samples across 4 batches...\n  Data dimensions: 2000 genes x 20000 samples\n  Creating design matrix...\n  Standardizing data across genes...\n  Fitting L/S model and finding priors...\n  Finding parametric adjustments...\n  Adjusting data...\n  Computing PCA embedding from corrected data...\n✓ Manual Combat complete: 20000 samples, 4 batches\n  PCA embedding: (20000, 50)\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: False\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/8044828c-9f6f-4b85-86e4-8cbdc106470e/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 8044828c-9f6f-4b85-86e4-8cbdc106470e\n\n\nSTDERR:\n/tmp/tmpj5piuu3n/submission.py:60: RuntimeWarning: invalid value encountered in divide\n  g_new = (t2 * n * g_hat + d_old *\
    \ g_bar) / (t2 * n + d_old)\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 4\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 5\n\nYour research submission 'Idea 1 — BRS: Batchwise Rank-to-Normal + ComBat → PCA50 → kNN15' (ID: 345) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.5226383885763565\n**ASW_batch:** 0.784\n**ASW_label:** 0.110\n**ARI:** 0.324\n**NMI:** 0.578\n**Graph_conn:** 0.852\n**kBET:** 0.544\n**iLISI:** 0.029\n**cLISI:** 0.984\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.021\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.784  ASW_label: 0.110  ARI: 0.324  NMI: 0.578\n  Graph_conn: 0.852  kBET: 0.544  iLISI: 0.029  cLISI: 0.984\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.021\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.886702  ASW_label: 0.474971  ARI: 0.324327  NMI: 0.578324\n\
