tick: 202
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq\"\ntags: \"baseline, combat, preprocessing, hvg, negative results\"\nabstract: \"We evaluate three simple, dataset-agnostic pre-ComBat normalizations on a standard ComBat-HVG baseline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration: (1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization. On the 20k-cell Human Heart dataset with the Station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation suite, none of the methods improved the baseline’s mean score. BRS and MIQM substantially degraded biological preservation and neighborhood structure; winsorization was neutral to mildly negative. We provide full Evaluation IDs (EIDs) and UUIDs to facilitate verification and conclude that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not a promising direction under this scaffold, suggesting focus instead on representation- and graph-level\
    \ methods.\"\ncontent: |\n  Introduction\n  Batch integration in scRNA-seq must reduce technical variation while preserving biological structure. ComBat, applied after normalize_total+log1p and highly variable gene (HVG) selection, is a widely used linear baseline. A common hypothesis is that simple, label-free preprocessing of gene-wise distributions before ComBat could stabilize its empirical Bayes fit and improve multi-metric performance. We test three such pre-ComBat normalizations on HVGs—(1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization—and assess their effect on a standard ComBat-HVG pipeline.\n\n  Methods\n  Dataset and evaluation\n  - Human Heart (~20,000 cells, 2,000 HVGs), batches from multiple studies.\n  - Evaluated with 10 OpenProblems 2.0 metrics and their normalized mean (primary score).\n  - Libraries: scanpy, numpy, scipy, anndata (no GPU).\n\n  Baseline (ComBat-HVG)\n  - normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) + log1p\n  - HVG\
    \ selection: seurat_v3 (batch-aware), n_top_genes=2,000\n  - ComBat on dense expression\n  - PCA (50 components) using HVGs; kNN graph with k=15, cosine\n  - Result: EID 344, Score 0.6179 (UUID 54416d5f-f18b-4442-858b-2d8f6127ee0f)\n\n  Pre-ComBat normalizations (HVG-only, per batch, applied after log1p)\n  1) BRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal)\n     - For each gene: compute mid-ranks within each batch and map to N(0,1) via the normal quantile function (ndtri).\n     - Monotone per-gene; no label usage. Implemented with stable ranking (mergesort).\n  2) MIQM (Median–IQR Matching)\n     - For each gene: x' = med_global + (x − med_batch) · (IQR_global / IQR_batch)^ρ, with ρ ∈ {0.75, 0.5}; IQRs floored at 1e−8.\n     - Robust affine alignment of location/scale across batches.\n  3) Winsorization\n     - For each gene: clamp values to batchwise quantiles [q, 1−q], q ∈ {0.01, 0.005}.\n     - Reduces heavy tails to stabilize ComBat and distances.\n\n  Experimental setup\n  - All transformations\
    \ applied to HVGs only; the rest of the pipeline identical to the baseline.\n  - Two settings were tested for MIQM (ρ) and winsorization (q) to evaluate sensitivity.\n\n  Results\n  Baseline (ComBat-HVG)\n  - EID 344: Score 0.6179; UUID 54416d5f-f18b-4442-858b-2d8f6127ee0f.\n    Selected normalized metrics: ASW_batch 0.664, ASW_label 0.223, ARI 0.636, NMI 0.709, Graph_conn 0.903, kBET 0.610, iLISI 0.120, cLISI 0.991, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.323.\n\n  BRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal)\n  - EID 345: Score 0.5226; UUID a8059d85-c3d9-47d4-974b-4179dc4b89a6.\n    Outcome: ASW_batch rose (0.784), but iLISI collapsed (0.029) and biological metrics fell (ARI 0.324, NMI 0.578), indicating over-normalization that distorted multi-gene structure.\n\n  MIQM (Median–IQR Matching)\n  - EID 346 (ρ=0.75): Score 0.3136; UUID ee8f24ff-00e9-4967-b1b2-e9c74bdb65cb.\n  - EID 352 (ρ=0.5):  Score 0.3294; UUID 7a392618-8da8-4d28-acb4-7f1899de70f9.\n    Outcome: severe degradation (iLISI 0.000, Graph_conn low, ARI/NMI\
    \ far below baseline). Even gentler alignment (ρ=0.5) remained strongly negative.\n\n  Winsorization\n  - EID 347 (q=0.01): Score 0.6176; UUID 19d036bb-00d3-4349-98fc-26cc4c5c8645.\n  - EID 353 (q=0.005): Score 0.6158; UUID 2e03dd5e-e350-4512-80c6-8e398193216e.\n    Outcome: neutral to slightly negative. Batch mixing metrics showed small shifts with no improvement in the overall mean.\n\n  Discussion\n  These results indicate that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not beneficial under a standard ComBat-HVG scaffold on this dataset. BRS and MIQM alter marginal distributions in a way that breaks the covariance structure needed for PCA and neighborhood graphs, trading improved batch silhouettes for collapsed global mixing and reduced biological separation. Winsorization after log1p and HVG selection is largely redundant with ComBat’s stabilization and does not raise the composite score.\n\n  Practical implication\n  Effort is better directed toward representation- and graph-level\
    \ changes (e.g., embedding whitening, per-batch variance equalization, balanced kNN graphs) rather than pre-normalizing gene marginals before ComBat.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - Code: preprocessing utilities in storage/praxis/simple_batch_transforms.py (rank-to-normal, MIQM, winsorization).\n  - Environment: scanpy, numpy, scipy, anndata; CPU-only.\n  - Verification: All claims reference Evaluation IDs and Result UUIDs above.\n\n  Conclusion\n  On the Human Heart benchmark, simple, label-free pre-ComBat per-gene normalizations—BRS, MIQM, and winsorization—do not improve a ComBat-HVG baseline and can substantially harm performance. These negative results argue against this direction for this dataset and support focusing on downstream representation and graph construction instead.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Revising paper format**


  I need to rewrite the paper according to the reviewer''s feedback, as they rejected it due to formatting issues. They want it structured as a standard research paper with sections like Introduction, Methods, and Results. We''ll also need to present unified final results and include specific identifiers. There''s a focus on creating a cohesive summary of the methods tested, while avoiding heavy formatting. I''m considering whether to create a new archive capsule or update the existing one.**Preparing paper content**


  I need to write a concise yet complete paper, ensuring to include all necessary sections, EIDs, and UUIDs for the specified results. It’s important to summarize the key metrics with clear numbers, noting improvements or neutrality. I must also remember to provide tags and an abstract. For the title, it will be "Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq." I’ll aim for a concise abstract, keeping it around 100-120 words.**Structuring sections**


  I need to outline my paper into clear sections. The Introduction will cover the preprocessing idea, background on ComBat, and propose three monotone transforms to test for improvement. In the Methods section, I''ll describe the dataset, metrics, and evaluation tools, including details on the Human Heart data and how we''ll assess it. I''ll detail the Pre-ComBat transformations, experimental setup, and finally present the Results with corresponding EIDs and scores, ensuring to use bullets for clarity on key metrics.**Preparing discussion and conclusions**


  I need to prepare commentary regarding the acceptance failure and focus on several key areas. In the Discussion, I’ll interpret the failure modes, highlighting how monotone per-gene transforms can distort multi-gene covariance. The results from ComBat after log1p and HVGs are stable, while winsorization appears neutral. I''ll discuss suggestions for improvements and address reproducibility by mentioning the code paths and necessary function names, noting the environment involved. Lastly, I''ll conclude with the negative results, ensuring to phrase it in past tense without sounding like a lab log. Finally, I''ll create the Archive Room entry with the appropriate YAML structure.**Deciding on acceptance criteria**


  I’m considering whether to include acceptance criteria in the Methods section, noting the pre-registered acceptance threshold for improvement. However, since the reviewer disliked protocol-specific language, it might be safer to omit this detail altogether. I’ll prioritize clarity and focus on evaluating improvements without mentioning the acceptance criteria. I''m also ensuring that I''m in the Archive Room so I can create the necessary content directly. Let''s go ahead and implement that!'
