parts:
- text: "Strong result: EtC-BRBG with PCA60 → ComBat on PCs scores 0.7328 (EID 391) and 0.7327 (k=45, EID 393), improving our prior 0.7314 and close to SOTA 0.7355. Proceeding with two focused ablations to try to close the gap: PCA64 and soft-whitening after ComBat in PC space.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log_addendum_etc_ablate.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # EtC-BRBG Ablations: PCA dimension and soft-whitening\n  Tick: 221\n\n  Baseline EtC-BRBG:\n  - EID 391 (PCA60, k=48): 0.7328 (UUID 6b1fe390-...)\n  - EID 393 (PCA60, k=45): 0.7327 (UUID 9b790627-...)\n\n  Next tests:\n  - PCA64 (keep graph k=48): may recover small variance beyond 60 PCs.\n  - Soft-whiten after ComBat in PC space (tau=0.95): isotropize embedding axes; hypothesis: slight kBET/ASW_batch gains without hurting ARI/NMI.\n\n  Success criterion: ≥0.7335 with ARI/NMI unchanged (±0.001) vs EID 391.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"EtC-BRBG: PCA64 → ComBat on PCs (Embedding) + Residualized\
    \ Balanced Graph (k=48)\"\ntags: \"embed-then-correct, pca64, combat, residualization, balanced-knn, cosine\"\nabstract: \"Embedding path: normalize/log1p → HVGs → PCA(64) → ComBat on PCs (EtC). Graph path: residualized PCA50 on HVGs with adaptive gamma and equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary). Tests higher PC dimension under EtC-BRBG.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n\
    \      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      Zhat = B @ W\n      return Zhat\n\n  def _build_balanced_knn(Z: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, k_total=48, metric='cosine'):\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      k_pb = max(1, int(np.ceil(k_total / max(1, uniq.size))))\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0:\n              continue\n          k = min(k_pb + 1, idx_train.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k,\
    \ metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_train[inds]\n          for i in range(n):\n              g = neigh_global[i]; di = dists[i]\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask][:k_pb]; di = di[mask][:k_pb]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n      from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows += [i, j]; cols += [j, i]; dist_data += [d, d]\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n\
    \      C = D.copy(); C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C.tocsr(), D.tocsr()\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      n_pcs_emb = 64\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      ad_emb = adata.copy()\n      ad_emb.var['highly_variable'] = hv\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Z = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc,\
    \ key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = ad_pc.X\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      Cg, Dg = _build_balanced_knn(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n      if Cg is not None and Dg is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"EtC-BRBG + Soft-Whiten Embedding (tau=0.95): PCA60 → ComBat PCs → Whitening\"\ntags: \"embed-then-correct,\
    \ pca60, combat, whitening, residualization, balanced-knn\"\nabstract: \"Embedding path: PCA(60) on HVGs → ComBat on PCs → soft-whitening in PC space with tau=0.95, then neighbors. Graph path unchanged (residualized PC50 + equal-quotas k=48). Tests if mild isotropization post-ComBat improves composite metrics.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n \
    \ def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      Zhat = B @ W\n      return Zhat\n\n  def _build_balanced_knn(Z: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, k_total=48, metric='cosine'):\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      k_pb = max(1, int(np.ceil(k_total / max(1, uniq.size))))\n      pairs_min_dist = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0:\n              continue\n          k = min(k_pb + 1, idx_train.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n\
    \          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k, return_distance=True)\n          neigh_global = idx_train[inds]\n          for i in range(n):\n              g = neigh_global[i]; di = dists[i]\n              mask = g != i\n              g = g[mask][:k_pb]; di = di[mask][:k_pb]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                  prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                  if (prev is None) or (d < prev):\n                      pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = float(d)\n      from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows += [i, j]; cols += [j, i]; dist_data += [d, d]\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy(); C.data[:] = 1.0\n\
    \      return C.tocsr(), D.tocsr()\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      tau = 0.95\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # EtC embedding: PCA(60) → ComBat on PCs → soft-whitening\n      ad_emb = adata.copy()\n      ad_emb.var['highly_variable'] = hv\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Z = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n\
    \      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      E = ad_pc.X\n      # center and soft-whiten\n      Ec = E - E.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      C = (Ec.T @ Ec) / max(1, Ec.shape[0] - 1)\n      S, U = np.linalg.eigh(C); S = np.maximum(S, 0.0)\n      S_pow = np.power(S + 1e-8, -0.5 * tau)\n      T = (U * S_pow) @ U.T\n      X_emb = Ec @ T\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = X_emb\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph path unchanged\n      Xg = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      Cg, Dg = _build_balanced_knn(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\
    \ k_total=k_total, metric=metric)\n      if Cg is not None and Dg is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Recording research results**\n\nI need to respond with the latest updates. At Tick 221, we've got results for EtC-BRBG: scores of 0.7328 and 0.7327, which are close to Daedalus II's 0.7355 and above our last baseline of 0.7314. The user hasn't asked a question, but I think I should log these results in our research log and possibly consider publishing an addendum or paper. Although the station is in stagnation, we have improved relative to our prior baseline. Now, I want to record these metrics and explore additional options for ablation testing.**Exploring improvement options**\n\nDaedalus has shown that using 60 is effective, but testing 62 or 64 may not be necessary. I'm considering another ablation: instead of PCA HVG for computing EtC, what if we tried using full features? However, I'm concerned about memory and time.\n\nSince the SOTA is 0.7355, I wonder if we can go beyond that. Pushing for better embedding mixing while maintaining ARI/NMI is key. Historically,\
  \ whitening helped, but since EtC uses ComBat on PCs, perhaps we can add mild whitening afterward. \n\nI also need to think about graph path improvements. Earlier gains came from using PBVE and whitened embedding, but we left the graph path as residualized PC50 with balanced kNN. Maybe we can combine that with our PBVE method, adjusting for variance in ComBat PC embedding? I worry that might overcorrect, but proposing a mild alpha of 0.3 could be a good start.**Considering optimization strategies**\n\nI’ve previously tried a similar technique with whitened embedding, and I'm thinking about \"soft whitening\" alongside ComBat PCs to equalize anisotropy, though EtC is already balanced. \n\nI could test using k=48 versus 45, but we’ve already checked that with no noticeable difference. For the graph metric, I remember that cosine worked better, and we're currently using cosine, so that’s good!\n\nEarlier, we found binary quotas to be better, and I wonder if I should adjust gamma_max for residualization—previously,\
  \ the best was 0.74, but maybe with EtC, it could go down to 0.72 or keep lambda at 0.9. Since our residualization is on graph PCs and hasn’t changed, I might look into testing lambda for further optimization.**Planning publication strategy**\n\nI don't think we need to take any immediate action, but since we have new results that are nearly SOTA, it might be time to publish an addendum or a new paper acknowledging that \"EtC-BRBG replicates and is slightly below SOTA.\" There are minor differences noted, like our pipeline yielding a score of 0.7328.\n\nWe could consider writing a short Archive reply about our previous or new paper, though it seems we can only respond to our own archive capsules. However, creating a new archive capsule to summarize our replication and incremental differences might be an acceptable approach!**Planning next steps for publication**\n\nThe instructions mention breaking stagnation with new methods, and while replicating is allowed, publications usually require\
  \ novelty. I can present an analysis comparing EtC and whitened approaches, focusing on geometry metrics.\n\nFor the next steps, I’ll test n_pcs_emb=64 or 58 since 64 could be better. I remember that Daedalus found a difference between 60 and 40, so testing 64 might push us closer to a new SOTA. \n\nI'm also considering combining EtC embedding with PBVE, though that could be tricky. Additionally, trying soft whitening with tau=0.95 on EtC PCs might help; I need to keep in mind how this will affect the overall metrics. \n\nI’ll propose two micro tests: 1) EtC-BRBG with PCA64 and 2) EtC-BRBG with soft whitening. I’ll also log these in the research log and prepare for submission. I think we are on the right track!"
