parts:
- text: "EtC-BRBG is stable at 0.7328 (EIDs 391/405). Micro-ablations around it are neutral or slightly negative: PCA64 (0.7320), post-ComBat whitening (0.7148), gamma_max=0.75 (0.7326), lam=1.0 (0.7326), dataset batch_hvg (0.7266), PBVE-Emb α=0.2 (0.7291). PBVE-Emb α=0.3 ties baseline (0.7328). k=51 is neutral/slightly down (0.7325). I’ll publish a concise replication/ablation report and record lineage defaults.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"EtC-BRBG near-SOTA: Replication and Micro-Ablations on Human Heart\"\ntags: \"analysis, replication, ablation, embed-then-correct, brbg\"\nabstract: \"We replicate the EtC-BRBG pipeline (PCA on HVGs then ComBat on PCs for embedding, plus residualized balanced graph) and probe micro-ablations. The best setting achieves 0.7328 (vs SOTA 0.7355). PCA64, post-ComBat whitening, residualization strength tweaks, dataset batch_hvg, and degree changes are neutral or slightly negative; gentle PBVE in PC space\
    \ is neutral. We release EIDs and guidance for future work.\"\ncontent: |\n  Summary\n  - Best (replicated): EtC-BRBG with PCA60 → ComBat on PCs for embedding; graph: residualized PCA50 (gamma_max=0.74, lam=0.9), equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n  - Score: 0.7328 (EIDs 391, 405), close to SOTA 0.7355 (EID 378).\n\n  Key Results (EIDs; normalized mean score)\n  - Baseline EtC-BRBG PCA60, k=48: 0.7328 (EID 391; UUID 6b1fe390-...).\n  - Degree k=45: 0.7327 (EID 393; UUID 9b790627-...).\n  - PCA64: 0.7320 (EID 395; UUID 03f50f17-...).\n  - Post-ComBat whitening (tau=0.95): 0.7148 (EID 396; UUID bbb0f92c-...).\n  - gamma_max=0.75: 0.7326 (EID 399; UUID b1a13e99-...).\n  - lam=1.0 (gamma_max=0.74): 0.7326 (EID 404; UUID 11fec58a-...).\n  - Dataset batch_hvg: 0.7266 (EID 402; UUID cde2a501-...).\n  - PBVE-Emb α=0.3 (PC post-ComBat): 0.7328 (EID 405; UUID b61fe53f-...).\n  - PBVE-Emb α=0.2: 0.7291 (EID 407; UUID 926e1668-...).\n  - Degree k=51: 0.7325 (EID 409; UUID 3e9d8748-...).\n\
    \n  Metric profile\n  - ARI/NMI, iLISI, cLISI: consistently strong and stable across ablations.\n  - Graph_conn ≈ 0.946 and Cell_cycle ≈ 0.352 remain the main deficits vs SOTA (≈0.966 and ≈0.372).\n  - kBET/iLISI respond weakly to micro-tweaks; whitening PC-embedding overshoots and harms ASW_label/Cell_cycle.\n\n  Takeaways\n  - The EtC-BRBG ridge is flat around PCA60 with k ∈ {45, 48, 51}. PCA64, stronger residualization (gamma_max/lam), and batch_hvg do not help.\n  - Gentle variance harmonization in PC space (PBVE-Emb α=0.3) is neutral (slight +kBET, same mean).\n  - Remaining gap to SOTA likely lies in embedding-side geometry beyond simple isotropization or in alternative graph constructions that improve Graph_conn without harming ARI/NMI.\n\n  Guidance for successors\n  - Freeze default: PCA60 → ComBat(PCs); residualized PCA50 graph (gamma_max=0.74, lam=0.9), balanced kNN k=48 cosine.\n  - Avoid: post-ComBat whitening (tau≈0.95), PCA64, dataset batch_hvg for this task.\n  - Promising\
    \ future directions: label-free multi-set CCA/GCCA in PC space; batch-contrastive graph augmentation that improves Graph_conn specifically; analytic study of why EtC stabilizes ComBat (EB estimates) and yields a flatter optimum.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Praxis I: EtC-BRBG default + lessons\"\ncontent: |\n  Default (freeze):\n  - Embedding: normalize/log1p → HVGs(seurat_v3) → PCA60 → ComBat on PCs → X_emb.\n  - Graph: PCA50 on HVGs → adaptive residualization (gamma = min(0.74, 0.9·R²)) → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n  - Best score: 0.7328 (EIDs 391/405).\n\n  Lessons:\n  - PCA60 is a sweet spot; PCA64 neutral/slightly worse.\n  - Post-ComBat whitening harms (0.7148).\n  - gamma_max=0.75 and lam=1.0 are neutral; keep 0.74/0.9.\n  - dataset batch_hvg underperforms recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs here.\n  - Gentle PBVE in PC space (α=0.3) is neutral (slight +kBET), α=0.2 regresses.\n  - Degree k=48 is robust;\
    \ k=45/51 are ≈.\n\n  Gaps to close:\n  - Graph_conn (~0.946) and Cell_cycle (~0.352) trail SOTA; ARI/NMI/iLISI/cLISI already strong.\n\n  Next research ideas (for successors):\n  - Label-free GCCA-style shared-subspace alignment in PC space prior to ComBat, with one scalar regularizer.\n  - Batch-contrastive graph construction that increases Graph_conn while preserving ARI/NMI (e.g., local stitches without oversmoothing).\n  - Theoretical note: why ComBat-in-PC stabilizes EB and yields a flat ridge; analyze per-PC batch predictability after EtC.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring Graph connectivity**\n\nI'm considering using neighbors total in sc.pp.neighbors for embedding, setting it to 15, which seems constant. The main Graph_conn is based on obsp connectivities, and it shows consistent normalization around 0.946 across runs, not significantly improved by k. Maybe we could adjust equal quotas to ensure better connectivity, perhaps by adding more within-batch edges. There’s also the option of creating an archive capsule with SOTA results for publishing. Ultimately, we might decide to freeze the config and prepare a more thorough paper, since simpler changes don’t seem sufficient.**Planning contributions and experiments**\n\nI'm looking at various contributions, including thorough ablations that confirm a robust plateau and negative results from modifications. This could become an \"EtC-BRBG replication and micro-ablation study,\" though it may not feel particularly novel, it's still valuable for analysis. We might need to log the agent's\
  \ actions and produce a response. I could either create an Archive reply about the replication study or summarize progress in Private Memory while proposing more experiments with PBVE-Emb. I wonder if testing k=45 could yield similar results to previous runs.**Exploring PCA and graph embedding**\n\nI'm considering using PCA with 60 dimensions but comparing cosine vs. Euclidean graph embeddings. Cosine is what we've been using, and previous tests showed Euclidean gave worse results under the whitened pipeline, so I think I’ll stick with cosine. The idea of increasing PCA dimensions to 62 seems minor, especially since 60 was deemed optimal in prior tests. We could refine our embeddings through per-PC shrinkage, but previous efforts with whitening and PBVE were neutral or harmful. Alternatively, we could keep using the same HVG as before, opting for Seurat v3 since it performed better.**Preparing publication submission**\n\nI think it's time to wrap up this work and publish an Archive addendum\
  \ or paper focused on our replication and the robust plateau, even if it's just shy of SOTA by about 0.0027. We could title it \"Near-SOTA replication and ablation study confirming results and negative modifications.\" The station rules encourage documenting negative results, which aligns with this effort. I’ll construct the submission capsule with relevant EIDs and tables of results while emphasizing key metrics, particularly Graph_conn and Cell_cycle deficits. I'll also suggest some theoretical reasoning about the impact of EtC ComBat and neighbor degrees on Graph_conn.**Proposing next steps for analysis**\n\nI'm thinking we should propose that \"EtC combined with BalancedGraph and PCA60 could yield a stable plateau.\" Future directions could explore embedding dimension interplay and RBC whitened with different tau values, although previous whitening efforts were harmful. It seems like per-batch mean alignment in PC space through ComBat already showed neutral variance equalization, indicating\
  \ we might need broader changes beyond minor micro-adjustments. \n\nNext, I’ll create an Archive Room capsule titled \"EtC-BRBG: Replication and Micro-Ablations Near-SOTA (0.7328)\" and include the necessary tags. I'll also document results in a Private Memory capsule, summarizing our identity and freezing the configuration. I’ll execute the necessary actions carefully to stay within limits."
