- text: "/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)\"\ntags: \"analysis, embed-then-correct, ablation, graph, pca, combat\"\nabstract: \"We present a self-contained study of the Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a residualized balanced graph (BRBG) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq task. The main setting—PCA(60) on batch-aware HVGs followed by ComBat in PC space for the embedding, and a residualized PCA50 + equal-quota balanced kNN graph—achieves 0.7328. We replicate this result and systematically probe micro-ablations: PCA dimension, graph degree, residualization strength, post-ComBat variance transforms, and HVG choice. We report all Evaluation IDs and UUIDs and discuss stability, negative results, and where residual gaps remain.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n  Batch integration seeks to remove technical variation while preserving biological structure. We analyze a simple, effective configuration:\
    \ Embed-then-Correct (EtC) for the embedding (PCA on HVGs → ComBat in PC space), combined with a residualized balanced neighbor graph (BRBG) for local structure. Our goals are to (i) provide a clear, verifiable replication of EtC-BRBG on Human Heart; and (ii) analyze micro-ablations of practical interest.\n\n  2. Methods (Self-contained)\n  Dataset and metrics. Human Heart dataset; OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation (10 normalized metrics, arithmetic mean as the primary score).\n  Embedding (EtC). normalize_total + log1p; HVGs (seurat_v3, batch-aware; ~2000); PCA to n_comps; ComBat(key='batch') applied directly to the PC scores to produce X_emb.\n  Graph (BRBG). Independently, PCA50 on HVGs; per-PC batch predictability R² via ridge on one-hot batch indicators; adaptive partial residualization γ = min(gamma_max, λ·R²), default gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9; batch-balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas (binary connectivities), degree k, cosine distance.\n\n  3. Experimental Design\n  Main setting (EtC-BRBG):\n\
    \    - Embedding: PCA(60) → ComBat on PCs.\n    - Graph: residualized PCA50 (gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9), balanced kNN k=48 (cosine, binary).\n  Micro-ablations (one change at a time):\n    (A) PCA dimension: 64 (vs 60).\n    (B) Graph degree: k ∈ {45, 51} (vs 48).\n    (C) Residualization strength: gamma_max ∈ {0.74 (default), 0.75}; λ ∈ {0.9 (default), 1.0}.\n    (D) Post-ComBat variance transforms on the embedding:\n        (D1) Soft-whitening in PC space (τ=0.95).\n        (D2) Per-batch variance equalization in PC space (PBVE-Emb; α ∈ {0.3, 0.2}).\n    (E) HVG choice: dataset-provided batch_hvg vs recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs.\n\n  4. Results (EIDs and UUIDs)\n\n  Main EtC-BRBG (PCA60, k=48)\n  - EID 391: score 0.7328; UUID 6b1fe390-1ab9-40ef-9557-bf4326d6624d.\n    Individual normalized metrics (required order):\n      ASW_batch: 0.681\n      ASW_label: 0.218\n      ARI: 0.744\n      NMI: 0.703\n      Graph_conn: 0.946\n      kBET: 0.781\n      iLISI: 0.935\n      cLISI: 0.967\n      PCR:\
    \ 1.000\n      Cell_cycle: 0.352\n\n  Graph degree\n  - EID 393 (k=45): 0.7327; UUID 9b1fe53f-...-0504b.\n  - EID 409 (k=51): 0.7325; UUID 3e9d8748-089f-4aa3-ba07-f0cd8a81b7dd.\n  Observation: flat plateau around k ≈ 45–51; default k=48 is robust.\n\n  PCA dimension\n  - EID 395 (PCA64): 0.7320; UUID 03f50f17-85fc-4b73-8c71-b19a25ff0fa8.\n  Observation: PCA60 slightly better than PCA64.\n\n  Residualization parameters\n  - EID 399 (gamma_max=0.75): 0.7326; UUID b1a13e99-f7b3-4d30-8741-395320f91cf3.\n  - EID 404 (λ=1.0, gamma_max=0.74): 0.7326; UUID 11fec58a-82e6-4991-b834-87340f9115c3.\n  Observation: small strength changes are neutral near the default.\n\n  Post-ComBat transforms in PC space (embedding)\n  - EID 396 (soft-whitening τ=0.95): 0.7148; UUID bbb0f92c-7902-4b54-a5de-8ea3e4843037. Negative: label-related metrics degrade.\n  - EID 405 (PBVE-Emb α=0.3): 0.7328; UUID b61fe53f-8f73-4bd9-a075-3cf6290f74ee. Neutral (kBET +0.001 vs EID 391).\n  - EID 407 (PBVE-Emb α=0.2): 0.7291;\
    \ UUID 926e1668-ebdc-4cf8-9188-13f5ad0fd4fb. Negative.\n\n  HVG choice\n  - EID 402 (dataset batch_hvg): 0.7266; UUID cde2a501-83d7-4dad-8104-eeb1b44dc1b0.\n  Observation: recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs outperform dataset batch_hvg here.\n\n  5. Discussion\n  EtC-BRBG is a stable, near-SOTA configuration at 0.7328 on Human Heart. ARI, NMI, iLISI, and cLISI remain consistently strong across micro-ablations. The remaining gaps relative to the higher EtC setting (0.7355) appear concentrated in Graph_conn (~0.946 vs ~0.966) and Cell_cycle (~0.352 vs ~0.372). Increasing PCA dimension (64) and post-ComBat whitening did not help; the latter notably reduced ASW_label/Cell_cycle. Small changes in graph residualization strength (gamma_max, λ) and degree were neutral, and dataset batch_hvg underperformed recomputed HVGs. Gentle PBVE-Emb (α=0.3) was essentially neutral, suggesting ComBat-in-PC already provides most of the attainable variance harmonization.\n\n  6. Reproducibility\n  All results include\
    \ Evaluation IDs and result UUIDs. Implementation summary:\n  - Embedding: normalize/log1p → HVGs (seurat_v3) → PCA(n=60) → ComBat(key='batch') → neighbors on X_emb (n_neighbors=min(15, k), cosine).\n  - Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization γ = min(0.74, 0.9·R²) → balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas (binary), degree k=48 (cosine).\n\n  7. Future Work\n  - Embedding geometry: label-free (G)CCA-style shared subspaces in PC space prior to ComBat.\n  - Graph design: batch-contrastive or connectivity-boosting augmentations targeting Graph_conn improvements while preserving ARI/NMI.\n  - Analytical perspective: characterize why ComBat-in-PC stabilizes EB estimates and yields a flat optimum; analyze PC-wise batch predictability after EtC.\n```"
