- text: "/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log_addendum_stitching_results.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Addendum Results: Minimal Component Stitching to Boost Graph_conn\n  Tick: 235\n\n  Goal\n  - Test whether adding a minimal number of cross-component edges after balanced kNN can raise Graph_conn without harming other metrics.\n\n  Base pipeline\n  - EtC-BRBG (Embedding: PCA60 → ComBat on PCs; Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization (gamma = min(0.74, 0.9·R²)) → equal-quotas balanced kNN k=48, cosine, binary).\n  - Baseline: EID 391 = 0.7328 (Graph_conn 0.946, Cell_cycle 0.352).\n\n  Stitching variants (1 edge per non-largest component)\n  - EID 414 (graph-space stitching; nearest in Zcorr): 0.7304\n      Metrics (normalized): ASW_batch 0.681, ASW_label 0.218, ARI 0.744, NMI 0.703,\n      Graph_conn 0.946, kBET 0.781, iLISI 0.935, cLISI 0.967, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.329\n      UUID: ee8f4934-4771-425f-b156-d01583d83dbb\n  - EID 415 (embedding-space stitching; nearest in X_emb):\
    \ 0.7271\n      Metrics: ASW_batch 0.681, ASW_label 0.218, ARI 0.744, NMI 0.703,\n      Graph_conn 0.946, kBET 0.781, iLISI 0.935, cLISI 0.967, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.297\n      UUID: 35101f33-4d34-407a-b578-dec4fe36a59c\n\n  Conclusion\n  - Graph_conn did not increase from 0.946; Cell_cycle decreased (notably in embedding-space stitching).\n  - Minimal component stitching is neutral-to-negative under EtC-BRBG on Human Heart. Line closed.\n\n  Note\n  - Likely, either the graph is already effectively connected for the metric definition, or the metric (label-wise connectivity) is insensitive to global stitches; added edges may act as weak long-range links, harming biology-related metrics.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum: Minimal graph component stitching is neutral-to-negative\n\n  We tested a minimal post-processing on the EtC-BRBG graph: after balanced kNN, add one cross-component edge from each non-largest component\
    \ to the largest component.\n\n  Results:\n  - Graph-space stitching (nearest in Zcorr): EID 414 = 0.7304 (UUID ee8f4934-...).\n  - Embedding-space stitching (nearest in X_emb): EID 415 = 0.7271 (UUID 35101f33-...).\n\n  In both cases, Graph_conn remained 0.946 (unchanged from baseline), while Cell_cycle decreased (notably for embedding-space stitches). Other metrics remained near baseline.\n\n  Interpretation:\n  - The connectivity metric here is likely insensitive to these global stitches (or the graph is already connected in the metric’s sense), and the added long-range edges introduce small mismatches that reduce Cell_cycle. This supports the conclusion that simple post-hoc stitching is not an effective path to close the Graph_conn gap under EtC-BRBG.\n\n  We therefore keep the standard EtC-BRBG configuration unchanged and recommend exploring more principled graph improvements (e.g., label-free, locality-preserving augmentations) in future work.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating research results**


  We need to respond properly with the research evaluation results on stitching. The scores for graph-space 414 and embedding-space 415 were lower than the baseline, meaning stitching didn’t improve our connectivity metric, which was already high. I wonder if this reflects only the average connectivity. Unfortunately, it seems stitching also negatively impacted cell cycle metrics, so I’ll log these results and consider stopping further runs while we reflect on new directions. The paper was accepted earlier, and now it’s crucial to close this line.**Preparing future actions**


  I might suggest increasing the cross-batch neighbor quota for future attempts, although it could degrade other metrics. We previously did Balanced Graph, so keeping things concise is essential given our verbosity settings. It’s also common to take action here. Though we’ve published, I could add an addendum to our Archive capsule summarizing the negatives of stitching, including updates in our storage log. Token usage is within budget, so no pruning is needed right now. I''ll prepare the research_log_addendum detailing EIDs 414 and 415, noting the metrics and drops.**Evaluating stitching results**


  The conclusion is clear: minimal stitching hasn’t been beneficial, so I propose we don’t proceed with it. We might want to check if the Graph was already a single connected component; if so, stitching wouldn’t contribute anything. Our Graph_conn remains at 0.946, which is interesting. It could suggest that the metric assesses label-wise connectivity instead of global connectivity, making arbitrary edge additions unhelpful and potentially worsening the cell cycle. Next, I’ll log details, including EIDs and UUIDs, and respond to Archive capsule 10 with a summary of these negative results.**Considering alternative approaches**


  I’m thinking of proposing a different method: using label-free "cross-batch nearest neighbor augmentation." Since we have equal quotas already, it seems within-label connectivity may not improve without labels. An alternative could be "distance-quantile constrained augmentation" to connect smaller components within batches while keeping local structure intact, but we can’t test that currently. For now, I’ll focus on logging everything and wrapping things up. I need to craft the necessary messages and use the /execute_action commands to finalize it.'
