- text: "Status: Stitching add-on was neutral-to-negative. EtC-BRBG remains our frozen near-SOTA default at 0.7328. Main deficits vs SOTA: Graph_conn (~0.946) and Cell_cycle (~0.352) prove resistant to small, label-free tweaks.\n\nNext step: brief reflection to plan truly new directions that target these two metrics without harming ARI/NMI.\n\n`/execute_action{goto reflect}`\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Focus: Why do Graph_conn (~0.946) and Cell_cycle (~0.352) resist change under EtC-BRBG? What structural changes could improve them without hurting ARI/NMI?\n  Consider:\n  - Whether Graph_conn is effectively saturated given our balanced kNN construction—does the metric depend on label-wise connectivity that is insensitive to global stitches?\n  - If embedding-side geometry must change (beyond ComBat-PCs) to shorten cross-type geodesics and boost connectivity without over-smoothing.\n  - Two label-free directions to prototype next:\n    1) Batch-shared subspaces (GCCA-style)\
    \ learned from per-batch PCs, then light ComBat in the shared space.\n    2) Local, distance-capped cross-batch edge replacement: replace only the farthest within-batch edge per node with the nearest cross-batch neighbor within a tight radius; enforce no long edges.\n  Deliverables: pick one minimal, implementable prototype; define safety checks (edge-length caps, degree preservation), success criteria (Graph_conn↑ with ARI/NMI stable), and fallbacks.\ntick: 5\n```"
thinking_content: '**Improving Graph_conn**


  I''m considering ways to enhance Graph_conn, maybe by increasing neighbor degrees. Previously, we tried a setting that decreased iLISI and NMI, which didn’t work out as hoped. One idea involves locality-preserving augmentation, where I’d add cross-batch neighbors but keep edges short to avoid unnatural connections. Alternatively, a batch-consensus graph could be computed, but that might not help either. I could also reconsider whether to use cosine or Euclidean metrics, though we’ve found Euclidean results to be worse.**Exploring Cross-Batch Boosting**


  I''m thinking about a new approach called "short-range cross-batch boosting." This would ensure each cell has at least q cross-batch neighbors within a specific radius. If not, we could fill in with edges from other batches. It seems our current quotas may already accomplish this, but they focus on ranking without considering distances fully. Earlier, I noticed that Graph_conn values were around 0.972 with previous embeddings, but with the EtC method, it dropped to 0.946. This might stem from changes in normalization, but I''d like to clarify the raw versus normalized scores here.**Analyzing Graph_conn Values**


  I''ve got normalized Graph_conn at 0.946, but the raw score shows 0.946639, down from an earlier 0.972. It seems like the graph connectivity is lower with the EtC method. There''s a chance that the graph-building algorithm uses 15 neighbors for embeddings, but Graph_conn itself comes from our custom connectivity graph, not that 15-neighbor version. To improve Graph_conn, I could increase k_total to 54 or 60, but I recall that k=51 had previously decreased the score. Another approach might be to experiment with a two-scale kNN graph again.**Exploring Symmetric Balancing and GCCA**


  I''m considering the idea of "symmetric balancing per batch pair," which would connect edges across all batch pairs, not just individual batches, though it might not make a difference. Another thought is to explore "HNSW approximate neighbors," which isn''t currently allowed. I could pivot to the label-free GCCA projection instead, computing PCA for each batch to find common variance directions. This approach would unify embeddings for graph building and is complex to implement. However, it could preserve biological data and enhance connectivity, even with memory risks.**Reflecting on Graph_conn and Future Steps**


  Implementing GCCA from scratch feels overwhelming, so I’m considering an approximation using Procrustes alignment of per-batch PCA. While earlier methods worked well, exploring new edge connections based on similarity in corrected embedding space might be beneficial. Currently, Graph_conn remains at 0.946, despite trying additional cross-batch neighbors and equal quotas. It seems the graph built earlier with a balanced method yielded a better score of 0.972. I think reflecting on these results and proposing a targeted plan for future experiments is the best next step.'
