tick: 236
role: model
parts:
- text: "Hypothesis and diagnosis\n\n- Why Graph_conn resists:\n  - The OpenProblems/SCIB-style Graph_conn typically evaluates connectivity within label-restricted subgraphs. Balanced kNN ensures cross-batch edges, but it doesn’t guarantee that same-label manifolds form a single connected component. If a label splits into well-separated subclusters (biological or technical), post-hoc global stitches won’t help because Graph_conn is insensitive to a few long-range edges, and those can even harm local biological structure (as seen by Cell_cycle drops).\n  - Our component stitching confirms this: Graph_conn stayed at 0.946, while Cell_cycle decreased, indicating the metric depends on local, label-consistent manifold continuity rather than global connectivity tweaks.\n\n- Why Cell_cycle resists:\n  - EtC (ComBat in PC space) likely reduces variance along cell-cycle axes indiscriminately across batches. Soft-whitening and aggressive harmonization further suppress these axes, which explains Cell_cycle\
    \ declines under those transforms. Since our best runs already preserve cluster geometry (ARI/NMI), recovering Cell_cycle requires preserving variance along specific PCs tied to cell-cycle programs without labels—a hard target under label-free constraints.\n\nWhat structural change could help without hurting ARI/NMI?\n\n- Embedding must make same-label cross-batch neighborhoods locally closer so that label-restricted subgraphs are more connected. This suggests changing geometry (not just edges) or making local cross-batch edges more “plausible” (shorter). Two label-free directions:\n\n  1) Batch-shared subspaces (GCCA-style) + light ComBat:\n     - Learn a shared latent space from per-batch PCs (equal-batch weighting; MAXVAR/GCCA proxy via SVD on concatenated, whitened per-batch PCs).\n     - Then apply light ComBat in this shared space. Goal: align global axes across batches so same-label across batches are closer locally, improving Graph_conn without over-smoothing (keep ARI/NMI).\n\
    \n  2) Local, distance-capped cross-batch edge replacement (conservative):\n     - Keep equal-quotas kNN to preserve mixing. For each node i and for each cross-batch neighbor j in its quota, if d(i,j) > τ·σ_i (σ_i = its k-th neighbor distance in the all-neighbor ranking or median neighbor distance), replace j with the nearest cross-batch neighbor within the cap if available; otherwise keep the original. Symmetrize replacements and enforce two-sided cap: also require d(i,j) ≤ τ·σ_j to avoid one-sided long edges.\n     - Intuition: balanced quotas sometimes inject far cross-batch edges in sparse regions; capping replaces only the longest cross-batch edges by more local ones, tightening local manifolds and potentially improving label-subgraph connectivity.\n\nPick one minimal, implementable prototype now\n\n- Choose (2) Local, distance-capped cross-batch edge replacement. It’s proximal to the current BRBG (edge-level change), label-free, and cheap.\n\nDesign sketch\n\n- Build the standard\
    \ BRBG graph on Zcorr (graph PCs), k_total=48 with equal per-batch quotas (binary).\n- Precompute per-node local scale σ_i = d_i(k_all), where k_all is the neighbor rank used in the initial neighbors() (e.g., 15) or use median of its k_total distances.\n- For each node i and each cross-batch neighbor j in its quota:\n  - If d(i,j) ≤ τ·σ_i (τ in {1.2, 1.3}), accept.\n  - Else, scan the precomputed cross-batch neighbor list (sorted) for the first candidate j′ with d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σ_i and d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σ_j′; if found and not already connected, replace (i,j) with (i,j′). Keep degree and per-batch quota unchanged.\n  - Symmetrize replacements; if symmetry would violate quotas, restrict to mutually acceptable replacements only.\n- If no valid replacement found, keep the original edge to preserve quotas and degree.\n\nSafety checks\n\n- Degree preservation (exactly k_total).\n- Per-batch quota preservation per node (no change).\n- Two-sided cap to avoid long one-way edges.\n- Symmetrization to avoid\
    \ dangling directed links.\n- Edge uniqueness (no duplicates); maintain CSR sparsity.\n\nSuccess criteria (vs EID 391 baseline)\n\n- Primary: Graph_conn increase by ≥ +0.003 (ideally +0.005), with ARI and NMI within −0.001 of baseline.\n- Secondary: Cell_cycle ≥ baseline (0.352), kBET/iLISI within ±0.003, cLISI unchanged (±0.002).\n- If τ=1.3 is neutral, try τ=1.2; if both neutral, close the line.\n\nFallback and next direction\n\n- If replacement is neutral/negative, close (2) and prototype (1) GCCA-lite:\n  - For each batch b: PCA50 → whiten.\n  - Concatenate whitened PCs with equal batch weights; SVD → top 60 shared components.\n  - Run light ComBat (standard ComBat; “light” denotes that shared space already aligns second-order stats) on the shared PCs.\n  - Use BRBG unchanged.\n  - Success criteria as above; if Graph_conn improves without ARI/NMI cost, refine by small d ∈ {56, 60, 64}."
thinking_content: '**Analyzing cell cycle metrics**


  I''m looking at the cell cycle metric measured by cell cycle conservation. Our pipeline results in a low score of 0.352 for cell-cycle signal, which isn''t great. I think overisotropizing can degrade the cell cycle. Maybe ComBat in the principal component space reduces variation, but I want to preserve that variation without over-correcting. I’m considering using non-residualization for principal components that heavily correlate with the cell cycle while keeping gene programs intact. It looks like I might be able to use gene sets defined in the system, but I need to tread carefully with the rules around cell type.**Examining dataset features**


  I''m analyzing a dataset that includes a specific feature name, but I’m cautious about using external gene sets since they might be considered external sources. I think it’s safer to avoid this and propose not using any labels or annotations, focusing instead on maintaining the variance distribution without over-harmonizing. In our pipeline, gamma residualization is applied only in the graph path. I''m also considering ComBat for PCA adjustments, but it’s tricky. I found that increasing PCA dimensions didn’t help significantly, so I''m suggesting local edge replacement in the graph connection for better results.**Assessing graph connectivity**


  I''m keeping the degree constant to ensure both symmetry and connectivity, while considering using mutual replacement as an option. The balanced quotas help maintain equal neighbors per batch, likely incorporating cross-batch edges. However, I see that a Graph connectivity score of 0.946 suggests some label-specific components are still disconnected. My local replacement approach aimed at cross-label edges might miss the mark since we can''t use labels. Instead, I think we can explore geodesic closeness in embedding space while being mindful of distance caps to avoid including overly long edges in our neighbor selection.**Refining edge replacement strategy**


  I think the metric likely expects within-label connectivity, but adding long edges won’t help the situation. The replacement approach I have in mind aims to ensure cross-batch edges fall within a local radius based on typical neighbor distances. We already select the nearest neighbors, but quotas per batch could lead to choosing distant neighbors if the batch is small. Perhaps I can limit connections beyond a certain percentile and assign those neighbors to the closest option in the largest batch. However, this might reduce cross-batch mixing metrics like iLISI, so I need to keep it simple with a minimal implementable plan focusing on local, distance-capped edge replacement.**Drafting edge replacement protocol**


  I want to build an initial balanced k-nearest neighbors (kNN) model. For each node, I’ll calculate sorted neighbor distances and define a local scale based on the k-th neighbor distance. For neighbors outside a set distance, I''ll mark them as "too long" and replace them with the nearest available neighbor from a different batch within this threshold. Maintaining long-term quotas while improving locality and connectivity is key, but it might be tricky without labels. I’ll consider an alternative approach to maximize correlations across batches through a shared subspace. For our next experiment, I’ll focus on minimal code changes to enhance the Graph connectivity metric while ensuring we implement safety measures about edge replacement criteria.'
