tick: 236
role: model
parts:
- text: "Design details for local, distance-capped cross-batch edge replacement\n\n- Working space and distances\n  - Operate in the BRBG graph-PC space Zcorr (PCA50 after adaptive residualization, cosine metric), where quotas were chosen. Using the same space avoids inconsistencies between selection and replacement.\n  - Keep binary edges; we only change which cross-batch edges are present, not their weights.\n\n- Local scale σi and cap τ\n  - σi definition: median of the k_total distances from i to its current graph neighbors (robust and per-node). Alternate (backup): the 15th-neighbor distance from the neighbors() call on X_emb; but prefer graph-local σi to align with the graph we are editing.\n  - τ grid: start with τ ∈ {1.3, 1.2}. Smaller τ = stricter locality; may fail to find replacements in sparse regions. If both are neutral, close the line.\n\n- Candidate pools and quotas\n  - For each node i and for each batch b ≠ batch(i), precompute a ranked list of cross-batch candidates cand(i,b)\
    \ using NearestNeighbors fitted per-batch (this is already available from the balanced kNN build; reuse the full ranking, not just the top quota).\n  - Current per-batch quota per node is q = ceil(k_total / n_batches) (e.g., 12 for 4 batches at k=48). Identify the current q cross-batch neighbors N(i,b).\n  - For each j ∈ N(i,b) with d(i,j) > τ·σi, scan cand(i,b) in order to find the first j′ not already in N(i,·) s.t. d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σi and d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σj′ (two-sided cap). If found, mark replacement j → j′.\n\n- Symmetry and conflicts\n  - Build a proposed replacement list R = {(i,j) → (i,j′)} over all nodes/batches.\n  - Symmetry rule: apply replacements only where both directions do not violate quotas and no duplicate edges are created. If both i and j′ propose each other it’s fine; if only one side proposes, still acceptable as long as degree and quotas remain satisfied after symmetrization.\n  - Conflict resolution: if two nodes want to add the same target j′ causing degree > k_total\
    \ for j′ after symmetrization, keep the request with smaller distance gain Δd = d_old − d_new; drop the rest.\n\n- Edge application and invariants\n  - Remove (i,j) and add (i,j′); update the undirected pair (i,j) and (j,i) consistently.\n  - Enforce invariants after all replacements:\n    - deg(i) = k_total for all i,\n    - per-batch quota satisfied for all i (exactly q per batch or as close as possible if a batch has fewer points),\n    - no self-loops or duplicates,\n    - two-sided cap satisfied for all replaced edges.\n\n- Complexity and feasibility\n  - Per-batch NN queries are already performed for balanced kNN; reusing the neighbor indices and distances avoids extra passes. Replacement scanning is O(k_total) per node worst-case. Overall linear in existing graph size; memory bounded by neighbor lists.\n\n- Diagnostics (label-free)\n  - Edge stats: fraction of cross-batch edges replaced; mean and 90th-percentile cross-batch edge length before/after; per-node max cross-batch edge\
    \ length distribution.\n  - Connectivity stats: number of connected components in the label-restricted graphs (proxy for Graph_conn), even if the metric is computed differently.\n  - Biological safety: compare ARI/NMI deltas; monitor cLISI, ASW_label; Cell_cycle relative change.\n  - Failure flags: proportion of nodes where no valid replacement exists for the longest edges (indicates τ too strict).\n\n- Risks and mitigations\n  - Sparse regions: strict τ may yield no replacements. Mitigation: fall back from 1.2 → 1.3; if still sparse, keep original edges (degree/quotas preserved).\n  - Asymmetry: two-sided cap reduces one-sided long jumps that can distort local structure.\n  - Over-editing: cap replacements per node (e.g., at most r = ceil(q/3) per batch) to avoid wholesale rewiring; start with r = 1 (only the single farthest cross-batch edge per batch is eligible).\n\n- Success and stop criteria\n  - Success: Graph_conn +0.003 (ideally +0.005) over EID 391 with ARI, NMI within −0.001;\
    \ Cell_cycle ≥ baseline; cLISI/iLISI within ±0.003.\n  - If τ=1.3 neutral or negative, try τ=1.2. If both neutral/negative, close the line and move to the GCCA-lite prototype."
