role: model
parts:
- text: "Refinement, theory, and concrete proto spec\n\nWhy Graph_conn and Cell_cycle resist under EtC-BRBG\n- Graph_conn: Balanced quotas ensure cross-batch edges, but the metric is label-wise (connectivity within label-induced subgraphs). If a label is split into multiple separated lobes (true biology or residual shifts), global stitches or indiscriminate long cross-batch links don’t merge those lobes locally; they may even create biologically implausible shortcuts, which explains unchanged Graph_conn and occasional Cell_cycle drops. The metric seems driven by local manifold continuity within (unobserved) labels, not global reachability.\n- Cell_cycle: EtC likely reduces variance along certain axes (including cell-cycle) uniformly; additional harmonization (whitening, aggressive PBVE) further damps those axes, lowering the Cell_cycle score. Since ARI/NMI are already strong, improving Cell_cycle without labels is hard; best we can do is avoid harming it while improving Graph_conn.\n\nMinimal,\
    \ implementable prototype (A): cluster-constrained, distance-capped cross-batch edge replacement\n- Intuition: Make cross-batch edges more locally plausible by (i) capping their length relative to each node’s local scale and (ii) only swapping to candidates within the same unsupervised cluster (Leiden on X_emb), which proxies label-consistency without labels.\n- Invariants: keep degree and per-batch quotas exactly; binary edges; symmetric undirected graph.\n\nAlgorithm sketch\n1) Unsupervised clusters on X_emb:\n   - neighbors on X_emb already exists (n_neighbors=15). Run Leiden (resolution ~0.5) to obtain cl[i] for each cell. Fix seed for determinism.\n2) Local scale per node: σ_i = median of the k_total (e.g., 48) distances to its current graph neighbors (Zcorr-space distances).\n3) For each node i and each non-self batch b:\n   - Let N(i,b) be the current quota-edges to batch b (size q).\n   - From the precomputed per-batch neighbor rankings (same used to build balanced kNN), construct\
    \ cand(i,b) with distances.\n   - Mark the farthest edge j ∈ N(i,b) as eligible if d(i,j) > τ·σ_i with τ ∈ {1.3, then 1.2 if needed}.\n   - Scan cand(i,b) to find j′ (not already connected) s.t.:\n     • d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σ_i AND d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σ_j′ (two-sided cap),\n     • cl[j′] == cl[i] (cluster-consistency),\n     • substituting j → j′ preserves quotas and degree.\n   - Limit to r=1 replacement per (i,b) to avoid over-editing.\n4) Collect proposed replacements R; resolve conflicts:\n   - If multiple i’s want the same j′ causing quota/degree overflow on j′, keep the proposal with largest distance gain Δd = d_old − d_new; drop others.\n5) Apply replacements symmetrically; assert invariants:\n   - deg(i) == k_total and per-batch quota per node preserved; no duplicates/self-loops; two-sided cap holds on all replaced edges.\n\nParameters and guards\n- τ grid: start 1.3 (looser), optionally 1.2 (stricter) only if neutral.\n- r=1 per (i,b).\n- Leiden resolution ~0.5; do not sweep unless replacements\
    \ are vanishingly rare; if so, adjust modestly (0.4–0.6).\n- If no valid replacement found for an eligible edge, keep the original edge (safe no-op).\n\nDiagnostics (label-free) to log\n- Fraction of cross-batch edges replaced; per-node max cross-batch distance (before/after); cluster-consistency rate of replacements.\n- Label-free proxies for Graph_conn: component counts in cluster-induced subgraphs (expect ↓).\n- Metric deltas target: Graph_conn +≥0.003; ARI/NMI within −0.001; Cell_cycle ≥ baseline; iLISI/kBET, cLISI within ±0.003.\n\nRisks and mitigations\n- Sparse regions: strict caps yield no replacements; τ=1.3 first, else 1.2; otherwise accept neutral outcome.\n- Over-restriction via cluster consistency: if clusters are too coarse/fine, replacements might be rare; tweak resolution slightly only if necessary.\n- Long asymmetric jumps: two-sided cap prevents one-way long edges.\n\nFallback prototype (B): GCCA-lite shared space + light ComBat\n- Goal: axis-level alignment across\
    \ batches so same-label, cross-batch pairs are closer locally (better label-wise connectivity) without smoothing clusters.\n- Procedure:\n  1) Per batch: PCA_r (r=50) on HVGs; center; whiten per PC by batch std.\n  2) Stack whitened PCs equally across batches; SVD → take d=60 shared directions; project each batch: Y_b.\n  3) Light ComBat on concat(Y_b) in shared space to remove residual mean/var shifts.\n  4) Use Y_combat as X_emb; keep BRBG graph unchanged.\n- Targets and guards: same deltas as A; if neutral, try d=56 once; else close.\n\nExecution plan\n- Implement A with τ=1.3, r=1, Leiden resolution 0.5; one run. If neutral/negative, a single follow-up at τ=1.2. Log diagnostics.\n- If A fails, implement B (d=60) once; if neutral, one follow-up at d=56; otherwise close.\n- Success threshold: Graph_conn uplift without ARI/NMI/Cell_cycle regressions; otherwise preserve frozen EtC-BRBG default.We don't need new experiments right now—this was a reflection tick. Here’s the concise plan\
    \ distilled from the analysis:\n\n- Diagnosis:\n  - Graph_conn is label-wise; balanced kNN ensures cross-batch mixing, but doesn’t guarantee connectivity within label manifolds. Global stitches are ignored by the metric and can hurt Cell_cycle.\n  - Cell_cycle drops when harmonization suppresses variance along cell-cycle axes; we should avoid further isotropization rather than try to “optimize” it without labels.\n\n- Minimal prototype to try first (A): cluster-constrained, distance-capped cross-batch edge replacement\n  - Where: edit the BRBG graph in Zcorr (graph PC space), leaving embedding untouched.\n  - How:\n    1) Unsupervised clusters: run Leiden on X_emb (neighbors already computed) at resolution ≈ 0.5 to get cl[i].\n    2) Local scale: σ_i = median of distances from i to its k_total graph neighbors.\n    3) For each node i and each non-self batch b:\n       - Let N(i,b) be current cross-batch quota-edges to batch b; mark the farthest edge j eligible if d(i,j) > τ·σ_i (τ=1.3).\n\
    \       - Scan the precomputed cross-batch candidate list cand(i,b) to find a j′ not yet connected such that:\n         • d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σ_i AND d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σ_j′ (two-sided cap),\n         • cl[j′] == cl[i] (cluster-consistency),\n         • replacing j → j′ preserves per-batch quota and degree.\n       - Limit to r=1 replacement per (i,b).\n    4) Resolve conflicts (prefer larger Δd = d_old − d_new), apply symmetrically, assert invariants: exact degree and quotas, no duplicates/self-loops, two-sided cap satisfied.\n  - Safety: If no valid j′ exists, keep the original edge; do not change embedding or quotas. If τ=1.3 is neutral/negative, a single follow-up at τ=1.2, then close.\n\n- Success criteria (vs EID 391 baseline, 0.7328):\n  - Graph_conn +≥0.003 (ideally +0.005),\n  - ARI, NMI ≥ baseline − 0.001,\n  - Cell_cycle ≥ baseline (0.352),\n  - iLISI, cLISI, kBET within ±0.003.\n\n- Fallback prototype (B): GCCA-lite shared subspace + light ComBat\n  - Per batch: PCA50 → center → whiten;\
    \ stack and SVD to get d=60 shared directions; project; run ComBat in shared space to produce X_emb; keep BRBG unchanged.\n  - One run at d=60, optional d=56 if neutral. Same success criteria; otherwise close.\n\n- Diagnostics to log (label-free):\n  - Fraction of cross-batch edges replaced; per-node max cross-batch edge length before/after; fraction of replacements passing cluster-consistency.\n  - Component counts in cluster-induced subgraphs (proxy for label-wise connectivity).\n  - Full metric deltas with EIDs/UUIDs; focus on Graph_conn and Cell_cycle.\n\nIf A and B both fail to lift Graph_conn without side effects, keep the frozen EtC-BRBG default (PCA60 → ComBat PCs; BRBG with gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9; k=48) and move on to more principled graph or embedding methods in future cycles."
thinking_content: '**Evaluating cell-cycle improvement**


  I''m thinking about improving cell-cycle analysis but need to preserve variance along gene sets. A label-free approach avoids residualization along principal components. I can compute cell cycle scores from known gene sets, yet rules complicate things since I can''t use cell-type info. The task says to stick to batch labels and gene expression, but does that mean external gene sets are off-limits? It''s risky to use those for cell cycle. I should really focus on avoiding harm to the cell cycle instead of trying to improve it.**Crafting Reflection Tick 4 content**


  I''m diving into creating content for Reflection Tick 4. Theoretical reasons point out that Graph_conn saturates because of equal quotas, yet label-wise connectivity remains disrupted by local label splits and harmful long cross-batch edges. We need local cross-batch edges that fit within cluster manifolds. Evaluating a cluster-constrained, distance-capped replacement means ensuring a symmetric undirected graph while keeping edges-per-batch constraints. For parameter selection, I''ll choose a resolution of 0.5 for replicability. I also need to log proportions replaced and potential pitfalls, like long edges reappearing if no suitable candidates are found.**Developing Reflection Content**


  I’m analyzing that earlier k sweeps seemed neutral. For B GCCA-lite, I need to provide more formulation details. Multi-view SVD will solve the sum of covariances, and whitening will ensure equal batch variance. This is equivalent to applying CCA to multiple views, so I’ve got to be careful about implementation and avoid using labels to unify everything. The complexity involves 20k x 2000, with PCA50 per batch, stacking batches down to dimension 50. I’ll monitor Graph_conn and check iLISI/kBET while ensuring ARI/NMI doesn''t drop. My plan is to implement A and then B if necessary.'
