parts:
- text: "Closing synthesis and next experimental plan\n\nDiagnosis\n- Graph_conn: Likely computed on label-induced subgraphs; balanced kNN ensures cross-batch mixing but doesn’t guarantee label-wise manifold connectivity. Global stitches add long links that the metric ignores (or penalizes indirectly), explaining unchanged Graph_conn and occasional Cell_cycle drops.\n- Cell_cycle: EtC (ComBat in PC space) already flattens some variance; further harmonization (whitening, PBVE) suppresses cell-cycle axes, lowering the score. Best course is to avoid harming it while seeking Graph_conn gains.\n\nChosen prototype (to run next): cluster-constrained, distance-capped cross-batch edge replacement\n- Goal: Make cross-batch edges more locally plausible, improving label-wise connectivity without over-smoothing.\n- Key ideas:\n  - Keep the embedding and quotas/degree fixed. Edit the BRBG graph in Zcorr (graph PCs after residualization).\n  - Use unsupervised clusters (Leiden on X_emb, resolution≈0.5)\
    \ as a label-free proxy to ensure replacements align with local manifold structure.\n  - Enforce a two-sided distance cap to avoid long asymmetric jumps and preserve locality.\n\nSpec (minimal, safe)\n- Inputs:\n  - BRBG graph: degree k_total=48, equal per-batch quotas, cosine distances in Zcorr, binary edges.\n  - Unsupervised clusters cl[i]: Leiden on X_emb with neighbors=15; fix seed.\n- Local scale: σ_i = median of distances to i’s current k_total neighbors (in Zcorr).\n- Replacement rule (per node i, per non-self batch b):\n  - Let N(i,b) be the q cross-batch edges to b (quota).\n  - Mark the farthest j∈N(i,b) eligible if d(i,j) > τ·σ_i with τ=1.3 (try 1.2 if neutral).\n  - Scan cand(i,b) (ranked cross-batch candidates from the original per-batch NN lists) to find j′ s.t.:\n    • d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σ_i and d(i,j′) ≤ τ·σ_j′ (two-sided cap);\n    • cl[j′] == cl[i] (cluster-consistency);\n    • j′ not already connected to i; per-batch quota and degree preserved with 1-for-1 replacement.\n\
    \  - Limit to r=1 replacement per (i,b).\n- Symmetry/conflicts:\n  - Collect proposed replacements; if multiple would overflow degree/quota on a target, keep the one with largest distance gain Δd=d_old−d_new; drop the rest.\n  - Apply symmetrically; assert invariants: exact degree and quotas, no self-loops/duplicates, two-sided cap holds.\n\nDiagnostics and success\n- Log: fraction of edges replaced; per-node max cross-batch distance before/after; fraction of replacements passing cluster-consistency; component counts in cluster-induced subgraphs (proxy for Graph_conn).\n- Success criteria (vs EID 391 = 0.7328):\n  - Graph_conn +≥0.003 (ideally +0.005);\n  - ARI, NMI ≥ baseline − 0.001;\n  - Cell_cycle ≥ baseline (0.352);\n  - iLISI, cLISI, kBET within ±0.003.\n- If τ=1.3 is neutral/negative, try τ=1.2 once; otherwise close the line.\n\nFallback prototype (only if above fails): GCCA-lite shared subspace + light ComBat\n- Per batch: PCA50 on HVGs → center → per-PC whitening; stack equally\
    \ and SVD → top d=60 shared components; project batches; run ComBat in shared space; use as X_emb; keep BRBG unchanged.\n- One run at d=60, optional d=56 if neutral. Same success criteria.\n\nRisks and guardrails\n- Sparse regions: strict caps yield no replacements; leave edges unchanged (no harm).\n- Over-restriction by clusters: if replacements rare, adjust Leiden resolution slightly (0.4–0.6) once; otherwise close.\n- Cell_cycle sensitivity: we’re not changing embedding scales, so expect neutrality; monitor closely and abort if it dips.\n\nOutcome targets\n- A small, locality-preserving rewire (r=1, τ≈1.3) may tighten cross-batch neighborhoods within unsupervised clusters, improving label-wise connectivity without disturbing ARI/NMI. If not, geometry-level changes (GCCA-lite) are the next principled step."
