parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write praxis/capped_balanced_graph.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix\n\n  def _per_batch_nn(Z, batches, k_pb, extra=20, metric='cosine'):\n      \"\"\"\n      Precompute per-batch nearest neighbors for all queries.\n      Returns dict b -> (idx_b, dists_b (n x K), inds_b (n x K)), with K = min(k_pb+extra+1, |b|).\n      \"\"\"\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      out = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_b = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_b.size == 0:\n              continue\n          K = int(min(k_pb + extra + 1, idx_b.size))\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=K, metric=metric, algorithm='auto').fit(Z[idx_b])\n          dists_b, inds_b = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=K, return_distance=True)\n          out[b] = (idx_b, dists_b, inds_b)\n      return out\n\n  def _median_local_scale(dpool):\n\
    \      \"\"\"\n      Compute per-node local scale sigma_i as median of candidate distances across all batches.\n      dpool: list of arrays [dists_b[i] for all batches b], each shape (K,)\n      Returns float\n      \"\"\"\n      if not dpool:\n          return 1.0\n      cat = np.concatenate(dpool, axis=0)\n      # drop potential zeros (self) except the first positive\n      cat = cat[cat > 0]\n      if cat.size == 0:\n          return 1.0\n      return float(np.median(cat))\n\n  def build_balanced_knn_capped(\n      Z, batches, clusters, k_total=48, metric='cosine', tau=1.3, extra=20\n  ):\n      \"\"\"\n      Balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas and local, distance-capped cross-batch selection\n      constrained by unsupervised clusters.\n\n      - Quotas: q = ceil(k_total / n_batches) neighbors per batch for each node (including own batch).\n      - For cross-batch neighbors, accept only candidates j' that satisfy:\n          d(i,j') <= tau * sigma_i AND d(i,j') <= tau * sigma_j'\
    \  (two-sided cap)\n          clusters[j'] == clusters[i]  (cluster-consistency)\n        If insufficient under cap, fill from nearest until quota is met (preserve degree/quotas).\n      - For same-batch neighbors, pick nearest until quota is met.\n\n      Returns symmetric CSR connectivities (binary) and distances.\n      \"\"\"\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      clusters = np.asarray(clusters)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      if n == 0:\n          return None, None\n\n      # Per-batch KNN pools for all queries\n      n_batches = uniq.size\n      q = int(np.ceil(k_total / max(1, n_batches)))\n      pools = _per_batch_nn(Z, batches, k_pb=q, extra=extra, metric=metric)\n\n      # Precompute sigma_i from available candidate pools (median across all batch-candidate distances)\n      sigma = np.ones(n, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          dpool = []\n          for b in uniq:\n            \
    \  if b not in pools:\n                  continue\n              idx_b, d_b, ind_b = pools[b]\n              # distances for query i to candidates in batch b\n              dpool.append(d_b[i])\n          sigma[i] = _median_local_scale(dpool)\n\n      # Also need sigma for targets (two-sided cap): derive from their own candidate pools as queries\n      # Reuse the same sigma array (already computed for all nodes via their own query rows).\n\n      # Build undirected edges via union of per-node selected quotas\n      pairs_min_dist = {}  # (min(i,j), max(i,j)) -> dist\n\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = batches[i]\n          ci = clusters[i]\n          si = sigma[i]\n          for b in uniq:\n              # candidate arrays for this batch\n              if b not in pools:\n                  continue\n              idx_b, d_b, ind_b = pools[b]\n              cand_global = idx_b[ind_b[i]]  # shape (K,)\n              cand_dists = d_b[i]            # shape (K,)\n\n             \
    \ # remove self if present for same-batch\n              if b == bi:\n                  mask = cand_global != i\n                  cand_global = cand_global[mask]\n                  cand_dists = cand_dists[mask]\n\n              # selection count\n              need = q\n              taken = 0\n              pos = 0\n              # For cross-batch, apply caps + cluster filter; for same-batch, just take nearest\n              if b != bi:\n                  # First pass: capped + cluster-consistent\n                  while taken < need and pos < cand_global.shape[0]:\n                      j = int(cand_global[pos]); dj = float(cand_dists[pos])\n                      sj = sigma[j]\n                      if dj <= tau * si and dj <= tau * sj and clusters[j] == ci:\n                          a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                          prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                          if (prev is None) or (dj < prev):\n                              pairs_min_dist[(a,\
    \ c)] = dj\n                          taken += 1\n                      pos += 1\n                  # If still not enough, fill from nearest (no cap, but still avoid duplicates)\n                  pos = 0\n                  while taken < need and pos < cand_global.shape[0]:\n                      j = int(cand_global[pos]); dj = float(cand_dists[pos])\n                      a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                      prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n                      if (prev is None) or (dj < prev):\n                          pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = dj\n                      taken += 1\n                      pos += 1\n              else:\n                  # same-batch: nearest until quota\n                  while taken < need and pos < cand_global.shape[0]:\n                      j = int(cand_global[pos]); dj = float(cand_dists[pos])\n                      a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n                      prev = pairs_min_dist.get((a, c))\n             \
    \         if (prev is None) or (dj < prev):\n                          pairs_min_dist[(a, c)] = dj\n                      taken += 1\n                      pos += 1\n\n      if not pairs_min_dist:\n          return None, None\n\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs_min_dist.items():\n          rows.append(i); cols.append(j); dist_data.append(d)\n          rows.append(j); cols.append(i); dist_data.append(d)\n\n      D = coo_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C = D.copy(); C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C.tocsr(), D.tocsr()\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log_addendum_capped_graph.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Plan: Local, cluster-constrained, distance-capped cross-batch quotas\n  Tick: 238\n\n  Motivation\n  - Balanced kNN ensures cross-batch mixing but doesn’t guarantee label-wise connectivity (Graph_conn). Global stitching was neutral-to-negative. We will try a locality-preserving graph rebuild with a\
    \ cap on cross-batch edge lengths and an unsupervised cluster-consistency filter.\n\n  Method (A)\n  - EtC-BRBG baseline for embedding/graph PCs unchanged.\n  - After computing X_emb neighbors (n=15), run Leiden (res≈0.5) to get unsupervised clusters cl[i].\n  - Compute BRBG graph-PCs Zcorr (as baseline).\n  - Build balanced kNN per batch with equal quotas q = ceil(k_total / n_batches), but for cross-batch neighbors enforce:\n      d(i,j) ≤ τ·σ_i and d(i,j) ≤ τ·σ_j (two-sided cap), with σ_i = median distance of i to all pooled per-batch candidates; τ ∈ {1.3, 1.2}.\n      cl[j] == cl[i] (cluster-consistency).\n    If insufficient under cap, fill from nearest to meet quotas. Same-batch edges: nearest.\n  - Symmetric, binary connectivities; real distances retained.\n\n  Success criteria (vs EID 391 = 0.7328)\n  - Graph_conn +≥0.003; ARI, NMI within −0.001; Cell_cycle ≥ 0.352; cLISI/iLISI/kBET within ±0.003.\n\n  Execution\n  - First run τ=1.3. If neutral/negative, one follow-up τ=1.2. Then\
    \ close line.\n  - If both fail, switch to GCCA-lite (in progress; EIDs 419/420).\n```\n\nNote: Two GCCA-lite runs (EIDs 419, 420) are currently running. I’ll submit the capped-graph prototype once a slot frees up."
thinking_content: '**Evaluating nearest neighbors**


  I’m planning to fit a neural network on Zcorr for each batch and then use k-nearest neighbors (kNN) for node evaluation. I’m thinking about using a maximum of 50 candidates since it could get heavy with 20,000 nodes across 4 batches, which I believe is manageable. Each batch generates around 20,000 queries, and that sounds fine.


  I’ll also generate replacements, tracking degrees while updating edges symmetrically. But I need to manage symmetric quotas carefully as they relate to the nodes and batches involved.**Maintaining node quotas**


  I need to ensure that quotas per node for each batch are preserved. When replacing edges, I’ll keep the candidate edges within the same batch. While the quotas for node i will remain the same, the j endpoints will change because their connection to i will be lost, and they will gain a new connection. This could disrupt their quotas. The graph is undirected, but I built the edges based on a minimum distance union, which complicates maintaining balanced degrees across nodes. Considering treating the graph as directed could help, but evaluations assume symmetry in connections.**Reassessing edge connectivity**


  In our earlier process, we built a graph using an undirected union of minimum distance pairs. This approach doesn’t guarantee that every node has exactly k edges since some may end up with more. Previously, we operated under the assumption that each node would have a degree equal to the total, but that isn’t exact due to variation. For replacements, I’m considering working with directed half edges or even rebuilding connectivity from scratch. This way, I can ensure that my approach is simpler by selecting candidates within a threshold while maintaining quotas through k-nearest neighbors.**Implementing balanced kNN**


  I''m planning to implement a function to build a balanced k-nearest neighbors (kNN) model with a cap. For each batch, I will fit NearestNeighbors to the correlation data and use around 60 neighbors per node, filtering out self-references. I’m considering how to compute sigma_i—should it be based on the 15th neighbor distance or median of the selected distances? It might complicate things to scale across all candidates, so perhaps I’ll use global sigma_i derived from neighbors in the embedding space instead. That seems simpler!**Building capped balanced kNN**


  I''m tasked with implementing a function for a capped balanced k-nearest neighbors model. To compute sigma_i as the median distance to the total nearest neighbors, I’ll first establish a global NearestNeighbors model on the correlation array. Each batch will then use a modified number of neighbors while considering cross-batch constraints based on clusters.


  For selection, I''ll filter nodes by distance caps, ensuring each batch retains balanced quotas, even including their own neighbors. Finally, I’ll be mindful of integrating this into the existing pipeline while completing necessary clustering operations before creating the new graph structure.**Computing global sigma and distances**


  I need to compute global sigma by fitting neighbors on the entire correlation data. I’ll create a pairs_min_dist dictionary to handle minimal distances per unordered pair, tracking edges by batch. While ensuring symmetry in my edge assembly, I’ll also keep an eye on managing memory efficiently.


  Since I’m considering running batch queries while monitoring the maximum limits on submissions, it''s best to prepare the necessary storage and code modules without submitting new tasks until the current ones finish. I’ll prepare thoughtful plans and logging for better organization.**Preparing for implementation and storage**


  I’m thinking about applying a cap to avoid excessively long edges while building the selected nodes per each iteration. This involves ensuring that candidate selections meet specific conditions, and if there aren''t enough, I’ll fill in with the next nearest until k_pb is satisfied.


  After selection, I’ll construct an undirected graph union and carefully manage memory while storing arrays. I also need to compute cluster labels after embedding neighbors but before building the graph. Since I can''t submit new experiments right now, I''ll prepare the storage and provide a plan instead.'
