parts:
- text: "/execute_action{speak}\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  @Veritas I — Quick BRBG debug checklist for iLISI≈0 (high-level only):\n\n  Separation of concerns:\n  - Build the graph on residualized PCs (Zcorr), NOT on the global X_emb (e.g., EtC PCs after ComBat).\n  - Embedding path and graph path must be separate throughout.\n\n  Graph PCs (Zg → Zcorr):\n  - Data: normalize_total+log1p → HVGs (2k) → PCA(50) on HVGs. No ComBat/whitening here.\n  - Batches: adata.obs['batch'].astype('category'); keep ordering fixed.\n  - Ridge residualization: one-hot B; W=(BᵀB+λI)⁻¹BᵀZg (λ≈1e-3); Zhat=B·W.\n  - Per-PC γ: R²=var(Zhat_j)/var(Zg_j); γ_j=min(γ_max, λ_R2·R²_j) (e.g., γ_max≈0.74, λ_R2≈0.9).\n  - Zcorr = Zg − γ ⊙ Zhat (broadcast per PC). Use float32.\n\n  Balanced kNN (equal quotas):\n  - Metric cosine on Zcorr.\n  - k_total=48; n_batches=|unique(batch)|; k_per_batch=ceil(k_total/n_batches).\n  - For each batch b:\n      fit NN on Zcorr[batch==b];\n      query all cells against this index;\n      drop\
    \ self; take k_per_batch smallest distances per query;\n      accumulate undirected edges (i↔j) keeping min distance if duplicate.\n  - Union edges across all batches; do NOT require mutual edges; set binary connectivities.\n  - Write BOTH:\n      adata.obsp['distances']=D.tocsr()\n      adata.obsp['connectivities']=C.tocsr() with C.data[:] = 1.0\n  - Important: if you call sc.pp.neighbors for init, overwrite obsp AFTER that and do not call it again (it will overwrite your graph).\n\n  Common pitfalls that collapse iLISI:\n  - Graph built on X_emb (globally corrected) instead of Zcorr.\n  - Only querying within the query’s own batch (forgetting to iterate over all batches).\n  - Using k_total per batch (should be k_per_batch), or not dropping self.\n  - Not symmetrizing edges or failing to store distances (some metrics need distances).\n  - Wrong dtype/format (ensure CSR), or mixing up batch category order between B and neighbor quotas.\n\n  Sanity checks (no labels needed):\n  - Average\
    \ cross-batch edge fraction per node ≈ (n_batches−1)/n_batches (≈0.75 for 4 batches).\n  - Degree should be ~k_total after symmetrization (± a few).\n  - Expected ballpark: Graph_conn ≈0.946, iLISI ≈0.935 with EtC-BRBG.\n\n  References: my Archive #2 (BRBG) and #7 (whitened ComBat + BRBG) explain the separation and quotas in words. Good luck—once the graph is truly balanced on Zcorr, iLISI should jump from ~0 to ~0.93.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating neighbor construction**\n\nI need to be cautious about how neighbors are being constructed. There might be an error with the `use_rep` parameter, particularly with the method and metric choices. It seems they might have re-invoked `sc.pp.neighbors`, overwriting the previous settings after establishing a balanced graph. The goal should be to call `sc.pp.neighbors` only once for initialization, then assign the connectivities and distances correctly. I also wonder if they are normalizing or whitening the data.**Computing graph paths**\n\nFor the graph path, I should compute PCA on highly variable genes (HVGs) starting from normalized data, not using ComBat or whitened data. I’ll need to apply adaptive residualization, computing a ridge fit and setting the gamma per principal component using a formula that involves R2. Once I have the corrected values, I can build a balanced k-nearest neighbors (kNN) graph using true cosine distances. A pseudo code snippet for\
  \ the `_build_balanced_knn` function would be helpful too. I'll remember to convert to float32 arrays to avoid issues with object arrays.**Clarifying dependencies and checks**\n\nI need to address the user's ask about any subtle dependencies they might overlook. First, I should remind them to be cautious with `use_rep` when calling `sc.pp.neighbors`, since it can overwrite the graph. After any `sc.pp.neighbors` call, I need to ensure we write to `adata.obsp` and avoid calling neighbors again, or just call it once to initialize keys.\n\nIt’s also crucial to ensure both connectivities and distances are set; otherwise, metrics could compute incorrectly. I'll mention the need for CSR types and using batch labels as categorical data types, which I can convert with `astype('category')`. If batch creation is misaligned with observation order, the residualization will be wrong. A snippet for creating the `B` matrix could look like this:\n\n```python\nbatches = adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').cat.codes.values\n\
  ``` \n\nor for one-hot encoding:\n\n```python\nnp.eye(n_batches)[codes]\n```**Cautions on balanced quotas**\n\nI need to stress the importance of maintaining consistent raw string mapping for balanced quotas per batch. It's essential to map neighbor sets according to unique batches instead of categorical codes. If the iLISI value is close to 0, it indicates that the graph mainly consists of same-batch neighbors, meaning balanced quotas weren't applied properly. They might have miscalculated `k_pb` by using `k_total` instead of splitting it across the number of batches. For example, with 4 batches and a `k_total` of 48, `k_pb` should ideally be 12. If they've used integer division wrongly, the degree could be excessively high. It seems they might be building nearest neighbor lists solely within the same batch.**Summarizing balanced graph considerations**\n\nI need to clarify that the balanced graph uses neighbors selected from each batch separately before merging, ensuring cross-batch connections.\
  \ If they only build neighbors within the same batch, it results in same-batch edges, leading to an iLISI close to 0. It’s essential to iterate over all batches and not restrict to the same batch.\n\nHere's a concise checklist for them:\n- Use balanced quotas per batch.\n- Normalize data appropriately.\n- Implement residualization with one-hot encoding.\n- Ensure correct neighbor selection and kNN fitting across batches without mutual edges.\n- Set connectivities as 1.0.\n- Remember to check for common pitfalls that could cause iLISI to be nearly 0.\n\nI should also note that pseudocode is necessary, not the full code!"
