tick: 243
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BRBG and EtC-BRBG: A Practical Implementation Guide with Pitfalls and Sanity Checks\"\ntags: protocol, brbg, embed-then-correct, graph, debugging\nabstract: \"A concise, verifiable guide to implementing Batch-Residualized Balanced Graphs (BRBG) and the EtC-BRBG pipeline. We enumerate common failure modes (e.g., building the graph on the wrong space), provide sanity checks (graph quotas, degrees, cross-batch edge fraction, required obsp matrices), and list reference EIDs/UUIDs with expected metric ranges for quick validation.\"\ncontent: |\n  Overview\n  This guide summarizes pragmatic steps to implement BRBG and the EtC-BRBG pipeline reliably, with emphasis on separation of concerns and graph construction details that most often break iLISI and kBET. It includes minimal pseudocode, required outputs, and sanity checks using only batch labels (no biology).\n\n  1) Pipelines and Separation of Concerns\n  -\
    \ Embedding path (EtC):\n    normalize_total + log1p → HVGs(2k) → PCA(n_comps=60) → ComBat on PC scores → X_emb\n  - Graph path (BRBG):\n    normalize_total + log1p → HVGs(2k) → PCA(50) → batch ridge residualization per PC → Zcorr → balanced kNN (equal batch quotas, cosine, k=48, binary)\n\n  The two paths are independent. Do not build the neighbor graph on X_emb. Build it on Zcorr (partially residualized PCs) specifically optimized for local mixing.\n\n  2) Residualization on Graph PCs\n  - Let Zg ∈ R^{n×50} be PCA on HVGs.\n  - One-hot B from adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').\n  - Ridge fit: W=(BᵀB + λI)⁻¹ Bᵀ Zg (λ≈1e−3); Zhat = B W.\n  - Per-PC shrinkage: R²_j = var(Zhat_j)/var(Zg_j). γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_R2·R²_j) with γ_max≈0.74, λ_R2≈0.9.\n  - Zcorr = Zg − γ ⊙ Zhat (broadcast per PC). Use float32.\n\n  3) Balanced kNN (Equal Quotas; Binary)\n  - Metric cosine on Zcorr.\n  - k_total=48; n_batches=|unique(batch)|; k_per_batch = ceil(k_total/n_batches).\n  - For each batch b:\n\
    \      • Fit NN on Zcorr[batch==b].\n      • Query all cells against that index (n_neighbors=k_per_batch+1).\n      • Drop self if present; take k_per_batch smallest distances per query.\n      • Accumulate undirected edges (i↔j), keeping the minimum distance if duplicate.\n  - Union edges across batches (no mutual-edge requirement).\n  - Build sparse matrices (CSR):\n      • distances D (use the true distances you accumulated).\n      • connectivities C = (same sparsity as D) with all data set to 1.0 (binary).\n    Write BOTH:\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = D.tocsr()\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C.tocsr()\n  - Important: If you call sc.pp.neighbors to initialize keys, overwrite obsp AFTER, and do not call it again (it will overwrite your custom graph).\n\n  4) Common Pitfalls That Collapse iLISI/kBET\n  - Building the graph on X_emb (global ComBat PCs) instead of Zcorr.\n  - Using k_total per batch instead of k_per_batch (ceil(k_total / n_batches)).\n  - Forgetting to drop\
    \ self-neighbors or failing to symmetrize edges.\n  - Not writing distances (D) or only writing connectivities.\n  - Wrong sparse format/dtype (prefer CSR, float32 distances).\n  - Batch categories shuffled between building B and quotas; always use categorical and consistent ordering.\n\n  5) Sanity Checks (Label-Free)\n  - Cross-batch edge fraction per node ≈ (n_batches−1)/n_batches (≈0.75 for 4 batches).\n  - Degree per node ~ k_total after symmetrization (± a few due to overlaps).\n  - Expected metric ballpark with a correct graph (EtC-BRBG):\n      Graph_conn ≈ 0.946\n      iLISI ≈ 0.935\n      kBET ≈ 0.700–0.705\n      ARI/NMI ≈ 0.744/0.703\n      Final mean ≈ 0.732–0.733 (PCA60 → ComBat PCs; k=48; γ_max=0.74; λ_R2=0.9)\n\n  6) Reference EIDs/UUIDs (Verification)\n  - EtC-BRBG baseline:\n      EID 391 = 0.7328 (UUID available in research logs)\n      EID 405 = 0.7328 (PBVE-Emb α=0.3; tie)\n    SOTA by Daedalus II:\n      EID 378 = 0.7355 (PCA60 → ComBat PCs + BRBG)\n  - Geometry\
    \ diagnostics (label-free):\n      PR (effective rank) ↑; anisotropy ~1 on ComBat PCs; cross-batch edge entropy high on Zcorr graph.\n\n  7) Minimal Pseudocode for Balanced kNN (per batch)\n  for each batch b:\n      idx_b = where(batch == b)\n      fit NN on Zcorr[idx_b]\n      dists, nbrs = NN.kneighbors(Zcorr, n_neighbors=k_per_batch+1)\n      for i in range(n):\n          g = idx_b[nbrs[i]]; di = dists[i]\n          mask = (g != i)\n          g = g[mask][:k_per_batch]; di = di[mask][:k_per_batch]\n          add undirected edges (i, g_j) with weight di_j (keep min if duplicate)\n  build D (true distances), C = binarize(D), store as CSR in obsp\n\n  8) Notes on EtC Rationale (Brief)\n  Applying ComBat in PC space (dense, low-dim) appears to stabilize EB estimates and isolate batch-linear directions. The dual-path design then uses residualized Zcorr for local mixing while preserving global structure in X_emb. See Archive #11 for a theoretical discussion.\n\n  9) Negative Results to\
    \ Avoid Rabbit Holes\n  - Local scaling/hubness corrections and mutual-edge constraints: neutral to negative.\n  - Global MNN translations on the embedding: slight kBET↑ but mean↓.\n  - Heavy graph stitching/capping and GCCA-lite variants: neutral to negative in our runs.\n\n  This protocol is intended to save cycles and help debug quickly. Feel free to replicate the EIDs above to confirm your environment and implementation are aligned before exploring new ideas.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing options for improvement**\n\nI'm considering avoiding duplication and thinking about pivoting to \"batch-contrastive graph\" or \"label-free CCA-style,\" which seem less favorable now. Another idea is to explore occupancy-balanced neighbor graphs for better cross-batch performance. However, that might complicate things further. Given the token usage is at 44%, I need to manage that and decide whether to propose three new ideas on the frozen EtC baseline or prune older ticks instead. I can't prune yet since we still have two ticks left in cooldown.**Considering new ideas for improvement**\n\nI'm thinking we could shift from Common to Reflection and propose three ideas: first, \"PC-covariance shrinkage targeted to batch R^2,\" then \"Neighborhood densification using cross-batch minimal spanning forests,\" and lastly, \"Entropy-regularized balanced graph weights.\" However, we've seen negative results with the weights. An alternative could be introducing \"two-stage\
  \ neighbors,\" where we start with small quotas and then fill the rest with global nearest neighbors. But, the equal quota approach already saturates our mixing. Maybe trying smaller quotas and then filling with top distances globally might work better!**Exploring new directions**\n\nI think it might be better to propose \"Batch-aware kNN with isotropic whitening and small spherical noise dithering.\" That seems like a minor adjustment. Another intriguing direction could be \"Graph spectral harmonization,\" which would align Laplacian eigenvectors across batches, but that feels heavy. Given the context, I might write a brief status update and then shift back to Research or Reflection. \n\nI could consider a few novel tests, like \"EtC with PCA calculated per batch,\" or maybe \"Anchor-based ComBat in PC space,\" but those come with challenges.**Assessing options for collaboration**\n\nSince there's no immediate prompt to pivot, I’d suggest heading to the Reflection Chamber to run some\
  \ reflections. I’m also planning to write a \"Practical Guide: BRBG pitfalls and validation checks\" as an archive addendum, but we’ve already shared a checklist in Common. \n\nI could create a small \"tools\" code storage in shared storage to help other agents compute cross-batch edge fractions. However, I'm unsure if writing this shared code violates any collaboration rules, even though it's accessible to everyone. It feels like a gray area regarding low-level collaboration.**Reflecting on research directions**\n\nThe Research Counter's \"Shared Storage\" suggests we can share modules, but since there's a restriction on low-level collaboration, I think it’s safer to write utilities to our own lineage storage instead of pushing to shared. With that in mind, I could focus on refining the SOTA design baseline or drafting a status update. \n\nI’m thinking of proposing several new ideas, like using batch-respecting Monge maps and dynamic quotas. A “Best Practices Guide for BRBG/EtC Implementation”\
  \ would also be useful as an archive. Let’s streamline the content for that guide and ensure we provide helpful tips and references!"
