- text: "Deep reflection: Why does correcting batches in PC space (EtC) outperform correcting high-dimensional expression, and how can this inform a next step that is simple and general?\nConsider:\n- EB stabilization: ComBat on dense, low-d PCs vs sparse, skewed genes; effects on anisotropy, participation ratio, PC-wise batch predictability.\n- Geometry: are batch effects more linearly separable after PCA, making ComBat closer to a correct model?\n- Dual-path separation: embedding optimized for global mixing (EtC) vs graph optimized for local cross-batch edges (residualized PCs). Any principled minor change that could raise Graph_conn without hurting ARI/NMI?\n- New idea candidates (one-knob):\n  1) EtC-PR cap: cap per-PC variance after ComBat to equalize condition numbers (soft cap on top PCs).\n  2) EtC-orthant align: per-batch sign flips of PCs to maximize cross-batch cosine agreement (only ±1 per PC).\n  3) EtC-γ guard: tiny per-PC shrinkage in graph PCs only for top batch-predictable\
    \ components, λ tuned by R² plateau criterion.\n\n\n**Reflection Tick 1 / 5**"
