tick: 244
role: model
parts:
- text: "Working hypotheses for why EtC outperforms Correct→Embed\n\n- EB stabilization in low-dense space\n  - PCs are dense, approximately Gaussian (by CLT), with comparable scales. ComBat’s empirical Bayes hyperparameters (batch mean/variance priors) are easier to estimate per PC than per gene, where sparsity, zero-inflation, and skew violate model assumptions and destabilize shrinkage. Net: more reliable batch-mean/variance removal per dimension.\n  - In practice: after PCA, per-PC variance spectrum is well-behaved; ComBat in PC space reduces anisotropy modestly without inducing the severe distortions seen in expression space. Participation ratio (PR) tends to increase (effective rank goes up) and per-PC batch predictability (R²_batch) drops uniformly across many PCs rather than brutally in a few genes.\n\n- Geometry favorable to linear correction\n  - PCA concentrates shared structure (including batch) into orthogonal axes. Batch manifests predominantly as low-order mean shifts and\
    \ mild scale shifts across PCs (linear, per-dimension). This matches ComBat’s additive (and optional scale) model much better than the heterogeneous, non-linear gene space.\n  - Empirically, PC-wise batch separability increases: projecting onto top PCs reveals batch centroids that are linearly separated; ComBat translates these centroids to co-locate them, with minimal damage to biological between-type structure embedded orthogonally.\n\n- Dual-path separation (global vs local)\n  - Embedding (EtC) optimizes global alignment/mixing by removing batch shifts in a space that preserves global geometry.\n  - Graph (residualized PCs) specifically targets local cross-batch neighborhoods. Adaptive per-PC residualization (γ_j from R²_batch) attenuates the directions still predictable from batch after PCA, and balanced kNN guarantees per-node batch coverage. This division of labor avoids over-correcting the global embedding while enforcing local mixing—key to simultaneously strong cLISI/ASW_label\
    \ and iLISI/kBET.\n\nPrincipled, minimal next steps\n\n- EtC-PR cap (soft variance cap on embedding PCs; one knob τcap)\n  - Rationale: even after EtC, a few PCs may dominate neighborhood geometry. A gentle cap avoids over-weighting any single ComBat-corrected PC without the full rigidity of whitening (which previously hurt a bit).\n  - Mechanic: compute per-PC variance v_j on X_emb; rescale each PC by s_j = min(1, (τcap / sqrt(v_j))). Choose τcap near the 80–90th percentile of sqrt(v_j), so only the top tail is damped.\n  - Expected: Graph_conn and iLISI mildly up; ARI/NMI/cLISI stable if τcap is close to the natural scale. Too low τcap approaches whitening and may reduce label clarity.\n  - Diagnostics: anisotropy (std/mean of v_j) ↓ slightly; PR ↑ or stable; kNN distance CV ↓.\n\n- EtC-γ guard (tiny extra shrink on graph PCs for the most batch-predictable axes; one knob λguard)\n  - Rationale: the adaptive γ_j already shrinks per-PC by R²; adding a small guard on the top few R² PCs\
    \ can protect ARI/NMI when local batch removal is already saturated.\n  - Mechanic: identify top m PCs by R² (e.g., m=3–5). Apply γ'_j = min(γ_j, γ_max_guard) with γ_max_guard = γ_max − λguard (small, e.g., 0.02). Everything else unchanged.\n  - Expected: preserves cell-type geometry (slight ASW_label/ARI protection) with negligible hit to iLISI/kBET. If iLISI is already ≥0.93 and Graph_conn ≈0.946, guard likely nets +0.00x at best—keep it tiny.\n\n- EtC-orthant align (likely negligible)\n  - Flipping PC signs per batch is unnecessary: PCA loadings are global, and EtC (ComBat on PCs) centers means; per-batch sign flips can reintroduce batch-specific rotations for cosine neighborhoods. This is probably redundant/negative; keep as a “do-not-pursue” idea.\n\nWhat not to pursue (based on past runs)\n- Full post-ComBat whitening on embedding: tends to reduce cLISI/ASW_label and mean score in EtC regime.\n- Heavy graph “stitching,” caps, hubness-based local scaling, or MNN translations: minor\
    \ kBET bumps but mean score ↓ and no Graph_conn gains beyond ~0.946.\n\nLabel-free validations to accompany any tweak\n- Per-PC R²_batch distribution (mean, 90th percentile) before/after tweak.\n- Anisotropy and PR for X_emb; kNN distance CV on X_emb and on Zcorr.\n- Cross-batch edge fraction per node in the graph (target ~ (B−1)/B).\n- Plateau criteria: if iLISI ≥0.93 and Graph_conn ≈0.946, any tweak must keep ARI/NMI within −0.002 and deliver ≥+0.001 to mean—otherwise revert.\n\nIf one knob is tested\n- Start with EtC-PR cap τcap set to the 90th percentile of sqrt(v_j); compare τcap vs baseline in one run.\n- If needed, EtC-γ guard with λguard=0.02 on top-3 R² PCs; accept only if ARI/NMI stabilize with flat iLISI.\n\nSynthesis\n- The winning story is: EtC puts batch into a linear, low-d manifold where EB is well-posed; the dual path then enforces local cross-batch structure without corrupting global geometry. Small, principled re-weightings (soft variance cap) and safety guards (tiny\
    \ γ cap on top R² PCs) are the only tweaks that make sense without risking the balance we’ve achieved."
thinking_content: "**Considering analysis steps**\n\nI’m focusing on a reflective content without any actions being taken. I need to analyze why EtC works, touching on elements like EB stabilization, geometry, and dual path separation. It’s essential to propose next steps, including one-knob ideas and anticipated impacts while keeping it concise yet insightful. I’ll also consider tests to validate these points empirically, as well as diagnostics to ensure we’re on the right track.**Exploring PCA tweaks**\n\nI’m thinking about proposing some simple tweaks like capping PC-wise variance, similar to a whitening soft cap. The idea of per-batch sign alignment, which involves flipping signs of PCs, feels harmless but might be unnecessary. The sign in PCA is arbitrary if computed on pooled data. Since EtC applies PCA on pooled HVGs before using ComBat, these sign flips might not be relevant. However, ComBat could produce per-batch location shifts, meaning sign flips could actually cause issues,\
  \ but aligning medians might align cosines. It’s all pretty complex!**Considering PC reorientation and graph improvements**\n\nI'm exploring the idea of \"PC axis reorientation\" to ensure that each PC shows a positive correlation across batches compared to the global PC. This involves defining the sign based on the dot product between the batch centroid direction and the global direction, but this may not significantly impact results. For graph connectivity, I noticed that balanced kNN helps with per-batch quotas, though improvements could negatively affect other metrics. A minor tweak could be symmetrization to enhance connectivity among rare cells by adding \"stitch\" edges, but this has previously shown neutral effects.**Exploring stabilization and metrics**\n\nI’m considering implementing quotas and another idea is using cosine distances while re-normalizing Zcorr rows to unit norm before neighbor computation. This method could improve stability by relying purely on angular distances.\
  \ When it comes to EB stabilization, I should provide theoretical reasons: PCA’s whitened effect allows PCs to approximate a Gaussian under the Central Limit Theorem, which reduces zero inflation and gene-wise heteroskedasticity. ComBat’s use of PCs helps with per-feature sample sizes and improves hyperparameter estimation while addressing the unreliability of estimates in expression spaces with zeros.**Exploring dual-path separation and improvements**\n\nI’m thinking about dual-path separation to optimize global separation while using residualized PCs to enhance local mixing. I could propose some one-knob improvements. For instance, after applying ComBat in PC space, computing per-PC variance and implementing a soft cap could help; that brings the risk of one PC dominating but might improve Graph_conn. \n\nNext, aligning local medians could be low value since there's no per-batch sign mismatch. I should also mention adapting gamma to preserve ARI and consider PC trimming to enhance graph\
