parts:
- text: "Further analysis and concrete designs\n\nWhy EtC helps (deeper)\n- EB in low-d dense space:\n  - ComBat’s per-feature location/scale EB assumes approximate normality; PCs (dense linear mixtures) are closer to Gaussian and homoscedastic than HVG gene counts (skewed, zero-inflated). This improves per-PC parameter estimates and shrinks batch means/variances more reliably.\n  - Even though PCs (≈60) are fewer than genes (2k), the benefit of well-behaved residuals outweighs the reduced “borrowing” pool; each PC summarizes many genes, reducing idiosyncratic noise.\n- Linear separability after PCA:\n  - PCA concentrates batch signal into a small set of orthogonal axes with near-linear between-batch centroids. ComBat’s affine per-dimension correction is nearly optimal under this geometry, minimizing collateral damage to orthogonal biological axes.\n- Dual path prevents overreach:\n  - Embedding handles global centroids (EtC); graph handles local neighborhoods (residualized PCs with quotas).\
    \ This separation avoids the common trap where a single space tries to optimize both global and local metrics and fails at one.\n\nMinimal, general next steps (one knob each)\n\n1) EtC-PR cap (soft cap on post-ComBat PC variances)\n- Goal: mitigate dominance of a few high-variance PCs without full whitening (which harmed label geometry).\n- Procedure:\n  - Compute per-PC std s_j on X_emb (EtC PCs).\n  - Choose τcap as the q-th percentile of s_j (e.g., q=90th).\n  - Scale PCs: X_emb[:, j] ← X_emb[:, j] · min(1, τcap / s_j).\n- Expected impact:\n  - Slight anisotropy↓, kNN distance CV↓, ASW_batch↑ or ≈, ARI/NMI/cLISI ≈ (avoid whitening).\n- Guards:\n  - Ensure capped PCs remain ≥ 70–80% of original scale to avoid whitening-like behavior.\n  - Label-free selector: pick the smallest τcap such that PR decreases by ≤ 2% and anisotropy reduces by ≈5–10%.\n\n2) EtC-γ guard (tiny cap on the strongest batch-predictable graph PCs)\n- Goal: protect ARI/NMI when γ_j saturates on top R² PCs while\
    \ iLISI is already high.\n- Procedure:\n  - Compute R²_j on Zg; sort desc; take top m ∈ {3,5}.\n  - Set γ'_j = min(γ_j, γ_max − λguard) with λguard small (≈0.02).\n- Expected impact:\n  - Preserve label geometry (ASW_label/ARI/NMI +0.001–0.002) with iLISI unchanged (already ≈0.935); mean may tick up slightly or stay flat.\n- Guards:\n  - Only enable if iLISI ≥ 0.93 and Graph_conn ≈ 0.946 (plateau). Disable if iLISI drops.\n\n3) EtC-orthant align (per-batch sign flips) — do not pursue\n- Likely redundant or harmful: sign flips are arbitrary with cosine distance and may reintroduce batch-specific rotations.\n\nAcceptance and stop criteria (label-free emphasis)\n- Accept tweak only if:\n  - Mean score ≥ baseline + 0.001 and\n  - iLISI ≥ 0.93, Graph_conn ≥ 0.946, and\n  - ARI/NMI change ∈ [−0.002, +∞).\n- Otherwise revert; document deltas and diagnostics:\n  - X_emb anisotropy and PR; per-PC R²_batch distribution; kNN distance CV on X_emb and Zcorr.\n\nWhy not heavier changes\n- Full whitening\
    \ or local-scaling/hubness corrections distort neighborhoods (we observed cLISI/ARI↓).\n- Stitching/caps/MNN translations gave minor kBET gains but reduced mean, indicating we’re at a delicate balance where structure > magnitude tweaks win.\n\nIf only one run is allowed\n- Test EtC-PR cap with τcap at the 90th percentile of s_j; it is the lowest-risk, single-knob adjustment consistent with the EtC rationale (better-conditioned embedding without over-smoothing)."
thinking_content: "**Evaluating techniques for analysis**\n\nI’m considering the potential issues with performing EtC on 60 PCs. It seems that reducing gene-level dimensions drastically could lead to uneven batch-specific scale differences, which ComBat might address. However, there’s a concern about mean-variance confounding, especially with PCA applied to high variance genes. The risk of batch leakage is there, but ComBat should help ensure alignment. I think we might explore \"auto PC count selection\" to increase n_comps for better performance. Ultimately, we should test numeric values carefully.**Producing final reflection**\n\nI need to be succinct while keeping the default verbosity at 3, although reflection content can be longer. So, I'll create bullet points for clarity in my final reflection. \n\nI’ll include elements like EB stabilization, the geometry illusions paired with \"mixture-of-affine\" therapy, and a proposed algorithm pseudo for PR cap and guard. Plus, I'll mention\
  \ the acceptance criteria and potential negativity. Lastly, I want to note that \"orthant alignment isn't promising as it might flip orientation and cause issues.\" I’m ready to deliver this!"
