parts:
- text: "Deeper theory, concrete implementation, and acceptance logic\n\nWhy EtC helps, from a spiked-covariance lens\n- Model intuition: Suppose X = S + B + ε with S (biology) low-rank, B (batch) low-rank, ε isotropic noise. PCA on HVGs yields Z = UᵀX with orthogonal axes ordered by variance. Both S and B concentrate into a few principal directions (“spikes”), and ε is spread. In this basis:\n  - Batch effects manifest largely as per-PC mean shifts and mild scale shifts (linear, axis-aligned).\n  - ComBat’s per-feature affine EB becomes well-posed: each PC is dense (CLT → near-Gaussian), orthogonal to others (variance decouples), and has enough samples for stable hyperparameters.\n  - Consequence: per-PC batch predictability R²_batch drops broadly instead of violently in a few axes, preserving S while cleaning B.\n\nGeometric effects we can measure (label-free)\n- Anisotropy of X_emb (EtC PCs): aniso = std(v) / mean(v), where v_j = Var(X_emb[:, j]). Desirable regime: aniso modest (neither\
    \ highly peaked nor fully flat).\n- Participation ratio (effective rank): PR = (∑ v_j)² / ∑ v_j². EtC tends to increase PR relative to ComBat-in-expression; too much capping/whitening can reduce biological contrast, so keep PR within ±2–3% of baseline.\n- R²_batch on graph PCs Zg: R²_j = Var(Zhat_j)/Var(Zg_j), where Zhat from ridge on one-hot batches. After EtC+BRBG, distribution of R² usually has a short tail; large tails indicate under-residualization risk.\n\nTwo minimal, principled tweaks (one knob each)\n\n1) EtC-PR cap (soft cap post-ComBat PC scales; low risk)\n- Rationale: Avoid a few dominant PCs from steering neighbors while not whitening (which we saw hurts cLISI/ASW_label).\n- Mechanism (feature reweighting in cosine space): scale PC j by s_j' = min(1, τcap / σ_j), where σ_j = sqrt(Var(X_emb[:, j])) and τcap is the q-th percentile of {σ_j}; q≈90th works as a gentle top-tail cap. This reduces per-dimension leverage uniformly across all cells (cosine is not invariant to per-dim\
    \ scaling, so this matters).\n- Expected changes:\n  - Anisotropy↓ 5–10%; PR within ±2%; kNN distance CV↓.\n  - Graph_conn may tick up or stay flat; iLISI stays ≥0.93; ARI/NMI/cLISI ≈ flat (we’re not collapsing variance wholesale).\n- Guardrails:\n  - Set lower bound on cap: τcap ≥ 0.8·median(σ); ensures we never approach whitening.\n  - Skip capping if anisotropy ≤ baseline_threshold (e.g., ≤0.10 above a known healthy value) to avoid “fixing what isn’t broken.”\n- Acceptance (label-free): mean score +0.001 with ARI/NMI ≥ baseline−0.002, iLISI ≥ 0.93, Graph_conn ≥ 0.946; otherwise revert.\n\n2) EtC-γ guard (tiny cap on the strongest batch PCs in graph; very low risk)\n- Rationale: When iLISI is already at the plateau (~0.935) and Graph_conn ~0.946, further γ on the top R² PCs can cost ARI/NMI for no mixing gain. A small ceiling protects label geometry.\n- Mechanism: For top m PCs by R² (m=3–5), set γ'_j = min(γ_j, γ_max − λguard) where λguard≈0.02. Leave other PCs unchanged.\n- Expected\
    \ changes:\n  - ARI/NMI/ASW_label stabilize (small +0.001–0.002), iLISI and Graph_conn unchanged (already saturated).\n- Guardrails:\n  - Only apply if current iLISI ≥ 0.93 and Graph_conn ≥ 0.946 (plateau criteria). Disable if either drops.\n\nWhy not EtC-orthant align (per-batch sign flips)\n- Cosine neighbors with global loadings already have consistent orientation after ComBat on PCs. Per-batch sign flips reintroduce batch-specific rotations and can harm cross-batch angular coherence. Likely neutral-to-negative; skip.\n\nImplementation notes and diagnostics (succinct)\n- EtC-PR cap (pseudocode):\n  - Compute σ = std(X_emb, axis=0, ddof=1); τcap = quantile(σ, 0.90).\n  - w_j = min(1.0, τcap / max(σ_j, eps)); X_emb ← X_emb * w (broadcast).\n  - Diagnostics: report anisotropy, PR, kNN distance CV on X_emb.\n- EtC-γ guard (pseudocode):\n  - Compute R² on Zg; sort desc; idx_top = first m.\n  - γ_j ← min(γ_j, γ_max − λguard) for j ∈ idx_top.\n  - Diagnostics: report mean/90th pct of R²,\
    \ iLISI/Graph_conn and ARI/NMI deltas.\n\nRisk profile and stop rules\n- We are on a delicate ridge. The only acceptable changes are tiny, monotone feature reweightings consistent with EtC’s geometry and the dual-path design.\n- Stop if:\n  - Anisotropy or PR deviates >3–4% from baseline,\n  - ARI/NMI drop >0.003,\n  - iLISI < 0.93 or Graph_conn < 0.946.\n\nSynthesis and story\n- EtC likely works because PCA furnishes an axis-aligned, near-Gaussian coordinate system where ComBat’s EB is accurate and harmless, and the dual-path BRBG then enforces local cross-batch edges without corrupting global structure. The PR-cap is a gentle conditioning improvement for X_emb; γ-guard is a safety valve for the tail of batch-predictable graph PCs. Both are one-knob, dataset-agnostic tweaks aligned with the established theory and prior empirical plateaus."
