    \ document stitching as a low-yield connectivity tweak.\n\n  References:\n  - EID 432 (0.7335; UUID 5073cf56-…)\n  - EID 438 (0.7328; UUID 841d567f-…)\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum: Local-aware stitching (Zcorr) is neutral under our setup.\n\n  - Praxis I (component-level, 2 edges/component in Zcorr):\n    • EID 438 = 0.7328 (Graph_conn 0.946; ARI/NMI unchanged) — neutral vs EtC-BRBG baseline (EIDs 391/405 ≈ 0.7328).\n\n  - Cross-reference: Daedalus II’s per-batch “local-aware stitching”:\n    • EID 432 (k_stitch=2) = 0.7335 (Graph_conn 0.966).\n    • EID 435 (k_stitch=1) = 0.7333.\n\n  Takeaway: While per-batch stitches can bump Graph_conn to ~0.966 with a small mean uptick (+~0.0007), our component-level variant was neutral. We keep the frozen default and document stitching as a low-yield option. Full details in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_stitching.md.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n\n\
    /execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"244-245\"\n    summary: |\n      Reflection and gentle-conditioning tweaks; stitching plan and results:\n      - Deep reflection on EtC rationale → propose two minimal tweaks:\n        • PR-cap on X_emb (post-ComBat PCs): cap per-PC std at 90th percentile to reduce anisotropy.\n        • gamma-guard on graph PCs: tiny cap (−0.02) on top-3 R² PCs.\n      - Submissions:\n        • EID 430 (PR-cap) = 0.7278 — ASW_batch↑, but Cell_cycle↓; mean↓ → negative.\n        • EID 431 (gamma-guard) = 0.7325 — ≈ neutral vs baseline (0.7326–0.7328).\n      - Local-aware stitching plan drafted (Zcorr; 2 edges/component). Later run:\n        • EID 438 = 0.7328 — neutral (Graph_conn 0.946 unchanged).\n      - Actions: Logged addenda to archive #10 and storage; closed PR-cap/gamma-guard/stitching as low-yield; retain EtC-BRBG defaults.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing evaluation results**\n\nI need to generate the next assistant message about our recent evaluations at the research station. Right now, we’re at Tick 252, and we just got results for evaluation ID 438, which show no improvement over the baseline. The top scoreboard indicates a slight lead, so it’s likely best to document this finding and consider token pruning. I also wonder if it's time for a new direction since our previous tweaks didn’t help. Maybe I should reflect on this in the archive, but I’m not sure if the cooldown affects it.**Considering next steps for archive**\n\nI’m reflecting on the cooldown from the last archive action at tick 244, which was 9 ticks ago, and now it’s just 8 ticks later. I’m unsure if the cooldown is still active. I could propose reviewing the \"Embed then Correct theoretical analysis,\" but reflecting feels important. Maybe I should brainstorm new directions to break our plateau, like \"Batch-contrastive Laplacian regularization\"\
  \ or \"Graph-of-batches densification,\" without making major changes to the pipeline. \n\nNext, I should write a storage log noting that the stitching result was neutral and consider pruning the overly lengthy output from tick 245.**Reviewing pruning options**\n\nI’m checking the dialogue history, and tick 245 has a length of 12,365, so it's not protected. According to token management, the last prune was at tick 249, and with the current cooldown, it’s possible to prune again now since it’s been three more ticks. My token budget is at 132,848, so pruning would help keep it under limit. I’m considering removing tick 245 since it’s large, and optionally I could also prune tick 244, even though we pruned it earlier.**Documenting results**\n\nI see that the user didn't specifically ask for anything, but I want to take some proactive steps. I need to document the result and close the line, following the principle of publishing frequently. Since we already posted an addendum, we can consider\
  \ replying to the archive with the stitching result or perhaps the Daedalus II theoretical piece. However, it seems I can only reply to my own capsules. \n\nInstead, I could create a Public Memory post summarizing the neutral effect with the proper EID, which could benefit others. Alternatively, I might also look at other high-level attempts related to \"stitching\" and \"connectivity,\" noting that others had slightly better improvements.**Evaluating adaptive residualization**\n\nAt tick 252, I see that Daedalus II's \"EtC + Adaptive Residualization\" achieved a score of 0.7341, improving from 0.7335 and nearing 0.7355. I should evaluate adopting this approach, as it has a high Graph_conn of 0.972 and decent other metrics. Their method might suggest additional edges or a different approach to connectivity. \n\nAlthough I’m considering how to implement this without copying their code, I'll log our results and close the line. Also, I should prune tick 245 since it’s large. Looking ahead,\
  \ it could be time to create a new archive about the \"EtC-BRBG Implementation Guide,\" as the previous cooldowns may have passed.**Planning assistant output**\n\nI’m outlining the steps for my next message. First, I need to summarize the results of EID 438, confirming that it's neutral, and then close the line. I should update the storage log and consider replying to archive #10 about the \"Local-aware component stitching.\"\n\nNext, I’ll check the cooldown status for pruning tick 245, which is quite long. If I’m allowed, I’ll prune both ticks 245 and 244 together, ensuring I include a concise summary with necessary context about the results. Time to implement these actions!"
