role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write praxis/research_log_addendum_ccr.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Addendum: Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR) for BRBG — Final Results\n\n  Baseline (EtC-BRBG): ≈0.7326–0.7328 (PCA60 → ComBat PCs; graph: residualized PC50, γ_max=0.74, λ_R2=0.9; k=48).\n\n  CCR idea: Fit batch residuals per cluster in graph PC space (Zg), then build equal-quota kNN.\n  - Steps: MiniBatchKMeans (n_clusters) → ridge(Zc ~ Bc) → per-PC R²_c → γ_jc = min(gamma_max, λ·R²_jc) → Zcorr_c = Zc − γ⊙Zhat_c → balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n\n  Results:\n  - EID 442 (n_clusters=64, γ_max=0.6, λ=0.9): 0.7244\n    Metrics: ARI 0.719 | NMI 0.670 | ASW_batch 0.681 | iLISI 0.935 | kBET 0.780 | Graph_conn 0.958 | cLISI 0.963 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.319\n    Diagnosis: Mixing/connectivity OK, but ARI/NMI drop ⇒ cluster-local over-residualization.\n\n  - EID 445 (n_clusters=48, γ_max=0.5, λ=0.9): 0.7282\n    Metrics: ARI 0.717 | NMI 0.671 | ASW_batch 0.681 | iLISI 0.938 | kBET\
    \ 0.780 | Graph_conn 0.961 | cLISI 0.964 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.352\n    Diagnosis: Coarser clustering and smaller γ_max improved stability but remained below baseline; ARI/NMI still depressed.\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - CCR is a negative line for this dataset and setup: it does not surpass EtC-BRBG, mainly due to ARI/NMI erosion despite decent mixing.\n  - We close CCR and retain the frozen EtC-BRBG defaults (~0.7328).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum: Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR) — Final\n\n  Following the earlier note (EID 442 = 0.7244), we ran a gentler CCR:\n  - EID 445 (n_clusters=48, γ_max=0.5, λ=0.9) = 0.7282\n    • ARI 0.717, NMI 0.671, ASW_batch 0.681, iLISI 0.938, kBET 0.780, Graph_conn 0.961.\n\n  Outcome: improved over the first CCR but still below the EtC-BRBG baseline (~0.7328). The recurring issue is ARI/NMI erosion, suggesting cluster-local over-residualization of shared biology.\n\n  Decision:\
    \ Close the CCR line and keep the frozen EtC-BRBG defaults. All code and EIDs/UUIDs are in storage/praxis and in the addendum logs.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering next steps**


  I need to outline our next actions. We''re in the Research Counter and have evaluated CCR variants: 442 shows a negative drop with 0.7244 and 445 at 0.7282, both below the baseline of about 0.7328. We should log this negative conclusion and possibly reflect on new directions. However, the token usage seems fine at 44%. We might explore the "label-free CCA-style shared-subspace" from earlier attempts, or summarize the negative CCR results in the Archive.**Evaluating next actions**


  I need to check the token usage; we''ve used 115k out of the 270k allowed, so that''s fine. I should finalize the CCR addendum and update the research log with EID 445. I''m considering proposing a "kBET-aware local adapt gamma" method, focusing on keeping complexity low. It’s worth noting that we can''t use labels beyond batch labels, which is permitted. The environment is stagnating, so exploring might not lead to improvements. The user seems to expect me to issue /execute_action commands.**Planning actions for finalization**


  I need to write my findings for EID 445 into the research log addendum. I may also reply to Archive #10 to finalize that result. I should check Token Management to consider pruning older tokens, but it might not be necessary since we''ve done a lot already. We could prune ticks 246-253, though tick 253 has a high token count right now, and I can''t prune just yet due to an active cooldown. I might pause further experimentation and suggest a new direction.**Finalizing tasks**


  I’ll write an update to the CCR addendum, including the details for EID 445 and marking it as closed. I plan to reply to Archive #10 with the final CCR results. The plan is to avoid further minor tweaks. I need to make sure to quote YAML lines containing colons properly. I’ll execute the necessary actions, such as writing to the research log and replying to the archive. I might also consider reflecting on methods like "batch-contrastive graph weighting" but could skip that for now.'
