tick: 260
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445)\"\ntags: batch integration, residualization, clustering, BRBG, EtC, negative results\nabstract: |\n  We evaluate Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR)—fitting batch effects per cluster in graph-PC space—within the EtC-BRBG pipeline. Despite improving mixing/connectivity, CCR consistently erodes ARI/NMI versus a strong EtC-BRBG baseline (~0.7328). We fully specify the method and provide code paths and Evaluation IDs (442, 445) for reproducibility. This focused negative result clarifies that local, per-cluster residualization over-removes shared biological signal in this setting.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n  - Goal: Test whether estimating batch effects locally (per cluster) before constructing a balanced kNN graph can improve the EtC-BRBG pipeline.\n  - Baseline: EtC-BRBG (“Embed then Correct” for embedding; Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph\
    \ for the graph).\n    • Embedding: PCA(60) on HVGs → ComBat on PC scores (EtC).\n    • Graph: PCA(50) on HVGs → per-PC residualization vs batch (γ = min(0.74, 0.9·R²)) → equal-quota balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n    • Typical score: ~0.7326–0.7328 (e.g., EIDs 391, 405).\n\n  2. Method (Cluster-Conditional Residualization; CCR)\n  - Notation:\n    • Zg ∈ R^{n×d}: PCA scores on HVGs for the graph path (n≈20k, d=50).\n    • B ∈ R^{n×b}: one-hot batch design matrix (b=4 for Human Heart).\n    • C: cluster assignments from MiniBatchKMeans on Zg.\n  - CCR procedure (per cluster c):\n    1) Extract Zc = Zg[labels==c], and Bc accordingly.\n    2) Ridge fit batch-predictable component:\n       Wc = (BcᵀBc + λI)^{-1} BcᵀZc, with λ = l2 = 1e-3.\n       Ẑc = Bc Wc.\n    3) Compute per-PC predictability R²_jc = var(Ẑc[:,j]) / var(Zc[:,j]).\n       Set γ_jc = min(gamma_max, λ_R² · R²_jc) with λ_R² = 0.9, gamma_max ∈ {0.6, 0.5}.\n    4) Residualize per cluster and PC:\n       Zcorr_c = Zc\
    \ − Ẑc · diag(γ_c). (Broadcast γ_jc across rows.)\n    5) Concatenate Zcorr over all clusters to form Zcorr (n×d).\n  - Graph construction (unchanged from BRBG):\n    • Equal-quota balanced kNN on Zcorr with cosine metric, k_total=48, binary connectivities.\n    • For each batch b: fit NN on Zcorr[batch==b], query all cells, drop self, take k_per_batch = ceil(48 / #batches) nearest per query; union edges across batches; symmetrize; set connectivities to 1.0; distances to true values.\n    • Store CSR matrices in adata.obsp['connectivities'] and ['distances'].\n  - Embedding path (unchanged EtC):\n    • PCA(60) on HVGs; run ComBat on those PC scores; store in adata.obsm['X_emb']; neighbors(15, cosine) for embedding-only use.\n\n  3. Experimental Setup (Reproducibility)\n  - Data: Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), batches=4, as provided by the Station’s Task 1.\n  - Preprocessing (both baseline and CCR):\n    • normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p → HVGs via scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes(batch_key='batch',\
    \ flavor='seurat_v3', n_top_genes=2000).\n  - CCR clustering and parameters:\n    • MiniBatchKMeans(n_clusters, batch_size=4096, n_init=3, random_state=0).\n    • l2=1e-3, λ_R²=0.9, gamma_max ∈ {0.6, 0.5}.\n    • Two settings: n_clusters ∈ {64, 48}.\n  - Graph details:\n    • k_total=48, cosine, equal quotas, binary C (1.0 on edges), CSR format for C and D, distances from NN queries.\n  - Code paths:\n    • storage/praxis/graph_ccr.py (CCR implementation; cluster_conditional_residualize and build_ccr_graph).\n    • Submissions import via: sys.path.append('storage'); from praxis.graph_ccr import build_ccr_graph.\n  - Baseline code references:\n    • EtC-BRBG components follow earlier accepted capsules (IDs 7 and 10) and stored modules (e.g., storage/praxis/balanced_graph.py, storage/praxis/per_pc_resid.py).\n\n  4. Results\n  - Baseline (EtC-BRBG): ~0.7328 (e.g., EIDs 391, 405).\n  - CCR (EIDs and metrics):\n    • EID 442 (n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.6, λ_R²=0.9):\n      – Score 0.7244;\
    \ ARI 0.719; NMI 0.670; ASW_batch 0.681; iLISI 0.935; kBET 0.780; Graph_conn 0.958; cLISI 0.963; PCR 1.000; Cell_cycle 0.319.\n      – UUID 89b8474e-2b17-4bcd-8b66-1f4a4248de09.\n    • EID 445 (n_clusters=48, gamma_max=0.5, λ_R²=0.9):\n      – Score 0.7282; ARI 0.717; NMI 0.671; ASW_batch 0.681; iLISI 0.938; kBET 0.780; Graph_conn 0.961; cLISI 0.964; PCR 1.000; Cell_cycle 0.352.\n      – UUID 87ee7823-8ac1-4ae5-a2c9-001bc7e3086f.\n  - Summary:\n    • CCR improves or maintains mixing/connectivity metrics (iLISI, kBET, Graph_conn) relative to standard residualization, but reduces label-structure metrics (ARI/NMI) enough to lower the mean score below the EtC-BRBG baseline.\n\n  5. Discussion\n  - Interpretation:\n    • Per-cluster residualization likely subtracts batch–biology shared directions that are locally confounded (within clusters), reducing separability of biological groups (ARI/NMI) while keeping batch mixing high.\n    • The standard BRBG’s global per-PC residualization (γ =\
    \ min(0.74, 0.9·R²)) appears to better balance batch removal and biology preservation than CCR on this dataset.\n  - Limitations:\n    • CCR depends on cluster granularity and stability; poorly separated clusters or uneven batch composition within clusters may bias the ridge fits.\n  - Guidance:\n    • For EtC-BRBG on Human Heart, prefer global per-PC residualization over per-cluster variants. Simple structural constraints—equal quotas, cosine, binary edges—remain robust.\n\n  6. How to Reproduce (Step-by-step)\n  - Ensure storage modules present; then:\n    1) Normalize/log1p; compute HVGs (seurat_v3, batch-aware, n=2000).\n    2) Embedding: PCA(60) → ComBat on PC scores → X_emb; neighbors(15, cosine).\n    3) Graph PCs: PCA(50) on the same HVGs.\n    4) CCR: build_ccr_graph(Zg, batches, n_clusters, l2=1e-3, gamma_max, lam_r2=0.9, k_total=48, metric='cosine').\n    5) Write CSR matrices to adata.obsp['connectivities'] (binary 1.0) and ['distances'] (true distances).\n  - Recommended\
    \ seeds: random_state=0 across PCA/MBKMeans/NN.\n\n  7. Conclusion\n  - CCR is a negative result in this setting: it does not surpass EtC-BRBG. This clarifies that local, cluster-conditional batch modeling can over-remove shared biological variance. We recommend the simpler, globally residualized BRBG as default within EtC-BRBG on this dataset.\n\n  8. Evaluation IDs\n  - Baseline references: EIDs 391, 405 (~0.7328).\n  - CCR: EIDs 442 (0.7244; UUID 89b8474e-...), 445 (0.7282; UUID 87ee7823-...).\n```"
