parts:
- text: "/execute_action{review 451}\n\n/execute_action{review 452}\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/ccr_paper_draft.md}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title: Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG: A Reproducible Negative Result\n\n  Introduction\n  We test whether estimating batch effects locally (per cluster) in graph-PC space (Zg) improves EtC-BRBG. Hypothesis: local residualization might remove confounded batch signal without eroding biology.\n\n  Methods\n  Pipeline:\n  - Preprocess: normalize_total(1e4) → log1p → HVGs (seurat_v3, batch-aware, n=2000).\n  - Embedding (EtC): PCA(60) on HVGs → ComBat on the 60 PCs → X_emb; neighbors(15, cosine).\n  - Graph (BRBG baseline): PCA(50) on HVGs → global per-PC residualization vs batch with γ_j = min(0.74, 0.9·R²_j) → equal-quota balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n  - CCR (this work): Cluster-conditional residualization in Zg:\n      * MiniBatchKMeans (n_clusters).\n      * Ridge per cluster: Wc=(BcᵀBc+λI)^{-1}BcᵀZc, λ=1e-3; Ẑc=BcWc.\n\
    \      * Per-PC R²_jc = var(Ẑc_j)/var(Zc_j); γ_jc = min(gamma_max, 0.9·R²_jc).\n      * Zcorr_c = Zc − Ẑc·diag(γ_c). Concatenate Zcorr across clusters.\n      * Build equal-quota balanced kNN on Zcorr (k_total=48; cosine; binary connectivities; CSR distances/connectivities).\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - Code: storage/praxis/graph_ccr.py (cluster_conditional_residualize, build_ccr_graph); submissions add sys.path.append('storage') then import praxis.graph_ccr.\n  - Seeds: random_state=0 for PCA/MBKMeans/NN. Batches: adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').\n\n  Results (Human Heart ~20k×2k; baseline EtC-BRBG ≈0.7328)\n  Completed:\n  - EID 442: n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.6 → Score 0.7244\n      ARI 0.719 | NMI 0.670 | ASW_batch 0.681 | iLISI 0.935 | kBET 0.780 | Graph_conn 0.958 | cLISI 0.963 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.319\n  - EID 445: n_clusters=48, gamma_max=0.5 → Score 0.7282\n      ARI 0.717 | NMI 0.671 | ASW_batch 0.681 | iLISI 0.938 | kBET 0.780 | Graph_conn 0.961 | cLISI 0.964 |\
    \ PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.352\n  - EID 448: n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.7 → Score 0.7255\n      ARI 0.721 | NMI 0.671 | ASW_batch 0.681 | iLISI 0.935 | kBET 0.781 | Graph_conn 0.958 | cLISI 0.963 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.327\n  - EID 449: n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.8 → Score 0.7270\n      ARI 0.720 | NMI 0.669 | ASW_batch 0.681 | iLISI 0.935 | kBET 0.782 | Graph_conn 0.958 | cLISI 0.963 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.343\n\n  Pending (to reach ≥5 experiments):\n  - EID 451: n_clusters=96, gamma_max=0.6 → pending\n  - EID 452: n_clusters=32, gamma_max=0.7 → pending\n\n  Summary\n  Across four completed CCR settings, mixing/connectivity (iLISI/kBET/Graph_conn) remain comparable to baseline, but ARI/NMI consistently drop, reducing the overall mean vs EtC-BRBG (~0.7328). Pending runs (n_clusters=96, 32) will complete the ≥5 experiment requirement and test cluster granularity. Expectation: trend holds; CCR is a negative line for this dataset.\n\n  Discussion\n  Likely cause: local residualization\
    \ subtracts batch–biology shared directions within clusters, eroding label structure while preserving mixing. The global per-PC residualization used in BRBG better balances removal vs preservation under EtC.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Deciding next steps**\n\nI need to figure out what to do next based on the latest user message. We currently have two CCR runs, 451 and 452, underway. In total, we have experiments 442, 445, 448, 449, and these two will potentially give us 6 or 7 after evaluation. I'll wait for the results before making any changes to the paper, but I can prepare the revised content centered on the CCR with those 5 experiments in mind.**Planning next steps for Archive**\n\nI can start preparing a reply for the Archive by using \"create\" again. The Archive needs both an abstract and content. It might be better to wait for the results to include actual EIDs instead of placeholders. \n\nI should review CCR runs 451 and 452 to see if they’re finished. If they aren't, I can work on the paper content with what we have so far, mentioning experiments 442, 445, 448, and 449, plus two more with EIDs pending. Meanwhile, I’ll save a draft of the revised paper and handle Token Management.**Reviewing\
  \ Archive actions**\n\nI see that the Cooldown might be active, but I'm considering pruning again since the message mentions a \"3 ticks cooldown earlier.\" It might be available now, but I’m not sure it’s necessary. \n\nOnce we get results, we should resubmit the Archive. We could also check the archive preview to read others, but that isn't essential. My response should be concise, including sequential commands with `/execute_action` and a YAML block if creating a storage file, keeping the formatting minimal.**Proposing next steps**\n\nI want to propose a few actions here. First, I'll review runs 451 and 452. Next, I’ll write a storage file called praxis/ccr_paper_draft.md, setting up a full structure with placeholders for metrics, including an initial table for the first four EIDs and blank rows for 451 and 452. Once I receive the review results, I’ll submit the updated Archive content. \n\nI’ll summarize the message indicating that I'm running 451 and 452 and outline the process to\
  \ revise the CCR paper. Then, I’ll execute the review commands and write the draft to storage.**Including measured results**\n\nI want to make sure we include the measured results we have so far: 442 scored 0.7244, 445 scored 0.7282, 448 at 0.7255, and 449 at 0.7270 (approximately). I plan to add full normalized metrics for these experiments as recorded in the System Messages into a Markdown table summarizing the EIDs and metrics. \n\nOnce results for 451 and 452 are available, I can update that information and resubmit. We might also consider planning EIDs 453 and 454 if we need exactly five, but since we already have six, it's manageable. Let’s keep everything concise as we proceed!"
