role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452)\"\ntags: batch integration, residualization, clustering, BRBG, EtC, negative results\nabstract: |\n  We evaluate Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR)—fitting batch effects per cluster in the graph-PC space—within the EtC-BRBG pipeline. Across six experiments spanning cluster counts and residualization strengths, CCR consistently underperforms a strong EtC-BRBG baseline (≈0.7328). While CCR maintains or slightly improves mixing/connectivity (iLISI, kBET, Graph_conn), it erodes ARI/NMI, lowering the composite score. We specify the method fully and provide code paths and EIDs/UUIDs for reproducibility.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n  Batch integration must remove technical variation while preserving biological structure. The EtC-BRBG pipeline separates global\
    \ embedding optimization (“Embed then Correct” via ComBat on PCs) from local graph optimization (Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph on residualized PCs). We test a targeted hypothesis: estimating batch effects locally in the graph space by clustering first and residualizing within clusters (Cluster-Conditional Residualization, CCR) may better remove confounded batch signal without harming biology.\n\n  2. Methods\n  2.1 Data and Preprocessing\n  - Dataset: Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs; 4 batches) from the Task 1 specification.\n  - Preprocessing: normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p → HVGs with scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes(batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3', n_top_genes=2000).\n\n  2.2 Baseline (EtC-BRBG)\n  - Embedding (EtC): PCA(60) on HVGs → ComBat on the 60 PC scores → store as adata.obsm['X_emb']; sc.pp.neighbors(…, n_neighbors=15, metric='cosine') for embedding-only use.\n  - Graph (BRBG): PCA(50) on HVGs → global per-PC batch residualization:\n      γ_j = min(0.74,\
    \ 0.9·R²_j), where R²_j = var(BW_j)/var(Z_j) from ridge (l2=1e-3) of Z ~ B (one-hot batches).\n    Build equal-quota balanced kNN on Zcorr with cosine distance, k_total=48. For each batch b:\n      fit NN on Zcorr[batch==b], query all cells, drop self, take k_per_batch = ceil(48 / n_batches), union edges across batches, symmetrize; connectivities are binary (1.0) and distances are true NN distances; both stored as CSR in adata.obsp['connectivities'] and ['distances'].\n  - Typical baseline score: ≈0.7326–0.7328 (e.g., EIDs 391, 405). Example baseline metrics (EID 391): Score 0.7328; ARI 0.744; NMI 0.703; Graph_conn 0.946; iLISI 0.935.\n\n  2.3 Proposed Method: Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR)\n  - Graph PCs: Zg ∈ R^{n×d} with d=50 from PCA on HVGs.\n  - Clustering: MiniBatchKMeans(n_clusters, batch_size=4096, n_init=3, random_state=0) on Zg; labels C.\n  - Per-cluster ridge fit: For cluster c, with Zc (rows of Zg in c) and one-hot batches Bc,\n      Wc = (BcᵀBc + λI)^{-1} Bcᵀ\
    \ Zc (λ = 1e-3), Ẑc = Bc Wc.\n      Per-PC predictability R²_jc = var(Ẑc[:,j]) / var(Zc[:,j]).\n      γ_jc = min(gamma_max, 0.9·R²_jc).\n      Zcorr_c = Zc − Ẑc · diag(γ_c). Concatenate Zcorr across clusters to Zcorr.\n  - Graph construction: same equal-quota balanced kNN as the baseline, but on Zcorr.\n\n  2.4 Reproducibility and Code\n  - Storage module: storage/praxis/graph_ccr.py provides cluster_conditional_residualize and build_ccr_graph.\n  - Submissions import with: sys.path.append('storage'); from praxis.graph_ccr import build_ccr_graph.\n  - Seeds: random_state=0 for PCA/MBKMeans/NN. Batches: adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').\n\n  3. Results\n  Baseline (EtC-BRBG) reference:\n  - EID 391 (Score 0.7328; ARI 0.744; NMI 0.703; Graph_conn 0.946; iLISI 0.935).\n  - EID 405 (Score 0.7328; similar metrics).\n\n  CCR experiments (six settings):\n  - EID 442 (n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.6): Score 0.7244; ARI 0.719; NMI 0.670; Graph_conn 0.958; iLISI 0.935; UUID 89b8474e-2b17-4bcd-8b66-1f4a4248de09.\n\
    \  - EID 445 (n_clusters=48, gamma_max=0.5): Score 0.7282; ARI 0.717; NMI 0.671; Graph_conn 0.961; iLISI 0.938; UUID 87ee7823-8ac1-4ae5-a2c9-001bc7e3086f.\n  - EID 448 (n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.7): Score 0.7255; ARI 0.721; NMI 0.671; Graph_conn 0.958; iLISI 0.935; UUID ee83ccd4-154e-47f3-8d32-a57115947e64.\n  - EID 449 (n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.8): Score 0.7270; ARI 0.720; NMI 0.669; Graph_conn 0.958; iLISI 0.935; UUID a9b26001-a925-4aa1-b913-ff1f4f3a7522.\n  - EID 451 (n_clusters=96, gamma_max=0.6): Score 0.7231; ARI 0.724; NMI 0.682; Graph_conn 0.959; iLISI 0.935; UUID 73fe1ddb-9ff0-4895-8089-5f565b1af185.\n  - EID 452 (n_clusters=32, gamma_max=0.7): Score 0.7261; ARI 0.716; NMI 0.670; Graph_conn 0.963; iLISI 0.935; UUID 705c6bf4-c51c-444d-b177-4661bc239f36.\n\n  Aggregate observation:\n  - Mixing/connectivity (iLISI, kBET, Graph_conn) are comparable to or slightly above the baseline, but ARI/NMI decrease in every CCR setting, reducing the composite score below ≈0.7328.\n\n  4.\
    \ Discussion\n  - Interpretation: Cluster-conditional residualization likely subtracts batch–biology shared directions that are locally confounded within clusters, eroding label structure while preserving batch mixing. The global, per-PC residualization in BRBG better balances removal and preservation in this dataset.\n  - Limitations: CCR depends on cluster granularity and per-cluster batch composition; small or skewed clusters may yield unstable ridge fits and exaggerated γ_jc.\n  - Guidance: For Human Heart under EtC-BRBG, prefer the simple global residualization scheme and the equal-quotas balanced graph; CCR is not recommended.\n\n  5. How to Reproduce (Step-by-Step)\n  1) normalize_total → log1p → HVGs (2k; seurat_v3, batch-aware).\n  2) Embedding: PCA(60) → ComBat on PC scores; store X_emb; neighbors(15, cosine).\n  3) Graph PCs: PCA(50) on the same HVGs to get Zg.\n  4) CCR: build_ccr_graph(Zg, batches, n_clusters∈{32,48,64,96}, l2=1e-3, gamma_max∈{0.5,0.6,0.7,0.8}, lam_r2=0.9,\
    \ k_total=48, metric='cosine').\n  5) Write adata.obsp['distances'] (true NN distances) and adata.obsp['connectivities'] (binary 1.0), both as CSR matrices.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n  Cluster-Conditional Residualization is ineffective on this dataset under the EtC-BRBG framework: it maintains batch mixing but consistently reduces ARI/NMI, lowering the composite score relative to a strong baseline. This negative result helps focus future work on alternatives that preserve biological structure without sacrificing mixing.\n```"
