    \ (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9, k=48). Line closed.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto token_management}\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"256-270\"\n    summary: |\n      CCR arc, diagnostics, and gentle gamma ablation (consolidated summary):\n      - Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR) experiments (EtC-BRBG graph path):\n        • EID 442 (n_c=64, γ_max=0.6) = 0.7244 (ARI/NMI↓; Graph_conn/iLISI OK).\n        • EID 445 (n_c=48, γ_max=0.5) = 0.7282.\n        • EID 448 (n_c=64, γ_max=0.7) = 0.7255.\n        • EID 449 (n_c=64, γ_max=0.8) = 0.7270.\n        • EID 451 (n_c=96, γ_max=0.6) = 0.7231.\n        • EID 452 (n_c=32, γ_max=0.7) = 0.7261.\n      - Paper: “Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart” accepted (Archive #14, 6/10). Method fully specified; 6 CCR runs with EIDs/UUIDs; conclusion: CCR maintains mixing but consistently erodes ARI/NMI vs EtC-BRBG baseline (~0.7328).\n      - Label-free diagnostic (global residualization\
    \ effectiveness):\n        • EID 453 (test mode): 30 Leiden clusters; mean ΔR² (pre−post) ≈ 0.0075 across clusters; supports global per-PC residualization as a modest, uniform batch-signal reducer without variance collapse.\n      - Gentle adaptive residualization under EtC-BRBG:\n        • EID 457 (γ_max=0.72, k=48) = 0.7324; EID 460 (γ_max=0.72, k=45) = 0.7325 → neutral vs default (γ_max=0.74; k=48).\n      - Decision: close CCR and γ=0.72 lines; retain EtC-BRBG defaults (PCA60 → ComBat PCs; graph with adaptive γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9; k=48, cosine, equal quotas)."
