tick: 272
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I. Location: Reflection Chamber. Recap: EtC-BRBG is stable at ~0.7326–0.7328; many conditioning tweaks and CCR were neutral/negative. Archives #12 and #13 report no synergy of Harmony with BRBG and harms from latent correlation minimization, respectively. I’ll propose three distinct, minimal-parameter, label-free ideas compatible with EtC-BRBG’s separation of concerns.\n\nIdea 1 — Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG)\n- What: After constructing the equal-quotas balanced kNN on Zcorr, reduce hubness by column-balancing cross-batch edges within each batch block via a single light Sinkhorn-like scaling, then binarize back to fixed degree per node.\n- Rationale: Equal quotas balance rows (each node takes k_per_batch per batch) but can leave column imbalance (popular “hub” targets). Soft column balancing should distribute connections more evenly across targets without enforcing reciprocity (which was harmful).\n- Procedure (one pass):\n  1) For each batch b, form adjacency\
    \ block A_b (rows: all queries; cols: nodes in batch b; entries 1 for selected edges).\n  2) Compute target column sums: roughly k_per_batch·n_rows/n_cols_b (or cap by percentile to avoid forcing tiny batches).\n  3) One Sinkhorn-like step: scale columns by s so col_sums move toward target; rescale rows to preserve row sum = k_per_batch (optional light renorm).\n  4) For each row, keep top k_per_batch entries by scaled weight, binarize; union across batches; symmetrize; keep distances from original graph.\n- Expected metrics:\n  - iLISI/kBET: ↑ or ≈ (fewer hubs, more even cross-batch mix).\n  - Graph_conn: ≈ (avoid over-sparsification; degree preserved).\n  - ARI/NMI/cLISI: ≈ (structure unchanged; still nearest neighbors; no reweighting at evaluation).\n- Risks:\n  - If column balancing suppresses true nearest edges, local structure may blur (ARI/NMI↓).\n- Tiny ablation: column target dampening α ∈ {0.8, 1.0} (default 1.0).\n- Go/no-go: accept if mean ≥ +0.002 vs baseline and ARI/NMI\
    \ drop ≤ 0.003; Graph_conn ≥ 0.94; iLISI not worse.\n\nIdea 2 — Dual-Temperature Neighbors (DTN)\n- What: Build two balanced kNN graphs on Zcorr using slightly different angular sharpness, then fuse by union with a per-node cap to retain k_total.\n- Rationale: A single scale may miss either very fine or slightly broader cross-batch alignments. A union of “normal” and “sharpened” angular neighborhoods (cosine transformed) can capture both without weights; degree cap preserves sparsity and prevents drift.\n- Procedure:\n  1) G0: standard balanced graph with cosine distance.\n  2) Gτ: recompute neighbors under a sharpened score, e.g., rank by (1 − cos)^τ with τ = 1.1 (monotone but changes ordering).\n  3) Union edges at node level; if degree > k_total, keep the k_total edges with smallest original cosine distances.\n  4) Binarize; distances remain the original cosine distances from G0 where available (or min of the two).\n- Expected metrics:\n  - iLISI/kBET: ↑ (capturing complementary neighbors),\
    \ Graph_conn: ≈, ARI/NMI: ≈.\n- Risks:\n  - If union introduces slightly farther edges and truncation drops some close ones, ARI/NMI could dip.\n- Tiny ablation: τ ∈ {1.05, 1.1} (default 1.1).\n- Go/no-go: same thresholds as above.\n\nIdea 3 — Batch-Mean Centered PCA for Graph PCs (BM-PCA)\n- What: Before PCA(50) for the graph path only, subtract per-batch gene-wise means from the normalized/log1p HVG matrix; then PCA → adaptive residualization → balanced graph. Embedding path (EtC) is unchanged.\n- Rationale: Remove pure location batch effects upstream so PCA axes reflect within-batch covariance; adaptive residualization then deals with remaining batch-predictable variance. Simpler than ComBat and avoids additional knobs on the graph side.\n- Procedure:\n  1) X_norm = log1p(normalize_total(counts)).\n  2) For each batch b and gene g in HVGs: X_norm[b,g] ← X_norm[b,g] − mean_b(g).\n  3) PCA(50) on centered HVGs → Zg; adaptive residualization (γ_max=0.74, λ_R2=0.9) → Zcorr; build equal-quotas\
    \ balanced kNN as usual.\n- Expected metrics:\n  - ASW_batch/kBET/iLISI: ↑ or ≈ (less mean shift), Graph_conn: ≈, ARI/NMI: ≈ (affine de-meaning preserves within-batch ranks).\n- Risks:\n  - If biology manifests as genuine mean differences across batches (composition), centering may slightly erode ARI/NMI; residualization may compound it.\n- Tiny ablation: blend λ_c ∈ {1.0, 0.8} where X_centered ← λ_c·(X − batch_mean) + (1−λ_c)·X.\n- Go/no-go: same thresholds; careful to monitor ARI/NMI.\n\nGeneral guardrails and plan\n- Keep embedding path fixed (EtC PCA60→ComBat PCs). Only alter graph construction on Zcorr or pre-PCA graph input (BM-PCA).\n- Degree stays k_total=48; cosine; equal quotas preserved (BBSG/DTN do not change quota logic).\n- Stop if ARI or NMI drops > 0.005 or iLISI/kBET deteriorate; document negatives with EIDs.\n- If one idea clears +0.002 mean without harming ARI/NMI, port to a second confirmatory run (e.g., k=45) to check ridge behavior."
