role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I. Location: Reflection Chamber. Recap: EtC-BRBG holds ~0.7326–0.7328; stitching/local-scaling/SBT/BCE/LCCM/CCR were neutral/negative. Archives #12 (Harmony+BRBG: no synergy) and #13 (LCCM: decorrelation hurts local mixing) caution against heavy latent post-processing. I’m detailing three minimal, label-free ideas (batch labels allowed) that respect EtC-BRBG’s separation of concerns.\n\nImplementation details, pitfalls, and diagnostics\n\n1) Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG)\n- Core: Start from the standard equal-quotas graph on Zcorr (binary). Balance inbound usage of targets within each batch block without altering per-row quotas. Then re-binarize.\n- Block structure and scaling:\n  - For each batch b, take the adjacency block A_b (rows: all queries; cols: targets in b) with entries in {0,1}.\n  - Compute column sums c_j = ∑_rows A_b[:, j]. Target inbound per column ≈ t = k_per_batch · (n_rows / n_cols_b). To avoid forcing tiny batches, use damped target t’\
    \ = α·t + (1−α)·median(c) with α in {1.0 (default), 0.8}.\n  - One light column scaling: s_j = min(cap, t’/max(c_j, 1)), cap e.g. 2.0. Score edges (i,j) by w_ij = s_j (no row scaling). For each row, keep top k_per_batch columns by w_ij; set those edges to 1, others 0.\n  - Union across batches, symmetrize, distances = original cosine distances for those edges.\n- Complexity: O(E) for sums + O(E log k_pb) worst-case per row selection; memory minimal (operate per block).\n- Pitfalls:\n  - If cap too high or α=1 with very unbalanced c_j, you can suppress true nearest targets → ARI/NMI↓. Use a single pass, moderate cap, and reselect per-row top-k.\n  - Never reweight at evaluation; always binarize before writing obsp.\n- Diagnostics:\n  - Column in-degree CV per batch block (expect ↓).\n  - Degree per node ~ k_total (± overlap).\n  - Cross-batch fraction per node ~ (B−1)/B (unchanged).\n  - Accept if mean ≥ baseline +0.002, ARI/NMI drop ≤ 0.003, Graph_conn ≥ 0.94, iLISI not worse.\n\n2)\
    \ Dual-Temperature Neighbors (DTN)\n- Core: Two balanced graphs on Zcorr using slightly different angular orderings; union then cap degree back to k_total, preserving binary structure.\n- Scoring/ordering:\n  - G0: standard cosine neighbors (distance d0 = 1−cos).\n  - Gτ: sharpened ordering with dτ = (1−cos)^τ, τ ∈ {1.05, 1.1} (default 1.1). Monotone transform reorders but doesn’t change geometry.\n  - Union edges per node; if degree > k_total, keep k_total edges with smallest original d0 (protect standard nearest).\n  - Symmetrize; distances = min(d0 over incident edges) for consistency (or store original d0 of the kept edge).\n- Complexity: 2× nearest-neighbor fits/queries per batch; still linear in batches. Memory: two adjacency sets plus a union pass.\n- Pitfalls:\n  - If union admits many mid-distance edges, truncation might evict a few very close ones; using d0 for truncation mitigates this.\n  - Do not average/weight edges; remain binary to avoid magnitude bias noted previously.\n\
    - Diagnostics:\n  - Edge Jaccard(G0,Gτ) per node; degree check; iLISI/kBET vs baseline.\n  - Same acceptance thresholds as above.\n\n3) Batch-Mean Centered PCA for Graph PCs (BM-PCA)\n- Core: Only for the graph path, pre-center normalized/log1p HVGs by per-batch gene means, then PCA(50) → adaptive residualization (γ_max=0.74, λ_R2=0.9) → balanced graph. Embedding (EtC) untouched.\n- Computation:\n  - X_norm = log1p(normalize_total(counts)); restrict to HVGs (2k).\n  - For each batch b: μ_b ∈ R^{p} = column-wise mean over HVG genes; set X_norm[b,:] ← X_norm[b,:] − μ_b (blend optional).\n  - PCA(50) on centered HVGs → Zg; proceed as usual with adaptive per-PC residualization and equal-quotas kNN.\n  - Memory: 20k×2k float32 ≈ 160 MB; if tight, process in-place or per-batch subtract on CSR->dense slices.\n- Pitfalls:\n  - If genuine biology manifests as mean shifts, centering can shave ARI/NMI. Provide blend λ_c ∈ {1.0 (default), 0.8}: X_centered ← λ_c·(X−μ_b)+(1−λ_c)·X.\n  - Ensure no\
    \ ComBat on graph path (keeps separation).\n- Diagnostics:\n  - Across-batch per-gene mean variance ↓; PC-wise batch R² (pre vs post) ↓ without PR collapse; iLISI/kBET ≥ baseline.\n  - Acceptance as above.\n\nGeneral safeguards and logging\n- Embedding path fixed: PCA60 on HVGs → ComBat on PCs → X_emb; neighbors(15) for keys only.\n- Graph path always from Zcorr (or BM-PCA→Zg→Zcorr), equal-quotas k_total=48, cosine, binary; write both obsp['distances'] (CSR float) and obsp['connectivities'] (CSR binary).\n- Print to stdout:\n  - Config (idea, α/τ/λ_c), counts (n_cells, n_HVG, k_total, k_per_batch), in-degree CV per block (BBSG), Jaccard(G0,Gτ) and retained fraction (DTN), Δmean variance per gene (BM-PCA), and PC batch R² summary.\n- Abort rules:\n  - If ARI or NMI ↓ > 0.005 or iLISI/kBET ↓ with no compensating gains, stop that idea (skip second setting).\n  - If degree deviates > 2 from k_total on median node or Graph_conn < 0.94, fix construction or abort.\n\nExecution order (cheapest\
    \ → more invasive)\n- DTN τ=1.1 → if promising, confirm τ=1.05 and k=45 ridge.\n- BBSG α=1.0 (single scaling pass, cap=2.0) → if promising, confirm α=0.8.\n- BM-PCA λ_c=1.0 → if promising, confirm λ_c=0.8 and k=45 ridge.\n\nWhy these differ from past negatives\n- We keep structure binary and quotas intact (avoids weight bias that hurt before).\n- We do not touch global embedding or add heavy post-hoc latent constraints (addressing #13’s pitfalls).\n- We refine neighbor selection very locally (DTN/BBSG) or remove only batch means upstream (BM-PCA), keeping adaptive residualization as the main variance cleaner."
