tick: 272
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I. Location: Reflection Chamber. Recap: EtC-BRBG is a stable baseline for Human Heart (~0.7326–0.7328) with PCA60→ComBat(PCs) for X_emb and an adaptive-residualized PC50 graph with equal-quotas k=48 (cosine, binary). Extensive lines were neutral/negative: local scaling, SBT, BCE/CORAL, latent decorrelation (LCCM), Harmony+BRBG synergy, graph stitching, gamma micro-tweaks, and cluster-conditional residualization (CCR). SOTA EtC is ~0.7355 (replicated ~0.7347–0.7353). New archives #12 and #13 reinforce that heavy latent post-processing or cross-method grafting tends to hurt local mixing or add no value. I will pursue three minimal, label-free (biology-label-free; batch labels allowed) ideas that preserve EtC-BRBG’s separation of concerns and binary/quotas structure.\n\n1) Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG)\n- Rationale: Equal-quotas balance rows (each node takes k_per_batch neighbors per batch) but inbound usage (column sums) inside a batch block can be uneven (hubs).\
    \ A single light column-balancing step per batch block can smooth hubness without introducing weights at evaluation.\n- Method (single pass):\n  - Build the standard equal-quotas graph on Zcorr (adaptive residualized PCs). For each batch b, take the adjacency block A_b (rows: all queries; cols: targets in batch b).\n  - Compute col sums c_j. Target inbound per column t_base ≈ k_per_batch · n_rows / n_cols_b. Use damped target t = α·t_base + (1−α)·median(c) with α in {1.0 (default), 0.8}. Scale columns s_j = min(cap, t / max(c_j,1)) with a modest cap (≈2.0).\n  - For each row, reselect the top k_per_batch previously-chosen columns by s_j; set those edges to 1, others 0. Union across batches; symmetrize; keep the original cosine distances; connectivities remain binary.\n- Expected metrics: iLISI/kBET ↑ or ≈; Graph_conn ≈ (degree preserved); ARI/NMI/cLISI ≈ (nearest structure largely unchanged).\n- Risks/guardrails: Over-balancing could drop genuinely nearest targets (ARI/NMI↓). Use one\
    \ pass, α∈{1.0,0.8}, cap=2.0, and re-binarize. Accept only if mean ≥ +0.002 vs baseline, ARI/NMI drop ≤ 0.003, Graph_conn ≥ 0.94, iLISI not worse.\n- Diagnostics to log: per-batch-block in-degree CV before/after (expect ↓), fraction of edges changed, degree distribution, cross-batch fraction stability.\n\n2) Dual-Temperature Neighbors (DTN)\n- Rationale: One angular scale may miss either very fine or slightly broader cross-batch alignments. Build two balanced graphs with slightly different angular orderings and fuse by union, then cap degree back to k_total using the original distance ordering.\n- Method:\n  - G0: standard balanced kNN using distance d0 = 1−cos.\n  - Gτ: balanced kNN using dτ = (1−cos)^τ, τ in {1.05, 1.1} (default 1.1), a monotone sharpening that changes neighbor ranking slightly.\n  - Union edges per node; if degree > k_total, keep k_total edges with smallest original d0 (protects the standard nearest). Symmetrize; binary connectivities; store original distances.\n\
    - Expected metrics: iLISI/kBET mildly ↑; Graph_conn ≈; ARI/NMI ≈ (since capping honors nearest by d0).\n- Risks/guardrails: If union admits too many mid-distance edges, truncation might eject a few close ones; capping by d0 mitigates this. Same acceptance thresholds as above.\n- Diagnostics: per-node Jaccard(G0,Gτ), fraction of novel edges retained after capping, degree/cross-batch fraction, iLISI/kBET deltas.\n\n3) Batch-Mean Centered PCA for Graph PCs (BM-PCA)\n- Rationale: Remove per-gene batch mean offsets upstream of graph PCA (on normalized/log1p HVGs) to reduce pure location effects before adaptive residualization. Embedding (EtC) remains unchanged (separation intact).\n- Method:\n  - X_norm = log1p(normalize_total(counts)); restrict to HVGs. For each batch b and gene g, subtract the batch mean: X_norm[b,g] ← X_norm[b,g] − μ_b(g). Optional blend λ_c∈{1.0 (default), 0.8}: X_centered = λ_c·(X−μ_b)+(1−λ_c)·X.\n  - PCA(50) → Zg; adaptive residualization (γ_max=0.74, λ_R2=0.9) → Zcorr;\
    \ build equal-quotas k=48 graph (cosine, binary) as usual.\n- Expected metrics: ASW_batch/iLISI/kBET ↑ or ≈; Graph_conn ≈; ARI/NMI ≈ (affine centering preserves within-batch rank structure).\n- Risks/guardrails: If genuine biology appears as mean shifts, ARI/NMI may dip; start with λ_c=1.0; bail if ARI/NMI harm exceeds threshold. Same acceptance thresholds.\n- Diagnostics: across-batch per-gene mean variance ↓; PC-wise batch R² ↓ without PR collapse; iLISI/kBET stable or better.\n\nPlan (pre-registered)\n- Keep EtC embedding fixed (PCA60→ComBat PCs). Modify only the graph path. Strictly binary connectivities, equal quotas, cosine metric, k_total=48.\n- Order (cheapest/invasive): DTN (τ=1.1) → BBSG (α=1.0, cap=2.0) → BM-PCA (λ_c=1.0).\n- Acceptance: mean ≥ +0.002; ARI/NMI drop ≤ 0.003; Graph_conn ≥ 0.94; iLISI not worse. If promising, confirm at k=45 (ridge check). If any idea fails by ≤ −0.005 or violates ARI/NMI/iLISI guardrails, stop that idea (no second setting).\n- Reporting: log\
    \ config, in-degree CV/Jaccard/Δmean-variance and PC batch R² diagnostics; publish results (positive or negative) with EIDs/UUIDs, code paths, and one-paragraph insight on why it did/didn’t help.\n\nWhy these might work where others didn’t\n- They keep structure-first principles (binary edges, preserved quotas), avoid magnitude reweighting that previously damaged mixing, and do not impose heavy latent decorrelation (addressing #13’s failure mode). They target specific, observed weaknesses: inbound hubness (BBSG), single-scale neighbor bias (DTN), and pure location batch effects upstream of graph PCA (BM-PCA), while leaving adaptive residualization as the main variance cleaner.\n\nNext steps after reflection\n- Implement DTN (τ=1.1) and evaluate; if neutral/negative, proceed to BBSG (α=1.0); if still neutral, test BM-PCA (λ_c=1.0). For any pass, run a k=45 confirmation, then fold into a “+” variant of EtC-BRBG and document thoroughly. If all fail, publish a succinct negative-results note\
    \ to guide others away from these micro-adjustments."
