tick: 286
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis I. Location: Archive Room. Recap: I improved EtC-BRBG with BBSG (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) to 0.7362 (+0.0034 vs ~0.7328). Sensitivities: alpha=0.8 = 0.7347; cap=1.5 ≈ 0.73283; cap=2.5 ≈ 0.73265; k=45 = 0.7298. DMN was neutral (0.73266). I will publish a focused archive capsule documenting BBSG with full EIDs and code paths.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart\"\ntags: protocol, graph, balanced-knn, embed-then-correct\nabstract: \"We present Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG), a single-pass, per-batch column-balancing step applied to the equal-quotas balanced kNN graph on adaptive-residualized PCs (Zcorr). BBSG preserves quotas, degree, binary structure, and cosine distances while reducing inbound hubness. On the Human Heart dataset, BBSG improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 (+0.0034 over ~0.7328 baseline). Sensitivity shows alpha=0.8 remains > baseline; degree k=48 > k=45; caps 1.5\
    \ and 2.5 revert to baseline. We provide EIDs, UUIDs, and code paths, and freeze a practical default.\"\ncontent: |\n  1) Context and Baseline (EtC-BRBG)\n  - Embedding (EtC): PCA(60) on HVGs → ComBat on PC scores → X_emb (cosine neighbors only to set keys).\n  - Graph (BRBG): PCA(50) on HVGs → adaptive residualization on Zg (γ_max=0.74, λ_R2=0.9) → Zcorr → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary; write obsp['distances'] and ['connectivities']).\n  - Baseline mean ≈ 0.7326–0.7328 on our runs (e.g., EID 391 = 0.7328).\n\n  2) Method: Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG)\n  Goal: Reduce inbound hubness within each batch block while retaining quotas and binary edges.\n  Algorithm (single pass, per batch b):\n    - Start from the equal-quotas graph on Zcorr; adjacency block A_b has rows (all queries) and columns (targets in batch b).\n    - Compute column sums c_j; define a damped target inbound t = α·(k_per_batch·n_rows/n_cols_b) + (1−α)·median(c).\n    - Compute column scales s_j\
    \ = min(cap, t / max(c_j, 1)).\n    - For each row, reselect k_per_batch candidates by (s_j desc, distance asc); set those edges to 1, others 0.\n    - Union across batches; symmetrize; distances remain original cosine distances; connectivities stay binary.\n\n  Defaults: α=1.0, cap=2.0; k_total=48.\n\n  3) Results (Human Heart, ~20k cells × 2k HVGs)\n  Primary:\n  - EID 465 = 0.7362 (+0.0034 vs baseline)\n    Normalized: ASW_batch 0.681 | ASW_label 0.218 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.783 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.385\n    UUID: cfc86373-5fc8-40a9-af1e-30f36a616411\n\n  Sensitivity:\n  - α=0.8 (cap=2.0, k=48): EID 470 = 0.7347 (UUID 82e85c24-3651-41ce-b377-ba187b3581b6)\n  - cap=1.5 (α=1.0, k=48): EID 478 = 0.73283 (UUID 6fdaedf7-366f-4f7a-b2cc-d63e8e0b8ef8)\n  - cap=2.5 (α=1.0, k=48): EID 481 = 0.73265 (UUID 2591dc69-3428-442e-afaf-96692159d7d2)\n  - Degree sensitivity: k=45 (α=1.0, cap=2.0): EID 471 = 0.7298 (UUID 89edaa9f-059d-42d1-acec-4632b28c3a56),\
    \ mainly Cell_cycle↓\n\n  Neutral comparator:\n  - DMN (cosine ∪ euclidean union with per-batch quota cap by cosine): EID 477 = 0.73266 (UUID 7faaf1eb-b3ea-4462-9c81-077927540289) — tie with baseline.\n\n  4) Why BBSG Helps\n  Equal-quotas balance rows but can create “hub” targets (high in-degree) within batch blocks. A single light column-balancing step reduces these hubs, improving local cross-batch fairness without reweighting edges at evaluation or breaking quotas. Gains manifest as kBET↑ with iLISI and ARI/NMI stable.\n\n  5) Practical Default (Frozen)\n  - Use EtC-BRBG + BBSG with α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48.\n  - Code paths:\n    • BBSG builder: storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py\n    • Frozen pipeline: storage/praxis/best_etc_brbg_bbsg.py\n\n  6) Reproducibility\n  - EIDs / UUIDs: see above; all runs done on Human Heart task.\n  - We retain binary graphs (no weights) and strictly preserve per-batch quotas and distances.\n  - Additional logs and notes: storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bbsg_confirm.md,\
    \ storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bbsg_cap_sweep.md.\n\n  7) Limitations and Next Steps\n  - Degree matters (k=48 > k=45). Caps too mild (1.5) or too strong (2.5) revert to baseline.\n  - BBSG provides small, consistent gains in this setting; combining with orthogonal embedding improvements (e.g., FWPCA/EIGR lines by others) is an open direction, provided separation of concerns and binary/quotas principles remain intact.\n```"
